// New articles, 17 November 2026 (part 2): filling thinner subjects — arts, science, space, Earth, technology and Oceania.
ADDART({id:"violin",t:"The Violin and Its Family",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🎻",w:"Violin",r:["orchestra","musicw","guitar","mozart","opera"],
p:[["A singing instrument","The violin is a small stringed instrument played with a bow."],["A family","The violin family includes the viola, cello and double bass."],["Legendary makers","Violins by Stradivari are among the most valuable instruments ever made."]],
l:"The violin is a stringed instrument with four strings tuned in fifths, usually played by drawing a bow strung with horsehair across the strings, or by plucking them. It is the smallest and highest-pitched member of the violin family, which also includes the viola, the cello and the double bass, and it forms the largest section of the symphony orchestra. The violin developed in northern Italy in the early 16th century; the oldest surviving examples were made by Andrea Amati in Cremona around the 1550s–60s. Later Cremona makers, especially Antonio Stradivari and Giuseppe Guarneri ‘del Gesù’, made instruments in the 17th and 18th centuries that are still prized by top performers and can sell for millions of dollars. The violin is used in classical music, folk traditions from Ireland to India, country fiddling, jazz and pop. Famous violinists include Niccolò Paganini, whose dazzling technique made him a 19th-century celebrity.",
s:[["How it works","A violin’s body is a hollow box usually made of spruce for the top and maple for the back and sides. When the bow makes a string vibrate, the vibrations pass through a small wooden bridge to the body, which amplifies the sound. Inside, a thin ‘sound post’ and ‘bass bar’ shape the tone. Players change pitch by pressing strings against the fingerboard, which has no frets."],["The Cremona makers","Andrea Amati founded the Cremona tradition, followed by his grandson Nicolò Amati, who taught Antonio Stradivari. Stradivari made about 1,100 instruments in his long life, of which around 650 survive. Scientists have studied the wood, varnish and shape of these instruments, but blind tests suggest that many players cannot reliably tell them from the best modern violins."],["The family","The viola is slightly larger and lower than the violin. The cello is played sitting down, held between the knees, and the double bass, the largest, is played standing or on a tall stool. String quartets, made of two violins, a viola and a cello, became a favorite form for composers such as Haydn, Mozart and Beethoven."],["Fiddle traditions","‘Fiddle’ is another name for the violin, especially in folk music. Irish, Scottish, Scandinavian, Appalachian and Cajun fiddlers play lively dance tunes. In South India, the violin was adopted into Carnatic classical music in the 18th century and is played sitting on the floor."]],
k:[["Strings","Four, tuned G, D, A, E"],["Family","Violin, viola, cello, double bass"],["Origin","Northern Italy, early 1500s"],["Most famous maker","Antonio Stradivari (c. 1644–1737)"],["Bow hair","Horsehair"]],
d:[["c. 1530","Violins appear in northern Italy"],["c. 1560","Andrea Amati makes the oldest surviving violins"],["1700–1720","Stradivari’s ‘golden period’"],["1820","Paganini’s 24 Caprices published"],["2011","A Stradivari violin sells for about 15.9 million dollars"]],
s2:[["The bow","Modern bows, perfected by the French maker François Tourte around 1780s, curve inward and are made of pernambuco wood strung with about 150–200 horsehairs. Players rub the hair with rosin, a sticky resin, so it grips the strings."],["Paganini","Niccolò Paganini (1782–1840) played with such speed and showmanship that rumors claimed he had sold his soul to the devil. His 24 Caprices for solo violin remain a test for virtuosos."]],
sp:["The ‘Messiah’ Stradivarius","The ‘Messiah’ Stradivarius, made in 1716, is kept in the Ashmolean Museum in Oxford and has hardly ever been played, so it looks almost new. Its condition helps researchers understand how Stradivari’s instruments originally appeared."],
g:[["Bow","A stick strung with horsehair, used to play strings"],["Bridge","The wooden piece that holds strings above the body"],["Fiddle","Another name for the violin, especially in folk music"],["Virtuoso","A highly skilled performer"]],
f:["About 650 Stradivari instruments survive.","A violin has no frets.","The violin section is the largest in an orchestra."],
fun:"Violin strings were traditionally made from sheep gut, and some players still prefer gut strings for their warm sound."});

ADDART({id:"piano",t:"The Piano",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🎹",w:"Piano",r:["musicw","mozart","orchestra","jazz","violin"],
p:[["Soft and loud","The piano is a keyboard instrument whose hammers strike strings."],["Invented in Italy","Bartolomeo Cristofori built the first pianos around 1700."],["88 keys","A modern piano has 88 keys covering more than seven octaves."]],
l:"The piano is a keyboard instrument in which pressing a key makes a felt-covered hammer strike one or more metal strings, which vibrate to produce sound. Unlike the earlier harpsichord, which plucked its strings and could not change volume, the piano can play softly or loudly depending on how hard the keys are pressed, which gave it its original name, gravicembalo col piano e forte, ‘harpsichord with soft and loud’. It was invented by Bartolomeo Cristofori in Florence around 1700. Over the next 150 years, makers added iron frames, more keys and stronger strings, and the modern piano has 88 keys, about 230 strings under enormous tension and thousands of parts. Grand pianos have horizontal strings; upright pianos have vertical strings to save space. The piano became central to classical music, from Mozart, Beethoven and Chopin to Debussy, and to jazz, blues, pop and film music, and digital pianos now bring it into many homes.",
s:[["Invention","Cristofori, an instrument keeper for the Medici family, created an action, the mechanism linking key to hammer, that let the hammer strike the string and fall back immediately. Only three of his pianos survive, in New York, Rome and Leipzig."],["Development","Early pianos, called fortepianos, had wooden frames and a lighter sound. In the 19th century, cast-iron frames allowed thicker strings under greater tension, giving a louder, richer tone suited to concert halls. Steinway & Sons, founded in New York in 1853, and other firms developed the modern grand piano."],["How it works","Each key is a lever. When pressed, it throws a hammer at the strings and lifts a damper so the strings can ring. When released, the damper stops the sound. Most pianos have pedals: the right ‘sustain’ pedal lifts all dampers, and the left ‘soft’ pedal makes the sound quieter."],["The piano in music","Mozart and Beethoven wrote many piano sonatas and concertos, while Chopin and Liszt made the piano the star of Romantic music. Ragtime, jazz and blues pianists such as Scott Joplin and Duke Ellington created new styles. Upright pianos were once a common feature of family homes, used for entertainment before radio and recorded music."]],
k:[["Inventor","Bartolomeo Cristofori"],["Invented","About 1700, Florence"],["Keys","88"],["Strings","About 230"],["Main types","Grand and upright"]],
d:[["c. 1700","Cristofori builds the first piano"],["1770s","Mozart begins writing for the fortepiano"],["1825","Cast-iron frames introduced"],["1853","Steinway & Sons founded"],["1880s","88 keys become standard"],["1980s","Digital pianos spread"]],
s2:[["Tuning","The strings of a concert grand pull with a combined force of around 20 metric tons. Pianos need regular tuning because strings slowly stretch and wood responds to humidity. Piano tuners adjust each string with a special wrench."],["Player pianos","Player pianos, popular around 1900–1930, used rolls of punched paper to play music automatically. They were an early form of recorded music, and some rolls preserve the playing of famous composers."]],
sp:["The black and white keys","The pattern of 7 white and 5 black keys in each octave comes from Western scales. The white keys play the natural notes, and the black keys play sharps and flats. On some old harpsichords and pianos, the colors were reversed."],
g:[["Action","The mechanism that makes the hammer strike the string"],["Octave","The distance between one note and the next note of the same name"],["Damper","A felt pad that stops a string vibrating"],["Fortepiano","An early type of piano"]],
f:["The word piano is short for pianoforte, ‘soft-loud’.","A modern piano has about 230 strings.","Only three of Cristofori’s pianos survive."],
fun:"A concert grand piano contains around 12,000 parts, many of them in the action that connects each key to its hammer."});

ADDART({id:"mosaic",t:"Mosaics",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🧩",w:"Mosaic",r:["byzant","islamicart","rome","hagiasophia","pompeii","tessellations","fresco"],
p:[["Pictures from pieces","A mosaic is a picture or pattern made from small pieces of stone, glass or tile."],["Ancient art","Mosaics decorated Greek and Roman floors and Byzantine churches."],["Still made today","Artists still create mosaics for buildings, parks and streets."]],
l:"A mosaic is a picture or decorative pattern made by setting small pieces of colored stone, glass, ceramic, shell or other materials, called tesserae, into a surface such as a floor, wall or ceiling. Some of the earliest mosaics were made in Mesopotamia around 3000 BC using colored clay cones. The ancient Greeks made pebble mosaics, and the Romans decorated floors throughout their empire with mosaics of gods, animals, hunting scenes and geometric patterns; the famous Alexander Mosaic from Pompeii shows Alexander the Great in battle. In the Byzantine Empire, artists covered church walls and domes with shimmering gold and glass mosaics, as in Ravenna, Italy, and Hagia Sophia in Istanbul. Islamic art developed intricate geometric tile work, and the Aztecs made masks covered in turquoise. In the 20th century, the architect Antoni Gaudí used broken tiles in Barcelona, and today mosaics brighten subway stations, parks and public buildings around the world.",
s:[["How mosaics are made","Artists cut stone or glass into small pieces, often with special hammers and cutters, and set them into wet mortar or glue, close together. Gaps are filled with grout. Mosaicists can place pieces at slight angles so that light reflects in different directions, making surfaces glitter."],["Roman mosaics","Wealthy Romans paved their houses with mosaics, sometimes signed by the artists. The Alexander Mosaic, made around 100 BC for the House of the Faun in Pompeii, used millions of tiny tesserae. Roman villas in Britain, North Africa and Sicily, such as the Villa Romana del Casale, have spectacular floors."],["Byzantine splendor","Byzantine artists used glass tesserae backed with gold leaf. In the Basilica of San Vitale in Ravenna, completed in 547, mosaics show the emperor Justinian and empress Theodora with their courts. Gold backgrounds made churches seem filled with heavenly light."],["Modern mosaics","Gaudí’s Park Güell in Barcelona uses trencadís, a technique of covering curved surfaces with broken tiles and plates. Mexican muralists created huge mosaic facades, such as the library of the National Autonomous University of Mexico. Street artists, such as the French artist known as Invader, place pixel-like mosaics on walls in cities around the world."]],
k:[["Pieces called","Tesserae"],["Earliest examples","Mesopotamia, about 3000 BC"],["Famous Roman mosaic","Alexander Mosaic, Pompeii (c. 100 BC)"],["Famous Byzantine site","Ravenna, Italy"],["Modern technique","Trencadís (broken tiles)"]],
d:[["c. 3000 BC","Clay cone mosaics in Uruk, Mesopotamia"],["c. 100 BC","Alexander Mosaic made in Pompeii"],["537","Hagia Sophia dedicated, later decorated with mosaics"],["547","San Vitale in Ravenna completed"],["1900–1914","Gaudí builds Park Güell"],["1956","Juan O’Gorman completes the UNAM library mosaic in Mexico City"]],
s2:[["Islamic tile work","In Morocco and Spain, zellij is a mosaic of hand-cut glazed tiles forming complex geometric stars. Persian and Central Asian mosques are covered with colorful tile mosaics. These designs follow mathematical rules of symmetry."],["Restoring mosaics","Conservators carefully clean ancient mosaics, reattach loose tesserae and sometimes lift whole floors to protect them. Many Roman mosaics were found by farmers plowing fields or builders digging foundations."]],
sp:["The Alexander Mosaic","The Alexander Mosaic shows Alexander the Great charging at the Persian king Darius III, probably at the Battle of Issus in 333 BC. It is thought to copy a lost Greek painting. Today it is in the National Archaeological Museum in Naples."],
g:[["Mosaic","An image made from small pieces of material"],["Tesserae","The small pieces used in a mosaic"],["Grout","Paste that fills gaps between tiles"],["Byzantine","Relating to the Eastern Roman Empire"]],
f:["The small pieces in a mosaic are called tesserae.","Byzantine mosaics often used real gold leaf.","Gaudí covered Park Güell with broken tiles."],
fun:"The French street artist Invader has placed thousands of small tile mosaics of 1970s video game aliens on walls around the world, and even one on the International Space Station."});

ADDART({id:"worlddance",t:"Dance Around the World",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"💃",w:"Dance",r:["ballet","phdance","indianmusic","musicals","kpop","folkmusic","flamenco","tango","breakdancing"],
p:[["Moving to music","Dance is the art of moving the body rhythmically, usually to music."],["Every culture dances","Dance is used for celebration, worship, storytelling and courtship."],["Living heritage","UNESCO protects dances such as tango, flamenco and capoeira."]],
l:"Dance is the art of moving the body in rhythmic and expressive ways, usually to music. Every known human culture dances, for celebration, worship, storytelling, courtship, exercise and social connection. Some dances are performed by trained professionals on stage, such as ballet, Indian classical dance and contemporary dance; others are social or folk dances that anyone can join, such as Irish ceili dances, the Latin American salsa and Filipino tinikling. Many dances carry deep cultural meaning: the Māori haka expresses identity and strength, the hula preserves Hawaiian history and religion, and flamenco expresses the passion of Andalusia’s Romani communities. UNESCO lists dances including Argentine tango (2009), flamenco (2010) and Brazilian capoeira (2014) as Intangible Cultural Heritage. In the 20th and 21st centuries, dance styles spread worldwide through film, television and social media, from swing and disco to breakdancing, which appeared at the 2024 Olympics, and K-pop dance routines.",
s:[["Ritual and storytelling","Cave paintings and ancient art show people dancing thousands of years ago. In many cultures, dances honor gods, mark harvests, heal the sick or prepare for war. Indian classical dances such as Bharatanatyam and Kathak tell religious stories through precise hand gestures, eye movements and footwork."],["Social dances","European court dances like the minuet gave way to the waltz in the 19th century, then shocking because partners held each other closely. The tango emerged in the dance halls of Buenos Aires and Montevideo in the late 1800s, and swing dances such as the Lindy Hop grew from African American communities in the 1920s–30s."],["Dances of identity","The haka is a Māori posture dance with chanting, foot-stamping and fierce expressions, performed at ceremonies and famously by New Zealand’s rugby team. Capoeira combines martial arts, acrobatics and music and was developed by enslaved Africans in Brazil. Flamenco combines song, guitar, clapping and dance."],["Dance today","Hip-hop dance styles, including breaking, popping and locking, began in New York and California in the 1970s. Breaking made its Olympic debut in Paris in 2024. Social media challenges spread dance moves around the world in days, and K-pop groups are famous for complex, synchronized choreography."]],
k:[["UNESCO-listed examples","Tango (2009), flamenco (2010), capoeira (2014)"],["Oldest evidence","Prehistoric rock art"],["Olympic debut of breaking","Paris 2024"],["Famous ceremonial dance","Haka (New Zealand)"],["International Dance Day","29 April"]],
d:[["c. 1820","The famous haka Ka Mate is composed"],["1880s","Tango develops in Buenos Aires and Montevideo"],["1920s","Lindy Hop emerges in Harlem"],["1970s","Hip-hop dance begins in New York"],["1994","Riverdance performed at Eurovision"],["2024","Breaking at the Paris Olympics"]],
s2:[["Why do we dance?","Scientists have found that moving in time with others increases feelings of closeness and trust and releases feel-good chemicals. Babies naturally bounce to music, suggesting that the urge to move to a beat is deeply human."],["Riverdance","A seven-minute performance of Irish dance during the 1994 Eurovision Song Contest interval became a worldwide phenomenon. The full Riverdance show has since been seen by tens of millions of people and revived interest in Irish step dancing."]],
sp:["Dances in many lands","Filipino tinikling dancers step between clapping bamboo poles; Chinese lion dancers perform at Lunar New Year; Greek dancers form lines in the syrtaki; and Ghanaian adowa dancers use graceful hand movements to communicate. Each reflects its people’s history, music and values."],
g:[["Choreography","The planned sequence of steps and movements in a dance"],["Folk dance","A traditional dance of a people or region"],["Intangible heritage","Living traditions, like dance and music, protected by UNESCO"],["Haka","A Māori ceremonial dance with chanting"]],
f:["Every known human culture dances.","The waltz was once considered shocking.","Breaking appeared at the Olympics for the first time in 2024."],
fun:"International Dance Day, 29 April, is the birthday of Jean-Georges Noverre, an 18th-century French dancer who helped create modern ballet."});

ADDART({id:"encyclopedias",t:"Encyclopedias",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"📚",w:"Encyclopedia",r:["libraries","print","internet","enlightenment","novel"],
p:[["All knowledge in one place","An encyclopedia is a reference work that summarizes knowledge on many subjects."],["Ancient and modern","From Pliny’s Natural History to Wikipedia, people have tried to gather all knowledge."],["From books to screens","Most encyclopedias are now online rather than printed."]],
l:"An encyclopedia is a reference work containing articles that summarize knowledge, either on all subjects or on a particular field, usually arranged alphabetically or by topic. The word comes from Greek words meaning ‘general education’. One of the earliest surviving encyclopedic works is the Roman writer Pliny the Elder’s Natural History, completed in 77 AD. China’s Yongle Encyclopedia, finished in 1408, filled more than 11,000 volumes. In the 18th century, Denis Diderot and Jean le Rond d’Alembert’s French Encyclopédie spread Enlightenment ideas, while the Encyclopaedia Britannica was first published in Edinburgh in 1768–71. In the 20th century, families bought multi-volume printed sets, and in the 1990s encyclopedias moved to CD-ROMs. Wikipedia, launched in 2001 as a free encyclopedia anyone can edit, grew to millions of articles in hundreds of languages, and Britannica stopped printing in 2012. This encyclopedia itself belongs to this long tradition of organizing knowledge for readers.",
s:[["Ancient and medieval encyclopedias","Pliny’s Natural History, in 37 books, covered astronomy, geography, animals, plants, medicine and art. In the 7th century, Isidore of Seville compiled the Etymologies, a summary of knowledge used in Europe for centuries. Chinese scholars compiled huge leishu, collections of writings arranged by subject."],["The Yongle Encyclopedia","Commissioned by the Yongle Emperor of the Ming dynasty, it involved more than 2,000 scholars and copied texts on every subject into 11,095 volumes. It was too large to print, and most volumes were lost over the centuries; fewer than 4 percent survive."],["The Enlightenment","Diderot’s Encyclopédie (1751–1772), in 28 volumes, included articles on crafts and technology as well as philosophy, and some articles challenged the authority of the Church and king, leading to bans. Britannica, started by Scottish printers, became the best-known English-language encyclopedia."],["The digital age","Microsoft’s Encarta (1993–2009) put an encyclopedia on CD-ROM. Wikipedia, founded by Jimmy Wales and Larry Sanger on 15 January 2001, relies on volunteer editors and citations to reliable sources. Studies have found its accuracy on many topics comparable to traditional encyclopedias, though errors and vandalism occur. Britannica published its last printed edition in 2010 and announced the end of printing in 2012."]],
k:[["Word origin","Greek for ‘general education’"],["Early example","Pliny’s Natural History (77 AD)"],["Largest historical encyclopedia","Yongle Encyclopedia (1408), 11,095 volumes"],["Britannica first published","1768–1771, Edinburgh"],["Wikipedia launched","15 January 2001"]],
d:[["77","Pliny completes his Natural History"],["c. 630","Isidore of Seville writes the Etymologies"],["1408","Yongle Encyclopedia completed"],["1751","Diderot’s Encyclopédie begins"],["1768","Encyclopaedia Britannica begins publication"],["1993","Encarta released on CD-ROM"],["2001","Wikipedia launched"],["2012","Britannica ends its print edition"]],
s2:[["How encyclopedias are written","Traditional encyclopedias hire experts and editors to write and check articles. Wikipedia relies on volunteers who must cite published sources and follow rules about neutrality. Both aim to summarize existing knowledge rather than publish new research or personal opinions."],["Using encyclopedias well","Encyclopedias are a good starting point for learning about a topic. For school work and research, readers are encouraged to follow the references to original sources, check several sources and note when information was last updated."]],
sp:["Pliny and the volcano","Pliny the Elder, author of the Natural History, was commander of a Roman fleet when Mount Vesuvius erupted in 79 AD. He sailed toward the eruption to observe it and rescue people and died on the shore, probably from the fumes."],
g:[["Encyclopedia","A reference work that summarizes knowledge"],["Reference work","A book or resource for looking up information"],["Citation","A note showing where information came from"],["Edition","A version of a published work"]],
f:["The Yongle Encyclopedia filled more than 11,000 volumes.","Pliny the Elder died during the eruption of Vesuvius.","Britannica stopped printing in 2012 after 244 years."],
fun:"The first edition of the Encyclopaedia Britannica described California as a large country of the West Indies, a reminder of how much knowledge has grown since 1771."});

ADDART({id:"ciphers",t:"Codes and Ciphers",c:"Mathematics",i:"🔐",w:"Cipher",r:["cybersecurity","binary","turing","ww2","prime"],
p:[["Secret messages","Codes and ciphers turn messages into secret forms that only intended readers can understand."],["Code-breaking changed history","Breaking enemy codes helped win battles and wars."],["Protecting the internet","Modern encryption keeps online shopping, banking and messages safe."]],
l:"Codes and ciphers are methods of disguising messages so that only the intended reader can understand them. In everyday speech the words are used interchangeably, but strictly a code replaces whole words or phrases with other words or symbols, using a codebook, while a cipher changes individual letters or bits using a rule called an algorithm and a secret key. The study of making and breaking them is called cryptography and cryptanalysis. Julius Caesar shifted letters of the alphabet to hide military messages, Arab scholars invented frequency analysis to break such ciphers in the 9th century, and cipher machines like Germany’s Enigma were cracked by Polish and British mathematicians during World War II, including Alan Turing at Bletchley Park. In 1976–77, public-key cryptography made it possible to communicate securely with strangers, and it now protects internet banking, shopping and messaging. Mathematics, especially prime numbers, lies at the heart of modern encryption.",
s:[["Early ciphers","In the Caesar cipher, each letter is replaced by one a fixed number of places further along the alphabet, so with a shift of three, A becomes D. The Spartans used a scytale, a rod around which a strip of leather was wound to write a message. Substitution ciphers like these were easy to use but could be broken."],["Frequency analysis","Around 850 AD, the Arab scholar al-Kindi described how to break substitution ciphers by counting how often each symbol appears and comparing with common letters in the language, such as E in English. In 1586, deciphered letters revealed that Mary, Queen of Scots, had approved a plot to kill Elizabeth I, leading to her execution."],["Code-breaking in war","In 1917, British code-breakers decoded the Zimmermann Telegram, in which Germany proposed an alliance with Mexico against the United States, helping bring the US into World War I. In World War II, the breaking of Enigma and other codes at Bletchley Park gave the Allies vital intelligence. The US Marine Corps used Navajo speakers as code talkers, whose code was never broken."],["Modern cryptography","Today’s encryption uses complex mathematics. In public-key systems like RSA, created in 1977, anyone can use a public key to lock a message, but only the holder of a matching private key can unlock it; breaking it would mean factoring enormous numbers into primes. Researchers are developing new ‘post-quantum’ methods because future quantum computers might break current systems."]],
k:[["Code","Replaces words or phrases"],["Cipher","Changes letters or bits using a key"],["Frequency analysis","Described by al-Kindi, c. 850"],["Enigma","German WWII cipher machine, broken by the Allies"],["Public-key cryptography","1976–77"]],
d:[["c. 50 BC","Julius Caesar uses a shift cipher"],["c. 850","Al-Kindi describes frequency analysis"],["1586","Deciphered letters condemn Mary, Queen of Scots"],["1917","Zimmermann Telegram decoded"],["1932","Polish mathematicians break Enigma"],["1977","RSA encryption invented"],["2024","First post-quantum encryption standards published"]],
s2:[["The Voynich manuscript","The Voynich manuscript is a 15th-century book written in an unknown script, with drawings of strange plants, stars and bathing figures. Carbon dating places its parchment around 1404–1438. Despite efforts by cryptographers, linguists and computers, no one has convincingly read it."],["Everyday encryption","When a web address begins with ‘https’ and shows a padlock, the connection is encrypted. Messaging apps with end-to-end encryption mean that only the sender and receiver can read messages, not even the company running the service."]],
sp:["Make your own cipher","A pigpen cipher replaces letters with symbols drawn from grids, and was used by Freemasons in the 18th century. A Vigenère cipher uses a keyword to apply different shifts to each letter and was once called ‘the indecipherable cipher’ until it was broken in the 19th century."],
g:[["Cipher","A method of changing letters to hide a message"],["Key","Secret information used to encrypt or decrypt"],["Encryption","Turning information into a secret form"],["Cryptanalysis","Breaking codes and ciphers"]],
f:["The Caesar cipher shifts each letter along the alphabet.","Navajo code talkers’ code was never broken.","Online banking relies on the difficulty of factoring large numbers."],
fun:"During World War II, Bletchley Park recruited some code-breakers through a newspaper crossword competition; people who solved it quickly were invited for interviews."});

ADDART({id:"corrosion",t:"Rust and Corrosion",c:"Science",i:"🔩",w:"Corrosion",r:["metals","reactions","oxygen","steel","statueofliberty"],
p:[["Metals wearing away","Corrosion is the slow destruction of materials, especially metals, by chemical reactions."],["Rust","Rust forms when iron reacts with oxygen and water."],["Costly","Corrosion costs the world trillions of dollars a year."]],
l:"Corrosion is the gradual destruction of materials, usually metals, by chemical reactions with their environment. The most familiar example is rust, the flaky reddish-brown iron oxide that forms when iron or steel reacts with oxygen and water. Salt speeds up rusting, which is why cars, ships and bridges near the sea or on salted winter roads corrode faster. Other metals corrode too: copper develops a green layer called patina, as seen on the Statue of Liberty, and silver tarnishes black. Unlike rust, these layers can protect the metal underneath. Corrosion weakens structures and machines and can cause dangerous failures, and studies estimate it costs about 3 to 4 percent of the world’s economic output each year. People fight corrosion with paint and coatings, galvanizing (coating steel with zinc), stainless steel, which contains chromium, and cathodic protection, in which a more reactive metal corrodes instead of the structure being protected.",
s:[["The chemistry of rust","Rusting is an electrochemical reaction. On the surface of iron, some areas lose electrons and iron dissolves, while elsewhere oxygen and water gain electrons. The iron combines with oxygen and water to form hydrated iron oxide. Because rust is porous and flakes off, fresh metal is exposed and rusting continues."],["Protective layers","Aluminum, chromium and titanium form thin, tough oxide layers that stop further corrosion, which is why aluminum cans do not rust away. The Statue of Liberty’s copper skin turned from shiny brown to green between the 1880s and about 1920 as patina formed, and the green layer now protects it."],["Fighting corrosion","Paint, oil and plastic coatings keep water and oxygen away. Galvanized steel is coated with zinc, which corrodes first. Stainless steel, invented by Harry Brearley in Sheffield in 1913, contains chromium that forms a protective film. Ships and pipelines are protected with blocks of zinc or magnesium, called sacrificial anodes, that corrode in their place."],["When corrosion causes disasters","Corrosion has contributed to collapsed bridges, leaking pipelines and aircraft accidents. In 1988, part of the roof of an Aloha Airlines plane tore off in flight, partly because of corrosion and metal fatigue. Engineers inspect structures regularly and use sensors to detect hidden corrosion."]],
k:[["Most common example","Rust (hydrated iron oxide)"],["Needed for rust","Iron, oxygen and water"],["Copper’s corrosion layer","Patina (green)"],["Stainless steel invented","1913, Harry Brearley"],["Global cost","About 3–4% of world economic output"]],
d:[["c. 400","Iron pillar of Delhi made"],["1742","Galvanizing process described in France"],["1824","Humphry Davy proposes cathodic protection for ships"],["1913","Stainless steel invented"],["1920","Statue of Liberty’s green patina complete"],["2016","Study estimates corrosion costs 2.5 trillion dollars a year"]],
s2:[["The Iron Pillar of Delhi","An iron pillar more than 7 meters tall, made around 1,600 years ago, stands in Delhi with remarkably little rust. Scientists found that its iron contains phosphorus, which helped form a thin protective layer, combined with Delhi’s dry climate."],["Rust in nature","Iron oxides give Mars its red color, color red soils and rocks on Earth, and were used as some of the first paints by prehistoric artists. Banded iron formations, rocks made of layers of iron oxide, record when oxygen first built up in Earth’s atmosphere."]],
sp:["Weathering steel","Some steels are designed to rust on purpose. Weathering steel forms a stable rust layer that protects it, giving a rich brown color. It is used in sculptures such as the Angel of the North in England and in bridges that never need painting."],
g:[["Corrosion","The slow destruction of a material by chemical reaction"],["Oxide","A compound of an element with oxygen"],["Patina","A thin layer formed on metal surfaces over time"],["Galvanizing","Coating steel with zinc to protect it"]],
f:["Rust needs both oxygen and water.","The Statue of Liberty is green because of copper corrosion.","Stainless steel resists rust because it contains chromium."],
fun:"The planet Mars is red because its surface dust is full of iron oxide, so Mars is, in a sense, a rusty planet."});

ADDART({id:"plasma",t:"Plasma, the Fourth State of Matter",c:"Science",i:"⚡",w:"Plasma (physics)",r:["states","sun","stars","fusion","aurora"],
p:[["Superheated gas","Plasma is a hot gas in which atoms have lost electrons."],["Most common state","Most of the visible matter in the universe, including stars, is plasma."],["Everyday plasma","Lightning, neon signs and auroras are all plasma."]],
l:"Plasma is often called the fourth state of matter, alongside solids, liquids and gases. It forms when a gas is heated to very high temperatures or exposed to strong electric fields, so that electrons are stripped from atoms, leaving a mixture of free electrons and positively charged ions. Because it contains charged particles, plasma conducts electricity and responds strongly to magnetic fields, and it often glows. Although plasma is rare in everyday life on Earth, it makes up more than 99 percent of the visible matter in the universe, because stars, including the Sun, are giant balls of plasma. On Earth, lightning, auroras, flames at very high temperature, neon and fluorescent lights and welding arcs contain plasma. Scientists use plasma to make computer chips, sterilize medical equipment and cut metal, and researchers are trying to harness the plasma inside fusion reactors to generate clean energy.",
s:[["Discovery","In the 1870s, William Crookes studied glowing gases in tubes and called this ‘radiant matter’. In 1928, the American chemist Irving Langmuir named it plasma, comparing the way it carries electrons and ions to blood plasma carrying blood cells."],["Plasma in space","The Sun’s surface is about 5,500 °C, and its outer atmosphere, the corona, is over a million degrees. The solar wind is a stream of plasma flowing outward from the Sun. When it reaches Earth, its particles follow the magnetic field toward the poles and make atoms in the upper atmosphere glow as auroras."],["Plasma on Earth","A lightning bolt heats air to around 30,000 °C, briefly turning it into plasma. In neon signs and fluorescent lamps, electricity turns gas into a glowing plasma. Plasma globes, popular toys, show glowing streams of plasma that follow your fingers when you touch the glass."],["Using plasma","Factories use plasma to etch tiny circuits on computer chips and to coat materials. Plasma torches cut through thick steel. Plasma televisions, popular from the late 1990s until the early 2010s, used millions of tiny cells of glowing gas. In fusion research, machines such as tokamaks hold plasma hotter than the Sun’s core with powerful magnetic fields."]],
k:[["Made of","Free electrons and ions"],["Named by","Irving Langmuir, 1928"],["Share of visible universe","Over 99%"],["Natural examples","Stars, lightning, auroras"],["Human uses","Lights, chip-making, cutting, fusion research"]],
d:[["1879","Crookes describes ‘radiant matter’"],["1928","Langmuir names plasma"],["1958","First tokamak experiments in the Soviet Union"],["1964","First plasma display panel invented"],["2014","Last major plasma TV production ends"],["2022","Fusion experiment produces more energy than the laser input"]],
s2:[["Plasma and fusion","In fusion, light atoms such as hydrogen join together, releasing huge energy, as in the Sun. To make fusion happen on Earth, plasma must be heated to over 100 million degrees and held in place without touching the walls. The international ITER project in France is building one of the largest fusion machines."],["Plasma in medicine","‘Cold plasma’ at room temperature can kill bacteria and viruses without heat, and is being studied for sterilizing equipment, healing wounds and keeping food fresh."]],
sp:["Ball lightning","Ball lightning is a rare phenomenon in which glowing spheres appear during thunderstorms, floating for a few seconds. It may involve plasma, but scientists are still not sure what causes it, and it is very hard to study because it is unpredictable."],
g:[["Plasma","A gas of charged particles: ions and free electrons"],["Ion","An atom with an electric charge"],["Electron","A tiny negatively charged particle in atoms"],["Tokamak","A doughnut-shaped machine for holding fusion plasma"]],
f:["Stars are made of plasma.","Lightning briefly heats air to about 30,000 °C.","Plasma was named after blood plasma."],
fun:"A lightning bolt is about five times hotter than the surface of the Sun."});

ADDART({id:"spacefood",t:"Space Food",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🍽️",w:"Space food",r:["astronauts","iss","nutri","marsrovers","artemis"],
p:[["Eating in orbit","Astronauts need food that is safe, light, nutritious and easy to eat in weightlessness."],["From tubes to tortillas","Early space food came in tubes; today astronauts eat a wide variety of meals."],["Growing food in space","Astronauts have grown and eaten lettuce and other plants on the ISS."]],
l:"Space food is food specially prepared for astronauts to eat in space. It must be safe, nutritious, light, compact, long-lasting and easy to eat in weightlessness, where crumbs and liquids float and can damage equipment or be inhaled. The first person to eat in space was Soviet cosmonaut Gherman Titov in 1961, and American John Glenn ate applesauce from a tube in 1962, proving that people could swallow in weightlessness. Early meals were puréed food in tubes and bite-sized cubes, but today astronauts on the International Space Station choose from more than 100 items, including freeze-dried and thermostabilized meals, fresh fruit delivered by supply ships and foods from different countries. Because taste is dulled in space, many astronauts prefer spicy foods. For future long missions to the Moon and Mars, scientists are developing ways to grow crops and produce food in space.",
s:[["Early space food","Titov ate a paste from a tube and reported feeling hungry. Glenn’s applesauce test in 1962 showed digestion worked. On Gemini 3 in 1965, astronaut John Young secretly brought a corned beef sandwich aboard, which shed crumbs and earned a reprimand. Apollo astronauts ate rehydratable food using hot water."],["Preparing food","Freeze-drying removes water so food stays light and keeps for years; astronauts add water before eating. Thermostabilized foods are heated to kill germs and sealed in pouches. Tortillas replaced bread on many missions because they make fewer crumbs. Salt and pepper come as liquids, because grains would float away."],["Eating on the ISS","Astronauts eat with spoons from pouches, using magnets, Velcro and tape to hold things down. Crews share meals from their countries, from Japanese ramen to Russian borscht. Fresh fruit is a treat after a supply ship arrives. Astronauts have a weekly menu and drink recycled water."],["Farming in space","In 2015, astronauts on the ISS ate red romaine lettuce grown in the station’s Veggie plant chamber, the first food grown and eaten by Americans in space. Plants such as radishes, chili peppers and cabbage have since been grown. Future Moon and Mars bases may use greenhouses, algae and even insects as food."]],
k:[["First to eat in space","Gherman Titov, 1961"],["First American to eat in space","John Glenn, 1962"],["ISS menu","More than 100 items"],["Popular bread substitute","Tortillas"],["First US space-grown food eaten","Lettuce, 2015"]],
d:[["1961","Titov eats in space"],["1962","John Glenn eats applesauce in orbit"],["1965","Smuggled corned beef sandwich on Gemini 3"],["1973","Skylab has a freezer and a dining table"],["1985","Tortillas first used on a shuttle mission"],["2015","Astronauts eat lettuce grown on the ISS"]],
s2:[["Why food tastes different","In weightlessness, body fluids shift toward the head, causing a blocked nose like a cold. Because smell is important for flavor, food tastes blander, so astronauts often add hot sauce and spices."],["Food for Mars","A trip to Mars and back could take two to three years, longer than most packaged food stays fresh and nutritious. NASA and other agencies have run competitions to develop new ways of producing food on long missions."]],
sp:["The space sandwich","When John Young pulled a corned beef sandwich from his pocket during Gemini 3 in 1965 and offered it to Gus Grissom, crumbs floated around the cabin. Congress held hearings about the incident, and the sandwich became a famous part of space history."],
g:[["Freeze-drying","Removing water by freezing and low pressure"],["Thermostabilized","Heated to kill germs and sealed"],["Weightlessness","Floating because of free fall in orbit"],["Rehydrate","To add water back to dried food"]],
f:["Salt and pepper are liquid on the ISS.","Tortillas are popular in space because they make few crumbs.","Food tastes blander in space."],
fun:"Astronauts have celebrated holidays on the ISS with special meals, including thermostabilized turkey and candied yams for American Thanksgiving."});

ADDART({id:"womeninspace",t:"Women in Space",c:"Space Exploration",i:"👩‍🚀",w:"Women in space",r:["astronauts","iss","artemis","spaceshuttle","feminism"],
p:[["Breaking barriers","Women have flown in space since Valentina Tereshkova in 1963."],["Records and firsts","Women have commanded spacecraft, walked in space and set endurance records."],["Around the Moon","In 2026, Christina Koch became the first woman to travel around the Moon."]],
l:"Women have played important roles in space exploration since the beginning of the space age, as astronauts, scientists, engineers and mathematicians. The Soviet cosmonaut Valentina Tereshkova became the first woman in space on 16 June 1963, orbiting Earth 48 times. Nearly twenty years passed before the next woman, Svetlana Savitskaya, flew in 1982; in 1984 she became the first woman to walk in space. Sally Ride became the first American woman in space in 1983, and Mae Jemison the first African American woman in 1992. Women have since commanded space shuttles and the International Space Station, conducted spacewalks and set records: Peggy Whitson has spent more time in space than any other American, and Christina Koch made the longest single spaceflight by a woman. In 2019, Koch and Jessica Meir carried out the first all-woman spacewalk, and in April 2026 Koch flew around the Moon on Artemis II, the first woman to do so. Women have flown for many countries, including the Soviet Union and Russia, the United States, China, Japan, Canada, France, the United Kingdom and South Korea.",
s:[["Early pioneers","In 1960–61, a privately funded program tested 13 American women pilots, later called the ‘Mercury 13’, who passed many of the same tests as male astronauts, but NASA did not accept women then. Tereshkova, a textile worker and amateur parachutist, was chosen from a group of female cosmonaut candidates."],["Shuttle era","Sally Ride flew on the shuttle Challenger in 1983 and later served on the panels investigating the Challenger and Columbia disasters. Kathryn Sullivan became the first American woman to walk in space in 1984. Eileen Collins became the first woman to pilot the shuttle in 1995 and the first to command it in 1999. Kalpana Chawla, the first India-born woman in space, died in the Columbia disaster in 2003."],["Space stations","Peggy Whitson became the first woman to command the ISS in 2007 and later set the American record for total time in space. Liu Yang became China’s first woman in space in 2012, and Wang Yaping made China’s first spacewalk by a woman in 2021. Christina Koch spent 328 days in orbit in 2019–20."],["Artemis","NASA’s Artemis program aims to land the first woman on the Moon. On Artemis II, launched on 1 April 2026, Christina Koch and three crewmates flew around the Moon and returned safely about ten days later, the first humans to travel to the Moon’s vicinity since 1972."]],
k:[["First woman in space","Valentina Tereshkova, 16 June 1963"],["First woman to walk in space","Svetlana Savitskaya, 1984"],["First American woman in space","Sally Ride, 1983"],["First all-woman spacewalk","18 October 2019"],["First woman around the Moon","Christina Koch, Artemis II, April 2026"]],
d:[["1963","Tereshkova becomes the first woman in space"],["1983","Sally Ride flies"],["1984","Savitskaya’s spacewalk; Sullivan’s spacewalk"],["1992","Mae Jemison flies"],["1999","Eileen Collins commands a shuttle"],["2019","First all-woman spacewalk"],["2026","Christina Koch flies around the Moon"]],
s2:[["Hidden figures","Women mathematicians such as Katherine Johnson, Dorothy Vaughan and Mary Jackson calculated trajectories for NASA’s early missions, work celebrated in the 2016 book and film Hidden Figures. Margaret Hamilton led the team that wrote the software for the Apollo guidance computer."],["Designing for everyone","In 2019, a planned all-woman spacewalk was postponed because not enough medium-sized spacesuit parts were ready on the station. The incident highlighted how equipment designed mainly for men can limit opportunities, and newer suits are designed to fit a wider range of body sizes."]],
sp:["Tereshkova’s call sign","Valentina Tereshkova’s call sign was Chaika, Russian for ‘seagull’. During her flight she kept a diary and took photographs used to study the atmosphere. She later became a politician and remains the only woman to have flown solo in space."],
g:[["Cosmonaut","A Russian or Soviet astronaut"],["Spacewalk","Activity outside a spacecraft in space"],["Commander","The person in charge of a mission"],["Call sign","A code name used in radio communication"]],
f:["Tereshkova is the only woman to fly a solo space mission.","The first all-woman spacewalk was in 2019.","Christina Koch was the first woman to travel around the Moon."],
fun:"Sally Ride was also an author, writing science books for children, and founded a company to inspire girls to study science."});

ADDART({id:"edgeofspace",t:"Where Space Begins",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🌌",w:"Kármán line",r:["atmosphere","rocket","commspace","sat","astronauts"],
p:[["No sharp edge","Earth’s atmosphere fades gradually into space."],["The Kármán line","Space is commonly said to begin 100 kilometers above sea level."],["Closer than you think","Space is only about as far away as a short car journey, straight up."]],
l:"There is no sharp boundary where Earth’s atmosphere ends and outer space begins; the air simply gets thinner with height until it fades into the near-vacuum of space. For practical purposes, however, a boundary is needed for records, laws and astronaut awards. The most widely used boundary is the Kármán line, 100 kilometers above sea level, named after the Hungarian-American engineer Theodore von Kármán, who calculated roughly where the air becomes too thin for aircraft wings to provide lift. The World Air Sports Federation (FAI) uses 100 kilometers, while the United States Air Force and NASA award astronaut wings to people who fly above 50 miles (about 80 kilometers). Commercial spaceflights have made the definition matter, because some suborbital tourist flights go above 100 kilometers and others above 80. For comparison, the International Space Station orbits at about 400 kilometers, and satellites can orbit much higher.",
s:[["Layers of the atmosphere","About three-quarters of the air lies in the lowest layer, the troposphere, up to about 12 kilometers, where weather happens and planes fly. Above are the stratosphere, with the ozone layer, the mesosphere, where meteors burn up, and the thermosphere, where auroras glow and the ISS orbits. The outermost exosphere fades into space thousands of kilometers up."],["The Kármán line","Von Kármán calculated that at around 80–100 kilometers, an aircraft would need to fly so fast to stay up that it would effectively be in orbit. The 100-kilometer figure became popular because it is a round number. Some scientists have argued that 80 kilometers is closer to the true physical boundary."],["Record jumps","In 1960, US Air Force officer Joseph Kittinger jumped from a balloon at about 31 kilometers. In 2012, Felix Baumgartner jumped from about 39 kilometers and broke the sound barrier in free fall. In 2014, Alan Eustace jumped from about 41 kilometers. None of these reached space, but they showed how thin the air is so high up."],["Space tourism","Blue Origin’s New Shepard capsule flies above 100 kilometers, giving passengers a few minutes of weightlessness and a view of Earth’s curve against black sky. Virgin Galactic’s spaceplane flew to over 80 kilometers. Orbital flights, which circle Earth, require much more speed, about 28,000 kilometers per hour."]],
k:[["Kármán line","100 km above sea level"],["US astronaut boundary","50 miles (about 80 km)"],["Named after","Theodore von Kármán"],["ISS altitude","About 400 km"],["Highest skydive","About 41 km (2014)"]],
d:[["1946","A V-2 rocket photographs Earth from about 105 km"],["1957","Sputnik 1 reaches orbit"],["1960","Kittinger’s balloon jump from 31 km"],["2004","SpaceShipOne makes the first private crewed spaceflight"],["2012","Baumgartner’s jump from 39 km"],["2021","Blue Origin and Virgin Galactic fly tourists"]],
s2:[["How thin is the air?","At the top of Mount Everest, about 8.8 kilometers up, the air is about a third as dense as at sea level. At 100 kilometers, it is about a millionth of sea-level density. Even at the height of the ISS, there is just enough air to slowly drag the station down, so it must regularly boost itself higher."],["Why define space?","International law says that countries control the airspace above them, but outer space is free for all to explore. Without an agreed line, it is unclear where national airspace ends, which matters for satellites, spaceplanes and future traffic rules."]],
sp:["‘An hour’s drive away’","The astronomer Fred Hoyle once remarked that space isn’t remote at all, but only an hour’s drive away if your car could go straight upwards. At highway speeds of 100 kilometers per hour, you would reach the Kármán line in about an hour."],
g:[["Atmosphere","The layer of gases surrounding a planet"],["Suborbital","Reaching space but not circling the Earth"],["Orbit","A curved path around a planet or star"],["Vacuum","A space with almost no matter"]],
f:["Space is commonly said to begin 100 kilometers up.","The US counts people flying above 80 kilometers as astronauts.","Driving straight up at highway speed, you would reach space in about an hour."],
fun:"The first photo of Earth from space was taken on 24 October 1946 by a camera on a captured German V-2 rocket launched from New Mexico."});

ADDART({id:"gems",t:"Gemstones",c:"Geography",i:"💎",w:"Gemstone",r:["rocks","crystals","carbonchem","metals","jewelry"],
p:[["Precious stones","Gemstones are minerals cut and polished for jewelry and decoration."],["Rare and beautiful","Diamonds, rubies, sapphires and emeralds are the best known."],["Formed deep underground","Most gems form under heat and pressure over millions of years."]],
l:"A gemstone is a mineral, rock or organic material that is cut and polished for use in jewelry or decoration because of its beauty, durability and rarity. Most gems are crystals that formed deep in the Earth under high heat and pressure or from mineral-rich fluids over millions of years. Traditionally, diamond, ruby, sapphire and emerald have been called precious stones, while others such as amethyst, topaz, garnet and opal are called semi-precious, although the distinction is not scientific. Some gems come from living things: pearls form in oysters and mussels, amber is fossilized tree resin and coral is made by tiny sea animals. Gems are valued by the ‘four Cs’: color, clarity, cut and carat weight. Diamond, the hardest natural material, is pure carbon. Since the 20th century, scientists have learned to grow synthetic gems, and laboratory-made diamonds are now common. Concerns about ‘blood diamonds’ and mining conditions have led to efforts to trace where gems come from.",
s:[["How gems form","Diamonds form about 150–200 kilometers deep in the Earth’s mantle and are carried up by volcanic eruptions through pipes of rock called kimberlite. Emeralds and many other gems crystallize from hot fluids in cracks in rocks. Some, such as opal, form from silica-rich water seeping into rocks near the surface."],["Famous gems","Ruby and sapphire are both forms of the mineral corundum; red corundum is ruby, and other colors are sapphire. Emerald is green beryl, colored by chromium. The Cullinan diamond, found in South Africa in 1905, weighed 3,106 carats and was cut into several stones in the British Crown Jewels."],["Cutting and value","Gem cutters study each stone to decide how to cut it, creating facets that reflect light and show off color and sparkle. A carat is a unit of weight equal to 0.2 grams. Color, clarity (how free of flaws a stone is), cut and carat weight together determine value."],["Ethics and synthetic gems","‘Blood diamonds’ mined in war zones funded conflicts in countries such as Sierra Leone and Angola in the 1990s, leading to the Kimberley Process certification scheme in 2003. Laboratory-grown diamonds, first made in 1954, are chemically identical to mined diamonds and have become much cheaper and more popular."]],
k:[["Traditional precious stones","Diamond, ruby, sapphire, emerald"],["Hardest natural material","Diamond (10 on the Mohs scale)"],["One carat","0.2 grams"],["Largest gem diamond found","Cullinan, 3,106 carats (1905)"],["Organic gems","Pearl, amber, coral"]],
d:[["1812","Friedrich Mohs creates his hardness scale"],["1867","Diamonds discovered in South Africa"],["1905","Cullinan diamond found"],["1954","First laboratory-grown diamonds made"],["2003","Kimberley Process begins"]],
s2:[["The Mohs scale","In 1812 the German mineralogist Friedrich Mohs ranked minerals by which could scratch which, from talc (1) to diamond (10). Quartz is 7, and gems harder than that resist scratching from everyday dust, which contains quartz."],["Birthstones","Many cultures link gems to months of birth, such as garnet for January and ruby for July. The modern Western list was set by American jewelers in 1912, but the idea goes back to ancient times."]],
sp:["The Hope Diamond","The Hope Diamond, a deep blue diamond of 45.52 carats, has a long history passing through French kings and wealthy owners, along with legends of a curse. It was donated to the Smithsonian Institution in 1958 and is one of the most visited museum objects in the world."],
g:[["Gemstone","A mineral cut and polished for jewelry"],["Carat","A unit of weight for gems, 0.2 grams"],["Facet","A flat, polished surface on a cut gem"],["Synthetic","Made in a laboratory rather than found in nature"]],
f:["Ruby and sapphire are the same mineral, corundum.","Diamond is pure carbon.","Pearls and amber come from living things."],
fun:"The Hope Diamond was mailed to the Smithsonian in 1958 in a plain brown package by registered mail."});

ADDART({id:"jetstream",t:"The Jet Stream",c:"Geography",i:"💨",w:"Jet stream",r:["atmosphere","weatherforecast","flight","storms","climchange"],
p:[["Rivers of wind","Jet streams are fast, narrow bands of wind high in the atmosphere."],["Shaping weather","They steer storms and separate cold and warm air."],["Helping planes","Planes flying east can ride the jet stream to save time and fuel."]],
l:"A jet stream is a fast-flowing, narrow current of air high in the atmosphere, usually 9 to 16 kilometers above the ground, near the top of the troposphere. The strongest jet streams flow from west to east and can reach speeds of more than 300 kilometers per hour. They form where cold polar air meets warmer air from the tropics, creating sharp differences in temperature and pressure, combined with the Earth’s rotation. Each hemisphere has a polar jet stream and a weaker subtropical jet stream. Jet streams meander in large waves around the planet, steering weather systems: storms often follow the polar jet, and a wavy or stuck jet stream can bring long periods of heat, cold or rain. Pilots use jet streams to speed up flights heading east and avoid them going west. Scientists are studying how climate change, by warming the Arctic faster than other regions, may be affecting the jet stream’s shape.",
s:[["Discovery","In the 1920s, the Japanese meteorologist Wasaburo Oishi measured strong high-altitude winds over Japan using weather balloons, publishing his results in Esperanto, so they were little known. During World War II, American bomber pilots flying to Japan encountered extremely strong headwinds, and the term ‘jet stream’ became widely used."],["How it forms","The difference in temperature between the cold poles and the warm tropics creates differences in air pressure, driving winds. The Earth’s rotation deflects these winds to flow east, a result of the Coriolis effect. The jet stream is strongest in winter, when the temperature contrast is greatest."],["Weather and the jet stream","When the jet stream flows straight, weather changes quickly. When it develops big loops, it can let cold Arctic air plunge south or warm air push north, and when loops stall, they can cause heat waves, droughts or floods that last for weeks. Forecasters watch the jet stream closely."],["Flying with the jet stream","Flights from North America to Europe are often shorter than the return journey because of tailwinds. In February 2020, a British Airways flight from New York to London, pushed by a strong jet stream during Storm Ciara, set a subsonic record of under five hours."]],
k:[["Height","About 9–16 km"],["Speed","Often over 300 km/h"],["Direction","Mainly west to east"],["Types","Polar and subtropical jets"],["Discovered","1920s, Wasaburo Oishi"]],
d:[["1920s","Wasaburo Oishi measures high winds over Japan"],["1944","US bomber pilots encounter the jet stream"],["1944–45","Japan launches balloon bombs carried by the jet stream"],["1950s","Airlines begin planning routes around jet streams"],["2020","Jet stream helps set a New York–London flight record"]],
s2:[["Balloon bombs","In 1944–45, Japan launched about 9,300 paper balloons carrying bombs into the jet stream, aiming to reach North America. Several hundred arrived, and one killed six people on a picnic in Oregon in May 1945, the only deaths from enemy action on the US mainland during World War II."],["Climate change","Some scientists suggest that because the Arctic is warming faster than the rest of the world, the temperature difference driving the jet stream is shrinking, which may make it wavier and more likely to stall. Others think the evidence is not yet clear, and research continues."]],
sp:["Jet streams on other planets","Jupiter and Saturn have powerful jet streams that form their colorful bands, with winds over 500 kilometers per hour. Saturn’s north pole has a strange six-sided jet stream called the hexagon, larger than the Earth."],
g:[["Jet stream","A fast, narrow band of high-altitude wind"],["Troposphere","The lowest layer of the atmosphere, where weather happens"],["Coriolis effect","The bending of moving air caused by Earth’s rotation"],["Tailwind","Wind blowing in the direction of travel"]],
f:["Jet streams flow mostly from west to east.","They can reach speeds of more than 300 km/h.","Flights going east are often faster because of the jet stream."],
fun:"Wasaburo Oishi published his discovery of the jet stream in Esperanto, an invented international language, which is one reason his work went unnoticed for years."});

ADDART({id:"oceancurrents",t:"Ocean Currents",c:"Geography",i:"🌊",w:"Ocean current",r:["pacific","atlantic","climate","elnino","plasticwaste","oceanacidification","seamounts"],
p:[["Rivers in the sea","Ocean currents are continuous movements of seawater."],["Moving heat","Currents carry warm and cold water around the globe, shaping climate."],["The Gulf Stream","The Gulf Stream helps keep northwestern Europe mild."]],
l:"Ocean currents are continuous, directed movements of seawater, flowing like huge rivers within the oceans. Surface currents are driven mainly by winds and shaped by the Earth’s rotation and the shapes of continents, forming giant circular systems called gyres in each major ocean basin. Deeper currents are driven by differences in water temperature and saltiness, which change its density; cold, salty water sinks near the poles and slowly spreads through the deep oceans, in a worldwide circulation sometimes called the global conveyor belt. Currents carry heat from the tropics toward the poles, influencing climate: the Gulf Stream and North Atlantic Current help make northwestern Europe much milder than places at similar latitudes in Canada. Currents also carry nutrients that support fisheries, transport plankton, larvae and floating debris, and have guided sailors and explorers for centuries. Scientists are monitoring signs that the Atlantic circulation may be weakening as the climate warms.",
s:[["Surface currents","Steady trade winds and westerlies push surface water, and the Coriolis effect turns currents clockwise in the Northern Hemisphere and anticlockwise in the Southern Hemisphere. The Antarctic Circumpolar Current, flowing around Antarctica, is the largest current, carrying more than 100 times the flow of all the world’s rivers."],["Deep currents","In the North Atlantic and around Antarctica, very cold, salty water sinks and flows along the ocean floor. It may take about 1,000 years for water to complete the full journey of the global conveyor belt. This circulation helps store heat and carbon dioxide in the deep ocean."],["Currents and climate","The Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation (AMOC), which includes the Gulf Stream, moves vast amounts of heat northward. Studies suggest it has weakened and could slow further as melting ice adds fresh water. The Humboldt Current off South America brings cold, nutrient-rich water that supports huge fish populations, and changes during El Niño can disrupt it."],["Tracking currents","Benjamin Franklin published one of the first charts of the Gulf Stream in 1770 to help mail ships cross the Atlantic faster. Today, satellites measure sea surface height and temperature, and thousands of floating robots called Argo floats dive and surface to record ocean conditions."]],
k:[["Driven by","Wind, Earth’s rotation, temperature and saltiness"],["Largest current","Antarctic Circumpolar Current"],["Famous warm current","Gulf Stream"],["Global conveyor belt cycle","About 1,000 years"],["First Gulf Stream chart","Benjamin Franklin, 1770"]],
d:[["1513","Ponce de León’s ships encounter the Gulf Stream"],["1770","Franklin publishes a Gulf Stream chart"],["1872–76","HMS Challenger expedition studies the oceans"],["1992","Thousands of bath toys spill into the Pacific"],["2000","Argo float program begins"],["2010s","Studies report signs of AMOC weakening"]],
s2:[["Garbage patches","Ocean gyres collect floating plastic in areas such as the Great Pacific Garbage Patch, between Hawaii and California. Most of the plastic is in small pieces, making it hard to clean up, and it harms sea life."],["Currents and migration","Sea turtles, eels and whales use currents to travel vast distances. Baby loggerhead turtles from Florida ride the Gulf Stream into the North Atlantic gyre, drifting for years before returning to coastal waters."]],
sp:["The rubber ducks","In January 1992, a container of 28,800 plastic bath toys, including yellow ducks, fell off a ship in the North Pacific. Over the following years, the ‘Friendly Floatees’ washed up in Alaska, Hawaii and even the Atlantic, helping oceanographers study currents."],
g:[["Ocean current","A continuous flow of seawater"],["Gyre","A large circular system of ocean currents"],["Density","How heavy something is for its size"],["Upwelling","Cold, deep water rising to the surface"]],
f:["The Antarctic Circumpolar Current is the world’s largest current.","Deep ocean water may take about 1,000 years to circulate.","The Gulf Stream helps keep Britain and Ireland mild."],
fun:"Benjamin Franklin noticed that British mail ships took weeks longer to cross the Atlantic than American whaling ships, because the whalers knew to avoid sailing against the Gulf Stream."});

ADDART({id:"clouds",t:"Clouds",c:"Geography",i:"☁️",w:"Cloud",r:["wcycle","storms","weatherforecast","thunderstorms","rainbows","fog","hail"],
p:[["Floating water","Clouds are made of tiny water droplets or ice crystals."],["Named in Latin","Clouds are classified by shape and height, using names like cumulus and cirrus."],["Weather clues","Clouds help forecast weather and affect Earth’s climate."]],
l:"A cloud is a visible mass of tiny water droplets or ice crystals floating in the atmosphere. Clouds form when air containing water vapor rises and cools, so the vapor condenses onto tiny particles of dust, salt or smoke. Although clouds look light, a typical fluffy cumulus cloud contains hundreds of metric tons of water, spread out in droplets so small they float. In 1802, the English chemist Luke Howard proposed naming clouds with Latin words describing their shape: cumulus (‘heap’), stratus (‘layer’), cirrus (‘curl of hair’) and nimbus (‘rain cloud’), a system still used today. The World Meteorological Organization’s International Cloud Atlas recognizes ten main types, grouped by height. Clouds bring rain and snow, warn of storms and shape climate, both reflecting sunlight back into space and trapping heat. Watching clouds has long been a way for farmers, sailors and pilots to predict the weather.",
s:[["How clouds form","As moist air rises, for example when warmed by the ground, pushed up mountains or forced over colder air, it expands and cools. When it cools to its dew point, water vapor condenses into droplets. Without tiny particles called condensation nuclei, droplets would struggle to form."],["Types of clouds","High clouds, such as wispy cirrus, are made of ice crystals. Mid-level clouds include altocumulus and altostratus. Low clouds include stratus, which can form gray blankets or fog, and stratocumulus. Cumulus and cumulonimbus grow upward; a cumulonimbus thunderstorm cloud can tower 15 kilometers or more."],["Rain, snow and hail","Cloud droplets are far too small to fall. They must collide and merge, or ice crystals must grow at the expense of droplets, until they become heavy enough to fall as rain or snow. Strong updrafts in thunderstorms can carry ice up and down repeatedly, building hailstones."],["Clouds and climate","Low, thick clouds tend to cool the Earth by reflecting sunlight, while high, thin clouds tend to warm it by trapping heat. How clouds will change as the climate warms is one of the biggest uncertainties in climate science. Aircraft contrails, made of ice crystals from engine exhaust, add artificial clouds."]],
k:[["Made of","Water droplets or ice crystals"],["Naming system","Luke Howard, 1802"],["Main types","Ten, in the International Cloud Atlas"],["Tallest clouds","Cumulonimbus, up to 15+ km"],["Highest clouds","Noctilucent clouds, about 80 km"]],
d:[["1802","Luke Howard names the clouds"],["1896","First International Cloud Atlas published"],["1960","First weather satellite images of clouds"],["2005","Cloud Appreciation Society founded"],["2017","New cloud types, such as asperitas, added to the Atlas"]],
s2:[["Unusual clouds","Lenticular clouds, shaped like lenses or flying saucers, form over mountains. Mammatus clouds hang like pouches under storm clouds. Noctilucent clouds, the highest on Earth, glow blue in summer twilight near the poles. Asperitas, with wave-like undersides, was officially recognized in 2017 after cloud-watchers shared photos."],["Cloud seeding","Cloud seeding sprays tiny particles, such as silver iodide, into clouds to encourage rain or snow. It is used in countries including China, the United Arab Emirates and the United States, though scientists debate how much extra rain it produces."]],
sp:["How heavy is a cloud?","Scientists estimate that an average cumulus cloud, about a kilometer across, contains roughly 500 metric tons of water, about as heavy as 100 elephants. It floats because the water is spread out in billions of tiny droplets supported by rising air."],
g:[["Condensation","Water vapor turning into liquid"],["Dew point","The temperature at which air becomes saturated"],["Cumulus","A puffy, heaped cloud"],["Cirrus","A thin, wispy high cloud made of ice"]],
f:["An average cumulus cloud holds about 500 metric tons of water.","Cloud names come from Latin.","Fog is a cloud touching the ground."],
fun:"The phrase ‘on cloud nine’ may come from an early cloud classification in which cloud number nine was the towering cumulonimbus, the highest of all."});

ADDART({id:"recommender",t:"Recommendation Algorithms",c:"Technology",i:"🎯",w:"Recommender system",r:["algorithm","mlearn","socialmedia","streaming","dataprivacy"],
p:[["‘You might also like’","Recommendation algorithms suggest videos, products, songs and posts."],["Everywhere online","They power feeds on YouTube, TikTok, Netflix, Spotify and online shops."],["Helpful and controversial","They help people find things, but raise concerns about addiction and bubbles."]],
l:"A recommendation algorithm, or recommender system, is software that predicts what a person is likely to want and suggests it, such as the next video to watch, a product to buy, a song to play or a post to see. Recommender systems analyze huge amounts of data, including what you have clicked, watched, bought, liked and skipped, and compare it with the behavior of millions of other users. Early systems used collaborative filtering, the idea that people who agreed in the past will agree again, and content-based filtering, which suggests items similar to ones you liked. Modern systems use machine learning and deep neural networks. They are central to online platforms: Netflix has said most of what members watch comes from recommendations, and YouTube has said recommendations drive most of its watch time. They help people discover things they enjoy, but critics argue they can encourage endless scrolling, spread extreme or misleading content, create ‘filter bubbles’ and depend on extensive personal data.",
s:[["How they work","Collaborative filtering looks for patterns: if many people who liked film A also liked film B, someone who likes A may be shown B. Content-based systems use features of items, such as genre or keywords. Modern platforms combine many signals, including how long you watch, what time it is and what device you use, to rank thousands of possible items in a fraction of a second."],["History","In the early 1990s, research projects such as GroupLens recommended news articles and films based on ratings. Amazon introduced item-to-item recommendations (‘customers who bought this also bought’) in the late 1990s. From 2006 to 2009, Netflix offered a one-million-dollar prize to anyone who could improve its recommendations by 10 percent; a team of researchers won in 2009."],["The feed","Social media feeds, once shown in time order, are now mostly ranked by algorithms predicting what will keep users engaged. TikTok’s ‘For You’ page, which quickly learns users’ tastes from their viewing, made short video recommendations especially powerful."],["Concerns and rules","Critics worry that maximizing engagement can favor sensational content and make it hard to stop scrolling, especially for young people. The term ‘filter bubble’, coined by Eli Pariser in 2011, describes people seeing mainly views that match their own, though studies disagree on how strong this effect is. The EU’s Digital Services Act requires large platforms to explain how their recommendations work and offer a feed not based on profiling."]],
k:[["Main methods","Collaborative and content-based filtering"],["Netflix Prize","2006–2009, one million dollars"],["Famous phrase","‘Customers who bought this also bought’"],["‘Filter bubble’ coined","2011, Eli Pariser"],["EU rules","Digital Services Act"]],
d:[["1992","GroupLens and other early recommender research"],["1998","Amazon develops item-to-item recommendations"],["2006","Netflix Prize launched"],["2009","Netflix Prize won"],["2016","TikTok’s algorithm-driven feed launches in China as Douyin"],["2024","EU Digital Services Act fully applies"]],
s2:[["Taking control","Many apps let users tell the algorithm they are ‘not interested’, clear their watch history, mute topics or switch to a chronological feed. Choosing who to follow and searching deliberately also shape what you see."],["Recommendations offline","Recommendation systems are also used for matching job seekers with jobs, suggesting friends, planning delivery routes and helping doctors find relevant research. Libraries and bookshops have long done a human version of the same thing."]],
sp:["The long tail","Recommendation systems help people discover less popular items, such as niche books, films and songs, that physical shops could not stock. The writer Chris Anderson called this the ‘long tail’ in 2004, arguing that many small sales could add up to a big market."],
g:[["Algorithm","A set of step-by-step instructions for a computer"],["Collaborative filtering","Recommending based on similar users’ choices"],["Engagement","How much people interact with content"],["Filter bubble","Seeing mostly information that matches your views"]],
f:["Most viewing on Netflix comes from recommendations.","Netflix once offered a one-million-dollar prize to improve its recommendations.","The EU requires large platforms to offer a feed not based on profiling."],
fun:"The team that won the Netflix Prize submitted its winning entry just 20 minutes before a rival team submitted one that scored exactly the same."});

ADDART({id:"coal",t:"Coal",c:"Technology",i:"⚫",w:"Coal",r:["fossilfuels","steam","airpollution","climchange","energytransition"],
p:[["Fossil fuel","Coal is a black rock formed from ancient plants, burned for energy."],["Fueled industry","Coal powered the Industrial Revolution and still makes over a third of the world’s electricity."],["High cost","Burning coal is the largest single source of carbon dioxide emissions."]],
l:"Coal is a black or brown sedimentary rock formed from the remains of plants that grew in swamps hundreds of millions of years ago, buried and compressed over time. It is mostly carbon and burns to release heat. Coal powered the Industrial Revolution, fueling steam engines, locomotives, ships, iron and steel works and gas lighting, and it transformed economies first in Britain and then around the world. Today coal is still used to generate more than a third of the world’s electricity and to make steel and cement. China burns more than half of the world’s coal, and India is the second-largest user. But coal is the most polluting fossil fuel: burning it releases more carbon dioxide per unit of energy than oil or gas, making it the largest single source of greenhouse gas emissions, and it causes air pollution that harms health. Coal mining has also been dangerous. Many countries plan to phase out coal power, and Britain closed its last coal power station in 2024.",
s:[["Formation","Most of the world’s coal formed during the Carboniferous period, about 359 to 299 million years ago, when vast swamp forests of giant ferns and tree-like plants covered parts of the land. As plants died, they formed peat, which was buried and pressed by layers of sediment, becoming lignite, then bituminous coal and finally hard anthracite."],["Coal and industry","Coal was used in China for heating and metalworking over 2,000 years ago. In 18th-century Britain, steam engines were first used to pump water from coal mines, and coal-fired engines then drove factories and railways. Coal towns grew around mines, and coal miners formed some of the first large trade unions."],["Mining dangers","Underground mining exposes workers to explosions of methane gas and coal dust, roof collapses and lung disease. The Courrières disaster in France in 1906 killed more than 1,000 miners, and the Benxihu colliery explosion in China in 1942 killed over 1,500. In 1966, a coal waste tip collapsed onto the village of Aberfan in Wales, killing 144 people, including 116 children."],["The future of coal","Many countries are replacing coal with gas, nuclear, wind and solar power. Britain, where industrial coal use began, closed its last coal power plant, Ratcliffe-on-Soar, on 30 September 2024. Yet global coal use reached record levels in the mid-2020s because of rising demand in Asia, and debates continue about how to help coal-mining communities in the transition."]],
k:[["Formed from","Ancient swamp plants"],["Main formation period","Carboniferous, 359–299 million years ago"],["Share of world electricity","More than a third"],["Largest user","China, over half of world consumption"],["Britain’s last coal plant closed","30 September 2024"]],
d:[["1712","Newcomen steam engine pumps water from a coal mine"],["1906","Courrières mining disaster in France"],["1942","Benxihu colliery disaster in China"],["1966","Aberfan disaster in Wales"],["2021","COP26 agrees to ‘phase down’ coal"],["2024","Britain closes its last coal power station"]],
s2:[["Types of coal","Peat is partly decayed plant matter, the first stage of coal formation. Lignite, or brown coal, is soft and crumbly. Bituminous coal is the most common, used in power stations and steel-making. Anthracite is hard, shiny and burns with little smoke but is the rarest."],["Coal and steel","Coking coal is heated without air to make coke, a nearly pure carbon fuel used in blast furnaces to turn iron ore into iron. Steelmakers are testing ways to replace coke with hydrogen to cut emissions."]],
sp:["Canaries in the mine","Until 1986, British coal miners took canaries underground because the birds were more sensitive to carbon monoxide gas than people. If a canary showed distress, miners knew to leave, giving rise to the phrase ‘canary in the coal mine’ for an early warning."],
g:[["Fossil fuel","A fuel formed from ancient living things, like coal, oil and gas"],["Carboniferous","The geological period when much coal formed"],["Anthracite","The hardest, highest-quality coal"],["Coke","A fuel made by heating coal without air"]],
f:["Most coal formed about 300 million years ago.","China burns more than half of the world’s coal.","Britain, birthplace of the Industrial Revolution, ended coal power in 2024."],
fun:"Jet, a black gemstone used in Victorian mourning jewelry, is a kind of coal formed from fossilized wood."});

ADDART({id:"locks",t:"Locks and Keys",c:"Technology",i:"🔑",w:"Lock and key",r:["machines","cybersecurity","ciphers","wheel","smarthome"],
p:[["Keeping things safe","Locks are devices that keep doors, boxes and containers closed until opened with a key or code."],["Ancient inventions","Wooden pin locks were used in Mesopotamia and Egypt about 4,000 years ago."],["From keys to codes","Modern locks use pin tumblers, combinations, cards, fingerprints and phones."]],
l:"A lock is a mechanical or electronic device that keeps a door, gate, container or vehicle closed until it is released by a key, code, card or other means. Some of the oldest known locks were large wooden pin locks used in Mesopotamia and Egypt around 4,000 years ago, opened with wooden keys that lifted pins out of a bolt. The Romans made metal locks and keys, some small enough to wear as rings. In the 18th and 19th centuries, inventors in Britain and America designed more secure locks, including Joseph Bramah’s famous challenge lock of 1784 and the pin tumbler lock developed by Linus Yale Jr. in the 1860s, still the most common door lock today. Combination locks, padlocks and safes followed. Modern locks include electronic keycards in hotels, keypads, fingerprint and face recognition and smart locks opened by phones. As locks improved, so did lock-picking, leading to a long contest between lock makers and those trying to defeat them.",
s:[["Ancient locks","The Egyptian-style pin lock had a wooden bolt with holes into which pins dropped from above, locking it. A large wooden key with matching pegs pushed the pins up so the bolt could slide. The ancient Greeks used simpler locks opened with hooked keys, and the Romans developed warded locks with internal obstacles that only the correct key could pass."],["The lock-picking race","In 1784, Joseph Bramah displayed a lock in his London shop window with a sign offering 200 guineas to anyone who could pick it. It remained unpicked for 67 years until the American locksmith Alfred Hobbs opened it in 1851 after about 51 hours of work, during the Great Exhibition, where he also picked other ‘unpickable’ locks."],["The pin tumbler lock","In a pin tumbler lock, spring-loaded pins of different lengths block a cylinder from turning. The right key pushes each pin to exactly the right height, lining them up so the cylinder turns. Linus Yale Sr. and his son Linus Yale Jr. developed modern versions in the mid-1800s, along with small flat keys."],["Electronic and smart locks","Hotel keycards, introduced from the 1970s, store codes on magnetic strips or chips. Smart locks connect to phones and the internet, letting owners unlock doors remotely or give temporary access. Security experts warn that electronic locks can be hacked, so they must be designed and updated carefully."]],
k:[["Oldest known locks","About 4,000 years ago, Mesopotamia and Egypt"],["Most common door lock","Pin tumbler lock"],["Developer of modern pin tumbler","Linus Yale Jr., 1860s"],["Bramah’s challenge","1784, picked in 1851"],["Modern types","Keycards, keypads, biometrics, smart locks"]],
d:[["c. 2000 BC","Wooden pin locks in Mesopotamia and Egypt"],["c. 100","Roman metal keys and ring keys"],["1778","Robert Barron patents the double-acting lever tumbler lock"],["1784","Bramah’s challenge lock"],["1851","Alfred Hobbs picks the Bramah lock"],["1861","Linus Yale Jr. patents a pin tumbler lock"],["1975","Electronic hotel keycards introduced"]],
s2:[["Combination locks","Combination locks open when dials are turned to the correct sequence of numbers. They are used on safes, bank vaults, luggage and bicycle locks. Bank vaults also use time locks that cannot be opened before a set time, even with the right combination."],["Lock sport","Lock sport is a hobby in which enthusiasts pick locks they own, or locks given to them with permission, to understand how they work. Security researchers use similar skills to find weaknesses that manufacturers can fix."]],
sp:["Houdini and locks","The escape artist Harry Houdini became famous in the early 1900s for escaping from handcuffs, jail cells and locked boxes, challenging police to restrain him. He studied locks carefully and sometimes hid small tools, keeping many of his methods secret."],
g:[["Lock","A device that keeps something closed"],["Tumbler","A movable part in a lock that must be set correctly to open it"],["Combination","A sequence of numbers that opens a lock"],["Biometric","Using body features, like fingerprints, for identification"]],
f:["Some of the oldest locks were made of wood.","Bramah’s lock went unpicked for 67 years.","Most door locks today are pin tumbler locks."],
fun:"Some Roman keys were made as rings so their owners could carry them on their fingers and keep them safe."});

ADDART({id:"uluru",t:"Uluru",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🪨",w:"Uluru",r:["australiacountry","aboriginal","desert","rocks","overtourism"],
p:[["A giant sandstone rock","Uluru is a huge sandstone rock in the desert of central Australia."],["Sacred to the Anangu","It is a sacred place for the Anangu, its Aboriginal owners."],["Changing colors","Uluru glows red and orange at sunrise and sunset."]],
l:"Uluru is an enormous sandstone rock formation, also known as Ayers Rock, in the desert of Australia’s Northern Territory, near the center of the continent. It rises about 348 meters above the surrounding plain, is about 3.6 kilometers long and has a circumference of about 9.4 kilometers, and much more of the rock lies hidden underground. Uluru is famous for appearing to change color, glowing deep red and orange at sunrise and sunset. It is sacred to the Anangu, the Aboriginal people of the area, whose ancestral stories, known in English as the Dreaming, explain its features, caves and waterholes, and who have lived there for tens of thousands of years. Together with the nearby domes of Kata Tjuta, it forms Uluṟu-Kata Tjuṯa National Park, a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Ownership was returned to the Anangu in 1985, and in 2019 climbing the rock was permanently banned out of respect for its cultural significance.",
s:[["Geology","Uluru is made of arkose, a coarse sandstone rich in feldspar, formed from sediments eroded from ancient mountains over 500 million years ago. Later earth movements tilted the layers nearly upright. Iron minerals in the rock rust when exposed to air, giving Uluru its red color; inside, the rock is gray."],["Anangu culture","For the Anangu, Uluru is a living cultural landscape. Tjukurpa, their system of law and knowledge, tells how ancestral beings shaped the land. Rock art in Uluru’s caves is thousands of years old. Some places around the base are especially sacred and should not be photographed."],["Handback","In 1873 the explorer William Gosse named the rock Ayers Rock after a South Australian politician. On 26 October 1985, the Australian government returned the title to the Anangu, who lease the land back to the national parks agency and manage the park jointly. The park was listed as a World Heritage Site in 1987 and recognized for its cultural values in 1994."],["Climbing ban","For decades, tourists climbed Uluru, although the Anangu asked them not to; at least 37 people died on the climb. The park board voted to close it, and the climb closed permanently on 26 October 2019. Visitors now walk around the base, take tours with Aboriginal guides and watch the sunset."]],
k:[["Height above plain","About 348 m"],["Length","About 3.6 km"],["Rock type","Arkose sandstone"],["Traditional owners","Anangu"],["Handback","26 October 1985"],["Climbing banned","26 October 2019"]],
d:[["Ancient times","Aboriginal people live in the area for tens of thousands of years"],["1873","William Gosse names it Ayers Rock"],["1985","Ownership returned to the Anangu"],["1987","World Heritage listing"],["2002","Official name becomes ‘Uluru / Ayers Rock’"],["2019","Climbing permanently banned"]],
s2:[["Kata Tjuta","About 25 kilometers west of Uluru, Kata Tjuta (‘many heads’) is a group of 36 rounded domes of conglomerate rock, the tallest rising higher than Uluru. It is also sacred to the Anangu, and the Valley of the Winds walk passes between the domes."],["Desert life","Despite the dry climate, the area supports red kangaroos, thorny devil lizards, many birds and hardy plants. After rare heavy rains, waterfalls pour down Uluru’s sides, a spectacular but brief sight."]],
sp:["The Field of Light","In 2016, British artist Bruce Munro installed the Field of Light near Uluru: tens of thousands of solar-powered glass spheres that glow in changing colors at night across the desert. It became one of the area’s most popular attractions."],
g:[["Sandstone","Rock formed from compressed sand"],["Anangu","The Aboriginal traditional owners of Uluru"],["Tjukurpa","Anangu law, knowledge and ancestral stories"],["World Heritage Site","A place protected by UNESCO for its importance"]],
f:["Uluru is 348 meters high.","Inside, the rock is gray; its red color comes from rusting iron.","Climbing Uluru was banned in 2019."],
fun:"Uluru’s color changes are so striking that many visitors photograph it every few minutes at sunset to capture the shift from orange to deep red to purple."});

ADDART({id:"tasmania",t:"Tasmania",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🏝️",w:"Tasmania",r:["australiacountry","aboriginal","galapagos","trees","kangaroo","thylacine"],
p:[["Australia’s island state","Tasmania is a mountainous island state south of mainland Australia."],["Wild nature","Nearly half of Tasmania is protected parks and wilderness."],["Unique animals","It is the last home of the wild Tasmanian devil."]],
l:"Tasmania is an island state of Australia, lying about 240 kilometers south of the mainland across Bass Strait. With about 575,000 people, it is the smallest Australian state by area and population. Its capital is Hobart. Tasmania has rugged mountains, rainforests, rivers, lakes and beaches, and around 40 percent of its land is protected in national parks and reserves, including the Tasmanian Wilderness World Heritage Area. Aboriginal Tasmanians, the palawa, lived on the island for at least 40,000 years. The Dutch explorer Abel Tasman reached it in 1642, and the British established a colony in 1803, using it as a major destination for convicts. Colonization devastated the Aboriginal population through violence and disease. Tasmania is famous for unique wildlife, including the Tasmanian devil and, until its extinction in the 20th century, the thylacine, or Tasmanian tiger. Today its clean environment, food, history and arts attract many visitors.",
s:[["Aboriginal history","When rising seas flooded Bass Strait about 10,000–12,000 years ago, Aboriginal Tasmanians became isolated from the mainland. After British settlement, conflict known as the Black War (1820s–1832) and disease killed most of the Aboriginal population, and survivors were removed to Flinders Island. Truganini, who died in 1876, was long wrongly described as the ‘last’ Aboriginal Tasmanian; thousands of palawa descendants live in Tasmania today."],["Convict colony","Tasmania, then called Van Diemen’s Land, received about 75,000 convicts between 1803 and 1853, when transportation ended. The Port Arthur penal settlement, founded in 1830, is now a historic site and part of the Australian Convict Sites World Heritage listing. The colony was renamed Tasmania in 1856."],["Wilderness and conservation","In the early 1980s, a campaign to stop a dam on the Franklin River became one of Australia’s most famous environmental battles; the High Court ruled for the river’s protection in 1983. The movement helped give rise to the world’s first green political party, the United Tasmania Group, in 1972."],["Wildlife","The Tasmanian devil, a fierce-sounding marsupial carnivore about the size of a small dog, survives in the wild only in Tasmania. Since 1996, a contagious facial tumor disease has killed most devils, and conservationists breed healthy populations. The last known thylacine died in Hobart Zoo on 7 September 1936."]],
k:[["Capital","Hobart"],["Population","About 575,000"],["Distance from mainland","About 240 km across Bass Strait"],["Protected land","About 40%"],["European arrival","Abel Tasman, 1642"],["Famous animal","Tasmanian devil"]],
d:[["Ancient times","Aboriginal people live in Tasmania for at least 40,000 years"],["1642","Abel Tasman reaches the island"],["1803","British colony founded"],["1830","Port Arthur penal settlement founded"],["1853","Transportation of convicts ends"],["1856","Van Diemen’s Land renamed Tasmania"],["1936","Last known thylacine dies"],["1982","Tasmanian Wilderness World Heritage listing"]],
s2:[["The thylacine","The thylacine, a striped, dog-like marsupial, was hunted to extinction after farmers blamed it for killing sheep. Unconfirmed sightings are still reported, and scientists have studied its preserved DNA, with some companies even proposing to bring it back."],["Cleanest air","The Cape Grim station on Tasmania’s northwest coast measures some of the cleanest air on Earth, because winds arrive after crossing thousands of kilometers of Southern Ocean. Its records are used to track changes in the global atmosphere."]],
sp:["MONA","The Museum of Old and New Art (MONA) in Hobart, opened in 2011 by gambler and collector David Walsh, is built into a riverside cliff and displays provocative ancient and modern art. It became one of Australia’s most talked-about museums."],
g:[["Marsupial","A mammal that carries its young in a pouch"],["Convict","A person serving a prison sentence, often sent to a colony"],["Penal settlement","A colony for prisoners"],["Wilderness","Wild, natural land largely untouched by people"]],
f:["Tasmania was first called Van Diemen’s Land.","Wild Tasmanian devils live only in Tasmania.","The last known thylacine died in 1936."],
fun:"Tasmanian devils got their name from early European settlers who heard their loud, eerie screeches at night."});

;
/* data/modern29.js */
// New articles, 17 November 2026 (part 3): more animals.
ADDART({id:"koalas",t:"Koalas",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐨",w:"Koala",r:["kangaroo","australiacountry","trees","firefighters","conservation"],
p:[["Not a bear","Koalas are marsupials from eastern Australia, not bears."],["Eucalyptus eaters","They eat almost nothing but eucalyptus leaves."],["Under threat","Habitat loss, disease and bushfires have reduced their numbers."]],
l:"The koala is a tree-dwelling marsupial found in eucalyptus forests and woodlands of eastern and southeastern Australia. Although often called ‘koala bears’, koalas are not bears; like kangaroos, they carry their young in a pouch. Koalas feed almost entirely on eucalyptus leaves, which are tough, low in nutrients and toxic to most animals. A long gut and special bacteria help them digest the leaves, but the diet gives them little energy, so they sleep or rest for up to 20 hours a day. A baby koala, called a joey, is about the size of a jellybean at birth and lives in its mother’s pouch for about six months. Koala numbers have fallen because of land clearing, car strikes, dog attacks, the disease chlamydia and droughts and bushfires, including the 2019–20 fires. In 2022, koalas in Queensland, New South Wales and the Australian Capital Territory were listed as endangered.",
s:[["Body and senses","Koalas have thick, woolly gray fur, large fluffy ears and a big leathery nose that helps them smell which leaves are safe to eat. Their hands have two opposable digits for gripping branches, and their fingerprints are so similar to human ones that they are hard to tell apart under a microscope."],["Diet","Of the hundreds of eucalyptus species, koalas eat only a few dozen, preferring certain ones in each region. An adult eats about 200–500 grams of leaves a day. Joeys eat a special soft dropping from their mother, called pap, which passes on the gut bacteria they need."],["Life cycle","Koalas usually live alone, with males bellowing loudly in the breeding season. After about 35 days of pregnancy, the tiny joey crawls into the pouch, which opens toward the mother’s back legs. It begins to peek out at about six months and rides on her back before becoming independent at about a year."],["Conservation","Experts estimate that koala numbers have declined sharply in parts of Queensland and New South Wales over recent decades. Protecting and replanting eucalyptus forests, building wildlife bridges and fences along roads, and developing a chlamydia vaccine are among the efforts to help them."]],
k:[["Type of animal","Marsupial"],["Native to","Eastern and southeastern Australia"],["Diet","Eucalyptus leaves"],["Sleep","Up to 20 hours a day"],["Status (eastern populations)","Endangered (2022)"]],
d:[["1798","First European record of a koala"],["1920s","Millions killed for the fur trade"],["1927","Koala hunting ends after public outcry"],["2019–20","Bushfires kill or injure tens of thousands of koalas"],["2022","Koalas listed as endangered in Queensland, NSW and the ACT"]],
s2:[["Why so sleepy?","Eucalyptus leaves provide very little energy, and digesting their tough fibers and toxins takes a lot of effort. Sleeping most of the day saves energy. Koalas also hug cool tree trunks on hot days to keep their bodies from overheating."],["The fur trade","In the early 20th century, millions of koalas were shot for their fur, which was exported to Britain and North America. Public anger after a huge open season in Queensland in 1927 helped end koala hunting."]],
sp:["Koala hospitals","The Port Macquarie Koala Hospital in New South Wales, founded in 1973, treats injured and sick koalas. During the 2019–20 bushfires, people around the world donated to help it rescue burned koalas and set up drinking stations in the forests."],
g:[["Marsupial","A mammal that raises its young in a pouch"],["Joey","A baby marsupial"],["Eucalyptus","A group of Australian trees, also called gum trees"],["Chlamydia","A bacterial disease that affects koalas"]],
f:["Koalas are marsupials, not bears.","Koala fingerprints look very like human fingerprints.","A newborn koala is about the size of a jellybean."],
fun:"The word ‘koala’ is often said to come from an Aboriginal Australian word meaning ‘no water’, because koalas get most of their water from leaves, though linguists are not certain."});

ADDART({id:"meerkats",t:"Meerkats",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦦",w:"Meerkat",r:["animalbehavior","desert","sahara","foodchains","snake"],
p:[["Desert teamwork","Meerkats are small mongooses that live in cooperative groups in southern Africa."],["Sentinels","One meerkat stands guard while others search for food."],["Studied for decades","Researchers have followed wild meerkats in the Kalahari since 1993."]],
l:"The meerkat is a small mammal of the mongoose family that lives in the dry grasslands and deserts of southern Africa, including the Kalahari. Meerkats live in groups called mobs, gangs or clans of up to about 50 animals, sharing burrow systems and working together to survive. While the group digs for food, one or more members act as sentinels, standing upright on a high point to watch for predators such as eagles, jackals and snakes, and give different alarm calls depending on the threat. Meerkats eat mainly insects and their larvae, as well as scorpions, lizards, eggs and small animals. They are partly resistant to scorpion venom and teach their young to handle dangerous prey. Usually one dominant female produces most of the pups, and other group members help raise them. Long-term research in the Kalahari has made meerkats among the best-studied wild mammals, and television series made them famous.",
s:[["Body","Meerkats are about 25–35 centimeters long, plus a long tail used for balance when standing up. Dark patches around their eyes reduce glare from the sun, and they can close their ears to keep out sand while digging. Strong curved claws let them dig quickly through hard ground."],["Life in the mob","A mob usually has a dominant breeding pair. Other adults babysit pups in the burrow, feed them and protect them. The dominant female sometimes evicts other females or kills their pups to keep resources for her own young."],["Teaching","Researchers found that meerkat helpers teach pups to handle scorpions step by step: first giving them dead scorpions, then scorpions with stings removed, and finally live ones. This was one of the first clear examples of teaching in wild animals."],["Danger and defense","When a predator approaches, meerkats run to bolt holes. If cornered, they bunch together, raise their tails and hiss, appearing as one larger animal. Sentinels often take turns, and recent studies suggest well-fed meerkats volunteer for guard duty more often."]],
k:[["Family","Mongooses"],["Range","Southern Africa"],["Group size","Up to about 50"],["Diet","Mainly insects, also scorpions and lizards"],["Research project","Kalahari Meerkat Project (since 1993)"]],
d:[["1993","Kalahari Meerkat Project begins"],["1994","The Lion King features the meerkat character Timon"],["2005","Meerkat Manor TV series begins"],["2006","Study shows meerkats teach pups to handle scorpions"]],
s2:[["Alarm calls","Meerkats have different calls for predators coming from the air and on the ground, and the calls also signal how urgent the danger is. Group members react differently, running to holes for an eagle or gathering to mob a snake."],["Sun-worshippers","On cold desert mornings, meerkats sit up facing the sun to warm the dark, thinly furred skin on their bellies, which helps them heat up quickly before foraging."]],
sp:["Meerkat Manor","The documentary series Meerkat Manor, filmed in the Kalahari from 2005, followed real meerkat families such as the Whiskers mob and their leader Flower. It became a hit worldwide and drew attention to long-term animal research."],
g:[["Mongoose","A family of small, active carnivorous mammals"],["Sentinel","An animal that keeps watch for danger"],["Mob","A group of meerkats"],["Forage","To search for food"]],
f:["Meerkats belong to the mongoose family.","Meerkats teach their young how to handle scorpions.","Dark eye patches act like sunglasses."],
fun:"Timon from The Lion King made meerkats famous, though real meerkats would be more likely to eat a warthog’s insects than befriend one."});

ADDART({id:"armadillos",t:"Armadillos",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🛡️",w:"Armadillo",r:["hedgehogs","pangolins","sloths","amazon","grasslands","glyptodon","anteaters"],
p:[["Armored mammals","Armadillos are mammals protected by bony plates covered in tough skin."],["From the Americas","About 20 species live in South, Central and North America."],["Ancient giants","Their extinct relatives, glyptodonts, were as big as small cars."]],
l:"Armadillos are mammals of the Americas covered by a shell of bony plates called osteoderms, overlaid with tough, scaly skin. The name is Spanish for ‘little armored one’. There are about 20 living species, ranging from the pink fairy armadillo, about 10 centimeters long, to the giant armadillo, up to 1.5 meters including its tail. Armadillos live in grasslands, forests and semi-deserts from Argentina to the southern United States. They have poor eyesight but a keen sense of smell and use strong claws to dig for insects, especially ants and termites, as well as grubs and worms, and to make burrows. Only the three-banded armadillos can roll into a complete ball for protection; others run, dig or press themselves flat. The nine-banded armadillo, which has spread across the southern United States, almost always gives birth to identical quadruplets from a single egg. Armadillos are related to anteaters and sloths.",
s:[["The shell","The armor covers the head, back, legs and tail. Flexible bands of skin between the plates in the middle of the back let the animal bend; species are often named by the number of bands. Despite the armor, armadillos are agile and can run and swim, some crossing streams by walking along the bottom while holding their breath."],["Diet and senses","Armadillos sniff out food underground and dig it up quickly, using long sticky tongues to lap up ants and termites. The giant armadillo can break open termite mounds and digs burrows used by many other animals, making it an important ‘ecosystem engineer’."],["Nine-banded armadillo","The nine-banded armadillo expanded from Mexico into Texas in the 19th century and has since spread north and east. Its identical quadruplets make it useful in medical research. It is one of the few animals besides humans that can carry the bacteria that cause leprosy, so people are advised not to handle or eat wild armadillos."],["Ancient relatives","Glyptodonts, giant relatives of armadillos, lived in the Americas until about 10,000 years ago. Some had domed shells nearly 1.5 meters tall and weighed as much as a small car. Early humans may have used their shells as shelters."]],
k:[["Name meaning","‘Little armored one’ (Spanish)"],["Number of species","About 20"],["Smallest","Pink fairy armadillo, about 10 cm"],["Largest","Giant armadillo, up to 1.5 m"],["Relatives","Anteaters and sloths"]],
d:[["c. 10,000 years ago","Giant glyptodonts die out"],["1850s","Nine-banded armadillos spread into Texas"],["1971","Armadillos first used in leprosy research"],["1995","Nine-banded armadillo named the state small mammal of Texas"]],
s2:[["Rolling into a ball","The Brazilian three-banded armadillo can curl into an almost perfect ball, fitting its head and tail plates together to close the gap. It was chosen as the mascot, named Fuleco, of the 2014 FIFA World Cup in Brazil to raise awareness of its endangered status."],["Jumping in fright","When startled, nine-banded armadillos jump straight up, sometimes nearly a meter into the air. This can scare predators but causes many armadillos to be hit by cars on roads."]],
sp:["The pink fairy armadillo","The pink fairy armadillo of Argentina is the smallest armadillo, with a pale pink shell and silky white fur. It spends most of its life underground, ‘swimming’ through sand, and is so rarely seen that little is known about it."],
g:[["Osteoderm","A bony plate in the skin"],["Burrow","A hole dug in the ground for shelter"],["Ecosystem engineer","An animal that changes its habitat in ways that help others"],["Quadruplets","Four babies born together"]],
f:["Armadillo means ‘little armored one’ in Spanish.","Nine-banded armadillos usually have identical quadruplets.","Only three-banded armadillos can roll into a full ball."],
fun:"Armadillos can hold their breath for about six minutes, letting them walk across the bottom of small streams."});

ADDART({id:"capybaras",t:"Capybaras",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦫",w:"Capybara",r:["rodents","amazon","wetlands","animalbehavior","symbiosis"],
p:[["The largest rodent","The capybara is the world’s biggest rodent."],["Water lovers","Capybaras live near rivers and swamps in South America and are excellent swimmers."],["Famously calm","Their relaxed nature has made them internet stars."]],
l:"The capybara is the largest living rodent, found across much of South America east of the Andes, in grasslands, savannas, forests and wetlands near rivers, lakes and swamps. Adults can be over a meter long and weigh up to about 65 kilograms. Capybaras are semi-aquatic: their slightly webbed feet make them strong swimmers, and their eyes, ears and nostrils sit high on the head so they can stay almost fully submerged to hide from predators such as jaguars, anacondas and caimans. They eat grasses and water plants and, like rabbits, eat some of their own droppings to digest tough plants fully. Capybaras live in social groups, usually 10 to 20 animals, and communicate with barks, whistles and purrs. Their calm behavior, often letting birds and other animals sit on them, has made them popular online and in zoos. In parts of South America they are hunted for meat and leather and farmed.",
s:[["Body","Capybaras have barrel-shaped bodies, short reddish-brown fur, a blunt face and no visible tail. Their front teeth, like those of all rodents, keep growing throughout life and are worn down by chewing. Males have a scent gland on the snout used to mark territory."],["Life in water","Capybaras cool off and escape danger in water and can stay under for up to five minutes. They often mate in water. In the dry season, large groups gather around remaining pools."],["Social life","A group usually includes a dominant male, several females, young and lower-ranking males. Females may nurse one another’s young. Capybaras are mostly active at dawn and dusk, resting during the hot part of the day."],["People and capybaras","In Venezuela, capybara meat is traditionally eaten during Lent; according to a famous story, 16th-century church authorities classified the semi-aquatic animal as fish. Capybaras have adapted to some cities and gated communities, such as Nordelta near Buenos Aires, where their arrival in 2021 sparked debate about building on wetlands."]],
k:[["Title","Largest living rodent"],["Weight","Up to about 65 kg"],["Range","South America"],["Habitat","Near rivers, lakes and swamps"],["Close relatives","Guinea pigs"]],
d:[["16th century","Capybara classified as ‘fish’ for Lent in Venezuela, according to tradition"],["1982","A Japanese zoo starts giving capybaras hot baths"],["2010s","Capybaras become internet stars"],["2021","Capybaras move into the Nordelta neighborhood near Buenos Aires"]],
s2:[["Friends with everyone?","Photos often show birds, monkeys, turtles and even cats sitting on capybaras. Birds eat ticks and insects from their skin, a helpful relationship. Their calmness is partly because sitting still saves energy and helps them watch for danger."],["Hot springs","At some Japanese zoos, capybaras bathe in warm water baths with floating citrus fruits in winter, a tradition that began by accident at Izu Shaboten Zoo in 1982 and now draws crowds of visitors."]],
sp:["Guinea pig cousins","Capybaras are closely related to guinea pigs and cavies. Their name comes from a Tupi word often translated as ‘grass eater’ or ‘master of the grasses’."],
g:[["Rodent","A mammal with constantly growing front teeth, like rats and squirrels"],["Semi-aquatic","Living partly in water and partly on land"],["Wetland","Land covered by shallow water, like a swamp"],["Lent","The Christian period of fasting before Easter"]],
f:["The capybara is the largest rodent in the world.","Capybaras can stay underwater for up to five minutes.","Their closest relatives include guinea pigs."],
fun:"Zoos in Japan hold capybara ‘bath-time’ events in winter, where the animals soak in hot tubs with floating yuzu fruit."});

ADDART({id:"raccoons",t:"Raccoons",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦝",w:"Raccoon",r:["megacities","invasiveplants","animalbehavior","foxes","rabies"],
p:[["Masked bandits","Raccoons are clever, adaptable mammals with black ‘mask’ markings."],["City dwellers","They thrive in cities, raiding garbage bins."],["Spreading worldwide","Introduced raccoons now live wild in Europe and Japan."]],
l:"The raccoon is a medium-sized mammal native to North and Central America, known for the black ‘mask’ across its eyes, its ringed tail and its nimble front paws. Raccoons are omnivores and eat almost anything: fruit, nuts, insects, frogs, fish, eggs and human food from garbage cans. Their front paws are extremely sensitive, with many nerve endings, and raccoons often feel food carefully, sometimes in water, which led to the myth that they wash their food. Raccoons are intelligent problem-solvers that can remember solutions to puzzles for years. Originally forest animals, they have adapted so well to people that they are often more numerous in cities than in the countryside. Raccoons can carry diseases such as rabies and roundworm, so people should not feed or touch them. Introduced for fur farming or as pets, raccoons have become invasive in Germany, other parts of Europe and Japan.",
s:[["Body","Raccoons weigh about 5–12 kilograms and have grayish fur, a bushy tail with dark rings and a black mask that may reduce glare. Their front feet look like small hands, and they can grip and open latches, jars and bins."],["Intelligence","In experiments, raccoons have opened complex locks and remembered how for up to three years. In Toronto, scientists observed raccoons defeating new ‘raccoon-proof’ green bins introduced in 2016, though most bins kept them out."],["The touch sense","Much of a raccoon’s brain is devoted to processing touch from its paws. Wetting the paws may make them even more sensitive, explaining the ‘washing’ behavior. The scientific name, lotor, means ‘washer’."],["Raccoons around the world","Raccoons were released in Germany in 1934 and escaped from fur farms, and they now number in the hundreds of thousands there. In Japan, after a 1970s anime about a pet raccoon became popular, thousands were imported as pets; many were released and now damage crops and temples."]],
k:[["Native to","North and Central America"],["Diet","Omnivore"],["Scientific name","Procyon lotor"],["Famous features","Black mask, ringed tail, nimble paws"],["Invasive in","Germany and other parts of Europe, Japan"]],
d:[["1934","Raccoons released in Germany"],["1977","Japanese anime about a pet raccoon airs"],["2016","Toronto introduces ‘raccoon-proof’ green bins"],["2016","EU lists raccoons as an invasive species"]],
s2:[["Trash pandas","Raccoons’ habit of raiding garbage earned them the nickname ‘trash pandas’ online. Securing bin lids, not leaving pet food outside and blocking access to attics and chimneys help prevent problems."],["Raccoon relatives","Raccoons belong to the family Procyonidae, which includes coatis, kinkajous and ringtails. Despite the nickname and similar markings, they are not closely related to red pandas or giant pandas."]],
sp:["Raccoons and rabies","In parts of the eastern United States, raccoons are a major carrier of rabies. Authorities drop millions of vaccine-laced baits from aircraft and vehicles each year to immunize wild raccoons and stop the disease spreading."],
g:[["Omnivore","An animal that eats both plants and animals"],["Invasive species","A non-native species that causes harm"],["Dexterous","Skillful with the hands"],["Rabies","A deadly viral disease spread by animal bites"]],
f:["Raccoons’ front paws are extremely sensitive.","Raccoons can remember puzzle solutions for years.","Raccoons do not actually wash their food."],
fun:"The word raccoon comes from a Powhatan word, recorded by English colonists, meaning ‘one who rubs, scrubs and scratches with its hands’."});

ADDART({id:"beavers",t:"Beavers",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦫",w:"Beaver",r:["rodents","wetlands","rivers","conservation","trees"],
p:[["Nature’s engineers","Beavers build dams and lodges that change rivers and create wetlands."],["Two species","The North American and Eurasian beavers are the only two living species."],["Back from the brink","Hunted almost to extinction for fur, beavers are returning."]],
l:"Beavers are large, semi-aquatic rodents known for building dams, canals and lodges from branches, mud and stones. There are two species: the North American beaver and the Eurasian beaver. They are the second-largest rodents after capybaras, weighing up to about 30 kilograms. Beavers have orange, iron-strengthened front teeth that never stop growing, which they use to cut down trees, a flat scaly tail used as a rudder and to slap the water as a warning, and webbed back feet. By damming streams, beavers create ponds that protect them from predators and give access to food. These ponds and wetlands store water, reduce floods and droughts, filter pollution and provide homes for fish, birds, insects and amphibians, so beavers are called ‘ecosystem engineers’. The fur trade nearly wiped out beavers in Europe and North America, but protection and reintroduction programs have brought them back to many areas.",
s:[["Builders","Beavers fell trees by gnawing around the trunk and then cut branches to build dams across streams, packing them with mud. Behind the dam, a pond forms. They build lodges, dome-shaped homes of sticks and mud with underwater entrances and a dry living chamber above the water."],["Life in the family","A beaver colony is usually a family of a breeding pair and their young from the last two years. Beavers store branches underwater near the lodge as winter food. They are mostly active at night."],["The fur trade","From the 1600s to the 1800s, beaver fur was used for felt hats in Europe, driving exploration and trade across North America. By 1900, beavers were almost gone from most of Europe and much of North America."],["Return of the beaver","Beavers were reintroduced to many European countries in the 20th century. In Scotland, a trial reintroduction began in 2009, and in 2025 England approved licensed releases into the wild. Scientists value beavers for restoring wetlands, while some farmers worry about flooded land and felled trees."]],
k:[["Species","North American and Eurasian beavers"],["Weight","Up to about 30 kg"],["Famous for","Building dams and lodges"],["Teeth","Orange, iron-strengthened, always growing"],["Role","Ecosystem engineer"]],
d:[["1600s–1800s","Fur trade drives beavers toward extinction"],["1922","Sweden reintroduces beavers"],["2007","World’s longest known beaver dam spotted by satellite in Canada"],["2009","Trial reintroduction begins in Scotland"],["2025","England approves licensed wild releases"]],
s2:[["The longest dam","In 2007, a researcher using satellite images found a beaver dam about 850 meters long in Wood Buffalo National Park in Canada, so long it can be seen from space. Several generations of beavers probably built it over decades."],["Beavers and climate","Beaver ponds hold back water during floods and keep streams flowing and land green during droughts, which can also slow wildfires. Some regions now use beavers or beaver-like dams to adapt to climate change."]],
sp:["Orange teeth","A beaver’s front teeth are orange because the enamel contains iron, which makes them stronger and more resistant to wear. The softer back of each tooth wears away faster, keeping the front edge sharp like a chisel."],
g:[["Ecosystem engineer","An animal that changes its habitat for many other species"],["Lodge","A beaver’s home of sticks and mud"],["Dam","A barrier that holds back water"],["Reintroduction","Returning a species to an area where it had died out"]],
f:["Beaver teeth are orange because they contain iron.","A beaver dam in Canada is about 850 meters long.","Beavers slap their tails on water to warn of danger."],
fun:"The beaver is the national animal of Canada and appears on the Canadian five-cent coin."});

ADDART({id:"axolotls",t:"Axolotls",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦎",w:"Axolotl",r:["salamander","frog","metamorph","extinction","mexicocountry"],
p:[["Forever young","Axolotls are salamanders that keep their juvenile features all their lives."],["Regeneration champions","They can regrow lost limbs and even parts of their heart and brain."],["Critically endangered","In the wild, they survive only in canals near Mexico City."]],
l:"The axolotl is a salamander native only to the lake and canal system of Xochimilco, near Mexico City. Unlike most amphibians, which change from water-living larvae into land-living adults, axolotls usually stay in their larval form for life, a condition called neoteny: they keep feathery external gills, a finned tail and live entirely in water, yet they grow to full size, about 15–30 centimeters, and can reproduce. Axolotls are famous for their extraordinary ability to regenerate: they can regrow lost legs, tails, parts of the spinal cord and heart, and even parts of the brain, without scarring, making them important in medical research. In the wild, axolotls are critically endangered because of pollution, urban growth, the draining of Lake Xochimilco and introduced fish such as tilapia and carp. Millions live in laboratories and home aquariums worldwide. The axolotl is a cultural symbol of Mexico and appeared on its 50-peso banknote in 2021.",
s:[["Neoteny","Axolotls have low levels of the thyroid hormones that trigger metamorphosis in other salamanders. Scientists can make them transform into a land-dwelling form by adding hormones, but this shortens their lives. Their pink, gray, black or golden colors come in many varieties in aquariums."],["Regeneration","If an axolotl loses a limb, cells at the wound form a structure called a blastema, which grows a complete new limb with bones, muscles and nerves in a few weeks. Researchers hope that understanding this process could one day help human wound healing and regeneration."],["In the wild","Surveys found about 6,000 axolotls per square kilometer in Xochimilco in 1998 but only about 35 per square kilometer by 2014. Conservationists work with farmers who use traditional floating gardens, called chinampas, to create clean refuges for axolotls."],["Culture","The name comes from the Nahuatl language, linked to Xolotl, an Aztec god associated with fire, lightning and transformation. Axolotls have become popular in art, toys and video games such as Minecraft, which added them in 2021."]],
k:[["Native to","Xochimilco, near Mexico City"],["Special trait","Neoteny (stays larval for life)"],["Superpower","Regenerates limbs, heart and brain tissue"],["Wild status","Critically endangered"],["Banknote","Mexican 50-peso note (2021)"]],
d:[["1864","Axolotls brought to Paris for study"],["1998","Survey finds about 6,000 per km² in Xochimilco"],["2014","Survey finds only about 35 per km²"],["2018","Axolotl genome sequenced"],["2021","Axolotl appears on Mexico’s 50-peso note"]],
s2:[["A giant genome","The axolotl genome, sequenced in 2018, is about ten times larger than the human genome, one of the largest of any animal ever sequenced. Its size made it hard to study and may hold clues to regeneration."],["Pet axolotls","Axolotls are popular pets but need cool, clean water and careful care. They should never be released into the wild, where they could spread disease or harm local species."]],
sp:["Chinampas","Chinampas are artificial islands built on lake beds by the Aztecs for farming. Some still exist in Xochimilco, and projects encourage farmers to keep canals around them clean, providing safe homes for axolotls."],
g:[["Neoteny","Keeping juvenile features as an adult"],["Regeneration","Regrowing lost body parts"],["Gills","Organs for breathing underwater"],["Larva","The young form of an animal that changes as it grows"]],
f:["Axolotls keep their gills all their lives.","They can regrow limbs without scars.","Their genome is about ten times bigger than ours."],
fun:"Because of their wide-mouthed ‘smiling’ faces, axolotls are sometimes called Mexican walking fish, even though they are amphibians, not fish."});

ADDART({id:"woodpeckers",t:"Woodpeckers",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐦",w:"Woodpecker",r:["birds","trees","insect","animalbehavior","owl"],
p:[["Drumming birds","Woodpeckers hammer on trees with their beaks to find food, make nests and communicate."],["Built for pecking","Strong neck muscles, a tough skull and a long tongue help them."],["Forest helpers","Their old holes become homes for many other animals."]],
l:"Woodpeckers are a family of about 240 species of birds that live in forests and woodlands on every continent except Australia and Antarctica, and are also absent from New Zealand and Madagascar. They are known for hammering on tree trunks with their strong, chisel-like beaks to dig out insects, excavate nest holes and drum loudly to attract mates and defend territory. A woodpecker may peck up to 20 times a second and thousands of times a day. Their bodies are specially adapted: stiff tail feathers prop them against trunks, two toes point forward and two backward for gripping, and a remarkably long tongue, supported by a bony structure that wraps around the skull, reaches deep into holes to catch insects with its sticky or barbed tip. Holes made by woodpeckers are later used by owls, bats, squirrels and other birds, so woodpeckers are important to forest ecosystems.",
s:[["How they avoid headaches","Scientists have studied how woodpeckers survive repeated impacts. A small brain, a skull with spongy bone, a short contact time with the wood and the angle of pecking all help. A 2022 study suggested the skull acts more like a stiff hammer than a shock absorber, and the forces stay below levels that would injure their small brains."],["Feeding","Most woodpeckers eat insects and their larvae from inside wood or under bark. Sapsuckers drill rows of small holes to drink tree sap, and acorn woodpeckers store thousands of acorns in holes in a single tree, called a granary."],["Drumming","Each species has its own drumming rhythm, which birds use like a song. Woodpeckers sometimes drum on metal gutters, chimneys or signs because they make louder sounds."],["Famous woodpeckers","The ivory-billed woodpecker of the southern United States and Cuba, one of the largest, may be extinct; possible sightings have been reported but not confirmed. The great spotted woodpecker is common across Europe and Asia, and the tiny downy woodpecker visits garden feeders in North America."]],
k:[["Number of species","About 240"],["Absent from","Australia, New Zealand, Madagascar, Antarctica"],["Pecking speed","Up to about 20 times a second"],["Special feature","Tongue wraps around the skull"],["Foot arrangement","Two toes forward, two back"]],
d:[["1940s","Woody Woodpecker cartoon character created"],["1944","Last widely accepted sighting of the ivory-billed woodpecker in the USA"],["2004","Disputed reports of ivory-billed woodpecker in Arkansas"],["2022","Study challenges the shock-absorber idea of woodpecker skulls"]],
s2:[["Helping other animals","An abandoned woodpecker hole may become a nest for bluebirds, chickadees, owls or parrots, or a shelter for bats and squirrels. Forest managers often leave dead trees standing so woodpeckers can use them."],["Inspiring engineers","Woodpecker skulls have inspired research into helmets and shock-resistant packaging for electronics, although new studies suggest the real story of how they avoid injury is more complicated than once thought."]],
sp:["The acorn granary","Acorn woodpeckers in western North America live in family groups that drill thousands of holes into a single tree or telephone pole and fill each with an acorn. One granary tree was found holding about 50,000 acorns."],
g:[["Drumming","Rapid pecking used to communicate"],["Excavate","To dig out"],["Granary","A storehouse for food"],["Larvae","Young insects, like grubs"]],
f:["There are no woodpeckers in Australia.","A woodpecker’s tongue can wrap around its skull.","Woodpeckers can peck about 20 times a second."],
fun:"Woodpeckers sometimes peck holes in the foam insulation of houses, possibly because the hollow sound resembles a tree with insects inside."});

ADDART({id:"peafowl",t:"Peafowl",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦚",w:"Peafowl",r:["birds","evolution","indiacountry","animalbehavior","parrot"],
p:[["The peacock’s tail","Male peafowl, called peacocks, display spectacular tails of eyespot feathers."],["Three species","There are Indian, green and Congo peafowl."],["A puzzle for Darwin","The peacock’s tail helped Darwin develop his theory of sexual selection."]],
l:"Peafowl are large birds of the pheasant family. The males are called peacocks, the females peahens, and the young peachicks. The best known is the Indian peafowl, native to South Asia and the national bird of India; the green peafowl lives in Southeast Asia and the Congo peafowl in the rainforests of central Africa. Peacocks are famous for their long ‘trains’ of up to about 200 shimmering feathers decorated with eyespots, which they raise into a fan and shake during courtship displays. The colors come not from pigments but from tiny structures in the feathers that reflect light. Peahens are mostly brown and gray, giving them camouflage when nesting. The peacock’s extravagant tail puzzled Charles Darwin, because it seems to make survival harder; he concluded that it evolved because peahens prefer males with impressive tails, an idea called sexual selection. Peafowl are kept in parks and gardens worldwide.",
s:[["The train","The train is not the tail itself but long feathers growing from the back, held up by shorter tail feathers underneath. It grows each year before the breeding season and is shed afterward. During display, the peacock rattles the feathers, producing a low sound that peahens may detect."],["Structural color","The blues and greens of peacock feathers are made by microscopic crystal-like structures that reflect certain wavelengths of light, like a soap bubble. Changing the viewing angle shifts the colors, which is why they shimmer."],["Darwin and sexual selection","In 1860 Darwin wrote that the sight of a peacock’s feather made him feel sick, because it was hard to explain by survival alone. In The Descent of Man (1871), he argued that features that attract mates can evolve even if they are costly. Studies of peahens’ choices continue to test the idea."],["Peafowl and people","Peafowl have been kept by humans for thousands of years and appear in Hindu, Buddhist, Christian and Persian art. The peacock is associated with the Hindu god Kartikeya and with Saraswati. The Peacock Throne of the Mughal emperors was decorated with jeweled peacocks."]],
k:[["Species","Indian, green and Congo peafowl"],["Male, female, young","Peacock, peahen, peachick"],["Train feathers","Up to about 200"],["National bird of","India (since 1963)"],["Color type","Structural color"]],
d:[["c. 1000 BC","Peafowl traded from India to the Mediterranean"],["1635","Mughal Peacock Throne completed"],["1860","Darwin writes that peacock feathers make him ‘sick’"],["1871","Darwin publishes The Descent of Man"],["1936","Congo peafowl described by scientists"],["1963","Indian peafowl declared national bird of India"]],
s2:[["The green peafowl","The green peafowl of Southeast Asia is taller and slimmer, and both males and females are brightly colored. It is endangered because of hunting and habitat loss and has disappeared from much of its former range."],["White peafowl","White peafowl are not albinos but have a genetic condition called leucism that removes color from their feathers while their eyes stay dark. They are popular in parks and gardens."]],
sp:["The Congo peafowl","The Congo peafowl was identified only in 1936, after an American scientist recognized unusual feathers in a hat and later found matching specimens in a museum. It lives in remote forests and is rarely seen."],
g:[["Train","The peacock’s long display feathers"],["Courtship","Behavior to attract a mate"],["Sexual selection","Evolution driven by choice of mates"],["Structural color","Color produced by tiny structures rather than pigment"]],
f:["Only male peafowl are peacocks.","Peacock feather colors come from structures, not pigments.","The Congo peafowl was discovered thanks to a feather in a hat."],
fun:"Peacocks can fly despite their long trains, usually flapping up into trees to roost safely at night."});

ADDART({id:"deepsea",t:"Deep-Sea Creatures",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐙",w:"Deep-sea creature",r:["trench","fishes","squid","jelly","pacific","sponges","coelacanth"],
p:[["Life in the dark","The deep sea is cold, dark and under crushing pressure, yet full of life."],["Glowing animals","Many deep-sea animals make their own light."],["Barely explored","Much of the deep ocean has never been seen by humans."]],
l:"The deep sea, usually defined as ocean below about 200 meters, is the largest habitat on Earth. Below about 1,000 meters, in the ‘midnight zone’, no sunlight reaches, temperatures are near freezing and pressure is hundreds of times that at the surface, increasing to more than 1,000 times in the deepest trenches. Yet a huge variety of animals live there, many with strange adaptations: anglerfish dangle glowing lures to attract prey, gulper eels have enormous mouths, giant squid have eyes as big as dinner plates, and many animals are transparent, red or black to stay hidden. Most deep-sea animals produce light by bioluminescence to find mates, lure food or confuse predators. Food is scarce, much of it ‘marine snow’ falling from above, but hydrothermal vents support rich communities fed by chemical energy. Scientists estimate that most deep-sea species have not yet been described, and debates continue over deep-sea mining.",
s:[["Zones of the ocean","The sunlit zone is the top 200 meters. The twilight zone, to 1,000 meters, receives faint light. The midnight zone reaches about 4,000 meters, and below that lie the abyssal plains and the hadal zone of ocean trenches, reaching nearly 11 kilometers in the Challenger Deep."],["Adaptations","Many deep-sea fish have large eyes to catch faint light, or no eyes at all. Some have huge mouths and stretchy stomachs to swallow rare meals bigger than themselves. Bodies often contain little bone or muscle and lots of water, which helps them withstand pressure."],["Hydrothermal vents","In 1977, scientists in the submersible Alvin discovered hot springs on the seafloor near the Galápagos Islands, surrounded by giant tube worms, clams and shrimp. Bacteria there make food from chemicals such as hydrogen sulfide, a process called chemosynthesis, showing life can thrive without sunlight."],["Exploration","In 1960, Jacques Piccard and Don Walsh reached the bottom of the Challenger Deep in the bathyscaphe Trieste, and filmmaker James Cameron made a solo dive there in 2012. Robots and cameras now explore the deep, and in 2022 a snailfish was filmed at about 8,300 meters, the deepest fish ever recorded."]],
k:[["Deep sea begins","About 200 m"],["Midnight zone","Below about 1,000 m"],["Deepest point","Challenger Deep, about 10,935 m"],["Light source","Bioluminescence"],["Vents discovered","1977, near the Galápagos"]],
d:[["1872–76","HMS Challenger expedition finds life on the deep seafloor"],["1960","Trieste reaches the Challenger Deep"],["1977","Hydrothermal vents discovered"],["2004","First photos of a live giant squid in the wild"],["2012","James Cameron’s solo dive to the Challenger Deep"],["2022","Deepest fish ever filmed, at about 8,300 m"]],
s2:[["Deep-sea mining","Some companies want to mine metal-rich nodules from the deep seafloor for batteries. Scientists warn that mining could destroy slow-growing habitats that take thousands of years to recover, and many countries have called for a pause until the effects are understood."],["Marine snow","Dead plankton, droppings and other particles drift down from the surface like snow, taking weeks to reach the deep seafloor. This ‘marine snow’ is the main food source for many deep-sea animals and carries carbon down into the ocean depths."]],
sp:["The anglerfish","Female deep-sea anglerfish have a fin ray on their head tipped with a glowing lure containing light-producing bacteria. In some species, tiny males bite onto females and fuse with them permanently, sharing their blood supply."],
g:[["Bioluminescence","Light produced by living things"],["Pressure","The force of water pressing down"],["Chemosynthesis","Making food using chemical energy instead of sunlight"],["Submersible","A small vehicle that dives deep underwater"]],
f:["Most of Earth’s living space is in the deep sea.","Most deep-sea animals can make light.","Life thrives around hot vents without sunlight."],
fun:"After Piccard and Walsh reached the bottom of the Challenger Deep in 1960, nobody went back for 52 years, until James Cameron’s solo dive in 2012."});

ADDART({id:"ladybugs",t:"Ladybugs",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐞",w:"Coccinellidae",r:["beetle","insect","urbanfarm","invasiveplants","foodchains"],
p:[["Spotted beetles","Ladybugs, or ladybirds, are small, colorful beetles."],["Gardeners’ friends","Many eat aphids and other plant pests."],["Bright warning","Their bright colors warn predators that they taste bad."]],
l:"Ladybugs, called ladybirds in Britain and lady beetles by some scientists, are a family of about 6,000 species of small beetles found around the world. Most are rounded and brightly colored, typically red or orange with black spots, though some are yellow, black, pink or striped. These bright colors warn birds and other predators that ladybugs taste bitter and can be toxic; when threatened, they release drops of foul-tasting yellow fluid from their leg joints, a defense called reflex bleeding. Many ladybugs are predators of aphids, scale insects and mites that damage crops and garden plants, and a single ladybug may eat thousands of aphids in its lifetime, so farmers and gardeners welcome them, and some are used for natural pest control. The number of spots does not show age; it differs by species. The harlequin ladybird, introduced from Asia for pest control, has spread across Europe and North America, outcompeting native species.",
s:[["Life cycle","Ladybugs go through complete metamorphosis. Females lay clusters of yellow eggs near aphid colonies. The larvae, which look like tiny spiky alligators, eat large numbers of aphids before forming a pupa, from which the adult emerges. The whole cycle can take about a month."],["Pest control","In the 1880s, the vedalia beetle, a ladybug from Australia, was brought to California to control cottony cushion scale insects destroying citrus trees, one of the first successful examples of biological pest control. Ladybugs are still sold to gardeners and farmers."],["Winter gatherings","Many ladybugs hibernate in large groups in sheltered places such as under bark, in rock crevices or inside houses. Some species gather by the thousands on mountaintops."],["The harlequin problem","The harlequin ladybird is large, hungry and highly variable in color, with anywhere from no spots to about 20. Introduced to control pests, it spread rapidly in North America from the late 1980s and Britain from 2004, eating native ladybugs’ food and even their larvae."]],
k:[["Number of species","About 6,000"],["Other names","Ladybird, lady beetle"],["Main prey","Aphids and scale insects"],["Defense","Warning colors and reflex bleeding"],["Invasive species","Harlequin ladybird"]],
d:[["1888","Vedalia beetles introduced to California to save citrus crops"],["1988","Harlequin ladybirds established in North America"],["2004","Harlequin ladybird first recorded in Britain"]],
s2:[["Why ‘lady’?","The name ladybird or ladybug is thought to come from medieval Europe, where farmers prayed to the Virgin Mary, ‘Our Lady’, for help against pests. When the beetles arrived and ate the pests, they were named after her. The red wings were linked to Mary’s red cloak in paintings, and the seven spots of one species to her seven sorrows."],["Ladybugs in culture","In many countries ladybugs are considered lucky. A traditional English rhyme begins ‘Ladybird, ladybird, fly away home’. In some places, a ladybug landing on you is said to bring good fortune."]],
sp:["Spot the difference","The seven-spot ladybird, common in Europe, has seven black spots on red wing covers. The two-spot ladybird has two, and the 22-spot ladybird is yellow with 22 small black spots. Counting spots helps identify species, not age."],
g:[["Aphid","A small sap-sucking insect that harms plants"],["Reflex bleeding","Releasing fluid from joints to deter predators"],["Biological pest control","Using living things to control pests"],["Metamorphosis","Change from larva to adult"]],
f:["There are about 6,000 species of ladybugs.","A ladybug’s spots do not show its age.","Ladybug larvae look like tiny spiky alligators."],
fun:"Ladybugs beat their wings about 85 times a second when they fly."});

;
/* data/modern30.js */
// New articles, 17 November 2026 (part 4): plants, technology and space.
ADDART({id:"tulips",t:"Tulips",c:"Plants",i:"🌷",w:"Tulip",r:["flower","netherlandscountry","ottoman","stockm","turkeycountry"],
p:[["Spring bulbs","Tulips are spring flowers that grow from bulbs."],["From Central Asia","Wild tulips come from the mountains of Central Asia and Turkey."],["A famous mania","In 1637, Dutch tulip prices soared and then collapsed."]],
l:"Tulips are flowering plants of the genus Tulipa, in the lily family, that grow from bulbs and bloom in spring. There are about 75 to 100 wild species, native to a band stretching from southern Europe and North Africa across Turkey and Iran to the mountains of Central Asia, where the greatest variety grows. Each plant usually has a single cup-shaped flower with six petal-like tepals, in almost every color except true blue. Tulips were grown in Persia and became beloved in the Ottoman Empire, where an early 18th-century period of luxury is called the Tulip Era. They reached Western Europe in the 16th century, and the Netherlands became their center. In the Dutch ‘tulip mania’ of the 1630s, rare bulbs sold for more than the price of a house before prices crashed in February 1637. Today the Netherlands grows most of the world’s tulip bulbs, billions a year, and its Keukenhof gardens draw visitors from around the world.",
s:[["How tulips grow","A tulip bulb is an underground bud wrapped in fleshy scale leaves that store food. Bulbs need a cold winter before they will flower. After blooming, the leaves make food that is stored in new bulbs for the next year. Gardeners plant bulbs in autumn for flowers in spring."],["Tulips in the Ottoman Empire","Ottoman sultans filled their gardens with tulips, prized long slender flowers and painted them on tiles, textiles and pottery. The reign of Sultan Ahmed III, from 1718 to 1730, is called the Tulip Era for its gardens, festivals and Western-influenced culture."],["Tulip mania","Botanist Carolus Clusius planted tulips in Leiden around 1594, and they became a status symbol in the Dutch Republic. In the 1630s, people traded contracts for bulbs, and prices for rare striped varieties such as ‘Semper Augustus’ climbed wildly until the market collapsed in February 1637. Historians debate how much real harm the crash caused, but it became a famous example of a speculative bubble."],["Broken tulips","The most prized tulips in the 1630s had feathered, flame-like stripes. Scientists discovered in the 20th century that these were caused by a virus, spread by aphids, that weakened the bulbs. Modern striped tulips are bred to have stable patterns without the virus."]],
k:[["Genus","Tulipa (lily family)"],["Wild species","About 75–100"],["Native range","Southern Europe to Central Asia"],["Biggest grower","The Netherlands"],["Tulip mania crash","February 1637"]],
d:[["c. 1594","Clusius plants tulips in Leiden"],["1637","Tulip mania collapses"],["1718–1730","The Tulip Era in the Ottoman Empire"],["1928","Scientists link striped ‘broken’ tulips to a virus"],["1950","Keukenhof gardens open to the public"],["1953","First Canadian Tulip Festival in Ottawa"]],
s2:[["A gift of thanks","During World War II, the Dutch royal family sheltered in Ottawa, Canada. In 1945 Princess Juliana sent 100,000 tulip bulbs to thank Canadians for helping liberate the Netherlands, and the Netherlands still sends bulbs each year. The gift inspired the Canadian Tulip Festival."],["Keukenhof","Keukenhof, near Lisse in the Netherlands, is one of the world’s largest flower gardens. About 7 million bulbs are planted each autumn for an eight-week spring season, attracting more than a million visitors."]],
sp:["The search for a black tulip","Breeders spent centuries trying to grow a black tulip, inspired partly by Alexandre Dumas’s 1850 novel The Black Tulip. Varieties such as ‘Queen of Night’ are a very deep purple, close to black."],
g:[["Bulb","An underground bud with food-storing leaves"],["Tepal","A petal-like part of a flower"],["Speculative bubble","When prices rise far above real value, then crash"],["Genus","A group of closely related species"]],
f:["Wild tulips come from the mountains of Central Asia.","Tulip bulbs need a cold winter to flower.","Striped ‘broken’ tulips were caused by a virus."],
fun:"Tulip petals are edible, and during the Dutch ‘Hunger Winter’ of 1944–45, starving people ate tulip bulbs to survive."});

ADDART({id:"bonsai",t:"Bonsai",c:"Plants",i:"🪴",w:"Bonsai",r:["japan","trees","urbanfarm","china","buddh"],
p:[["Trees in pots","Bonsai is the art of growing miniature trees in containers."],["From China to Japan","It developed from Chinese penjing and was refined in Japan."],["Living for centuries","Some bonsai trees are hundreds of years old."]],
l:"Bonsai, a Japanese word meaning ‘planted in a container’, is the art of growing small trees in pots and shaping them to look like full-sized trees in nature. Bonsai are not special dwarf species: almost any tree or shrub, such as pines, junipers, maples, elms and figs, can be kept small by careful pruning of branches and roots, wiring branches into shape and growing the tree in a shallow pot. The art developed from penjing, the Chinese practice of creating miniature landscapes in trays, which spread to Japan by about the 12th century. Japanese Buddhist monks and later wealthy collectors refined it into a distinct art form valuing simplicity, balance and the appearance of age. Bonsai spread worldwide in the 20th century after international exhibitions. Well-cared-for bonsai can live for centuries and are passed down through generations; a Japanese white pine that survived the 1945 atomic bombing of Hiroshima is displayed in Washington, D.C.",
s:[["How bonsai are made","Growers start from seeds, cuttings, nursery plants or trees collected from the wild. They prune branches and roots, pinch new growth, and wrap copper or aluminum wire around branches to bend them, removing it before it cuts into the bark. Trees are repotted every few years to trim roots and refresh soil."],["Styles","Classic styles include formal upright, with a straight trunk; informal upright; slanting; cascade, where the tree hangs below the pot like a tree on a cliff; and literati, with a tall, slender, bare trunk. Groups of trees can create a miniature forest."],["Care","Most bonsai trees must live outdoors, experiencing seasons like normal trees; only tropical species such as figs do well indoors. Because pots are shallow, bonsai need frequent watering, sometimes daily. They also need sunlight, fertilizer and protection from frost."],["Bonsai around the world","Bonsai became widely known outside Asia after exhibitions such as the 1970 Osaka World Expo. Japan’s Omiya Bonsai Village near Tokyo has famous nurseries and a bonsai museum. Clubs and exhibitions now exist in many countries."]],
k:[["Meaning","‘Planted in a container’"],["Origin","Chinese penjing"],["Common trees","Pines, junipers, maples, figs"],["Lifespan","Can be centuries"],["Famous survivor","Hiroshima white pine"]],
d:[["c. 700 AD","Chinese art of penjing depicted in tomb paintings"],["c. 1100s","Miniature trees spread to Japan"],["1625","The Yamaki white pine’s training begins (by tradition)"],["1970","Osaka World Expo shows bonsai to the world"],["1976","Japan gives 53 bonsai to the United States"]],
s2:[["The Hiroshima survivor","A Japanese white pine trained by the Yamaki family since at least 1625 survived the atomic bombing of Hiroshima in 1945, protected by a wall around their nursery. In 1976 it was among 53 bonsai given by Japan to the United States, and it is displayed at the National Bonsai and Penjing Museum in Washington."],["Patience and philosophy","Bonsai is often linked with Zen ideas of patience, simplicity and harmony with nature. Creating a mature bonsai takes many years, and growers say the tree is never truly finished."]],
sp:["Penjing and saikei","In Chinese penjing, trees are often combined with rocks, water and tiny figures to create whole landscapes. A related Japanese art, saikei, arranges several trees and stones in a tray to form a miniature scene."],
g:[["Pruning","Cutting branches or roots to shape a plant"],["Wiring","Wrapping wire around branches to bend them"],["Penjing","Chinese art of miniature landscapes"],["Cascade","A style where the tree hangs below the pot"]],
f:["Bonsai are normal trees kept small, not special dwarf species.","Most bonsai must live outdoors.","Some bonsai are hundreds of years old."],
fun:"Some bonsai trees sell for more than a million dollars, and a pine at a 1981 exhibition in Japan was reported to sell for about 1.3 million dollars."});

ADDART({id:"roses",t:"Roses",c:"Plants",i:"🌹",w:"Rose",r:["flower","weddings","urbanfarm","fruits","ukcountry"],
p:[["A favorite flower","Roses are woody shrubs with fragrant flowers, grown for thousands of years."],["Thousands of varieties","There are about 300 wild species and tens of thousands of garden varieties."],["Symbol of love","Roses carry meanings of love, beauty and politics."]],
l:"Roses are woody flowering shrubs and climbers of the genus Rosa, in the rose family, which also includes apples, cherries, strawberries and almonds. There are about 300 wild species, mostly native to Asia, with others in Europe, North America and northwest Africa, and tens of thousands of cultivated varieties. Fossils show roses existed at least 35 million years ago. Wild roses usually have five petals, while garden roses are bred for many petals, rich colors and scent. Their stems have sharp prickles, often called thorns, and after flowering many produce fruits called rose hips, rich in vitamin C. People have grown roses for thousands of years in China, Persia, Greece and Rome for beauty, perfume and medicine. Rose oil and rosewater are used in perfumes and foods. Roses symbolize love, beauty and secrecy, and are national flowers of the United States and England.",
s:[["Wild and garden roses","Old garden roses, grown before 1867, include gallica, damask and China roses. Roses from China, brought to Europe around 1800, could flower repeatedly through the season. Crossing them with European roses produced modern roses; ‘La France’, introduced in 1867, is considered the first hybrid tea, the classic long-stemmed florist’s rose."],["Perfume and food","Damask roses are grown in Bulgaria’s Rose Valley, Turkey and Iran for rose oil, also called attar of roses. It takes about 3,000–5,000 kilograms of petals to make one kilogram of oil. Rosewater flavors sweets in the Middle East and South Asia, and rose hips make tea and syrup."],["Roses in history","The Wars of the Roses (1455–1487) between the English royal houses of Lancaster and York were later named after their badges, the red and white roses. The Tudor rose combined both. Empress Josephine of France collected about 250 kinds of roses at her garden at Malmaison."],["Meanings","Red roses traditionally mean love, white roses purity, and yellow roses friendship. The Latin phrase sub rosa, ‘under the rose’, means in secret, from an old custom of hanging a rose over meetings. Roses are a symbol of socialist and labor parties in Europe."]],
k:[["Genus","Rosa (rose family)"],["Wild species","About 300"],["Oldest fossils","About 35 million years old"],["Fruit","Rose hip"],["National flower of","USA, England and others"]],
d:[["c. 500 BC","Roses grown in Chinese gardens"],["1455–1487","Wars of the Roses in England"],["c. 1800","Repeat-flowering China roses reach Europe"],["1867","‘La France’, the first hybrid tea rose"],["1945","The ‘Peace’ rose is named"],["1986","Rose becomes US national flower"]],
s2:[["The Peace rose","A French breeder, Francis Meilland, created a pale yellow rose with pink edges just before World War II and sent cuttings abroad to save it. In the United States it was named ‘Peace’ on 29 April 1945, and delegates at the founding of the United Nations later that year each received one. It became one of the best-selling roses ever."],["Thorns or prickles?","Botanists say roses have prickles, not thorns: prickles grow from the outer skin of the stem and snap off easily, while true thorns are modified branches. The prickles help climbing roses grip and protect them from animals."]],
sp:["The oldest rose","The ‘Thousand-year Rose’ climbing the wall of Hildesheim Cathedral in Germany is often said to be the oldest living rose. It survived the destruction of the cathedral in a 1945 air raid; its roots sprouted again from the rubble."],
g:[["Rose hip","The fruit of a rose"],["Hybrid tea","A popular type of modern garden rose"],["Attar","Fragrant oil made from flowers"],["Prickle","A sharp outgrowth from a stem’s skin"]],
f:["Apples and strawberries belong to the rose family.","Rose oil takes thousands of kilograms of petals per kilogram.","Roses have prickles, not true thorns."],
fun:"Rose hips contain more vitamin C than oranges, and during World War II, British children collected them to make syrup."});

ADDART({id:"plantsenses",t:"How Plants Sense and Communicate",c:"Plants",i:"🌱",w:"Plant perception (physiology)",r:["photo","leaves","roots","fungi","carnivplant","mimosa","plantdefenses","plantgrowth"],
p:[["Plants sense the world","Plants detect light, gravity, touch, water and chemicals."],["Chemical messages","Plants release chemicals that warn neighbors and attract helpers."],["No brain needed","Plants respond without nerves or brains, through cells and hormones."]],
l:"Although plants cannot move from place to place, they constantly sense and respond to their surroundings. Plants detect the direction, color and length of daylight, grow their roots downward and shoots upward by sensing gravity, bend toward light, close leaves when touched and track water and nutrients in the soil. They use chemical signals called hormones and electrical signals to send information between parts of the plant. Plants also communicate: when insects chew their leaves, many release scented chemicals that warn nearby plants to strengthen defenses and attract predators of the pests. Roots exchange chemical signals with soil microbes and fungi. Scientists have shown that Venus flytraps count touches and that some plants respond to the sound of chewing insects. Some researchers describe these abilities as plant ‘intelligence’, while others argue the term is misleading because plants have no brains or nerves, and claims about a ‘wood-wide web’ remain debated.",
s:[["Sensing light and gravity","Plants have light-sensing proteins that detect red, far-red and blue light, telling them when to sprout, flower or grow taller to escape shade. Charles Darwin and his son Francis showed in 1880 that seedlings sense light at their tips and bend toward it. Special cells in roots contain heavy starch grains that settle downward, helping the root sense gravity."],["Touch and movement","The sensitive plant Mimosa pudica folds its leaves within seconds when touched. Climbing plants coil tendrils around supports. A 2016 study found that the Venus flytrap counts touches on its trigger hairs: two touches snap the trap shut, and five start digestion."],["Talking with chemicals","When caterpillars attack a plant, it can release airborne chemicals that attract wasps that lay eggs in the caterpillars. Acacia trees and tomato plants raise their defenses when neighbors are damaged. A 2014 study showed that thale cress plants made more defensive chemicals after hearing recordings of caterpillars chewing."],["The ‘wood-wide web’","Many plants partner with fungi whose threads connect roots underground, trading sugars for nutrients. Some studies suggested trees share resources and warnings through these networks. A 2023 review found that much of the evidence was weaker than popular accounts claim, and scientists continue to investigate."]],
k:[["Senses","Light, gravity, touch, water, chemicals"],["Internal signals","Hormones and electrical signals"],["Fast mover","Mimosa pudica"],["Counting plant","Venus flytrap"],["Debated idea","The ‘wood-wide web’"]],
d:[["1880","Darwin publishes The Power of Movement in Plants"],["1920","Day length found to control flowering"],["1983","Studies suggest damaged trees warn neighbors"],["1997","Fungal networks found linking trees"],["2016","Venus flytraps shown to count touches"],["2023","Stressed plants found to make ultrasonic clicks"]],
s2:[["Plants that click","In 2023, Israeli scientists reported that tomato and tobacco plants make ultrasonic popping sounds, too high for humans to hear, when they are dry or cut. The sounds may come from air bubbles in their water tubes. Whether insects or other plants respond to the sounds is not yet known."],["Are plants intelligent?","Some scientists use the term ‘plant neurobiology’ to describe how plants process information. Many others reject it, saying that plants have no neurons and that their responses, though complex, are not the same as thinking. Both sides agree that plants are far more active and responsive than they seem."]],
sp:["The sensitive plant","Mimosa pudica folds its leaflets when touched by moving water out of cells at the base of each leaf. Experiments in 2014 showed that plants dropped repeatedly without harm stopped folding, suggesting they ‘learned’ to ignore the harmless disturbance, and remembered for weeks."],
g:[["Hormone","A chemical messenger in a living thing"],["Tropism","Growth toward or away from something, like light"],["Mycorrhiza","A partnership between plant roots and fungi"],["Ultrasonic","Sound too high-pitched for humans to hear"]],
f:["Venus flytraps count touches before closing.","Roots sense gravity using heavy starch grains.","Stressed tomato plants make ultrasonic clicks."],
fun:"Freshly cut grass smells the way it does because the damaged blades release chemicals as a distress signal."});

ADDART({id:"herbs",t:"Herbs",c:"Plants",i:"🌿",w:"Herb",r:["spice","medicinalplants","urbanfarm","photo","plantbased","dandelions","saffron","lavender","teaplant"],
p:[["Useful leaves","Herbs are plants whose leaves, flowers or seeds are used in cooking, medicine and fragrance."],["Ancient remedies","Many modern medicines came from plants used as herbal remedies."],["Easy to grow","Herbs like basil, mint and parsley grow well in small gardens and pots."]],
l:"In cooking, herbs are plants whose fresh or dried leaves, and sometimes flowers, are used to add flavor, such as basil, parsley, mint, rosemary, thyme, oregano, coriander (cilantro), dill and sage; they differ from spices, which usually come from seeds, bark, roots or fruit. In botany, the word ‘herb’ also means any plant without a woody stem. Many cooking herbs belong to the mint family, which have square stems and aromatic oils, or the carrot family. Herbs have been used as medicine for thousands of years: the Greek physician Dioscorides described about 600 medicinal plants in the first century AD, and monasteries grew herb gardens in medieval Europe. Several important modern drugs came from plants, including aspirin, based on a chemical in willow bark, and the malaria drug artemisinin, from sweet wormwood. Herbal remedies can have real effects but also side effects and drug interactions, so scientists test them carefully.",
s:[["Cooking herbs","Many cuisines are identified by their herbs: basil in Italian dishes, coriander and lemongrass in Southeast Asian food, dill in Scandinavian cooking and mint in Middle Eastern dishes. Soft herbs such as basil and parsley are usually added at the end of cooking, while woody herbs such as rosemary and thyme can cook for longer."],["Herbal medicine","Traditional systems of medicine, including Chinese medicine and Ayurveda in India, use thousands of plants. Scientists study these traditions to find new drugs. Some herbal remedies, such as St John’s wort, can interfere with prescription medicines, so doctors advise telling them about any herbs people take."],["From willow to aspirin","Ancient Egyptians and Greeks used willow bark for pain and fever. In the 19th century, chemists isolated salicin from it, and in 1897 chemists at the Bayer company made acetylsalicylic acid, sold as aspirin from 1899. It remains one of the world’s most widely used medicines."],["Growing herbs","Many herbs come from the sunny Mediterranean and like well-drained soil. Basil needs warmth, while mint spreads quickly and is often grown in pots to stop it taking over gardens. Herbs can grow on windowsills, making them popular in small homes and urban farms."]],
k:[["Common herbs","Basil, mint, parsley, rosemary, thyme"],["Main plant families","Mint and carrot families"],["Herb vs spice","Herbs are usually leaves; spices are seeds, bark or roots"],["Plant-based drug","Aspirin (from willow bark)"],["Nobel-winning herb drug","Artemisinin (2015)"]],
d:[["c. 1550 BC","Egyptian Ebers Papyrus lists hundreds of herbal remedies"],["c. 60 AD","Dioscorides writes De Materia Medica"],["1545","First university botanical garden founded in Padua"],["1673","Chelsea Physic Garden founded in London"],["1899","Aspirin goes on sale"],["2015","Tu Youyou wins Nobel Prize for artemisinin"]],
s2:[["Tu Youyou and artemisinin","In the 1970s, Chinese scientist Tu Youyou searched ancient texts for malaria treatments and found a 4th-century recipe using sweet wormwood soaked in cold water. Her team isolated artemisinin, which has saved millions of lives. She won the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 2015."],["Physic gardens","Medieval monasteries and early universities grew ‘physic gardens’ of medicinal herbs to teach students. The garden founded in Padua, Italy, in 1545 is the oldest university botanical garden still in its original location and is a UNESCO World Heritage Site."]],
sp:["Coriander and genes","Some people think fresh coriander (cilantro) tastes like soap. Studies suggest this is partly genetic: people with certain variations in a smell-receptor gene are more sensitive to soapy-smelling chemicals in the leaves."],
g:[["Herb","A plant used for flavor, medicine or scent; also a non-woody plant"],["Spice","A flavoring from seeds, bark, roots or fruit"],["Aromatic","Having a strong, pleasant smell"],["Physic garden","A garden of medicinal plants"]],
f:["Mint, basil and rosemary are all in the mint family.","Aspirin was developed from a chemical in willow bark.","Some people’s genes make coriander taste like soap."],
fun:"Members of the mint family have square stems, which you can feel by rolling a stem between your fingers."});

ADDART({id:"olives",t:"Olives",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🫒",w:"Olive",r:["med","greece","agri","climchange","spaincountry"],
p:[["A Mediterranean tree","The olive tree has been grown for thousands of years around the Mediterranean."],["Fruit and oil","Olives are eaten cured and pressed for olive oil."],["Symbol of peace","The olive branch is a symbol of peace."]],
l:"The olive is a small evergreen tree native to the Mediterranean region, grown for its fruit, which is eaten after curing and pressed to make olive oil. Olive trees have silvery-green leaves, gnarled trunks and can live for many centuries; some trees in the Mediterranean are thought to be more than 2,000 years old. People domesticated olives in the eastern Mediterranean at least 6,000–7,000 years ago, and olive oil became central to ancient Greek, Roman and Middle Eastern life, used for food, lamps, medicine, soap and religious rituals. Fresh olives are extremely bitter and must be cured in water, brine or lye before eating. Spain is by far the largest producer of olive oil, followed by countries including Italy, Greece, Turkey, Tunisia and Morocco. Droughts and heat waves linked to climate change and a deadly bacterial disease have hurt harvests in recent years, sending olive oil prices to record highs in 2023–24.",
s:[["From tree to oil","Olives are harvested in autumn and winter by hand, with rakes or by machines that shake the trees. The fruit is crushed into a paste, mixed and spun to separate the oil. ‘Extra virgin’ olive oil is made without heat or chemicals and has the best flavor; it takes about 5 kilograms of olives to make a liter of oil."],["Green and black olives","Green and black olives can come from the same tree: green olives are picked unripe, while black olives are picked ripe. Some black olives sold in cans are green olives darkened by exposure to air during processing."],["Olives in history and religion","The ancient Greeks believed the goddess Athena gave the olive tree to Athens. Winners at the ancient Olympic Games received olive wreaths. In the Bible, a dove brings Noah an olive leaf, and in Islam the olive is called a blessed tree. Olive oil is used in religious anointing."],["Threats","In 2013, the bacterium Xylella fastidiosa was found in olive trees in Puglia, southern Italy. Spread by insects, it has killed millions of trees, including centuries-old ones. Severe droughts in Spain in 2022 and 2023 roughly halved harvests, driving prices up worldwide before recovering in 2024–25."]],
k:[["Native region","Mediterranean"],["Domesticated","At least 6,000 years ago"],["Largest producer","Spain"],["Oil per liter","About 5 kg of olives"],["Threat","Xylella fastidiosa bacterium"]],
d:[["c. 5000 BC","Olives domesticated in the eastern Mediterranean"],["776 BC","Ancient Olympics begin, with olive wreaths for winners"],["16th century","Spanish colonists take olives to the Americas"],["2013","Xylella disease discovered in Puglia"],["2023–24","Drought pushes olive oil prices to record highs"]],
s2:[["The olive branch","Offering an olive branch has meant making peace since ancient times. Olive branches appear on the flag of the United Nations, surrounding a map of the world, and on the Great Seal of the United States, held by an eagle."],["Ancient trees","Olive trees can regrow from their roots even when the trunk dies, so some are extremely old. The olive tree of Vouves in Crete is estimated to be at least 2,000 years old and still produces olives."]],
sp:["Why fresh olives are bitter","Fresh olives contain a bitter chemical called oleuropein. Curing with water, salt or lye over weeks or months breaks it down. Some olives, such as the Thrubolea of Greece, ripen partly on the tree and are less bitter."],
g:[["Curing","Treating food to preserve it or remove bitterness"],["Extra virgin","The highest grade of olive oil"],["Brine","Salty water used to preserve food"],["Evergreen","A tree that keeps its leaves all year"]],
f:["Green and black olives can grow on the same tree.","Fresh olives are too bitter to eat.","Some olive trees are over 2,000 years old."],
fun:"Ancient Greek athletes rubbed olive oil on their bodies before exercising and scraped it off afterward with a curved tool called a strigil."});

ADDART({id:"grapes",t:"Grapes and Vineyards",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🍇",w:"Grape",r:["fruits","vines","agri","francecountry","domestication"],
p:[["Ancient fruit","Grapes were among the first fruits people domesticated."],["Wine, raisins and juice","Grapes are eaten fresh, dried into raisins and made into wine and juice."],["Saved by grafting","An American insect nearly destroyed Europe’s vineyards in the 1800s."]],
l:"Grapes are the berries of woody climbing vines of the genus Vitis, grown in vineyards on every continent except Antarctica. Most cultivated grapes come from the Eurasian grapevine, Vitis vinifera, which a 2023 genetic study suggests was domesticated about 11,000 years ago in western Asia and the Caucasus. Grapes are eaten fresh as table grapes, dried into raisins, sultanas and currants, pressed for juice and fermented into wine. The oldest known evidence of grape wine comes from Georgia, about 8,000 years ago. Grapes need warm, dry summers and grow best in regions with a Mediterranean-type climate. China grows the most grapes, while Italy, France and Spain lead wine production. In the late 19th century, a tiny insect called phylloxera, accidentally brought from North America, destroyed most of Europe’s vineyards; they were saved by grafting European vines onto resistant American roots, which is still done today.",
s:[["The vine","Grapevines climb using tendrils and are trained on wires or posts in vineyards. Growers prune vines heavily each winter. Grapes grow in bunches and change color as they ripen, from green to yellow, red or purple, depending on the variety."],["Thousands of varieties","There are thousands of grape varieties, including Cabernet Sauvignon, Chardonnay and Merlot for wine and Thompson Seedless for eating and raisins. Seedless grapes are produced from natural mutations and grown from cuttings."],["Phylloxera","Phylloxera is a tiny aphid-like insect that feeds on vine roots. It arrived in France in the 1860s and over the next decades destroyed most European vineyards. American vines had evolved resistance, so growers grafted European vines onto American rootstocks, saving the wine industry."],["Climate and grapes","Grapes are sensitive to temperature, and harvest dates in Europe have moved earlier as summers have warmed. Winemakers are planting at higher altitudes and in cooler regions such as southern England and Scandinavia."]],
k:[["Genus","Vitis"],["Main species","Vitis vinifera"],["Domesticated","About 11,000 years ago"],["Oldest wine evidence","Georgia, about 8,000 years ago"],["Largest grape grower","China"]],
d:[["c. 9000 BC","Grapevines domesticated in western Asia"],["c. 6000 BC","Earliest known grape wine in Georgia"],["1863","Phylloxera detected in France"],["1880s","Grafting onto American roots saves European vineyards"],["2023","Genetic study traces grape domestication"]],
s2:[["Raisins","Raisins are grapes dried in the sun or by machines, concentrating their sugar. California and Turkey are major producers. Sultanas are made from green seedless grapes, and currants traditionally from small dark grapes from Greece."],["Grapes and pets","Grapes and raisins can cause sudden kidney failure in dogs, even in small amounts. Scientists believe tartaric acid in the fruit may be responsible. Owners are advised never to feed grapes to dogs."]],
sp:["Ice wine","In Canada and Germany, some grapes are left on the vine until they freeze in winter. Pressing the frozen grapes squeezes out a small amount of very sweet juice, made into a dessert wine called ice wine."],
g:[["Vineyard","A farm or field of grapevines"],["Grafting","Joining part of one plant onto the roots of another"],["Fermentation","Yeast turning sugar into alcohol"],["Rootstock","The root part of a grafted plant"]],
f:["Grapes were domesticated about 11,000 years ago.","The oldest known wine comes from Georgia.","Grapes and raisins are poisonous to dogs."],
fun:"Grapes held near a flame in a microwave oven can spark balls of glowing plasma, an effect scientists explained in a 2019 study, though trying it can damage the oven."});

ADDART({id:"wifi",t:"Wi-Fi",c:"Technology",i:"📶",w:"Wi-Fi",r:["internet","radio","smartphone","smarthome","cybersecurity"],
p:[["Wireless internet","Wi-Fi connects devices to networks and the internet using radio waves."],["A global standard","Wi-Fi follows IEEE 802.11 standards, first published in 1997."],["Everywhere","Billions of devices use Wi-Fi in homes, offices and public places."]],
l:"Wi-Fi is a family of wireless networking technologies that let computers, phones, televisions and other devices connect to a local network and the internet using radio waves instead of cables. A Wi-Fi router connects to the internet through a cable or fiber line and sends and receives data by radio, mostly in frequency bands around 2.4, 5 and 6 gigahertz. Wi-Fi is based on the IEEE 802.11 standards, first published in 1997; the name ‘Wi-Fi’ was created in 1999 by the industry group now called the Wi-Fi Alliance, which certifies devices. Over generations, Wi-Fi has become much faster: the 1999 version reached 11 megabits per second, while Wi-Fi 7, certified from 2024, can in theory exceed 40 gigabits per second. Wi-Fi made laptops and smartphones truly mobile and connects smart home devices. Networks should be protected with strong passwords and encryption, because open networks can let others intercept data.",
s:[["How Wi-Fi works","A router or access point broadcasts a network name. Devices that join exchange data in small packets, encoded onto radio waves. Signals weaken with distance and are blocked by walls and metal, so larger homes may use several access points in a ‘mesh’. The 2.4 GHz band travels farther; the 5 and 6 GHz bands carry more data over shorter distances."],["Origins","In 1971, ALOHAnet in Hawaii linked computers by radio. In 1985, the US government opened certain radio bands for unlicensed use. Researchers in the Netherlands and at Australia’s CSIRO developed key technologies, and the first 802.11 standard appeared in 1997. Apple’s AirPort in 1999 helped bring Wi-Fi to homes."],["Generations","Wi-Fi versions were renamed with simple numbers in 2018: Wi-Fi 4 (802.11n, 2009), Wi-Fi 5 (802.11ac, 2013), Wi-Fi 6 (802.11ax, 2019), Wi-Fi 6E, which added the 6 GHz band, and Wi-Fi 7 (802.11be, certified in 2024). Each generation improved speed and handled more devices at once."],["Security","Early Wi-Fi used WEP encryption, which was easily broken. WPA2, from 2004, and WPA3, from 2018, are much stronger. Public Wi-Fi in cafes and airports may be unencrypted, so experts recommend using secure websites or a virtual private network (VPN) for sensitive information."]],
k:[["Based on","IEEE 802.11 standards"],["First standard","1997"],["Name created","1999"],["Main bands","2.4, 5 and 6 GHz"],["Latest generation","Wi-Fi 7 (certified 2024)"]],
d:[["1971","ALOHAnet links computers by radio in Hawaii"],["1997","First IEEE 802.11 standard"],["1999","The name ‘Wi-Fi’ is introduced"],["2004","WPA2 security introduced"],["2019","Wi-Fi 6 certification begins"],["2024","Wi-Fi 7 certification begins"]],
s2:[["Does Wi-Fi stand for anything?","Wi-Fi is not short for ‘wireless fidelity’, although it was once advertised that way. It was a catchy brand name created by a marketing company, chosen partly to echo ‘hi-fi’ audio equipment."],["Hedy Lamarr","In 1942, actress Hedy Lamarr and composer George Antheil patented a ‘frequency hopping’ system to stop enemies jamming radio-guided torpedoes. Similar spread-spectrum ideas were later used in some early Wi-Fi and in Bluetooth, and Lamarr is often celebrated as a pioneer of wireless technology."]],
sp:["Wi-Fi and health","Wi-Fi uses low-power radio waves that do not have enough energy to damage cells or DNA. The World Health Organization and other health bodies say there is no evidence that Wi-Fi signals at normal levels harm health."],
g:[["Router","A device that directs data between networks"],["Bandwidth","How much data can be sent per second"],["Encryption","Scrambling data so only intended users can read it"],["Gigahertz","Billions of cycles per second"]],
f:["Wi-Fi does not stand for ‘wireless fidelity’.","Wi-Fi standards were first published in 1997.","Walls and metal weaken Wi-Fi signals."],
fun:"Microwave ovens use frequencies close to Wi-Fi’s 2.4 GHz band, which is why a running microwave can sometimes slow down a nearby Wi-Fi connection."});

ADDART({id:"radar",t:"Radar",c:"Technology",i:"📡",w:"Radar",r:["radio","ww2","weatherforecast","airports","microwave","dopplereffect"],
p:[["Seeing with radio waves","Radar finds objects by sending radio waves and timing their echoes."],["Born for war","Radar was developed rapidly before and during World War II."],["Everyday uses","Radar guides planes and ships, forecasts weather and helps cars."]],
l:"Radar, short for ‘radio detection and ranging’, is a system that uses radio waves to detect objects and measure their distance, direction and speed. A radar transmitter sends out pulses of radio waves; when they hit an object such as an aircraft, ship, raindrop or mountain, some energy bounces back to a receiver. Because radio waves travel at the speed of light, the time taken for the echo to return shows how far away the object is, and changes in the echo’s frequency, the Doppler effect, reveal its speed. Early experiments took place in the early 20th century, but radar was developed rapidly in the 1930s by several countries, especially Britain, whose Chain Home network helped detect German aircraft during the Battle of Britain in 1940. Today radar guides air traffic, tracks storms, measures vehicle speeds, helps ships navigate in fog, maps planets and enables driver-assistance systems in cars.",
s:[["How radar works","A radar antenna sends out a narrow beam and then listens for echoes. A display shows each detected object; on older screens, a rotating line sweeps around, leaving bright dots called ‘blips’. Longer radio waves travel farther, while shorter microwaves give more detailed images."],["Radar in World War II","Robert Watson-Watt’s team demonstrated in February 1935 that aircraft could be detected by radio echoes. Britain built the Chain Home stations along its coast by 1939. In 1940, British scientists invented the cavity magnetron, which produced powerful microwaves for compact radar sets in aircraft and ships, and shared it with the United States."],["Weather radar","Weather radars detect raindrops, snow and hail. Doppler weather radar can see winds inside storms, including the rotation that may form tornadoes, giving people more warning time. National weather services operate networks of radar stations."],["Other uses","Police use radar guns to measure car speeds. Ground-penetrating radar helps archaeologists find buried objects. Satellites use radar to map Earth through clouds, and radar from Earth and spacecraft has mapped Venus beneath its thick clouds. Many new cars use small radars for automatic braking and cruise control."]],
k:[["Stands for","Radio detection and ranging"],["Measures","Distance, direction and speed"],["Key wartime system","Chain Home (UK)"],["Key invention","Cavity magnetron (1940)"],["Speed method","Doppler effect"]],
d:[["1904","Christian Hülsmeyer patents a radio ship detector"],["1935","Daventry experiment detects an aircraft by radio echo"],["1939","Chain Home radar network operational"],["1940","Cavity magnetron invented"],["1946","Radar echoes bounced off the Moon"],["1990–94","Magellan spacecraft maps Venus by radar"]],
s2:[["From radar to microwave ovens","In 1945, Percy Spencer, an engineer working on radar magnetrons, noticed that a candy bar in his pocket had melted. He experimented with popcorn and an egg and went on to invent the microwave oven, using the same magnetron technology."],["Stealth","Stealth aircraft are designed to reflect radar waves away from the receiver and absorb some of them with special coatings, making them hard to detect. Engineers develop new types of radar to counter stealth designs."]],
sp:["Bats and dolphins","Bats and dolphins use echolocation, sending out sound waves and listening to echoes, which works on the same principle as radar. Sonar, used by ships to detect submarines and map the seafloor, uses sound instead of radio waves."],
g:[["Echo","A reflected wave"],["Doppler effect","A change in wave frequency caused by motion"],["Magnetron","A device that produces powerful microwaves"],["Sonar","A system that uses sound echoes"]],
f:["Radar stands for radio detection and ranging.","The microwave oven grew out of radar research.","Radar helped Britain in the Battle of Britain."],
fun:"Early radar operators were puzzled by mysterious blips they called ‘angels’, which turned out to be flocks of birds, insects and weather."});

ADDART({id:"jetengine",t:"Jet Engines",c:"Technology",i:"✈️",w:"Jet engine",r:["flight","airports","rocket","steam","ww2"],
p:[["Pushing air backward","Jet engines suck in air, burn fuel and blast hot gas out the back."],["Two inventors","Frank Whittle and Hans von Ohain invented the jet engine separately in the 1930s."],["Shrinking the world","Jet airliners made fast, affordable long-distance travel possible."]],
l:"A jet engine is an engine that produces thrust by pushing out a fast stream of gas, following Isaac Newton’s third law: every action has an equal and opposite reaction. In a typical jet engine, a fan and compressor draw in air and squeeze it, fuel is sprayed in and burned in a combustion chamber, and the hot expanding gases rush out through a turbine, which drives the compressor, and then out of the back at high speed. The jet engine was invented independently in the 1930s by Frank Whittle in Britain and Hans von Ohain in Germany; von Ohain’s engine powered the first jet-powered flight, the Heinkel He 178, on 27 August 1939. Jet fighters appeared late in World War II, and the British de Havilland Comet began the first jet airliner service in 1952. Modern airliners use turbofan engines, which are quieter and more efficient. Aviation produces about 2.5 percent of global carbon dioxide emissions, and engineers are developing cleaner fuels and engines.",
s:[["Types of jet engine","A turbojet pushes all its air through the core and is fast but noisy and thirsty. A turbofan uses a large front fan to push most air around the core, giving more thrust with less fuel and noise; it powers almost all modern airliners. A turboprop uses a jet engine to turn a propeller, and a ramjet has no moving parts but works only at high speeds."],["The inventors","Frank Whittle, a Royal Air Force officer, patented his jet engine design in 1930 but struggled to get funding. Hans von Ohain built his first engine in Germany with support from aircraft maker Ernst Heinkel. Whittle’s engine first flew in the Gloster E.28/39 in May 1941. The two men later met and became friends."],["The jet age","The German Messerschmitt Me 262 became the first operational jet fighter in 1944. The de Havilland Comet began passenger service in May 1952, but crashes caused by metal fatigue grounded it. The Boeing 707, which entered service in 1958, made jet travel common. The Concorde flew passengers at twice the speed of sound from 1976 to 2003."],["Engines today","A large modern turbofan can produce more than 400 kilonewtons of thrust, and its fan can be over 3 meters wide. Engineers are testing sustainable aviation fuels, more efficient open-fan designs and hydrogen-powered engines to reduce emissions."]],
k:[["Principle","Newton’s third law"],["Inventors","Frank Whittle and Hans von Ohain"],["First jet flight","Heinkel He 178, 27 August 1939"],["First jet airliner service","de Havilland Comet, 1952"],["Main modern type","Turbofan"]],
d:[["1930","Frank Whittle patents a jet engine"],["1939","Heinkel He 178 makes the first jet flight"],["1941","Gloster E.28/39 flies with Whittle’s engine"],["1944","Me 262 becomes the first operational jet fighter"],["1952","Comet begins the first jet airliner service"],["1958","Boeing 707 enters service"],["1976","Concorde begins supersonic passenger service"]],
s2:[["Bird strikes","Birds sucked into jet engines can damage them. In January 2009, US Airways Flight 1549 lost both engines after hitting geese and was safely landed on the Hudson River in New York by Captain Chesley Sullenberger, with all 155 people surviving. Engines are tested by firing bird-sized objects into them."],["Why planes leave trails","The white trails behind high-flying jets, called contrails, form when hot, moist exhaust freezes into ice crystals in the cold air. Some contrails spread into thin clouds that trap heat, and airlines are studying ways to avoid creating them."]],
sp:["Hero’s engine","About 2,000 years ago, Hero of Alexandria described the aeolipile, a sphere that spun as steam jets shot out of two bent nozzles. It used the same reaction principle as jet engines but was treated as a curiosity."],
g:[["Thrust","The force that pushes an aircraft forward"],["Turbine","A wheel turned by flowing gas or liquid"],["Compressor","A device that squeezes air"],["Turbofan","A jet engine with a large front fan"]],
f:["The first jet flight was on 27 August 1939.","Most modern airliners use turbofan engines.","Contrails are made of ice crystals."],
fun:"The fan blades of large jet engines spin so fast that their tips can move faster than the speed of sound."});

ADDART({id:"helicopters",t:"Helicopters",c:"Technology",i:"🚁",w:"Helicopter",r:["flight","drones","davinci","marsrovers","firefighters"],
p:[["Rotating wings","Helicopters fly using spinning rotor blades."],["Hover and land anywhere","They can take off vertically, hover and fly in any direction."],["Rescue workers","Helicopters rescue people, fight fires and carry patients."]],
l:"A helicopter is an aircraft that gets lift and thrust from spinning rotor blades, allowing it to take off and land vertically, hover in place and fly forward, backward and sideways. The main rotor’s blades work like spinning wings; tilting them changes the direction of flight. Because the spinning rotor would make the helicopter’s body turn the opposite way, most helicopters have a small tail rotor to counter it, while others use two main rotors spinning in opposite directions. Leonardo da Vinci sketched an ‘aerial screw’ around 1489, but practical helicopters arrived only in the 1930s and 1940s; Igor Sikorsky’s VS-300 first flew in 1939, and his R-4 of 1942 was the first mass-produced helicopter. Helicopters now carry out search and rescue, medical evacuation, firefighting, police and military work, construction and offshore transport. In 2021, NASA’s Ingenuity became the first aircraft to make a powered, controlled flight on another planet, Mars.",
s:[["How a helicopter flies","The pilot uses the collective lever to change the angle of all main blades together, making the helicopter rise or descend, and the cyclic stick to tilt the rotor and move in any direction. Foot pedals change the tail rotor’s thrust to turn the nose. Flying a helicopter requires constant small adjustments."],["Early helicopters","In 1907, the French Breguet and Cornu machines briefly lifted off the ground. The German Focke-Wulf Fw 61, which flew in 1936, was one of the first practical helicopters. Igor Sikorsky’s single-rotor VS-300 design became the model for most later helicopters."],["Helicopters at work","Helicopters were first used to rescue people in the 1940s and to evacuate wounded soldiers in the Korean War. Air ambulances can reach remote accidents quickly. Helicopters drop water on wildfires, lift heavy loads into places without roads and carry workers to oil platforms at sea."],["Ingenuity on Mars","NASA’s Ingenuity, weighing only 1.8 kilograms, traveled to Mars with the Perseverance rover. On 19 April 2021, it made the first powered flight on another planet. Planned for five flights, it made 72 before a damaged blade ended its mission in January 2024."]],
k:[["Lift from","Spinning rotor blades"],["Abilities","Vertical takeoff, hovering, any direction"],["First mass-produced","Sikorsky R-4 (1942)"],["Early sketch","Leonardo da Vinci, c. 1489"],["First flight on Mars","Ingenuity, 19 April 2021"]],
d:[["c. 1489","Leonardo da Vinci sketches an ‘aerial screw’"],["1936","Focke-Wulf Fw 61 flies"],["1939","Sikorsky VS-300 makes its first flight"],["1942","Sikorsky R-4 enters production"],["1946","Bell 47 becomes the first certified civilian helicopter"],["2021","Ingenuity flies on Mars"]],
s2:[["Why a tail rotor?","When an engine spins the main rotor one way, the helicopter’s body tries to spin the other way, an effect called torque. The tail rotor pushes sideways to stop this. Some helicopters, like the Chinook, use two big rotors spinning in opposite directions instead."],["The bamboo-copter","A Chinese toy, the bamboo-copter, a stick with propeller blades spun between the hands, dates back to about 400 BC. Toys like it inspired early aviation pioneers, including the Wright brothers, who played with a rubber-band-powered helicopter as children."]],
sp:["Flying on Mars","Mars’s atmosphere is only about 1 percent as dense as Earth’s, making flight very hard. Ingenuity’s blades were extra long and spun at about 2,500 revolutions per minute, several times faster than a normal helicopter’s, to generate enough lift."],
g:[["Rotor","The spinning blades of a helicopter"],["Hover","To stay in one place in the air"],["Torque","A twisting force"],["Collective and cyclic","The main helicopter flight controls"]],
f:["Leonardo da Vinci sketched a helicopter-like machine around 1489.","Most helicopters need a tail rotor to stop spinning.","A helicopter flew on Mars in 2021."],
fun:"The word helicopter comes from the Greek words helix, ‘spiral’, and pteron, ‘wing’."});

ADDART({id:"washingmachine",t:"Washing Machines",c:"Technology",i:"🧺",w:"Washing machine",r:["elec","feminism","weaving","soap","indrev"],
p:[["Laundry made easy","Washing machines clean clothes with water, detergent and motion."],["Freeing time","They saved people, mostly women, many hours of hard work each week."],["Ever more efficient","Modern machines use much less water and energy than older ones."]],
l:"A washing machine is a household appliance that cleans clothes and other textiles using water, detergent and mechanical action. Before washing machines, laundry was exhausting work done by hand, scrubbing clothes on washboards or beating them on rocks, boiling them in tubs and wringing them out, and could take a whole day each week; this work fell mostly on women and servants. Hand-cranked machines appeared in the 18th and 19th centuries, and electric washing machines were sold in the United States from the early 20th century. Automatic machines that washed, rinsed and spun without help became common after World War II. Today, top-loading machines spin clothes around a vertical drum with an agitator or impeller, while front-loaders tumble clothes in a horizontal drum, using less water. Historians and economists argue that washing machines and other appliances transformed daily life, freeing time for education and paid work.",
s:[["How it works","A washing machine fills with water, adds detergent and moves the clothes to loosen dirt. It then drains the dirty water, rinses the clothes with clean water and spins the drum very fast, flinging water out through holes by centrifugal force. Sensors control water level, temperature and time."],["History","Early patents for washing devices appeared in the 1690s and 1700s. Electric machines were sold from about 1908, including the Thor, though early models had dangerous exposed parts. Bendix introduced an automatic front-loading machine in 1937. By the 1950s and 1960s, washing machines were common in homes in North America and Europe."],["Energy and water","Modern washing machines use far less water and energy than those of a few decades ago. Washing at lower temperatures, such as 30°C, saves energy, and modern detergents work well in cool water. Washing synthetic fabrics releases tiny plastic fibers, and some countries now require filters to catch these microplastics."],["Around the world","Many households still wash clothes by hand, especially in lower-income countries. Public laundries and laundromats serve people without machines. Engineers have designed low-cost, hand-powered machines for homes without electricity."]],
k:[["Main types","Top-loading and front-loading"],["Electric machines sold from","About 1908"],["First automatic","Bendix, 1937"],["Spin cycle","Removes water by centrifugal force"],["Issue","Microplastic fibers"]],
d:[["1797","Washboard patented in the United States"],["c. 1908","Electric washing machines go on sale"],["1937","Bendix introduces an automatic washer"],["1950s","Washing machines become common in Western homes"],["2025","France requires microfiber filters in new machines"]],
s2:[["The washing machine and the internet","Economist Ha-Joon Chang argued in 2010 that the washing machine changed the world more than the internet, because it transformed women’s lives and work. The statistician Hans Rosling told how his grandmother watched the first family washing machine in amazement."],["Laundromats","Coin-operated laundries, called laundromats or launderettes, spread from the 1930s and 1940s. They remain common in cities where apartments lack space for machines and have become community gathering places."]],
sp:["Before washing machines","In many places, laundry day involved carrying water, heating it over a fire, scrubbing with lye soap, rinsing, wringing through rollers called mangles and hanging clothes to dry. A family’s weekly wash could take ten hours or more."],
g:[["Detergent","A chemical that removes dirt and grease"],["Agitator","A post in the drum that moves clothes"],["Centrifugal force","The outward push felt by spinning objects"],["Laundromat","A shop with coin-operated washing machines"]],
f:["Electric washing machines were sold from about 1908.","Front-loaders use less water than top-loaders.","Washing synthetic clothes releases microplastics."],
fun:"Hans Rosling famously called the washing machine ‘the greatest invention of the industrial revolution’, because it freed time for reading and learning."});

ADDART({id:"prosthetics",t:"Prosthetics",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🦾",w:"Prosthesis",r:["olymp","robot","printing3d","nervous","egypt"],
p:[["Replacing body parts","Prostheses are artificial limbs and body parts."],["Ancient history","An artificial toe from ancient Egypt is about 3,000 years old."],["High-tech limbs","Modern prosthetics can be controlled by muscle signals and even give a sense of touch."]],
l:"A prosthesis is an artificial device that replaces a missing body part, such as an arm, leg, hand, foot, eye, tooth or heart valve; the field of designing and fitting them is called prosthetics. People may lose limbs through injury, disease such as diabetes or cancer, or be born without them. Prosthetics have a long history: a wooden and leather toe from ancient Egypt, about 3,000 years old, is one of the oldest known working prostheses. Wars have repeatedly driven improvements, from the iron hands of medieval knights to the lightweight designs developed for wounded soldiers in the 20th and 21st centuries. Modern prosthetic legs include computer-controlled knees and springy carbon-fiber blades used by Paralympic sprinters. Myoelectric arms use sensors to detect muscle signals, letting users open and close a hand by thinking about it, and researchers have connected prostheses to nerves to restore a sense of touch. Cost remains a major barrier: the World Health Organization estimates that only a small share of people who need prosthetic and orthotic devices have access to them.",
s:[["How prosthetic limbs work","A prosthetic limb usually has a socket that fits the remaining limb, a frame and parts such as a knee, ankle or hand. The socket must be carefully shaped to be comfortable. Some prostheses attach directly to the bone through the skin, a technique called osseointegration."],["Historical prostheses","The Cairo toe was found on an Egyptian mummy and shows signs of wear. A Roman bronze-covered leg found in Capua dated to about 300 BC. In the 16th century, French surgeon Ambroise Paré designed hinged legs and hands, and the German knight Götz von Berlichingen used an iron hand with movable fingers."],["Bionic limbs","Myoelectric prostheses were first developed in the Soviet Union in the late 1950s. Today, advanced hands can grip in different ways, and some users can feel pressure through electrodes connected to their nerves. Brain-computer interfaces let some paralyzed people move robotic arms."],["Affordable prosthetics","Many people in lower-income countries cannot afford prostheses. The Jaipur Foot, developed in India in 1968, is a cheap, durable rubber foot that allows squatting and walking barefoot, and has helped more than a million people. Volunteers have used 3D printers to make low-cost hands for children since about 2013."]],
k:[["Definition","An artificial body part"],["Oldest working example","Egyptian toe, about 3,000 years old"],["Muscle-controlled type","Myoelectric prosthesis"],["Affordable design","Jaipur Foot (1968)"],["Sports use","Carbon-fiber running blades"]],
d:[["c. 950–710 BC","Egyptian wooden toe made"],["c. 300 BC","Roman Capua leg made"],["1504","Götz von Berlichingen gets an iron hand"],["1960s","Myoelectric arms come into use"],["1968","Jaipur Foot developed in India"],["2013","Volunteers begin 3D-printing prosthetic hands"]],
s2:[["Running blades","Carbon-fiber running blades, shaped like the letter J, store and release energy like springs. They allow Paralympic athletes to sprint 100 meters in under 11 seconds. Debates have arisen over whether blades could give an advantage over biological legs."],["Not just limbs","Prostheses also include artificial eyes, dental implants, artificial heart valves, hip and knee replacements, and cochlear implants, which help deaf people hear. Breast prostheses are used after cancer surgery."]],
sp:["The Cairo toe","The Cairo toe, made of wood and leather, was found attached to the foot of a mummy of a noblewoman. Tests with volunteers in 2011 showed that replicas helped people walk comfortably in Egyptian-style sandals, suggesting it was a working prosthesis rather than decoration."],
g:[["Prosthesis","An artificial body part"],["Amputation","Removal of a limb"],["Myoelectric","Controlled by muscle electrical signals"],["Socket","The part of a prosthesis that fits the body"]],
f:["The oldest known working prosthesis is an Egyptian toe.","Some prosthetic hands can restore a sense of touch.","The Jaipur Foot has helped over a million people."],
fun:"Pirates in stories often have peg legs and hooks, but real wooden peg legs were common among soldiers and sailors who lost limbs before modern medicine."});

ADDART({id:"motors",t:"Electric Motors",c:"Technology",i:"⚙️",w:"Electric motor",r:["elec","magnet","circuits","currents","ev"],
p:[["Electricity into motion","Electric motors turn electrical energy into movement using magnetism."],["Everywhere","Motors run fans, washing machines, trains, elevators and electric cars."],["A big energy user","Electric motors use almost half of the world’s electricity."]],
l:"An electric motor is a machine that converts electrical energy into mechanical motion, usually rotation. Motors work because an electric current flowing through a wire in a magnetic field experiences a force; by arranging coils of wire and magnets, the force can keep a shaft turning. Michael Faraday demonstrated electromagnetic rotation in 1821, and practical motors followed in the 1830s, but motors spread widely only after power grids were built in the late 19th century. Nikola Tesla and Galileo Ferraris developed the alternating-current induction motor in the 1880s, still the workhorse of industry. Electric motors now power countless machines, from tiny vibration motors in phones to giant motors in ships, trains and factories, as well as fans, pumps, elevators, refrigerators, washing machines, drills and electric cars. The International Energy Agency has estimated that motors and the systems they drive use about 45 percent of the world’s electricity, so making them more efficient saves large amounts of energy.",
s:[["How a motor works","In a simple direct-current motor, a coil of wire sits between magnets. When current flows, the magnetic force pushes one side of the coil up and the other down, making it turn. A device called a commutator reverses the current every half turn so the coil keeps spinning. Generators work the same way in reverse."],["Kinds of motor","DC motors are simple and used in toys and small devices. AC induction motors are rugged and power pumps and factory machines. Brushless DC motors, controlled by electronics, are efficient and used in drones, computer fans and many electric cars. Stepper motors move in precise steps in 3D printers and robots."],["History","Faraday’s 1821 experiment made a wire spin around a magnet. Moritz Jacobi built a practical motor in 1834, and Thomas Davenport patented one in the United States in 1837. Tesla’s 1888 patents for AC motors and the growth of electric power changed factories, which no longer needed a single steam engine driving belts."],["Motors and the future","Electric cars, heat pumps and electric trains all rely on motors. Many powerful motors use magnets made with rare earth elements such as neodymium, mostly mined and processed in China, so engineers are developing designs that need fewer of them."]],
k:[["Converts","Electrical energy to motion"],["Principle","Electromagnetism"],["First demonstration","Michael Faraday, 1821"],["Industrial workhorse","AC induction motor"],["Share of world electricity","About 45% (IEA estimate)"]],
d:[["1821","Faraday demonstrates electromagnetic rotation"],["1834","Moritz Jacobi builds a practical motor"],["1837","Thomas Davenport patents an electric motor"],["1888","Tesla patents the AC induction motor"],["1960s","Brushless DC motors developed"]],
s2:[["Motors in your home","A typical home may contain dozens of electric motors: in the refrigerator, washing machine, vacuum cleaner, fans, hair dryer, blender, computer, phone and electric toothbrush. Many are hidden inside devices."],["Efficiency","Large electric motors can convert more than 95 percent of electrical energy into motion, far more than gasoline engines, which waste most of their energy as heat. Variable-speed drives that adjust motor speed to demand save even more energy."]],
sp:["Faraday’s spinning wire","In September 1821, Michael Faraday hung a wire into a bowl of mercury with a magnet in the middle. When he passed a current through the wire, it circled around the magnet: the first demonstration of electricity producing continuous motion."],
g:[["Electromagnetism","The link between electricity and magnetism"],["Commutator","A switch that reverses current in a motor"],["Induction motor","An AC motor with no brushes"],["Rare earths","A group of metals used in strong magnets"]],
f:["Motors use about 45% of the world’s electricity.","Faraday made the first electric motor in 1821.","A generator is a motor working in reverse."],
fun:"Your phone’s vibration comes from a tiny motor spinning an off-center weight, which shakes the whole phone."});

ADDART({id:"planetdefense",t:"Planetary Defense",c:"Space Exploration",i:"☄️",w:"Planetary defense",r:["comet","asteroidbelt","meteors","dino","scope"],
p:[["Protecting Earth","Planetary defense means finding and stopping dangerous asteroids and comets."],["DART’s success","In 2022, NASA’s DART spacecraft changed an asteroid’s orbit by crashing into it."],["Finding them first","Telescopes search the sky for objects that could hit Earth."]],
l:"Planetary defense is the effort to detect asteroids and comets that might collide with Earth and to develop ways to prevent or prepare for an impact. Large impacts are rare but can be devastating: an asteroid about 10 kilometers wide helped wipe out the dinosaurs 66 million years ago, and a much smaller object flattened about 2,000 square kilometers of forest at Tunguska, Siberia, in 1908. In 2013, a meteor about 20 meters wide exploded over Chelyabinsk, Russia, injuring about 1,500 people, mostly from broken glass. Telescopes around the world have found more than 35,000 near-Earth asteroids, including most of those larger than a kilometer, and none is known to pose a significant threat for at least the next century. On 26 September 2022, NASA’s DART spacecraft deliberately crashed into the small asteroid moon Dimorphos and shortened its orbit by about 33 minutes, the first test of changing an asteroid’s path. The European Space Agency’s Hera spacecraft arrived in November 2026 to study the results.",
s:[["Finding asteroids","Survey telescopes scan the sky each night for moving points of light. Astronomers calculate their orbits and estimate the chance of a future impact. NASA’s NEO Surveyor, an infrared space telescope, is planned for launch around 2027 to find dark asteroids that are hard to see."],["Ways to deflect an asteroid","A spacecraft could crash into an asteroid to nudge it, as DART did. Other ideas include a ‘gravity tractor’, a spacecraft hovering near an asteroid to pull it slowly, or, as a last resort, a nuclear explosion near its surface. The earlier a threat is found, the smaller the nudge needed."],["DART and Hera","DART, about the size of a vending machine with long solar panels, hit Dimorphos at about 6.6 kilometers per second. The impact blasted out a huge cloud of rock and changed the moonlet’s orbit around its larger partner, Didymos, more than expected. Hera, launched in 2024, arrived in November 2026 to measure the crater and the asteroids’ masses."],["Close calls","In December 2024, astronomers found asteroid 2024 YR4, which for a time was calculated to have about a 3 percent chance of hitting Earth in 2032, the highest ever for a large asteroid. Further observations ruled out an Earth impact within weeks. Asteroid Apophis will pass very close to Earth, but safely, on 13 April 2029."]],
k:[["Goal","Detect and deflect dangerous asteroids and comets"],["First deflection test","DART, 26 September 2022"],["DART’s effect","Orbit shortened by about 33 minutes"],["Follow-up mission","ESA’s Hera (arrived November 2026)"],["Next close pass","Apophis, 13 April 2029"]],
d:[["66 million years ago","Asteroid impact helps end the age of dinosaurs"],["1908","Tunguska explosion in Siberia"],["2013","Chelyabinsk meteor injures about 1,500 people"],["2016","NASA sets up its Planetary Defense Coordination Office"],["2022","DART hits Dimorphos"],["2024","Hera launches"],["2026","Hera arrives at Didymos"]],
s2:[["How big is dangerous?","Objects smaller than about 25 meters usually burn up in the atmosphere. Asteroids about 140 meters or larger could destroy a region, and those over 1 kilometer could cause global effects. Astronomers have found most kilometer-sized near-Earth asteroids but still only a fraction of the 140-meter ones."],["Asteroid Day","Asteroid Day, held every year on 30 June, the anniversary of the Tunguska event, raises awareness of asteroid impacts. The United Nations recognized it as International Asteroid Day in 2016."]],
sp:["The Torino scale","Scientists rate the hazard of a possible asteroid impact on the Torino scale from 0 (no hazard) to 10 (certain global catastrophe). Apophis briefly reached level 4 after its discovery in 2004, and 2024 YR4 reached level 3, before both were ruled out as Earth impactors."],
g:[["Near-Earth object","An asteroid or comet whose orbit comes close to Earth’s"],["Deflection","Changing an object’s path"],["Impactor","An object that hits another"],["Kinetic impact","Changing an asteroid’s orbit by hitting it"]],
f:["DART changed an asteroid’s orbit in 2022.","The Chelyabinsk meteor injured about 1,500 people.","Apophis will pass close to Earth in 2029."],
fun:"When Apophis passes Earth in 2029, it will come closer than some satellites and may be visible to the naked eye from parts of the world."});

ADDART({id:"lagrange",t:"Lagrange Points",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🎯",w:"Lagrange point",r:["grav","jwst","sun","jupiter","asteroidbelt"],
p:[["Parking spots in space","Lagrange points are places where gravity and orbital motion balance."],["Five points","Every pair of large bodies, like the Sun and Earth, has five."],["Home of telescopes","The James Webb Space Telescope orbits the Sun–Earth L2 point."]],
l:"Lagrange points are five special positions in space around two large orbiting bodies, such as the Sun and Earth or Earth and the Moon, where a small object can stay in a fixed position relative to the two. At these points, the gravitational pull of the two bodies combines with the motion of orbiting to balance out. They are named after the Italian-French mathematician Joseph-Louis Lagrange, who described two of them in 1772; Leonhard Euler had found the other three a few years earlier. Three points, L1, L2 and L3, lie on a line through the two bodies and are unstable, so spacecraft there must make small corrections, like balancing a ball on a hilltop. L4 and L5, which lie 60 degrees ahead of and behind the smaller body in its orbit, are stable, and natural objects collect there, such as the thousands of Trojan asteroids that share Jupiter’s orbit. Lagrange points are valuable locations for space telescopes and Sun-watching spacecraft.",
s:[["The five points","For the Sun and Earth, L1 lies about 1.5 million kilometers toward the Sun and L2 about the same distance beyond Earth, away from the Sun. L3 is on the far side of the Sun. L4 and L5 form equilateral triangles with the Sun and Earth."],["Uses of L1 and L2","L1 gives an uninterrupted view of the Sun, so solar observatories such as SOHO, operating since 1996, are stationed there to watch the Sun and warn of space weather. L2 keeps the Sun, Earth and Moon in roughly the same direction, so telescopes like the James Webb Space Telescope, Gaia and Euclid can shield themselves and stay cold."],["Trojan asteroids","Jupiter has thousands of known Trojan asteroids at its L4 and L5 points; the first, 588 Achilles, was found in 1906. NASA’s Lucy spacecraft, launched in 2021, will begin visiting Jupiter Trojans in 2027. Earth has a few small Trojan asteroids, the first, 2010 TK7, found in 2010."],["Halo orbits","Spacecraft do not sit exactly at L1 or L2 but travel in large loops around them, called halo orbits. These keep them out of Earth’s shadow and require only small, occasional engine burns."]],
k:[["Number of points","Five for each pair of bodies"],["Named after","Joseph-Louis Lagrange"],["Stable points","L4 and L5"],["Distance of Sun–Earth L2","About 1.5 million km from Earth"],["Famous resident","James Webb Space Telescope (L2)"]],
d:[["1767","Euler describes L1, L2 and L3"],["1772","Lagrange describes L4 and L5"],["1906","First Jupiter Trojan asteroid found"],["1996","SOHO begins observing the Sun from L1"],["2022","James Webb Space Telescope reaches L2"]],
s2:[["Space dust clouds","Faint clouds of dust, called Kordylewski clouds, may gather at the Earth–Moon L4 and L5 points. Hungarian astronomers reported evidence for them in 2018, but they are very hard to see and still debated."],["A science-fiction favorite","Because L4 and L5 are stable, scientists in the 1970s, led by Gerard O’Neill, proposed building giant space colonies there. The idea inspired science fiction stories and the L5 Society, which promoted space settlement."]],
sp:["Webb’s journey to L2","The James Webb Space Telescope launched on 25 December 2021 and reached its halo orbit around the Sun–Earth L2 point on 24 January 2022, about 1.5 million kilometers from Earth, four times farther than the Moon."],
g:[["Equilibrium","A state where forces balance"],["Halo orbit","A looping orbit around a Lagrange point"],["Trojan asteroid","An asteroid sharing a planet’s orbit at L4 or L5"],["Unstable","Easily pushed away from balance"]],
f:["Every pair of orbiting bodies has five Lagrange points.","Jupiter has thousands of Trojan asteroids.","The James Webb Space Telescope orbits L2."],
fun:"Jupiter’s Trojan asteroids are named after heroes of the Trojan War: Greeks mostly at L4 and Trojans at L5, though a couple of ‘spies’ ended up in the wrong camp."});

ADDART({id:"seasons",t:"The Seasons",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🍂",w:"Season",r:["stonehenge","sun","climate","calendars","monsoon","autumncolors"],
p:[["Tilted Earth","Seasons happen because Earth’s axis is tilted about 23.4 degrees."],["Not about distance","Earth is actually closest to the Sun in early January."],["Opposite hemispheres","When it is summer in the north, it is winter in the south."]],
l:"The seasons are the yearly cycle of changes in weather, temperature and daylight. They happen because Earth’s axis of rotation is tilted by about 23.4 degrees relative to its orbit around the Sun. As Earth travels around the Sun, the tilt stays pointed in the same direction in space, so for part of the year the Northern Hemisphere leans toward the Sun, receiving more direct sunlight and longer days, which brings summer, while the Southern Hemisphere leans away and has winter. Six months later the situation reverses. A common myth is that seasons are caused by Earth’s changing distance from the Sun; in fact, Earth is closest to the Sun in early January, during northern winter. The solstices, around 21 June and 21 December, mark the longest and shortest days, and the equinoxes, around 20 March and 22 September, have nearly equal day and night everywhere. Tropical regions have little temperature change and often mark wet and dry seasons instead.",
s:[["Why tilt matters","When the Sun is high in the sky, its rays strike the ground more directly, concentrating energy on a smaller area. Long days also give more hours of heating. In winter, the Sun is lower, its light spreads over a larger area, and days are shorter, so less heat is received."],["Solstices and equinoxes","At the June solstice, the Sun is overhead at the Tropic of Cancer, and the Arctic has 24-hour daylight. At the December solstice, it is overhead at the Tropic of Capricorn. At the equinoxes, the Sun is overhead at the equator. Many cultures held festivals at these times, and monuments such as Stonehenge line up with the solstice sunrise or sunset."],["Seasons around the world","Temperate regions have four seasons: spring, summer, autumn and winter. Near the equator, temperatures stay warm all year, and seasons are defined by rainfall, such as monsoon and dry seasons. Polar regions have long, dark winters and summers when the Sun never sets."],["Seasons on other planets","Mars has a tilt similar to Earth’s, giving it seasons about twice as long. Uranus is tilted about 98 degrees, so each pole has about 42 years of continuous sunlight followed by 42 years of darkness. Jupiter, tilted only about 3 degrees, has almost no seasons."]],
k:[["Cause","Earth’s axial tilt of about 23.4°"],["Closest to the Sun","Early January"],["Solstices","About 21 June and 21 December"],["Equinoxes","About 20 March and 22 September"],["Most extreme tilt","Uranus, about 98°"]],
d:[["c. 3000–2500 BC","Stonehenge built, aligned with solstice sunrise and sunset"],["c. 130 BC","Hipparchus measures the timing of the seasons"],["1543","Copernicus explains seasons with a moving Earth"],["1582","Gregorian calendar keeps equinox near 21 March"]],
s2:[["Astronomical and meteorological seasons","Astronomers start the seasons at the solstices and equinoxes, so northern spring begins around 20 March. Weather scientists use whole months instead: in the Northern Hemisphere, spring is March to May, summer June to August, autumn September to November and winter December to February."],["Seasons and living things","Plants and animals respond to changing day length and temperature. Trees drop leaves in autumn, birds migrate, and some mammals hibernate. Climate change is shifting the timing of seasons, with spring arriving earlier in many regions."]],
sp:["The midnight sun","North of the Arctic Circle and south of the Antarctic Circle, the Sun stays above the horizon all day for at least one day in summer, and at the poles for about six months. In winter, these regions have a ‘polar night’ when the Sun does not rise."],
g:[["Axis","An imaginary line around which a planet spins"],["Solstice","The day with the longest or shortest daylight"],["Equinox","When day and night are about equal"],["Hemisphere","Half of the Earth"]],
f:["Seasons are caused by Earth’s tilt, not its distance from the Sun.","Earth is closest to the Sun in January.","Uranus has 42-year seasons."],
fun:"Because Earth moves faster when closer to the Sun, northern winter is about five days shorter than northern summer."});

ADDART({id:"spacewalks",t:"Spacewalks",c:"Space Exploration",i:"👩‍🚀",w:"Extravehicular activity",r:["astronauts","iss","gagarin","hubblest","commspace"],
p:[["Working outside","A spacewalk is when an astronaut leaves a spacecraft to work in space."],["First in 1965","Alexei Leonov made the first spacewalk on 18 March 1965."],["Building the ISS","Hundreds of spacewalks built and maintain the International Space Station."]],
l:"A spacewalk, or extravehicular activity (EVA), is any activity done by an astronaut outside a spacecraft in space or on the surface of another world. Spacewalkers wear pressurized spacesuits that provide oxygen, remove carbon dioxide, control temperature and protect against radiation and tiny meteoroids; each suit is effectively a small personal spacecraft. Soviet cosmonaut Alexei Leonov made the first spacewalk on 18 March 1965, and American Ed White followed in June 1965. Spacewalks have been used to repair satellites, service the Hubble Space Telescope, build and maintain space stations and explore the Moon. More than 270 spacewalks have been carried out at the International Space Station. In 2024, Jared Isaacman and Sarah Gillis made the first commercial spacewalk during the private Polaris Dawn mission. The longest spacewalk, by Chinese astronauts Cai Xuzhe and Song Lingdong in December 2024, lasted 9 hours and 6 minutes.",
s:[["The spacesuit","A spacewalking suit has many layers, a helmet with a sun visor, gloves and a backpack containing oxygen, batteries, water for cooling and radios. Suits are pressurized, which makes them stiff; moving the hands in gloves is especially tiring. Astronauts breathe pure oxygen for hours beforehand to avoid decompression sickness."],["Staying safe","Spacewalkers are tethered to the station and move hand over hand along rails. NASA astronauts wear a small jet backpack called SAFER for emergencies. Spacewalks are usually done in pairs and practiced for months in giant swimming pools, where floating underwater resembles weightlessness."],["Famous spacewalks","Leonov’s suit ballooned in the vacuum, and he had to let out air to squeeze back inside. In 1984, Bruce McCandless made the first untethered spacewalk using a jet backpack, and Svetlana Savitskaya became the first woman to walk in space. Hubble repair spacewalks from 1993 to 2009 kept the telescope working. In 2019, Christina Koch and Jessica Meir made the first all-female spacewalk."],["Dangers","In 2013, Italian astronaut Luca Parmitano’s helmet began filling with water during a spacewalk, and he returned safely despite being nearly blinded. Spacewalkers face extreme temperatures, from about 120°C in sunlight to −150°C in shadow, as well as sharp edges and debris."]],
k:[["Also called","Extravehicular activity (EVA)"],["First spacewalk","Alexei Leonov, 18 March 1965"],["First woman","Svetlana Savitskaya, 1984"],["First commercial","Polaris Dawn, 12 September 2024"],["Longest","9 h 6 min (December 2024)"]],
d:[["1965","Leonov makes the first spacewalk"],["1969","First moonwalk, by Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin"],["1984","First untethered spacewalk; first woman spacewalker"],["1993","First Hubble servicing mission"],["2019","First all-female spacewalk"],["2024","First commercial spacewalk; longest spacewalk"]],
s2:[["Records","Russian cosmonaut Anatoly Solovyev holds the record for the most total time spacewalking, about 82 hours over 16 spacewalks. NASA’s Peggy Whitson and Sunita Williams have each spent more than 60 hours outside, the most of any women."],["Lost in space?","Astronauts have occasionally lost tools during spacewalks. In 2008, Heidemarie Stefanyshyn-Piper lost a tool bag that orbited Earth for months and could be seen from the ground with binoculars before burning up in the atmosphere."]],
sp:["Training underwater","NASA’s Neutral Buoyancy Laboratory in Houston is a pool 62 meters long and 12 meters deep containing full-size models of parts of the space station. Astronauts train there in spacesuits for about seven hours for every hour they will spend spacewalking."],
g:[["EVA","Extravehicular activity, a spacewalk"],["Tether","A safety line"],["Pressurized","Filled with air at a set pressure"],["Decompression sickness","Illness caused by gas bubbles forming in the body"]],
f:["The first spacewalk was in 1965.","The first commercial spacewalk was in 2024.","Astronauts train for spacewalks in giant pools."],
fun:"After his spacewalk, Alexei Leonov became an artist and painted some of the first artworks made in space, using colored pencils aboard Voskhod 2."});

ADDART({id:"spaceports",t:"Spaceports",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🛫",w:"Spaceport",r:["rocket","commspace","sputnik","gagarin","apollo"],
p:[["Where rockets launch","Spaceports are sites for launching and sometimes landing spacecraft."],["Near the equator","Launch sites near the equator give rockets a speed boost from Earth’s spin."],["A growing number","New spaceports are being built around the world for commercial launches."]],
l:"A spaceport, or cosmodrome, is a site for launching and sometimes landing spacecraft. Spaceports include launch pads, assembly buildings, fuel storage, control centers and tracking stations. They are usually built near coasts or in remote areas so that rockets fly over water or empty land, reducing danger if something goes wrong. Sites close to the equator give rockets a free boost from Earth’s rotation, which is fastest there, and are good for launching into orbits above the equator. The world’s first and largest spaceport, Baikonur Cosmodrome in Kazakhstan, launched Sputnik 1 in 1957 and Yuri Gagarin in 1961. Other major spaceports include the Kennedy Space Center and Cape Canaveral in Florida, from which the Apollo missions launched; Europe’s Guiana Space Centre in French Guiana, close to the equator; China’s Jiuquan and Wenchang centers; India’s Satish Dhawan Space Centre; and Japan’s Tanegashima. Private companies such as SpaceX now run their own launch sites, including Starbase in Texas.",
s:[["Choosing a site","Spaceports need safe areas downrange where rockets and falling stages will not harm people. East-facing coasts are ideal because rockets usually launch eastward to use Earth’s spin. Polar orbits are reached by launching north or south, as from Vandenberg in California."],["Baikonur","Built by the Soviet Union in the 1950s on the steppes of Kazakhstan, Baikonur launched the first satellite, the first human in space and the first space station. Russia leases it from Kazakhstan, and Soyuz rockets still carry astronauts from there to the International Space Station."],["Cape Canaveral and Kennedy","Florida’s Space Coast hosted the Mercury, Gemini and Apollo programs and the Space Shuttle. Launch Complex 39A sent Apollo 11 to the Moon and is now used by SpaceX. The area has become one of the busiest launch sites in the world."],["New spaceports","Countries including the United Kingdom, Norway, Sweden, Australia and South Korea have built or are building spaceports for small rockets. SpaceX’s Starbase in southern Texas, where Starship is tested, became an official city in 2025. Floating platforms and drone ships at sea are also used for launching and landing rockets."]],
k:[["Also called","Cosmodrome"],["First and largest","Baikonur Cosmodrome, Kazakhstan"],["Apollo launch site","Kennedy Space Center, Florida"],["Europe’s spaceport","Guiana Space Centre, Kourou"],["Equator advantage","Boost from Earth’s rotation"]],
d:[["1955","Baikonur Cosmodrome founded"],["1957","Sputnik 1 launched from Baikonur"],["1961","Gagarin launches from Baikonur"],["1968","First launch from Kourou, French Guiana"],["1969","Apollo 11 launches from Launch Complex 39A"],["2025","Starbase in Texas becomes a city"]],
s2:[["Why so close to the equator?","Earth’s surface at the equator moves eastward at about 1,670 kilometers per hour. A rocket launched eastward there starts with this speed, saving fuel. Kourou, at about 5 degrees north, has an advantage over Baikonur, at about 46 degrees north."],["Gagarin’s Start","The pad at Baikonur from which Yuri Gagarin launched in 1961, known as Gagarin’s Start, was used for crewed flights for decades. Cosmonauts follow traditions there, such as planting trees and watching a particular film before launch."]],
sp:["Launching from the sea","Rockets have also been launched from ships and floating platforms, which can move to the equator. SpaceX lands rocket boosters on autonomous drone ships at sea so they can be reused."],
g:[["Spaceport","A site for launching spacecraft"],["Launch pad","The platform a rocket launches from"],["Downrange","The area a rocket flies over after launch"],["Cosmodrome","A Russian term for spaceport"]],
f:["Baikonur is the world’s first and largest spaceport.","Rockets launch eastward to use Earth’s spin.","Starbase in Texas became a city in 2025."],
fun:"Cosmonauts launching from Baikonur traditionally stop on the way to the pad to relieve themselves on the bus’s rear wheel, as Yuri Gagarin is said to have done in 1961."});

ADDART({id:"solarsails",t:"Solar Sails",c:"Space Exploration",i:"⛵",w:"Solar sail",r:["sun","light","rocket","sailing","sat"],
p:[["Sailing on sunlight","Solar sails push spacecraft using the pressure of sunlight."],["No fuel needed","They can keep accelerating as long as sunlight shines on them."],["Tested in space","Japan’s IKAROS first flew a solar sail between planets in 2010."]],
l:"A solar sail is a method of propelling a spacecraft using the tiny pressure of sunlight on a large, thin, mirror-like sheet. Light carries momentum, so when particles of light, called photons, bounce off a sail, they give it a very gentle push. The force is extremely small, but in the vacuum of space there is no air resistance, and a sail never runs out of fuel, so the spacecraft can gradually build up great speed. Sails are made of plastic films only a few micrometers thick, coated with aluminum, and must be very large and light. The idea was proposed in the early 20th century by scientists including Konstantin Tsiolkovsky and Friedrich Zander. In 2010, Japan’s IKAROS became the first spacecraft to use a solar sail in interplanetary space, and The Planetary Society’s LightSail 2 showed controlled solar sailing in Earth orbit in 2019. NASA’s Advanced Composite Solar Sail System tested lightweight booms in 2024.",
s:[["How a sail works","When sunlight reflects from the sail, it pushes the sail away from the Sun. By tilting the sail, like a sailboat, the spacecraft can direct the force to speed up or slow down in its orbit, moving outward or inward through the solar system. The push near Earth is about the weight of a sheet of paper on a large sail."],["Missions","Japan’s IKAROS, launched in 2010 with the Akatsuki Venus probe, unfurled a 14-meter-wide sail and steered by changing the brightness of panels on its edges. NASA’s NanoSail-D2 flew in 2011, and LightSail 2 raised its orbit using sunlight in 2019. NASA’s ACS3, launched in April 2024, deployed an 80-square-meter sail on composite booms."],["Future uses","Solar sails could hover in unusual positions to watch the Sun, carry cargo slowly through the solar system or visit asteroids without fuel. Breakthrough Starshot, announced in 2016, proposed using powerful lasers on Earth to push tiny sails to 20 percent of the speed of light toward Alpha Centauri, though it faces enormous technical challenges."],["Challenges","Sails must be extremely large, light and strong, and packed into small spacecraft before unfolding in space. Deploying them without tears or tangles is difficult, and their push is weak near the outer planets, where sunlight is faint."]],
k:[["Propulsion","Pressure of sunlight"],["Material","Thin aluminum-coated plastic"],["First interplanetary sail","IKAROS (Japan, 2010)"],["Orbit-raising demonstration","LightSail 2 (2019)"],["Fuel needed","None"]],
d:[["1920s","Tsiolkovsky and Zander propose sailing on sunlight"],["2010","IKAROS unfurls its sail between Earth and Venus"],["2016","Breakthrough Starshot announced"],["2019","LightSail 2 raises its orbit using sunlight"],["2024","NASA’s ACS3 deploys its sail"]],
s2:[["Kepler’s idea","In 1608, Johannes Kepler noticed that comet tails point away from the Sun and suggested that a ‘solar breeze’ pushed them. He wrote to Galileo that ships could one day sail through space with sails adapted to this breeze."],["Sails and science fiction","Solar sails appear in many science fiction stories, including Arthur C. Clarke’s ‘The Wind from the Sun’ (1964), about a solar yacht race. Space sailing remains a popular image of gentle, fuel-free travel."]],
sp:["IKAROS","The name IKAROS stands for Interplanetary Kite-craft Accelerated by Radiation Of the Sun, a reference to Icarus of Greek myth, who flew too close to the Sun. Its sail was thinner than a human hair and also carried thin-film solar cells to generate power."],
g:[["Photon","A particle of light"],["Momentum","The quantity of motion an object has"],["Propulsion","The force that moves a vehicle"],["Boom","A long pole that holds a sail open"]],
f:["Sunlight can push a spacecraft.","Solar sails need no fuel.","IKAROS was the first interplanetary solar sail."],
fun:"Johannes Kepler imagined sailing to the stars in 1608, more than 400 years before the first solar sail flew."});

;
/* data/modern31.js */
// New articles, 18 November 2026 (part 1): animals and plants.
ADDART({id:"wombats",t:"Wombats",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐾",w:"Wombat",r:["kangaroo","koalas","australiacountry","tasmania","rodents"],
p:[["Australian diggers","Wombats are sturdy, burrowing marsupials from Australia."],["Cube-shaped droppings","They are the only animals known to produce cube-shaped poo."],["Backward pouch","Their pouch opens toward the back so it does not fill with soil."]],
l:"Wombats are short-legged, muscular marsupials native to Australia. There are three living species: the common wombat of southeastern Australia and Tasmania, the southern hairy-nosed wombat and the critically endangered northern hairy-nosed wombat, which survives in only a few places in Queensland. Wombats are about a meter long and can weigh up to about 35 kilograms. They are expert diggers, using strong claws to build long burrow systems where they shelter from heat and predators. Like koalas, their closest living relatives, they carry their young in a pouch, but a wombat’s pouch faces backward so that soil does not get in while the mother digs. Wombats eat grasses, roots and bark, and have slow metabolisms that help them survive on poor food and little water. Famously, wombats produce cube-shaped droppings, which they stack on rocks and logs to mark their territory.",
s:[["Built for digging","Wombats have broad heads, powerful shoulders and flat claws. A wombat can dig a burrow up to 30 meters long, with several entrances and rooms. Their teeth, like rodents’ teeth, never stop growing, which suits their tough diet of grasses and roots."],["Cube poo","How wombats make cubes puzzled scientists for years. A 2021 study found that the final part of the wombat’s intestine has stiffer and more flexible bands, which shape the droppings into cubes as they dry. The research won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2019, before the full study was published."],["Defense","A wombat’s rear end is covered by a hard plate of cartilage. When a predator such as a dingo or fox follows it into a burrow, the wombat blocks the tunnel with its tough backside. Wombats can also run at up to about 40 kilometers per hour for short distances."],["Conservation","The northern hairy-nosed wombat fell to about 35 animals in the 1980s. Protection of its habitat, predator-proof fences and a second colony started in 2009 have increased the population to more than 300. Common wombats suffer from a skin disease called mange, which wildlife carers treat."]],
k:[["Type of animal","Marsupial"],["Species","Three"],["Native to","Australia"],["Closest relatives","Koalas"],["Famous for","Cube-shaped droppings"]],
d:[["1798","Europeans first record wombats"],["1980s","Northern hairy-nosed wombats drop to about 35"],["2009","Second colony of northern hairy-nosed wombats established"],["2019","Wombat poo research wins an Ig Nobel Prize"],["2021","Study explains how wombats make cubes"]],
s2:[["Burrows as shelters","During the 2019–20 Australian bushfires, stories spread that wombats were herding other animals into their burrows. Scientists said that wombats were not deliberately rescuing other animals, but their deep burrows did shelter some creatures from the flames."],["Ancient giants","Diprotodon, a relative of wombats that lived until about 40,000 years ago, was the largest marsupial ever, as big as a rhinoceros and weighing up to about 2,700 kilograms."]],
sp:["The northern hairy-nosed wombat","One of the world’s rarest mammals, the northern hairy-nosed wombat lives mainly in Epping Forest National Park in Queensland, protected by a fence 20 kilometers long to keep out wild dogs."],
g:[["Marsupial","A mammal that raises its young in a pouch"],["Burrow","A tunnel dug as a home"],["Metabolism","The chemical processes that use energy in the body"],["Mange","A skin disease caused by mites"]],
f:["Wombats produce cube-shaped droppings.","A wombat’s pouch opens backward.","Wombats block burrows with their hard rear ends."],
fun:"A wombat can produce about 80 to 100 small cubes of poo in a single night."});

ADDART({id:"narwhals",t:"Narwhals",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦄",w:"Narwhal",r:["whale","arctic","climchange","inuit","seals"],
p:[["Unicorns of the sea","Narwhals are Arctic whales; males usually have a long spiral tusk."],["The tusk is a tooth","The tusk is an enormously long tooth with millions of nerve endings."],["Deep divers","Narwhals dive to more than 1,500 meters under Arctic ice."]],
l:"The narwhal is a medium-sized toothed whale that lives year-round in the Arctic waters of Canada, Greenland, Norway and Russia. Narwhals are best known for the long, straight, spiral tusk that grows from the upper jaw of most males and some females. The tusk, which can be up to about 3 meters long, is actually a canine tooth that grows through the upper lip, usually twisting counterclockwise. Scientists think it is used mainly to impress females and compete with other males, and it contains millions of nerve endings that may sense changes in water. Narwhals are about 4 to 5.5 meters long, not counting the tusk, and live in groups called pods. They feed on fish, squid and shrimp, diving to more than 1,500 meters. In medieval Europe, narwhal tusks were sold as ‘unicorn horns’ worth more than gold. Today narwhals are hunted sustainably by Inuit communities and face threats from melting sea ice, shipping noise and changing prey.",
s:[["The tusk","About one in 500 males grows two tusks, and some females grow one. Drone footage filmed in 2017 showed narwhals using their tusks to tap and stun Arctic cod before eating them. The tusk is flexible and can bend about 30 centimeters without breaking."],["Life in the ice","Narwhals spend winters in the dense pack ice of Baffin Bay and Davis Strait, breathing through cracks. In summer they move into ice-free bays and fjords. They are sensitive to changes in ice and rarely leave the Arctic."],["Diving","Narwhals make some of the deepest dives of any marine mammal, staying underwater for up to about 25 minutes. Their muscles are rich in myoglobin, a protein that stores oxygen, and their flexible ribs let their chests compress under pressure."],["Narwhals and people","Inuit have hunted narwhals for thousands of years for food, oil and ivory, and narwhal skin, called maktaaq, is rich in vitamin C. Vikings traded tusks to Europe, where they were thought to cure poisons. The Throne Chair of Denmark, made in the 17th century, is decorated with narwhal tusks."]],
k:[["Type","Toothed whale"],["Habitat","Arctic Ocean"],["Tusk length","Up to about 3 m"],["Tusk is","An overgrown canine tooth"],["Deepest dives","More than 1,500 m"]],
d:[["c. 1000","Vikings trade narwhal tusks to Europe"],["1638","Danish scholar Ole Worm shows ‘unicorn horns’ come from narwhals"],["1662–1671","Danish throne decorated with narwhal tusks built"],["2014","Study finds the tusk is a sensory organ"],["2017","Drones film narwhals stunning fish with tusks"]],
s2:[["Unicorn horns","Medieval Europeans believed powdered ‘unicorn horn’ could detect and cure poison, and kings drank from cups made of narwhal ivory. In 1638 the Danish scholar Ole Worm demonstrated that these horns came from narwhals, though the trade continued."],["Arctic changes","As Arctic sea ice shrinks, killer whales can reach areas where narwhals once found safety, and more ships bring noise that can disturb narwhals’ hearing and feeding. Scientists study how narwhals will cope with these rapid changes."]],
sp:["Narwhal songs","Narwhals communicate with clicks, whistles and knocking sounds and use echolocation to find prey under the ice. Their sounds can travel for kilometers through the water."],
g:[["Tusk","A long, protruding tooth"],["Pod","A group of whales"],["Echolocation","Finding objects by sending out sound and hearing echoes"],["Myoglobin","A protein that stores oxygen in muscles"]],
f:["The narwhal’s tusk is a tooth.","Narwhal tusks were sold as unicorn horns.","Narwhals can dive deeper than 1,500 meters."],
fun:"The name narwhal comes from Old Norse words meaning ‘corpse whale’, possibly because of its mottled gray skin."});

ADDART({id:"lobsters",t:"Lobsters",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦞",w:"Lobster",r:["crustacean","atlantic","fish","animaltesting","oceancurrents"],
p:[["Armored sea animals","Lobsters are large crustaceans with hard shells and ten legs."],["Long-lived","Some lobsters live for more than 50 years."],["From cheap food to luxury","Lobster was once fed to prisoners but is now an expensive delicacy."]],
l:"Lobsters are large marine crustaceans with long bodies, hard outer skeletons and ten legs, related to crabs and shrimp. True clawed lobsters, such as the American and European lobsters, have two large front claws: a heavy crusher claw and a thinner cutter claw. Spiny lobsters, found in warmer seas, have no large claws but long spiny antennae. Lobsters live on rocky, sandy or muddy seafloors, hiding in crevices by day and coming out at night to eat fish, mollusks, worms and sometimes other lobsters. Like all crustaceans, they grow by molting, shedding their old shell and forming a new, larger one. Lobsters can live for decades, and some American lobsters probably exceed 100 years. Their blood is bluish because it uses copper to carry oxygen. Lobster fishing is a major industry in Maine and Atlantic Canada. Scientific reviews have concluded that lobsters can probably feel pain, leading the United Kingdom to recognize them as sentient beings in 2022.",
s:[["Body","A lobster’s body has a head joined to a thorax, covered by a shell called a carapace, and a muscular tail used to shoot backward through the water when escaping. Its eyes are on stalks, and it smells and tastes with hairs on its antennae and legs."],["Molting and growing","A young lobster molts many times a year; adults molt about once a year or less. After molting, the soft lobster hides while its new shell hardens. It can regrow lost claws and legs over several molts."],["Colors","Most living lobsters are greenish-brown, turning red only when cooked because heat releases a red pigment. About one in two million lobsters is bright blue because of a genetic difference, and yellow, orange, split-colored and even white lobsters have been found."],["Lobster as food","In colonial North America, lobsters were so plentiful that they were used as fertilizer and fed to servants and prisoners. Railways and canning in the 19th century spread lobster inland, and it gradually became a luxury food. Many lobster fisheries now limit catches and protect egg-bearing females."]],
k:[["Group","Crustaceans"],["Legs","Ten"],["Blood color","Bluish (copper-based)"],["Lifespan","Can be 50–100 years or more"],["Blue lobsters","About 1 in 2 million"]],
d:[["1600s","Lobsters plentiful along New England coasts"],["1840s","Lobster canning begins in Maine"],["1872","Maine passes early laws to protect lobsters"],["2021","UK review finds evidence that lobsters can feel pain"],["2022","UK law recognizes lobsters as sentient"]],
s2:[["Are lobsters immortal?","A popular internet myth claims lobsters never die of old age. Lobsters do keep growing and produce an enzyme that repairs DNA, but molting takes more and more energy as they grow, and very old lobsters may die during a molt or stop molting and develop shell disease."],["Lobsters and warming seas","Warming waters have shifted American lobster populations north. Southern New England catches collapsed after the 1990s, while catches in Maine and Canada rose, though scientists warn that further warming could reduce them too."]],
sp:["Lobster pots","Lobsters are usually caught in baited traps called pots, lowered to the seafloor on ropes. Escape gaps let small lobsters leave, and in Maine, fishers notch the tails of egg-bearing females, which must then be released if caught again."],
g:[["Crustacean","An animal with a hard shell and jointed legs, like a crab"],["Molting","Shedding an old shell to grow"],["Carapace","The shell covering the head and thorax"],["Sentient","Able to have feelings such as pain"]],
f:["Lobster blood is bluish.","Lobsters turn red when cooked.","About one in two million lobsters is blue."],
fun:"Lobsters taste with their legs and smell with tiny hairs on their antennae."});

ADDART({id:"reindeer",t:"Reindeer and Caribou",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦌",w:"Reindeer",r:["deer","arctic","tundra","finlandcountry","birdmigr"],
p:[["One species, two names","Reindeer in Europe and Asia and caribou in North America are the same species."],["Both sexes grow antlers","They are the only deer in which females usually grow antlers."],["Herded for thousands of years","Arctic peoples such as the Sámi herd reindeer for food and transport."]],
l:"Reindeer, called caribou in North America, are deer adapted to the cold Arctic and sub-Arctic tundra and forests of Europe, Asia and North America. They belong to a single species, Rangifer tarandus, with several subspecies. Reindeer are the only deer in which both males and females usually grow antlers, which are shed and regrown each year. Thick, hollow-haired coats insulate them from extreme cold, and their broad hooves act like snowshoes and help them dig through snow for food, mainly lichens called reindeer moss. Some caribou herds make the longest land migrations of any mammal, traveling thousands of kilometers each year. In northern Scandinavia, Russia and Mongolia, people including the Sámi, Nenets and Evenki have herded semi-domesticated reindeer for thousands of years for meat, milk, hides and transport. Wild reindeer and caribou populations have declined sharply in many areas because of climate change, habitat loss and disease.",
s:[["Adaptations","Reindeer noses warm cold air before it reaches the lungs. Their eyes change color with the seasons, from gold in summer to blue in winter, which may help them see in the long Arctic night. They can also see ultraviolet light, making lichens and predators stand out against snow."],["Migration","The Porcupine caribou herd of Alaska and Canada travels up to about 2,400 kilometers a year between winter forests and summer calving grounds on the coastal plain. Some herds number hundreds of thousands of animals. Their movements shape the lives of wolves, bears and Indigenous hunters."],["Reindeer herding","Reindeer herding is an important part of the culture of the Sámi people of Norway, Sweden, Finland and Russia. Herders follow the animals between seasonal pastures, now often using snowmobiles and GPS. Herding is threatened by mining, wind farms, forestry and warmer winters, when rain freezes over the snow and locks away lichens."],["Declines","Studies have found that many North American caribou herds have declined by more than half since the 1990s or 2000s. Woodland caribou in southern Canada are threatened by logging and roads, which help wolves hunt them."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Rangifer tarandus"],["Antlers","Both males and females"],["Main winter food","Lichens (reindeer moss)"],["Longest migrations","Up to about 2,400 km a year"],["Herded by","Sámi, Nenets, Evenki and others"]],
d:[["c. 3,000 years ago","Reindeer herding develops in northern Eurasia"],["1823","‘A Visit from St. Nicholas’ describes eight flying reindeer"],["1939","Rudolph the Red-Nosed Reindeer created"],["1990s","Many North American caribou herds begin to decline"],["2016","Anthrax outbreak kills thousands of reindeer in Siberia"]],
s2:[["Santa’s reindeer","The poem ‘A Visit from St. Nicholas’ (1823) gave Santa Claus eight flying reindeer. Rudolph, with his glowing red nose, was invented in 1939 for a department store booklet. Male reindeer shed their antlers in early winter, while females keep theirs until spring, so Santa’s antlered reindeer are probably female."],["Clicking knees","As reindeer walk, a tendon slips over a bone in their feet, making a clicking sound. Scientists think it helps herds stay together in snowstorms and darkness."]],
sp:["The Sámi","The Sámi are the Indigenous people of northern Scandinavia and Russia’s Kola Peninsula. In Norway and Sweden, reindeer herding is legally reserved mainly for Sámi people, and herding families have their own earmarks to identify their animals."],
g:[["Tundra","Cold, treeless land in the far north"],["Antler","A bony growth shed and regrown each year"],["Lichen","A partnership of fungus and algae"],["Semi-domesticated","Partly tamed but living mostly free"]],
f:["Reindeer and caribou are the same species.","Female reindeer grow antlers.","Reindeer eyes change color in winter."],
fun:"Reindeer can see ultraviolet light, which makes white wolves and urine trails stand out clearly against the snow."});

ADDART({id:"llamas",t:"Llamas and Alpacas",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦙",w:"Llama",r:["camel","andes","perucountry","inca","domestication"],
p:[["Camels of the Andes","Llamas and alpacas are South American relatives of camels."],["Domesticated long ago","People domesticated them in the Andes thousands of years ago."],["Wool and work","Alpacas are raised for soft fiber; llamas mainly carry loads."]],
l:"Llamas and alpacas are domesticated members of the camel family from the Andes Mountains of South America. Their wild relatives are the guanaco and the vicuña. Llamas are larger, standing about 1.7 to 1.8 meters tall at the head and weighing up to about 200 kilograms, with long banana-shaped ears; they were bred mainly as pack animals. Alpacas are smaller, with short spear-shaped ears, and were bred for their soft, warm fleece. People in the Andes domesticated llamas from guanacos and alpacas from vicuñas about 6,000 to 7,000 years ago. Llamas carried goods across the vast Inca Empire, and both animals provided wool, meat, leather and dung for fuel. Today Peru and Bolivia have most of the world’s alpacas and llamas, and they are also kept on farms and as guard animals around the world. Both animals hum to communicate and can spit when annoyed, usually at each other.",
s:[["Llama or alpaca?","Llamas have long faces, tall curved ears and coarser outer hair. Alpacas have shorter, fluffier faces and dense, fine fleece. There are two breeds of alpaca: the fluffy huacaya and the suri, whose fleece hangs in long silky locks."],["Life at high altitude","Llamas and alpacas live at altitudes of 3,500 to 5,000 meters, where the air is thin and cold. Their blood carries oxygen very efficiently, and padded feet with two toes help them walk on rocky slopes without damaging the ground."],["The Inca and camelids","The Inca kept huge herds of llamas and alpacas, owned largely by the state and religious institutions. Llama caravans carried goods along the Inca road network, and the animals were sacrificed in important ceremonies. Spanish conquest and introduced sheep and diseases caused their numbers to fall sharply."],["Wild relatives","The vicuña, the smallest camelid, has one of the finest natural fibers in the world. Overhunting reduced vicuñas to about 10,000 by the 1960s, but protection and community-managed shearing have brought them back to more than 450,000."]],
k:[["Family","Camelids"],["Native to","Andes of South America"],["Domesticated","About 6,000–7,000 years ago"],["Wild ancestors","Guanaco (llama) and vicuña (alpaca)"],["Main uses","Llamas carry loads; alpacas give fiber"]],
d:[["c. 5000 BC","Llamas and alpacas domesticated in the Andes"],["1400s","Inca Empire relies on llama caravans"],["1532","Spanish conquest begins; camelid numbers fall"],["1960s","Vicuñas reduced to about 10,000"],["1984","Alpacas first exported in large numbers to North America"]],
s2:[["Guard llamas","Farmers in North America, Australia and elsewhere use llamas to guard sheep and goats from foxes, coyotes and dogs. A llama will chase, kick or spit at intruders and often bonds with the flock it protects."],["Llamas and medicine","Llamas and alpacas make unusually small antibodies, called nanobodies. Scientists have studied llama nanobodies as possible treatments for diseases, including COVID-19."]],
sp:["Spitting","Llamas and alpacas spit mainly to settle arguments with each other over food or rank. The ‘spit’ is a smelly mix of saliva and partly digested grass from their stomachs. They rarely spit at people unless mistreated."],
g:[["Camelid","A member of the camel family"],["Fleece","The wool coat of an animal"],["Pack animal","An animal used to carry loads"],["Antibody","A protein that helps fight infection"]],
f:["Llamas and alpacas are relatives of camels.","Alpacas were bred for fiber and llamas for carrying loads.","Llamas are used to guard sheep."],
fun:"Llamas and alpacas share communal dung piles, which makes them easy to house-train."});

ADDART({id:"okapis",t:"Okapis",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦓",w:"Okapi",r:["giraffe","drccountry","leopold2","conservation","zebras"],
p:[["The forest giraffe","The okapi looks part zebra, but it is the giraffe’s only living relative."],["Discovered in 1901","Western scientists described it only in 1901."],["Endangered","Okapis live only in the rainforests of the Democratic Republic of the Congo."]],
l:"The okapi is a shy forest animal of central Africa and the only living relative of the giraffe. It has a dark reddish-brown body, a giraffe-like head, and striking black and white stripes on its legs and rump, which once led people to think it was a kind of zebra. Okapis stand about 1.5 meters tall at the shoulder and weigh up to about 350 kilograms. They live alone in the dense Ituri rainforest and other forests of the Democratic Republic of the Congo, feeding on leaves, buds, fruit and fungi, which they strip with a long, blue-black tongue that can reach their own ears. Local people knew the okapi well, but European scientists described it only in 1901. Okapis are endangered: their numbers have declined because of habitat loss, hunting, mining and armed conflict. The Okapi Wildlife Reserve, a UNESCO World Heritage Site, protects part of their range.",
s:[["Stripes and camouflage","The stripes on an okapi’s legs break up its outline in the dappled light of the rainforest, making it hard to see. They may also help a young okapi follow its mother through the forest."],["Giraffe relatives","Like giraffes, okapis have a long prehensile tongue, short skin-covered horns called ossicones on males, and walk by moving both legs on one side at once. Genetic studies show okapis and giraffes split from a common ancestor about 11 million years ago."],["Discovery","In the 1890s, explorer Henry Morton Stanley reported a donkey-like animal in the Congo forest. British official Harry Johnston obtained pieces of striped skin and a skull, and the animal was described as a new species in 1901. It became a symbol of the unknown animals still hidden in the world’s forests."],["Conservation","Okapis were listed as endangered in 2013. The Okapi Wildlife Reserve covers about 14,000 square kilometers, but rangers there face armed groups and illegal gold miners. In 2012, an attack on the reserve’s headquarters killed people and 14 captive okapis."]],
k:[["Closest relative","Giraffe"],["Native to","Democratic Republic of the Congo"],["Habitat","Dense rainforest"],["Described by scientists","1901"],["Status","Endangered"]],
d:[["1890","Stanley reports a mysterious forest animal"],["1901","Okapi described as a new species"],["1992","Okapi Wildlife Reserve established"],["1996","Reserve becomes a UNESCO World Heritage Site"],["2013","Okapi listed as endangered"]],
s2:[["A national symbol","The okapi is a national symbol of the Democratic Republic of the Congo and appears on its banknotes. World Okapi Day is celebrated on 18 October to raise awareness of the species."],["Hard to study","Because okapis are shy and live in dense forest, scientists rarely see them in the wild. Much of what is known comes from camera traps, droppings and animals in zoos."]],
sp:["A long tongue","An okapi’s tongue is about 30–45 centimeters long. It uses it to strip leaves from branches and to clean its eyes and ears."],
g:[["Ossicone","A skin-covered horn on giraffes and okapis"],["Prehensile","Able to grasp"],["Camera trap","A camera triggered by passing animals"],["Endangered","At high risk of extinction"]],
f:["The okapi is the giraffe’s only living relative.","Okapis were described by scientists only in 1901.","An okapi can lick its own ears."],
fun:"Okapis communicate with infrasound, sounds too low for humans to hear, which helps mothers and calves stay in touch in the forest."});

ADDART({id:"pufferfish",t:"Pufferfish",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐡",w:"Tetraodontidae",r:["fishes","coral","poisonousplants","japancountry","seahorse"],
p:[["Balloon fish","Pufferfish inflate themselves with water to scare predators."],["Deadly poison","Many contain tetrodotoxin, one of the strongest natural poisons."],["A risky delicacy","In Japan, specially licensed chefs prepare puffer, called fugu."]],
l:"Pufferfish are a family of about 200 species of fish found mostly in warm seas, with some in fresh and brackish water. When threatened, a pufferfish rapidly gulps water, or air if out of water, into a very stretchy stomach, swelling into a ball several times its normal size that is hard for predators to swallow; many species are also covered in spines. Most pufferfish contain tetrodotoxin, a nerve poison produced by bacteria and concentrated in the liver, ovaries and skin, which can paralyze and kill a person in small amounts and has no antidote. Despite this, puffer meat, called fugu, is a prized delicacy in Japan, where chefs must train for years and pass an exam to prepare it. Pufferfish have strong, beak-like teeth that crack shells of crabs and mollusks, and their teeth keep growing. Male white-spotted pufferfish create elaborate circular sand patterns on the seafloor to attract mates.",
s:[["Inflating","A pufferfish can inflate in a few seconds by pumping water into its stomach, which has folds that unfold like an accordion. Inflating takes energy and is stressful, so they do it only when threatened."],["Tetrodotoxin","Tetrodotoxin blocks signals in nerves, causing numbness, paralysis and breathing failure. It is made by bacteria that pufferfish get from their food; puffers raised on clean food in fish farms are nontoxic. Treatment involves supporting breathing until the poison wears off."],["Fugu","In Japan, fugu is served as thin, translucent slices of raw fish arranged like flower petals, or in hot pots. Licensed chefs remove the poisonous organs carefully. Poisoning cases still happen each year, mostly when people prepare puffers they caught themselves."],["Underwater artists","In 1995, divers in Japan spotted mysterious circular patterns about 2 meters wide on the seafloor. In 2011, scientists discovered that they were made by small male pufferfish, which swim back and forth for days to sculpt ridges in the sand, where females come to lay eggs."]],
k:[["Number of species","About 200"],["Defense","Inflating and spines"],["Poison","Tetrodotoxin"],["Delicacy","Fugu (Japan)"],["Famous behavior","Males build sand circles"]],
d:[["1774","Captain James Cook’s crew poisoned by a puffer"],["1888","Fugu ban lifted in Yamaguchi Prefecture, Japan"],["1909","Tetrodotoxin named by Japanese scientist Yoshizumi Tahara"],["1995","Mysterious sand circles found off Japan"],["2011","Pufferfish identified as the sand-circle builders"]],
s2:[["Cook’s close call","During Captain James Cook’s second voyage in 1774, Cook and two naturalists tasted the liver and roe of a pufferfish caught near New Caledonia. They became numb and weak but survived, while a pig that ate the leftovers died."],["Puffers and porcupinefish","Porcupinefish are close relatives with larger spines that stand up when they inflate. Boxfish and ocean sunfish are also in the same group of fish."]],
sp:["Dolphins and puffers","Dolphins have been filmed gently chewing pufferfish and passing them around, apparently becoming dazed by small amounts of toxin. Scientists debate whether they do it deliberately."],
g:[["Tetrodotoxin","A deadly nerve poison found in pufferfish"],["Brackish","Slightly salty water, like in estuaries"],["Antidote","A medicine that counteracts a poison"],["Fugu","Japanese name for pufferfish as food"]],
f:["Pufferfish inflate with water to scare predators.","Tetrodotoxin has no antidote.","Male pufferfish sculpt circles in the sand."],
fun:"A single pufferfish can contain enough tetrodotoxin to kill about 30 adult people."});

ADDART({id:"puffins",t:"Puffins",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐧",w:"Puffin",r:["birds","albatross","iceland","norwaycountry","climchange"],
p:[["Colorful seabirds","Puffins are seabirds with bright, triangular beaks in breeding season."],["Expert divers","They ‘fly’ underwater with their wings to catch small fish."],["Cliff-top burrows","Puffins nest in burrows on islands and sea cliffs."]],
l:"Puffins are small seabirds of the auk family that live in the cold waters of the North Atlantic and North Pacific. There are three species: the Atlantic puffin, the horned puffin and the tufted puffin. Puffins are known for their large, brightly colored beaks, which in the breeding season are striped with orange, yellow and blue-gray; after breeding, the colorful outer plates are shed. Puffins spend most of their lives at sea and come ashore only to breed, gathering in large colonies on islands and cliffs, where pairs dig burrows or use rock crevices. They are strong swimmers, using their wings to ‘fly’ underwater to depths of about 60 meters, and catch small fish such as sand eels, holding several crosswise in their beaks. Pairs often return to the same burrow and partner year after year. The Atlantic puffin is listed as vulnerable because of declining food supplies linked to warming seas and overfishing.",
s:[["Beaks","A puffin’s beak has backward-pointing spines on its tongue and roof, letting it hold a row of fish while catching more. Puffins have been seen carrying more than 60 small fish at once. In 2019, scientists found that puffin beaks glow under ultraviolet light, which other puffins may be able to see."],["Breeding","A puffin pair lays a single egg in a burrow and both parents incubate it for about six weeks. The chick, called a puffling, is fed fish for about six weeks before leaving the burrow at night to fly out to sea alone."],["Colonies","The largest Atlantic puffin colonies are in Iceland, Norway, the Faroe Islands, Newfoundland and Scotland. Iceland hosts about half of the world’s Atlantic puffins. Colonies can number tens of thousands of birds, and many islands are popular with birdwatchers."],["Threats","Warmer seas have changed the numbers and timing of sand eels and other fish, causing breeding failures in some colonies. Rats and other introduced predators raid burrows, and oil spills and storms kill birds at sea. Projects to remove rats from islands have helped puffins return."]],
k:[["Family","Auks"],["Species","Atlantic, horned and tufted puffins"],["Diving depth","Up to about 60 m"],["Chick name","Puffling"],["Atlantic puffin status","Vulnerable"]],
d:[["1973","Project Puffin begins restoring puffins to Maine islands"],["1981","Puffins return to breed on Eastern Egg Rock, Maine"],["2015","Atlantic puffin listed as vulnerable"],["2019","Puffin beaks found to glow under ultraviolet light"]],
s2:[["Puffling patrols","In the Westman Islands of Iceland, young pufflings flying out to sea at night are often confused by town lights and land in streets. Each August, local children collect them in boxes and release them by the sea the next morning."],["Project Puffin","Puffins had disappeared from most Maine islands by 1900 because of hunting. From 1973, scientists moved chicks from Newfoundland to Eastern Egg Rock and used decoys and mirrors to attract adults. Puffins began nesting there again in 1981."]],
sp:["Clowns of the sea","Puffins’ colorful beaks, orange feet and waddling walk have earned them nicknames such as ‘clowns of the sea’ and ‘sea parrots’. A group of puffins is sometimes called a circus or an improbability."],
g:[["Auk","A family of diving seabirds"],["Colony","A large group of animals nesting together"],["Puffling","A young puffin"],["Incubate","To keep eggs warm until they hatch"]],
f:["Puffins can carry dozens of fish in their beaks.","Puffin beaks glow under ultraviolet light.","Iceland has about half of all Atlantic puffins."],
fun:"Puffins flap their wings up to 400 times a minute and can fly at about 80 kilometers per hour."});

ADDART({id:"kiwis",t:"Kiwi Birds",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🥝",w:"Kiwi (bird)",r:["birds","nzcountry","ostrich","invasiveplants","conservation"],
p:[["Flightless birds","Kiwi are small, flightless birds found only in New Zealand."],["Huge eggs","A kiwi egg can be about 20 percent of the mother’s weight."],["National symbol","New Zealanders are nicknamed ‘Kiwis’ after the bird."]],
l:"Kiwi are flightless birds native only to New Zealand. There are five species, about the size of a chicken, with shaggy, hair-like feathers, tiny wings hidden under their plumage and no tail. Unusually for birds, kiwi have nostrils at the tip of their long beaks and find food mainly by smell, probing the ground at night for worms, insects and grubs. Female kiwi lay enormous eggs, up to about 20 percent of their body weight, the largest egg relative to body size of any bird; in most species, the male incubates the egg for around 70 to 90 days. Kiwi evolved in a land without ground mammals, so they have no natural defenses against introduced predators such as stoats, cats, dogs and ferrets, which kill most kiwi chicks. The kiwi is New Zealand’s national symbol, and New Zealanders call themselves ‘Kiwis’. Predator control and protected sanctuaries have helped some kiwi populations grow again.",
s:[["Unusual birds","Kiwi have a low body temperature for birds, about 38°C, heavy bones filled with marrow, and whiskers around their beaks for feeling their way in the dark. Their closest relatives are not the nearby moa but the extinct elephant birds of Madagascar."],["The giant egg","A kiwi egg takes up so much space that the female cannot eat much in the final days before laying. Kiwi chicks hatch fully feathered and live off the yolk for their first week or so before leaving the nest to feed."],["Threats and conservation","Stoats, introduced to New Zealand in the 1880s to control rabbits, are the main killers of kiwi chicks. Operation Nest Egg collects eggs from the wild, hatches them in safety and returns young kiwi once they are big enough to defend themselves. New Zealand aims to remove rats, stoats and possums from the whole country by 2050."],["Kiwi and people","For Māori, the kiwi is a taonga, or treasure, and kiwi feathers were woven into prized cloaks. The bird has appeared on New Zealand coins, stamps and military badges. The fruit called kiwifruit, originally known as Chinese gooseberry, was renamed in the 1950s for export."]],
k:[["Native to","New Zealand only"],["Species","Five"],["Flight","Flightless"],["Egg size","Up to about 20% of the female’s weight"],["Nostrils","At the tip of the beak"]],
d:[["1813","Kiwi first described by European scientists"],["1880s","Stoats introduced to New Zealand"],["1959","Chinese gooseberry renamed ‘kiwifruit’ for export"],["1994","Operation Nest Egg begins"],["2016","Predator Free 2050 goal announced"]],
s2:[["Elephant bird cousins","A 2014 DNA study found that the kiwi’s closest relative was the huge, extinct elephant bird of Madagascar, suggesting that the ancestors of kiwi flew to New Zealand and later lost the ability to fly."],["Kiwi calls","Kiwi are rarely seen but often heard at night. Males make a high-pitched whistle and females a lower, hoarser cry, and pairs call to each other across the forest."]],
sp:["Kiwi sanctuaries","Fenced sanctuaries such as Zealandia in Wellington keep out predators, letting kiwi and other native birds live safely. Since 2022, kiwi have been released into hills around Wellington, where community groups trap predators."],
g:[["Flightless","Unable to fly"],["Incubate","To keep eggs warm until they hatch"],["Stoat","A small predatory mammal related to weasels"],["Taonga","A Māori word for a treasure"]],
f:["Kiwi have nostrils at the tip of their beaks.","Kiwi lay the largest eggs for their size of any bird.","Kiwi are found only in New Zealand."],
fun:"Kiwi are so unusual for birds, with hair-like feathers and whiskers, that they are sometimes called ‘honorary mammals’."});

ADDART({id:"cicadas",t:"Cicadas",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦗",w:"Cicada",r:["insect","metamorph","sound","trees","animalbehavior"],
p:[["Loud insects","Male cicadas make some of the loudest sounds of any insect."],["Underground lives","Young cicadas live underground for years, sucking sap from roots."],["Periodical swarms","Some North American cicadas emerge in vast numbers every 13 or 17 years."]],
l:"Cicadas are insects known for their loud buzzing songs on summer days. There are about 3,000 species worldwide, mostly in warm regions. Male cicadas make sound with drum-like organs called tymbals on their abdomens, which they buckle rapidly in and out; their hollow bodies amplify the clicks, and some species reach about 100 decibels, as loud as a motorcycle. Females are attracted by the songs. After mating, females lay eggs in tree twigs, and the hatched nymphs drop to the ground and burrow underground, where they feed on sap from roots for years. Finally they dig out, climb a tree, shed their skins and emerge as winged adults that live only a few weeks. Most species have life cycles of a few years, but periodical cicadas of eastern North America emerge together every 13 or 17 years in enormous numbers, up to millions per hectare. In 2024, two broods emerged together for the first time since 1803.",
s:[["Making sound","The tymbal is a ribbed membrane that clicks as muscles pull it in and out hundreds of times a second. Each species has its own song. Males often sing together in choruses, and both sexes have ears called tympana to hear them."],["Periodical cicadas","Periodical cicadas spend 13 or 17 years underground and then emerge almost all at once when the soil warms to about 18°C in spring. Scientists think this strategy overwhelms predators: birds and other animals cannot eat them all, so many survive to breed. The prime-number cycles may help them avoid predators and other broods with shorter cycles."],["Broods","Periodical cicada populations are grouped into broods, numbered with Roman numerals, that emerge in different years and regions. Brood X, which emerged in 2021, covered much of the eastern United States. In 2024, Brood XIII (17-year) and Brood XIX (13-year) emerged at the same time in parts of Illinois."],["Cicadas and people","Cicadas do not bite or sting and do little harm to mature trees. They are eaten in parts of Asia, Latin America and by some people in the United States. In ancient China, jade cicadas were placed in the mouths of the dead as symbols of rebirth, and cicadas appear in Greek and Japanese poetry."]],
k:[["Species","About 3,000"],["Sound organ","Tymbal"],["Loudest songs","About 100 decibels"],["Periodical cycles","13 or 17 years"],["Adult lifespan","A few weeks"]],
d:[["1634","European settlers record a periodical cicada emergence"],["1803","Broods XIII and XIX last emerge together before 2024"],["2004","Brood X emerges across the eastern United States"],["2021","Brood X emerges again"],["2024","Two broods emerge together for the first time in 221 years"]],
s2:[["Why prime numbers?","Thirteen and 17 are prime numbers, divisible only by themselves and 1. Some scientists suggest prime-number cycles make it unlikely that cicadas will emerge in the same year as predators with regular population cycles, or that different broods will interbreed often."],["Zombie cicadas","A fungus called Massospora infects some periodical cicadas, replacing parts of their bodies with spores. Infected cicadas keep flying and trying to mate, spreading the fungus to others."]],
sp:["Emergence","On spring nights, cicada nymphs crawl out of holes in the ground, climb tree trunks and split open their skins. The soft, pale adults pump blood into their wings and harden over a few hours, leaving empty shells clinging to bark."],
g:[["Tymbal","A drum-like sound organ in cicadas"],["Nymph","A young insect that looks somewhat like an adult"],["Brood","A group of cicadas that emerge in the same year"],["Prime number","A number divisible only by itself and 1"]],
f:["Cicadas can be as loud as a motorcycle.","Periodical cicadas live underground for 13 or 17 years.","Cicadas neither bite nor sting."],
fun:"In some periodical cicada emergences, there can be more than a million cicadas per acre of land."});

ADDART({id:"tomatoes",t:"Tomatoes",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🍅",w:"Tomato",r:["fruits","perucountry","mexicocountry","potato","explor"],
p:[["A fruit used as a vegetable","Botanically, tomatoes are berries."],["From the Americas","Tomatoes were domesticated in Mexico from wild plants of the Andes."],["Once feared","Many Europeans once thought tomatoes were poisonous."]],
l:"The tomato is the edible fruit of Solanum lycopersicum, a plant of the nightshade family, which also includes potatoes, eggplants, peppers and tobacco. Wild tomatoes, with tiny fruits, grow in the Andes of western South America; people in Mexico and Central America domesticated larger tomatoes, and the Aztecs called them xitomatl. Spanish explorers brought tomatoes to Europe in the 16th century. At first many Europeans grew them as ornamental plants, partly because their leaves and stems, like those of other nightshades, are toxic, but tomatoes became central to Italian and Spanish cooking by the 18th and 19th centuries. Today tomatoes are among the world’s most important vegetables, with China, India and Turkey the largest producers. Botanically the tomato is a berry, but in 1893 the US Supreme Court ruled that it should be taxed as a vegetable because it is eaten as one. Tomatoes are rich in vitamin C and lycopene, the red pigment.",
s:[["Varieties","There are thousands of tomato varieties, from tiny cherry tomatoes to huge beefsteaks, and in colors including yellow, orange, purple, green and striped. Heirloom varieties are old types passed down through generations, while most supermarket tomatoes are bred to ripen evenly and survive shipping."],["Tomatoes in Europe","The first known European description of the tomato, by Italian botanist Pietro Andrea Mattioli in 1544, called it pomi d’oro, ‘golden apples’. In England and North America, some people avoided tomatoes until the 19th century. Tomato sauce for pasta was first recorded in Italy in the late 17th century."],["Fruit or vegetable?","In the case Nix v. Hedden (1893), the US Supreme Court agreed that tomatoes are botanically fruits but decided that in ordinary language and for import taxes, they count as vegetables, like cucumbers and beans."],["Growing tomatoes","Tomatoes need warmth and sunlight and are grown outdoors and in large greenhouses. The Netherlands produces huge yields in greenhouses using little water. Tomatoes picked green are often ripened with ethylene gas, a natural plant hormone."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Solanum lycopersicum"],["Family","Nightshades"],["Origin","Andes (wild); Mexico (domesticated)"],["Largest producer","China"],["Red pigment","Lycopene"]],
d:[["c. 500 BC","Tomatoes cultivated in Mexico"],["1544","First European description of tomatoes"],["1692","First recorded Italian recipe with tomato sauce"],["1893","US Supreme Court rules tomatoes are vegetables"],["1994","First genetically modified food sold, the Flavr Savr tomato"]],
s2:[["La Tomatina","Every August, the town of Buñol in Spain holds La Tomatina, a festival in which thousands of people throw about 120,000 kilograms of overripe tomatoes at each other in the streets. It began in 1945."],["The poison myth","A popular story claims that wealthy Europeans died from eating tomatoes off pewter plates containing lead. Historians say this is probably a myth, but the tomato’s resemblance to poisonous relatives such as nightshade did make some people suspicious."]],
sp:["The Flavr Savr","In 1994, the Flavr Savr tomato, engineered to soften more slowly, became the first genetically modified whole food sold in stores. It was not a commercial success and was withdrawn after a few years."],
g:[["Nightshade family","A plant family including tomatoes, potatoes and peppers"],["Berry","A fleshy fruit with seeds inside"],["Heirloom","An old variety passed down through generations"],["Lycopene","The red pigment in tomatoes"]],
f:["Tomatoes are botanically berries.","The US Supreme Court ruled tomatoes are vegetables.","Tomato leaves are toxic."],
fun:"The early Italian name for the tomato, pomodoro, means ‘golden apple’, because the first tomatoes in Italy were probably yellow."});

ADDART({id:"apples",t:"Apples",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🍎",w:"Apple",r:["fruits","roses","silk","newton","grapes"],
p:[["A fruit from Central Asia","Apples come from wild forests in Kazakhstan."],["Thousands of varieties","There are more than 7,500 apple varieties."],["Grown by grafting","Apple trees do not grow true from seed, so varieties are copied by grafting."]],
l:"The apple is the fruit of the apple tree, Malus domestica, a member of the rose family and one of the most widely grown fruits in the world. Apples descend mainly from a wild apple, Malus sieversii, that still grows in the mountain forests of Kazakhstan, whose largest city, Almaty, was long thought to mean ‘father of apples’. Traders along the Silk Road carried apples west, where they crossed with European crab apples. Apples have been grown in Europe since ancient Greek and Roman times and were brought to the Americas by European colonists. Because each apple seed produces a tree that differs from its parent, growers keep varieties by grafting cuttings onto rootstocks. There are more than 7,500 known varieties, including Gala, Fuji, Granny Smith and Honeycrisp. China produces about half of the world’s apples. Apples feature in myths and stories from the Garden of Eden to Snow White and Isaac Newton’s famous falling apple.",
s:[["From seed to tree","Apple seeds are genetically mixed, so a tree grown from a Fuji seed will produce different, usually small and sour apples. Nearly all orchard trees are grafted, and the rootstock controls how big the tree grows. Dwarf rootstocks let modern orchards plant thousands of small trees per hectare."],["Johnny Appleseed","John Chapman, known as Johnny Appleseed, planted apple nurseries across the American frontier in the early 1800s. His trees grew from seeds, so most produced sour apples used mainly for cider rather than eating."],["Storing apples","Apples can be stored for months in cold rooms with low oxygen, which slows ripening, so fresh apples are available all year. Cut apples turn brown because enzymes react with oxygen. A genetically engineered non-browning apple was approved in the United States in 2015."],["Apples in stories","In Greek myth, a golden apple started the quarrel that led to the Trojan War. William Tell shot an apple from his son’s head. Isaac Newton said that watching an apple fall helped him think about gravity, and a tree said to be descended from Newton’s still grows at his childhood home."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Malus domestica"],["Family","Rose family"],["Wild ancestor","Malus sieversii (Kazakhstan)"],["Varieties","More than 7,500"],["Largest producer","China"]],
d:[["c. 2000 BC","Apples spread west along trade routes from Central Asia"],["1600s","Colonists bring apple trees to North America"],["1666","Newton watches an apple fall, by his own account"],["1868","Granny Smith apple discovered in Australia"],["1991","Honeycrisp apple released"]],
s2:[["Granny Smith","The Granny Smith apple grew from a chance seedling found by Maria Ann Smith in her orchard near Sydney, Australia, in 1868. Its bright green, tart fruit became famous worldwide."],["An apple a day","The saying ‘An apple a day keeps the doctor away’ comes from a 19th-century Welsh proverb. Apples are a good source of fiber and vitamin C, though a 2015 study found that apple eaters visited doctors about as often as others."]],
sp:["Wild apple forests","The wild apple forests of the Tian Shan mountains in Kazakhstan contain trees with fruit of many sizes, colors and flavors. Scientists study them for genes that could make apples more resistant to disease and climate change."],
g:[["Grafting","Joining a cutting from one plant onto the roots of another"],["Rootstock","The root part of a grafted tree"],["Variety","A distinct type of a plant"],["Orchard","A planting of fruit trees"]],
f:["Apples belong to the rose family.","Apples come from wild forests in Kazakhstan.","Apple trees do not grow true from seed."],
fun:"Apples float in water because about a quarter of their volume is air, which makes the game of bobbing for apples possible."});

ADDART({id:"bananas",t:"Bananas",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🍌",w:"Banana",r:["fruits","fungi","trade","cloning","gmos"],
p:[["The world’s favorite fruit","Bananas are among the most eaten fruits on Earth."],["Clones","Nearly all export bananas are genetically identical Cavendish clones."],["A disease threat","A fungus called Panama disease threatens banana farms worldwide."]],
l:"Bananas are long, curved fruits produced by large herbaceous plants of the genus Musa, native to tropical Southeast Asia and New Guinea, where people domesticated them at least 7,000 years ago. Although banana plants look like trees, they have no woody trunk; the ‘stem’ is made of tightly packed leaf bases. Wild bananas are full of hard seeds, but cultivated bananas are seedless and are grown from cuttings, so plants of each variety are genetically identical clones. Bananas include sweet dessert bananas and starchy cooking bananas, often called plantains, which are a staple food for hundreds of millions of people in Africa, Asia and Latin America. India is the largest producer, while Ecuador, the Philippines and Central American countries are major exporters. Almost all exported bananas are of one variety, the Cavendish, which replaced the Gros Michel after a fungus called Panama disease devastated plantations in the mid-20th century. A new strain, Tropical Race 4, now threatens the Cavendish.",
s:[["The banana plant","A banana plant grows from an underground stem called a corm and can reach several meters tall in under a year. It flowers once, producing a bunch of up to 200 bananas in rows called hands, and then dies, replaced by new shoots from the base."],["Gros Michel and Cavendish","Until the 1950s, the main export banana was the Gros Michel, said to be sweeter and creamier. Panama disease, caused by a soil fungus, wiped out plantations in Central America, and exporters switched to the resistant Cavendish."],["Tropical Race 4","A new strain of Panama disease, Tropical Race 4 (TR4), began killing Cavendish bananas in Asia in the 1990s and reached Colombia in 2019. There is no cure, and the fungus survives in soil for decades. Scientists are breeding and genetically engineering resistant bananas, and Australia approved a resistant GM Cavendish in 2024."],["Bananas and history","Large companies such as the United Fruit Company controlled banana growing in Central America in the early 20th century and had great influence over governments, giving rise to the term ‘banana republic’. Workers’ strikes, such as the one that ended in the 1928 Banana Massacre in Colombia, were sometimes violently suppressed."]],
k:[["Genus","Musa"],["Origin","Southeast Asia and New Guinea"],["Main export variety","Cavendish"],["Largest producer","India"],["Main threat","Panama disease (TR4)"]],
d:[["c. 5000 BC","Bananas domesticated in New Guinea"],["1870s","Bananas begin to be shipped to North America"],["1928","Banana Massacre in Colombia"],["1950s","Cavendish replaces the Gros Michel"],["2019","TR4 detected in Colombia"],["2024","Australia approves a TR4-resistant GM banana"]],
s2:[["Radioactive bananas","Bananas contain potassium, a small part of which is naturally radioactive. Scientists sometimes use the ‘banana equivalent dose’ to explain tiny radiation amounts, but eating bananas is perfectly safe because the body keeps potassium levels steady."],["Why bananas curve","Bananas start growing downward but then bend upward toward the light, a response called negative geotropism, which gives them their curved shape."]],
sp:["Plantains","Plantains are starchy bananas eaten cooked: boiled, fried or mashed. In countries such as Uganda, where people eat some of the most bananas in the world, the word for food is closely linked to cooked bananas called matooke."],
g:[["Clone","A genetically identical copy"],["Corm","An underground stem that stores food"],["Plantain","A starchy cooking banana"],["Herbaceous","Having a soft, non-woody stem"]],
f:["Banana plants are not trees.","Most export bananas are Cavendish clones.","Bananas are slightly radioactive."],
fun:"Botanically, bananas are berries, while strawberries are not."});

ADDART({id:"citrus",t:"Citrus Fruits",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🍊",w:"Citrus",r:["fruits","vitamins","sailing","china","indiacountry"],
p:[["Oranges, lemons and limes","Citrus fruits include oranges, lemons, limes, grapefruits and mandarins."],["Ancient hybrids","Most citrus are hybrids of a few wild ancestors from Asia."],["Fighting scurvy","Citrus juice helped sailors prevent scurvy."]],
l:"Citrus is a group of flowering trees and shrubs in the rue family whose fruits include oranges, lemons, limes, grapefruits, mandarins and pomelos. Citrus plants originated in the foothills of the Himalayas in southern China, northeastern India and Myanmar, and in Southeast Asia and Australia. Most familiar citrus fruits are hybrids of a few ancestral species, including the pomelo, the mandarin and the citron; the sweet orange, for example, is a cross between pomelo and mandarin. Citrus fruits have a thick rind with oil glands and juicy segments filled with tiny sacs of juice. They are rich in vitamin C, and in 1747 Scottish naval surgeon James Lind showed in one of the first controlled trials that citrus juice cured scurvy, a disease that killed many sailors. Oranges are the most widely grown citrus, with Brazil, China and India among the largest producers. A bacterial disease called citrus greening has devastated orange groves in Florida.",
s:[["Family tree","Genetic studies published in 2018 traced citrus to the southeastern Himalayas about 8 million years ago. Lemons are a hybrid of citron and sour orange; grapefruit is a cross between pomelo and sweet orange, first found in Barbados in the 18th century."],["Scurvy and the sea","Scurvy, caused by lack of vitamin C, causes bleeding gums, weakness and death. Lind’s trial on HMS Salisbury gave different treatments to sailors with scurvy, and those who ate oranges and lemons recovered fastest. The British navy began issuing lemon juice in 1795, leading to the nickname ‘limeys’ for British sailors."],["Uses","Citrus fruits are eaten fresh and made into juice, marmalade, sweets and drinks. Lemon and lime juice flavor cooking around the world. Oils from the peel are used in perfumes, cleaning products and flavorings, and pectin from the peel thickens jams."],["Citrus greening","Citrus greening, or huanglongbing, is caused by bacteria spread by a tiny insect, the Asian citrus psyllid. Infected trees produce small, bitter, green fruit and eventually die. Since it was found in Florida in 2005, the state’s orange harvest has fallen by more than 90 percent."]],
k:[["Family","Rue family"],["Origin","Southeastern Himalayas and Southeast Asia"],["Ancestors","Pomelo, mandarin, citron and others"],["Rich in","Vitamin C"],["Main disease","Citrus greening"]],
d:[["c. 8 million years ago","Citrus ancestors evolve near the Himalayas"],["c. 300 BC","Citrons reach the Mediterranean"],["1747","James Lind’s scurvy trial"],["1795","British navy issues lemon juice to sailors"],["2005","Citrus greening found in Florida"]],
s2:[["Why oranges are orange","In tropical countries, ripe oranges often stay green because the warm nights do not break down the green chlorophyll in the peel. Oranges turn orange in places with cool nights, and some exporters treat green oranges with ethylene gas to change their color."],["The Valencia and the navel","Navel oranges are seedless and have a second small fruit at the end. All navel oranges descend from a single mutated tree found in Brazil in the 1820s, and cuttings were sent to California in 1873."]],
sp:["Buddha’s hand","Buddha’s hand is a type of citron whose fruit splits into long finger-like segments with almost no pulp. It is valued in China and Japan for its fragrance and is used in temple offerings."],
g:[["Hybrid","The offspring of two different species or varieties"],["Scurvy","A disease caused by lack of vitamin C"],["Rind","The tough outer skin of a fruit"],["Pectin","A substance from fruit used to thicken jams"]],
f:["Sweet oranges are hybrids of pomelo and mandarin.","Citrus juice prevents scurvy.","All navel oranges come from one Brazilian tree."],
fun:"Lemons float in water but limes usually sink, because limes are slightly denser."});

ADDART({id:"chilies",t:"Chili Peppers",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🌶️",w:"Chili pepper",r:["spice","mexicocountry","tomatoes","indiacountry","explor"],
p:[["Hot fruits","Chili peppers get their heat from a chemical called capsaicin."],["From the Americas","Chilies were domesticated in Mexico about 6,000 years ago."],["Spread worldwide","After 1492, chilies quickly became central to Asian and African cooking."]],
l:"Chili peppers are the fruits of plants in the genus Capsicum, part of the nightshade family. They were domesticated in Mexico and South America at least 6,000 years ago and were widely used by the Maya and Aztecs. Their heat comes from capsaicin, a chemical that triggers pain receptors in the mouth, causing a burning sensation; birds cannot feel it, so they eat chilies and spread the seeds, while mammals are deterred. After Christopher Columbus brought chilies to Europe, Portuguese and Spanish traders carried them around the world within a century, and they became essential in the cooking of India, Thailand, China, Korea, Hungary and Africa. Chili heat is measured on the Scoville scale, from zero for bell peppers to more than 2 million Scoville heat units for the hottest varieties. Pepper X was named the world’s hottest pepper in 2023. India is the largest producer and consumer of chilies. Capsaicin is also used in pain-relief creams and pepper spray.",
s:[["The Scoville scale","Pharmacist Wilbur Scoville devised his test in 1912, diluting chili extract in sugar water until tasters could no longer detect the heat. Today, heat is measured precisely with laboratory instruments. A jalapeño rates about 2,500–8,000 Scoville heat units, a habanero about 100,000–350,000, and Pepper X about 2.7 million."],["Why chilies burn","Capsaicin binds to a receptor called TRPV1, which normally detects heat, so the brain feels burning without any real temperature change. Scientists David Julius and Ardem Patapoutian shared the 2021 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine partly for discovering how this receptor works. Milk helps cool the burn because casein proteins wash capsaicin away."],["A global spread","Chilies spread from Lisbon and Seville to Africa, India and Southeast Asia in the 16th century. Within decades, Indian cooks had adopted them, and chilies replaced black pepper as the main source of heat in many cuisines. Paprika became the national spice of Hungary."],["Varieties","There are five main domesticated species, including Capsicum annuum, which includes bell peppers, jalapeños and cayenne, and Capsicum chinense, which includes habaneros and many superhot peppers. Despite its name, Capsicum chinense comes from the Americas."]],
k:[["Genus","Capsicum"],["Heat chemical","Capsaicin"],["Domesticated","At least 6,000 years ago"],["Heat scale","Scoville scale (1912)"],["Hottest (2023 record)","Pepper X, about 2.7 million SHU"]],
d:[["c. 4000 BC","Chilies domesticated in Mexico"],["1493","Columbus brings chilies to Spain"],["1500s","Portuguese traders carry chilies to India and Asia"],["1912","Wilbur Scoville devises his heat test"],["2021","Nobel Prize for discovering heat receptors"],["2023","Pepper X named the world’s hottest pepper"]],
s2:[["Why ‘pepper’?","Columbus was searching for black pepper, a valuable spice from India. He called the hot fruits he found in the Caribbean ‘peppers’, and the name stuck, even though chilies are not related to black pepper."],["Birds and chilies","Birds lack the receptor that makes capsaicin burn, so they happily eat hot chilies and spread the seeds in their droppings. Scientists think chilies evolved capsaicin partly to discourage mammals, whose teeth crush seeds."]],
sp:["Bell peppers","Bell peppers, also called capsicums or sweet peppers, belong to the same species as many hot chilies but have a gene that stops them making capsaicin, so they rate zero on the Scoville scale."],
g:[["Capsaicin","The chemical that makes chilies hot"],["Scoville heat unit","A measure of chili heat"],["Receptor","A protein that detects a signal"],["Paprika","A spice made from dried red peppers"]],
f:["Birds cannot taste the heat of chilies.","Milk cools chili burn better than water.","India produces the most chilies."],
fun:"The Bhut Jolokia, or ghost pepper, became so famous for its heat that the Indian army tested it for use in smoke grenades."});

ADDART({id:"onions",t:"Onions and Garlic",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🧅",w:"Allium",r:["spice","egypt","medicinalplants","plantbased","vitamins"],
p:[["The allium family","Onions, garlic, leeks and chives belong to the genus Allium."],["Ancient foods","Onions and garlic were eaten in ancient Egypt and Mesopotamia."],["Tears and smell","Sulfur chemicals give them their strong flavors and make us cry."]],
l:"Onions, garlic, leeks, shallots and chives are plants of the genus Allium, a group of several hundred species that grow from bulbs and have strong-smelling sulfur compounds. They originated in Central Asia and have been cultivated for at least 5,000 years. Ancient Egyptians fed onions and garlic to pyramid builders and placed them in tombs, including that of Tutankhamun; the Greeks and Romans ate them and believed garlic gave strength. Today onions are among the most widely grown vegetables in the world, used in almost every cuisine, with India and China the largest producers, and China grows about three-quarters of the world’s garlic. When an onion is cut, damaged cells release enzymes that produce a gas called syn-propanethial-S-oxide, which irritates the eyes and causes tears. Garlic’s smell comes from allicin, made when cloves are crushed. Alliums are toxic to dogs and cats.",
s:[["Onions","An onion bulb is made of layers of fleshy leaf bases that store food for the plant. Onions come in yellow, red and white varieties, and sweet onions such as Vidalia have less sulfur. Cooking onions slowly breaks down their sugars, producing sweet, brown caramelized onions."],["Garlic","A garlic bulb, or head, is made of cloves, each of which can grow into a new plant. Garlic is planted from cloves rather than seeds, because most cultivated garlic no longer produces fertile seeds. Black garlic is made by aging garlic at warm temperatures for weeks."],["Why onions make you cry","Onions absorb sulfur from the soil. When cells are broken, an enzyme turns sulfur compounds into an irritating gas that reaches the eyes, where it triggers tears to wash it away. Chilling onions, using a sharp knife or cutting under a fan reduces tears."],["Folklore and medicine","Garlic was used as medicine in many traditions and was believed to ward off evil spirits and vampires in European folklore. Studies suggest garlic may slightly lower blood pressure and cholesterol, though evidence for many claimed benefits is weak."]],
k:[["Genus","Allium"],["Members","Onions, garlic, leeks, shallots, chives"],["Origin","Central Asia"],["Tear gas","Syn-propanethial-S-oxide"],["Largest garlic producer","China"]],
d:[["c. 3500 BC","Onions grown in Egypt and Mesopotamia"],["c. 1323 BC","Garlic placed in Tutankhamun’s tomb"],["1897","Bram Stoker’s Dracula links garlic and vampires"],["2002","Scientists identify the enzyme that makes onions cause tears"],["2018","‘Tearless’ onions go on sale in the United States"]],
s2:[["The tearless onion","In 2002, Japanese scientists identified the enzyme that makes the tear-causing gas. Breeders later developed onion varieties with less of it, and ‘tearless’ onions went on sale in the United Kingdom and United States in the 2010s and 2020s."],["Onions in history","Ancient Egyptians sometimes swore oaths on onions, whose rings symbolized eternity. In the Middle Ages, onions were used to pay rent and given as gifts. American Civil War general Ulysses S. Grant reportedly demanded onions for his troops to prevent illness."]],
sp:["Garlic breath","Garlic breath lasts because sulfur compounds such as allyl methyl sulfide are absorbed into the blood and released through the lungs and skin for hours. Eating raw apples, lettuce or mint can reduce the smell."],
g:[["Allium","The plant group including onions and garlic"],["Bulb","An underground bud with food-storing layers"],["Clove","One segment of a garlic bulb"],["Enzyme","A protein that speeds up chemical reactions"]],
f:["Onions and garlic are toxic to dogs and cats.","China grows about three-quarters of the world’s garlic.","Garlic was placed in Tutankhamun’s tomb."],
fun:"Chives, leeks and onions all belong to the same genus, and their flowers, which form round purple or white balls, are edible."});

;
/* data/modern32.js */
// New articles, 18 November 2026 (part 2): technology and space.
ADDART({id:"vacuumcleaner",t:"Vacuum Cleaners",c:"Technology",i:"🧹",w:"Vacuum cleaner",r:["washingmachine","motors","robot","elec","smarthome"],
p:[["Sucking up dust","Vacuum cleaners use a fan to create suction that pulls in dirt."],["Horse-drawn beginnings","Early vacuum cleaners were so big they were parked outside houses."],["Robots at home","Robot vacuums now clean floors by themselves."]],
l:"A vacuum cleaner is a machine that removes dust and dirt from floors, carpets and furniture by suction. An electric motor spins a fan that lowers the air pressure inside the machine, so outside air rushes in through a nozzle, carrying dirt with it; the dirt is trapped in a bag, a container or filters, and the cleaned air is blown out. Before vacuum cleaners, people swept with brooms, beat rugs outdoors and used hand-pushed carpet sweepers. In 1901, British engineer Hubert Cecil Booth built a huge gasoline-powered vacuum machine pulled by horses, which was parked outside buildings with long hoses fed through the windows. In 1907, American janitor James Murray Spangler invented a portable electric upright cleaner and sold the patent to the Hoover company, whose name became a word for vacuuming in Britain. Later developments included bagless cyclonic cleaners, popularized by James Dyson in the 1990s, cordless stick vacuums and robot vacuums such as the Roomba, introduced in 2002.",
s:[["How suction works","The fan does not create a true vacuum but a partial one, where air pressure is lower than outside. Brushes in the cleaner head loosen dirt from carpets. Filters, including HEPA filters, which trap at least 99.97 percent of very small particles, help people with allergies."],["Booth’s ‘Puffing Billy’","After seeing a machine that blew dust off train seats, Booth tested sucking dust instead by putting a handkerchief over his mouth and breathing in through it on a chair. His company’s large vacuum vans cleaned Westminster Abbey’s carpets before the coronation of King Edward VII in 1902."],["Cyclonic vacuums","James Dyson built 5,127 prototypes over five years before perfecting his bagless cyclone vacuum, which spins air so fast that dust is flung out into a clear bin. His DC01 went on sale in the United Kingdom in 1993 and became a best-seller."],["Robot vacuums","Robot vacuums navigate rooms using bumpers, infrared sensors, cameras or laser scanners and map homes with software. Many return to a charging dock and empty their own bins. Some people have raised privacy concerns about robot vacuums that send maps and images of homes to company servers."]],
k:[["Works by","Suction from a motor-driven fan"],["First powered vacuum","Hubert Cecil Booth, 1901"],["Portable upright","James Murray Spangler, 1907"],["Bagless cyclonic","Dyson DC01, 1993"],["First popular robot vacuum","Roomba, 2002"]],
d:[["1860","Hand-powered carpet sweeper patented"],["1901","Booth builds a horse-drawn vacuum"],["1907","Spangler invents the portable electric vacuum"],["1908","Hoover begins selling Spangler’s design"],["1993","Dyson DC01 goes on sale"],["2002","Roomba robot vacuum introduced"]],
s2:[["Hoovering","In the United Kingdom and Ireland, ‘hoover’ is used as a verb meaning to vacuum, after the Hoover company, even when the machine is made by another brand. Similar brand names that became words include ‘thermos’ and ‘escalator’."],["Vacuums in space","Astronauts on the International Space Station use small vacuum cleaners to clean filters and collect floating crumbs and dust, which do not settle on surfaces in weightlessness."]],
sp:["Dust and allergies","Household dust contains skin flakes, fibers, pollen, soil and tiny dust mites. Regular vacuuming with a good filter reduces allergens, but poorly sealed cleaners can blow fine particles back into the air."],
g:[["Suction","Pulling by creating lower pressure"],["HEPA filter","A high-efficiency filter for tiny particles"],["Cyclonic","Using spinning air to separate dust"],["Prototype","An early test version of an invention"]],
f:["The first powered vacuum was pulled by horses.","Dyson built 5,127 prototypes.","Robot vacuums map rooms to clean them."],
fun:"Hubert Cecil Booth’s horse-drawn vacuum cleaner was so noisy that he was sued by cab drivers whose horses it frightened."});

ADDART({id:"sonar",t:"Sonar",c:"Technology",i:"🔊",w:"Sonar",r:["radar","sound","submarine","titanic","trench","echolocation"],
p:[["Seeing with sound","Sonar uses sound waves to find objects underwater."],["Born from disaster and war","The Titanic sinking and submarine warfare spurred its invention."],["Mapping the seafloor","Sonar has mapped the ocean floor and found shipwrecks."]],
l:"Sonar, short for ‘sound navigation and ranging’, is a technology that uses sound waves to detect, locate and identify objects underwater. Sound travels much farther through water than light or radio waves, so sonar is the main way of ‘seeing’ in the ocean. Active sonar sends out a pulse of sound, called a ping, and listens for echoes; the time an echo takes to return shows how far away the object is, since sound travels at about 1,500 meters per second in seawater. Passive sonar only listens, detecting sounds made by ships, submarines or animals. Sonar was developed in the early 20th century, spurred by the sinking of the Titanic in 1912 and by submarine warfare in World War I. Today it is used by navies, fishing boats to find schools of fish, scientists to map the seafloor and study marine life, and searchers to locate shipwrecks and lost aircraft. Dolphins, whales and bats use a natural form of sonar called echolocation.",
s:[["How it works","A sonar transducer turns electrical signals into sound pulses and turns returning echoes back into signals. Multibeam sonars send out a fan of hundreds of beams to map wide strips of the seafloor in detail, while side-scan sonar creates picture-like images of objects lying on the bottom."],["History","After the Titanic struck an iceberg, Canadian inventor Reginald Fessenden developed an underwater sound device that detected an iceberg about 3 kilometers away in 1914. During World War I, French physicist Paul Langevin used quartz crystals to produce ultrasonic pulses for detecting submarines. Britain called its system ASDIC, and the American term ‘sonar’ was adopted in the 1940s."],["Mapping the ocean","Echo sounders revealed the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and deep trenches. Marie Tharp used sonar soundings to draw the first maps of the ocean floor in the 1950s, which helped prove plate tectonics. The Seabed 2030 project aims to map the whole seafloor; by 2024, about a quarter had been mapped in detail."],["Sonar and whales","Powerful military sonar has been linked to mass strandings of beaked whales, which may surface too quickly in panic. Navies now try to avoid using strong sonar in sensitive areas, and courts have sometimes limited exercises."]],
k:[["Stands for","Sound navigation and ranging"],["Active sonar","Sends pings and listens for echoes"],["Passive sonar","Only listens"],["Speed of sound in seawater","About 1,500 m/s"],["Natural version","Echolocation"]],
d:[["1912","Titanic sinks, spurring iceberg-detection research"],["1914","Fessenden detects an iceberg with sound"],["1918","Langevin’s ultrasonic submarine detection"],["1950s","Marie Tharp maps the seafloor using sonar data"],["1985","Titanic wreck found using side-scan sonar"],["2017","Seabed 2030 project launched"]],
s2:[["Finding the Titanic","In 1985, a French–American team led by Robert Ballard and Jean-Louis Michel found the Titanic about 3,800 meters deep using side-scan sonar and cameras towed behind a ship. Ballard had been searching partly as cover for a secret US Navy mission to inspect sunken nuclear submarines."],["Fish finders","Many fishing boats and even recreational anglers use small sonars called fish finders, which show fish as marks on a screen. Scientists use similar sonars to estimate fish stocks and track krill."]],
sp:["Echolocation","Dolphins produce rapid clicks and listen for echoes, which let them detect a small ball from about 100 meters away and even sense objects buried in sand. Bats use echolocation in air to catch insects in the dark."],
g:[["Ping","A short pulse of sound sent by sonar"],["Transducer","A device that converts electricity to sound and back"],["Echo","A reflected sound"],["Multibeam","Sonar using many beams at once"]],
f:["Sound travels about 1,500 meters a second in seawater.","Sonar helped find the Titanic.","Only about a quarter of the seafloor is mapped in detail."],
fun:"During World War II, sonar operators were puzzled by a ‘false bottom’ in the ocean that rose at night; it turned out to be huge layers of tiny animals migrating up and down."});

ADDART({id:"trafficlights",t:"Traffic Lights",c:"Technology",i:"🚦",w:"Traffic light",r:["cars","roads","lightbulb","smartcities","leds"],
p:[["Red, yellow, green","Traffic lights control the flow of vehicles and pedestrians at intersections."],["First in London","The first traffic signal, gas-lit, was installed in London in 1868."],["Getting smarter","Modern signals use sensors and computers to adjust timing."]],
l:"Traffic lights, also called traffic signals, are signaling devices at road intersections and crossings that control the flow of vehicles, cyclists and pedestrians using colored lights. The standard system uses red for stop, green for go and yellow or amber for caution. The first traffic signal was installed outside the Houses of Parliament in London in 1868; designed by railway engineer John Peake Knight, it used semaphore arms by day and red and green gas lamps at night, but it exploded in January 1869, injuring the police officer operating it. Electric traffic lights appeared in the United States in the 1910s; Cleveland, Ohio, installed one in 1914, and Detroit introduced the first three-color, four-way signal in 1920. Automatic signals with timers soon replaced police officers. Today, traffic lights use energy-efficient LEDs and are often linked to sensors, cameras and computer systems that adjust timing to traffic. They have helped reduce collisions and organize the growing number of cars in cities.",
s:[["Why red, yellow and green?","Railways used red for danger and, later, green for safe before roads did. Red light is easy to see from a distance. Yellow was added to warn drivers that the signal was about to change. Arrow signals, pedestrian figures and countdown timers were added later."],["Garrett Morgan","Garrett Morgan, an African American inventor and businessman in Cleveland, patented a traffic signal in 1923 with a ‘stop all’ position that halted traffic in all directions so pedestrians could cross. He sold the rights to General Electric."],["Smart signals","Many cities use sensors in the road or cameras to detect waiting vehicles. Coordinated signals create ‘green waves’ along main roads. Some systems give priority to buses, trams and emergency vehicles, and researchers test signals that communicate directly with connected and self-driving cars."],["Rules around the world","In some countries, drivers may turn right (or left, where traffic drives on the left) on red after stopping. In Japan, some green lights look blue, partly because traditional Japanese uses the word ao for both green and blue. Pedestrian signals often beep to help blind people cross."]],
k:[["Colors","Red (stop), yellow (caution), green (go)"],["First signal","London, 1868"],["First electric signal","Cleveland, 1914"],["First three-color four-way signal","Detroit, 1920"],["Modern lights","LEDs and sensors"]],
d:[["1868","First traffic signal installed in London"],["1869","London signal explodes"],["1914","Electric traffic light installed in Cleveland"],["1920","Detroit introduces a three-color, four-way signal"],["1923","Garrett Morgan patents a traffic signal"],["1990s","LED traffic lights come into use"]],
s2:[["The green man","Pedestrian signals with walking and standing figures spread from the mid-20th century. East Germany’s Ampelmännchen, a little man in a hat designed in 1961, became so loved that he survived German reunification and is now a popular symbol of Berlin."],["Roundabouts instead","Many traffic engineers prefer roundabouts to traffic lights at some intersections because they slow traffic and reduce serious crashes. Some towns have removed traffic lights entirely in ‘shared space’ experiments."]],
sp:["The exploding signal","London’s first traffic signal was operated by a police officer who turned a lever. Less than a month after installation, a gas leak caused the lamp to explode, and the officer was badly burned. Traffic signals did not return to London until 1926."],
g:[["Intersection","A place where roads cross"],["Semaphore","Signaling with movable arms"],["Green wave","A series of lights timed to turn green in sequence"],["Sensor","A device that detects something"]],
f:["The first traffic signal was in London in 1868.","London’s first signal exploded.","Some green lights in Japan look blue."],
fun:"There are no traffic lights in the whole country of Bhutan; police officers direct traffic at busy junctions in the capital, Thimphu."});

ADDART({id:"vrar",t:"Virtual and Augmented Reality",c:"Technology",i:"🥽",w:"Virtual reality",r:["videogames","smartphone","compvision","wearables","ai"],
p:[["Digital worlds","Virtual reality places users in a computer-generated world."],["Digital layers","Augmented reality adds digital images to the real world."],["Many uses","VR and AR are used in games, training, medicine and design."]],
l:"Virtual reality (VR) is technology that immerses users in a computer-generated environment, usually through a headset that covers the eyes, shows separate images to each eye to create depth and tracks head movements so the view changes as the user looks around. Augmented reality (AR) overlays digital images and information onto the real world, seen through a phone screen, glasses or a headset; mixed reality blends the two. Early experiments included Morton Heilig’s Sensorama booth of 1962 and Ivan Sutherland’s 1968 head-mounted display, nicknamed the ‘Sword of Damocles’ because it hung from the ceiling. VR became affordable for consumers with headsets such as the Oculus Rift in 2016, and the game Pokémon Go brought AR to hundreds of millions of phone users the same year. Today VR and AR are used for gaming, flight and surgical training, architecture, education and remote work. Challenges include motion sickness, high costs, privacy and the effects of long use on health.",
s:[["How VR works","A VR headset contains small screens and lenses that fill the user’s field of view. Sensors and cameras track the position and rotation of the head and hands many times a second. If the image lags behind head movements, users can feel sick, so headsets need fast displays and processing."],["How AR works","AR devices use cameras and sensors to understand the surroundings, detecting surfaces and objects so digital images appear fixed in place. Phone apps let people preview furniture in their homes or translate signs. Head-up displays in aircraft and some cars show information on the windshield."],["Uses","Pilots, surgeons, soldiers and factory workers train with VR simulations. Architects walk through buildings before they are built. Therapists use VR to treat phobias and post-traumatic stress, and museums recreate historical sites. VR is also used to help people manage pain."],["Concerns","Many people experience motion sickness or eye strain in VR. Headsets collect detailed data about movements, eyes and surroundings, raising privacy questions. Manufacturers recommend that young children not use headsets, and researchers study how long use affects vision and wellbeing."]],
k:[["VR","Immersion in a digital world"],["AR","Digital images added to the real world"],["Early headset","Sutherland’s ‘Sword of Damocles’, 1968"],["Consumer VR boom","2016 (Oculus Rift, HTC Vive)"],["AR hit","Pokémon Go, 2016"]],
d:[["1838","Charles Wheatstone invents the stereoscope"],["1962","Sensorama booth patented"],["1968","Ivan Sutherland’s head-mounted display"],["2016","Oculus Rift and Pokémon Go released"],["2019","Oculus Quest standalone headset released"],["2024","Apple Vision Pro released"]],
s2:[["The stereoscope","In 1838, Charles Wheatstone showed that two slightly different pictures viewed one by each eye create an illusion of depth. Stereoscopes and later View-Master toys used this idea, which is the basis of VR headsets."],["The metaverse","In the early 2020s, some technology companies promoted the idea of a ‘metaverse’, a shared virtual world for work and play. Facebook renamed itself Meta in 2021 to focus on it, though public interest grew more slowly than predicted."]],
sp:["Pokémon Go","Released in July 2016, Pokémon Go let players catch virtual creatures that appeared on their phone screens in real places. It was downloaded more than 500 million times in its first year and sent crowds into parks and streets, along with some accidents."],
g:[["Immersion","Feeling surrounded by an environment"],["Headset","A device worn over the eyes"],["Motion sickness","Nausea caused by mismatched senses"],["Mixed reality","Blending virtual and real objects"]],
f:["The first VR headset hung from the ceiling.","Pokémon Go brought AR to millions in 2016.","VR is used to train surgeons and pilots."],
fun:"The stereoscope, invented in 1838, worked on the same principle as modern VR headsets, showing a slightly different picture to each eye."});

ADDART({id:"passwords",t:"Passwords and Passkeys",c:"Technology",i:"🔑",w:"Password",r:["cybersecurity","crypto","hacking","dataprivacy","locks","webcookies","twofactor"],
p:[["Secret words","Passwords prove who you are when you log in."],["Easy to steal","Weak and reused passwords are a major cause of hacking."],["Beyond passwords","Passkeys and two-factor authentication make accounts safer."]],
l:"A password is a secret string of characters used to prove a person’s identity when logging in to a computer, website or device. Passwords have ancient roots in the watchwords used by soldiers, but computer passwords began in 1961 with the Compatible Time-Sharing System at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, which let many users share one computer. Today people may have dozens or hundreds of online accounts, and many choose weak passwords, such as ‘123456’, or reuse the same password on several sites, so that one data breach exposes many accounts. Criminals use guessing programs, stolen password lists and phishing messages to steal them. Security experts recommend long, unique passwords stored in a password manager, along with two-factor authentication, which requires a second proof such as a code from a phone. Passkeys, introduced widely from 2022, replace passwords with cryptographic keys stored on a device and unlocked by a fingerprint, face or PIN, and are much harder to steal.",
s:[["How passwords are stored","Well-designed systems do not store passwords directly. Instead they store a ‘hash’, the result of a one-way mathematical function, so even if the database is stolen, the passwords cannot easily be read. Adding random data called salt makes hashes harder to crack."],["Strong passwords","Length matters more than complexity: a passphrase of several random words is easier to remember and harder to guess than a short password with symbols. Guidance from the US National Institute of Standards and Technology no longer recommends forcing people to change passwords regularly unless they may be compromised."],["Two-factor authentication","Two-factor authentication combines something you know, such as a password, with something you have, such as a phone or security key, or something you are, such as a fingerprint. Codes sent by text message are better than nothing but can be intercepted; authenticator apps and hardware keys are safer."],["Passkeys","Passkeys use public-key cryptography: a website stores a public key, while the private key stays on the user’s device. Because nothing secret is typed or sent, passkeys cannot be phished or leaked in a server breach. Apple, Google and Microsoft announced support for passkeys in 2022."]],
k:[["First computer passwords","MIT, 1961"],["Most common password","‘123456’"],["Safer storage","Hashing with salt"],["Extra protection","Two-factor authentication"],["Password replacement","Passkeys (from 2022)"]],
d:[["1961","MIT’s time-sharing system introduces computer passwords"],["1962","First known password theft, at MIT"],["1970s","Password hashing comes into use"],["2004","Bill Gates predicts the death of the password"],["2013","Yahoo breach exposes data of 3 billion accounts"],["2022","Tech companies commit to passkeys"]],
s2:[["The first password theft","In 1962, MIT researcher Allan Scherr printed out the file of all passwords on the time-sharing system so he could get more computer time than his limit allowed, and shared it with others. It is often called the first computer password theft."],["Password managers","Password managers generate and store strong, unique passwords for each account, protected by one master password. They make good security easier, though users must protect the master password and turn on two-factor authentication."]],
sp:["World Password Day","World Password Day, held on the first Thursday of May since 2013, reminds people to check and strengthen their passwords and turn on extra security."],
g:[["Hash","The result of a one-way mathematical scramble"],["Phishing","Tricking people into revealing secrets"],["Two-factor authentication","Logging in with two kinds of proof"],["Passkey","A login method using cryptographic keys"]],
f:["Computer passwords began at MIT in 1961.","Length matters more than complexity in passwords.","Passkeys cannot be phished."],
fun:"A study of leaked passwords repeatedly finds ‘123456’ and ‘password’ among the most common, used by millions of people."});

ADDART({id:"headphones",t:"Headphones and Loudspeakers",c:"Technology",i:"🎧",w:"Headphones",r:["sound","ear","radio","streaming","smartphone"],
p:[["Turning signals into sound","Speakers and headphones turn electrical signals into sound waves."],["Moving coils","Most use a coil and magnet to vibrate a cone or diaphragm."],["Music on the move","The Walkman and wireless earbuds changed how people listen."]],
l:"Loudspeakers and headphones are devices that convert electrical audio signals into sound. In the most common design, the moving-coil driver, an electrical signal flows through a coil of wire attached to a cone or diaphragm and placed near a magnet; the changing magnetic force pushes the coil back and forth, making the cone vibrate and push the air into sound waves. Early telephones of the 1870s used simple speakers in their earpieces, and in 1910 American inventor Nathaniel Baldwin made headphones in his kitchen that he sold to the US Navy. The moving-coil loudspeaker, invented by Chester Rice and Edward Kellogg in 1925, made radios, record players and cinemas sound fuller. Sony’s Walkman cassette player, launched in 1979, made personal listening with headphones a global habit, and wireless earbuds such as Apple’s AirPods in 2016 spread rapidly. Noise-canceling headphones use microphones and opposing sound waves to quiet background noise. Listening at high volume for long periods can permanently damage hearing.",
s:[["Types of speakers","Large woofers reproduce low bass sounds, and small tweeters reproduce high sounds; many speakers combine several drivers. Electrostatic and planar magnetic speakers use thin films instead of cones. Sound systems in cinemas and concerts use many speakers to fill large spaces."],["Kinds of headphones","Over-ear headphones cover the ears, on-ear headphones rest on them, and earbuds or in-ear monitors fit in the ear canal. Bone-conduction headphones send vibrations through the cheekbones, leaving the ears open to hear traffic."],["Noise canceling","Active noise-canceling headphones use microphones to pick up outside noise and produce a mirror-image sound wave that cancels it out, a process called destructive interference. Bose developed noise-canceling headsets for pilots in the 1980s, and they became popular with travelers."],["Protecting hearing","The World Health Organization estimates that more than a billion young people are at risk of hearing loss from unsafe listening. Experts suggest the ‘60/60 rule’: no more than 60 percent volume for 60 minutes at a time. Many phones now warn users about loud listening."]],
k:[["Main design","Moving-coil driver"],["Early headphones","Nathaniel Baldwin, 1910"],["Moving-coil loudspeaker","Rice and Kellogg, 1925"],["Personal stereo","Sony Walkman, 1979"],["Wireless earbuds boom","From 2016"]],
d:[["1876","Telephone patented, with a speaker in the earpiece"],["1910","Baldwin makes headphones in his kitchen"],["1925","Moving-coil loudspeaker invented"],["1958","First stereo headphones sold"],["1979","Sony Walkman launched"],["2016","Apple AirPods released"]],
s2:[["The Walkman","Sony’s Walkman TPS-L2 went on sale on 1 July 1979. Engineers built it partly so company co-founder Masaru Ibuka could listen to opera on long flights. More than 200 million Walkmans were sold, and it made headphones a fashion item."],["Bluetooth","Most wireless headphones use Bluetooth, a short-range radio technology introduced in 1999 and named after Harald Bluetooth, a 10th-century Danish king who united tribes, just as the technology unites devices."]],
sp:["Kitchen headphones","Nathaniel Baldwin built his first headphones at his kitchen table in Utah. When he offered them to the US Navy, it was so impressed that it ordered a hundred sets, and he began manufacturing them in his kitchen."],
g:[["Driver","The part of a speaker that makes sound"],["Diaphragm","A thin vibrating sheet"],["Destructive interference","When waves cancel each other out"],["Bluetooth","A short-range wireless technology"]],
f:["The Walkman went on sale in 1979.","Noise-canceling uses opposing sound waves.","Bluetooth is named after a Danish king."],
fun:"A loudspeaker can work as a microphone in reverse: speak into a speaker, and it will produce a tiny electrical signal."});

ADDART({id:"reusablerockets",t:"Reusable Rockets",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🚀",w:"Reusable launch vehicle",r:["rocket","commspace","spaceports","starship","sat"],
p:[["Flying again and again","Reusable rockets land after launch so they can fly again."],["Cutting costs","Reuse has made launching satellites far cheaper."],["Falcon 9 records","One Falcon 9 booster has flown 37 times."]],
l:"A reusable rocket is a launch vehicle, or part of one, that is recovered after flight and flown again, instead of being discarded. For most of the space age, rockets were used only once, like throwing away an airliner after every trip, which made spaceflight extremely expensive. The Space Shuttle, first launched in 1981, was partly reusable, but refurbishing it between flights was slow and costly. SpaceX made reuse routine: on 21 December 2015, a Falcon 9 first stage landed upright back at Cape Canaveral, and in March 2017 a booster flew for a second time. Falcon 9 boosters now land on ships at sea or landing pads, and in August 2026 one booster made its 37th flight. Reuse, together with mass production, has greatly reduced launch costs and helped make large satellite constellations possible. Blue Origin landed its New Shepard booster in 2015 and its large New Glenn booster in 2025, and SpaceX catches its giant Starship boosters with tower arms. Chinese companies are also developing reusable rockets.",
s:[["How landing works","After separating from the upper stage, a reusable booster flips around, fires some engines to slow down and steer, deploys grid fins to guide its fall through the atmosphere, and makes a final landing burn, touching down on legs. Computers make thousands of adjustments to land precisely."],["The Space Shuttle","The Space Shuttle orbiter returned to Earth like a glider, and its solid rocket boosters were recovered from the ocean, but its large external tank was discarded. Each orbiter needed months of inspection and repair between flights, and two orbiters were lost, in 1986 and 2003."],["Falcon 9","Since its first landing, the Falcon 9 first stage has landed hundreds of times. SpaceX also recovers the fairings that protect payloads, catching them from the sea. Reused boosters have carried astronauts to the International Space Station."],["Catching rockets","SpaceX’s Starship launch towers have mechanical ‘chopstick’ arms that catch descending Super Heavy boosters, first achieved on 13 October 2024. In November 2026, the tower caught a Starship upper stage returning from orbit for the first time, a step toward fully reusable rockets."]],
k:[["Partly reusable pioneer","Space Shuttle (1981)"],["First orbital booster landing","Falcon 9, 21 December 2015"],["First reflight","Falcon 9, March 2017"],["Most flights by one booster","37 (August 2026)"],["First tower catch","Super Heavy, 13 October 2024"]],
d:[["1981","Space Shuttle makes its first flight"],["1993","DC-X test rocket makes vertical landings"],["2015","New Shepard and Falcon 9 boosters land"],["2017","First reflight of an orbital booster"],["2024","First booster caught by tower arms"],["2025","New Glenn booster lands at sea"],["2026","Starship upper stage caught after orbital flight"]],
s2:[["Why reuse saves money","The first stage is the largest and most expensive part of a rocket. Reusing it means the main costs per launch are fuel, refurbishment and the upper stage. Launch prices per kilogram to orbit fell sharply in the 2010s and 2020s."],["Concerns","Cheap, frequent launches have filled low Earth orbit with satellites, raising concerns about space debris, astronomy and pollution from rocket exhaust in the upper atmosphere. Scientists are studying these effects."]],
sp:["Grid fins","Grid fins are lattice-shaped panels near the top of a Falcon 9 booster that unfold during descent. Like the steering fins of a dart, they help guide the booster through the air toward its landing site."],
g:[["Booster","The first stage of a rocket"],["Landing burn","Firing engines to slow down before touchdown"],["Fairing","A nose cone protecting a payload"],["Refurbishment","Repairing and preparing for reuse"]],
f:["Falcon 9 boosters land upright.","One booster has flown 37 times.","Starship boosters are caught by tower arms."],
fun:"SpaceX’s drone ships at sea are named after spaceships from Iain M. Banks’s science fiction novels, such as ‘Of Course I Still Love You’."});

ADDART({id:"spacelaw",t:"Space Law",c:"Space Exploration",i:"⚖️",w:"Space law",r:["moonbases","un","spaceagencies","spacejunk","artemis"],
p:[["Rules for space","Space law is the body of agreements that governs activities in outer space."],["The Outer Space Treaty","The 1967 treaty says no country can own the Moon or other celestial bodies."],["New questions","Mining, megaconstellations and Moon bases raise new legal debates."]],
l:"Space law is the set of international treaties, national laws and guidelines that govern human activities in outer space. Its foundation is the Outer Space Treaty, which opened for signature on 27 January 1967 and has been joined by more than 110 countries, including all major spacefaring nations. It declares that space is free for exploration and use by all countries, that no nation can claim sovereignty over the Moon or other celestial bodies, that nuclear weapons and other weapons of mass destruction may not be placed in orbit or on celestial bodies, and that countries are responsible for activities by their governments and companies, and liable for damage caused by their space objects. Further United Nations treaties cover rescuing astronauts (1968), liability for damage (1972), registering space objects (1975) and the Moon (1979), though the Moon Agreement has few members. The United Nations Committee on the Peaceful Uses of Outer Space, set up in 1959, discusses new rules. Recent issues include mining resources from the Moon and asteroids, space debris, crowded orbits and military activities in space.",
s:[["The Outer Space Treaty","Negotiated during the Cold War between the United States, the Soviet Union and others, the treaty aimed to keep the space race peaceful. Astronauts are described as ‘envoys of mankind’, and countries must help astronauts in distress. The treaty entered into force on 10 October 1967."],["Liability","Under the 1972 Liability Convention, a launching country must pay for damage its space objects cause on Earth or to aircraft. It has been formally used only once: after the Soviet nuclear-powered satellite Cosmos 954 crashed in northern Canada in 1978, spreading radioactive debris, the Soviet Union paid Canada 3 million Canadian dollars."],["Space resources","The Outer Space Treaty bans claiming territory but does not clearly say whether companies may own resources they extract. The United States passed a law in 2015 allowing citizens to own space resources, followed by Luxembourg, the United Arab Emirates and Japan. Critics argue that such laws could amount to claiming parts of space."],["The Artemis Accords","Launched by the United States in October 2020, the Artemis Accords are non-binding principles for exploring the Moon and beyond, covering transparency, sharing scientific data, protecting historic sites such as Apollo landing sites, and ‘safety zones’ around operations. More than 75 countries had signed by 2026. China and Russia have not joined and are developing their own lunar partnership."]],
k:[["Main treaty","Outer Space Treaty (1967)"],["Key principle","No national ownership of celestial bodies"],["UN committee","COPUOS (1959)"],["Only liability claim","Cosmos 954, Canada (1978)"],["Artemis Accords","Launched 2020; 75+ signatories"]],
d:[["1959","UN Committee on the Peaceful Uses of Outer Space set up"],["1967","Outer Space Treaty enters into force"],["1972","Liability Convention adopted"],["1978","Cosmos 954 crashes in Canada"],["1979","Moon Agreement adopted"],["2015","US law allows ownership of space resources"],["2020","Artemis Accords launched"]],
s2:[["Who owns the Moon?","No one: the Outer Space Treaty forbids national claims. Over the years, some people have claimed to sell plots of land on the Moon, but these sales have no legal value. Footprints, flags and equipment left by the Apollo missions remain the property of the United States."],["Weapons in space","The treaty bans weapons of mass destruction in orbit but not conventional weapons or satellites with military uses. Anti-satellite missile tests, which create debris, have led many countries to pledge not to conduct them, and the UN has debated new rules to prevent an arms race in space."]],
sp:["The Bogotá Declaration","In 1976, eight equatorial countries, including Colombia, Ecuador and Indonesia, signed the Bogotá Declaration claiming the parts of geostationary orbit above their territories. Other countries rejected the claim as conflicting with the Outer Space Treaty."],
g:[["Treaty","A formal agreement between countries"],["Sovereignty","Supreme authority over a territory"],["Liability","Legal responsibility for damage"],["Celestial body","A natural object in space, such as a planet or moon"]],
f:["No country can own the Moon.","The Liability Convention has been used only once.","More than 75 countries have signed the Artemis Accords."],
fun:"The Outer Space Treaty calls astronauts ‘envoys of mankind’, and countries must return astronauts who land on their territory by accident."});

ADDART({id:"sunspots",t:"Sunspots and the Solar Cycle",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🌞",w:"Sunspot",r:["sun","spaceweather","aurora","galileo","scope"],
p:[["Dark spots on the Sun","Sunspots are cooler, darker areas caused by strong magnetic fields."],["An 11-year cycle","The number of sunspots rises and falls about every 11 years."],["Solar storms","Active periods bring more solar flares, auroras and space weather."]],
l:"Sunspots are temporary dark patches on the Sun’s surface, the photosphere, where intense magnetic fields slow the flow of heat from inside, making them about 1,500°C cooler than their surroundings. They still glow brightly but look dark by contrast. Sunspots range from small specks to groups many times wider than Earth and last from hours to months. Their number rises and falls in a solar cycle lasting about 11 years, from solar minimum, when there may be no spots for weeks, to solar maximum, with many spots, powerful solar flares and coronal mass ejections. These eruptions can cause geomagnetic storms that create bright auroras but can also disrupt satellites, radio communications, GPS and power grids. Sunspots were observed with the naked eye in ancient China and studied with telescopes by Galileo and others from 1610. The current cycle, Solar Cycle 25, reached its maximum in October 2024. Viewing the Sun directly is dangerous; sunspots should only be observed with proper solar filters or projection.",
s:[["What causes sunspots","The Sun’s gas is electrically charged and rotates faster at the equator than near the poles, twisting its magnetic field. Bundles of magnetic field rise to the surface, where they block heat and form sunspots, usually in pairs of opposite magnetic polarity. A sunspot has a dark center called the umbra and a lighter outer region called the penumbra."],["The solar cycle","German astronomer Samuel Heinrich Schwabe discovered the sunspot cycle in 1843 after counting spots for 17 years. The cycles are numbered from 1755. Every 11 years the Sun’s magnetic poles flip, so the full magnetic cycle lasts about 22 years. Solar Cycle 25 peaked with a smoothed sunspot number of about 161."],["The Maunder Minimum","Between about 1645 and 1715, very few sunspots were seen, a period called the Maunder Minimum. It overlapped with the coldest part of the Little Ice Age in Europe, though scientists think volcanic eruptions and other factors also played a large role in the cold."],["Solar storms","On 1 September 1859, astronomer Richard Carrington saw a brilliant solar flare. The following geomagnetic storm, the Carrington Event, produced auroras as far south as the Caribbean and sparked fires in telegraph offices. In May 2024, the strongest storm in two decades brought auroras to much of the world."]],
k:[["Temperature","About 1,500°C cooler than surroundings"],["Cycle length","About 11 years"],["Cycle discovered","Samuel Heinrich Schwabe, 1843"],["Solar Cycle 25 maximum","October 2024"],["Quiet period","Maunder Minimum (1645–1715)"]],
d:[["28 BC","Chinese astronomers record sunspots"],["1610–1613","Galileo and others observe sunspots by telescope"],["1645–1715","Maunder Minimum"],["1843","Schwabe discovers the sunspot cycle"],["1859","Carrington Event"],["2024","Solar Cycle 25 reaches maximum"]],
s2:[["Sunspots and climate","The Sun is slightly brighter at solar maximum, because bright regions called faculae outweigh the dark spots, but the change is only about 0.1 percent. Scientists have found that the solar cycle does not explain the recent warming of Earth’s climate."],["Predicting the cycle","Scientists predicted that Solar Cycle 25 would be weak, peaking at about 115, but it turned out much stronger. Forecasting the strength of cycles remains difficult, though magnetic fields at the Sun’s poles during minimum give clues."]],
sp:["Watching safely","Never look at the Sun directly or through binoculars or a telescope without a certified solar filter, which can cause permanent eye damage. Projecting the Sun’s image through a telescope or pinhole onto white card is a safe way to see sunspots."],
g:[["Photosphere","The visible surface of the Sun"],["Solar flare","A sudden burst of energy from the Sun"],["Coronal mass ejection","A huge cloud of solar material thrown into space"],["Umbra","The dark center of a sunspot"]],
f:["Sunspots are cooler than the rest of the Sun’s surface.","The sunspot cycle lasts about 11 years.","Solar Cycle 25 peaked in October 2024."],
fun:"A large sunspot group can be many times wider than Earth, and the biggest can be seen without a telescope at sunset through haze, with proper eye protection."});

ADDART({id:"impactcraters",t:"Impact Craters",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🕳️",w:"Impact crater",r:["meteors","moon","planetdefense","dino","comet"],
p:[["Scars from space","Impact craters form when asteroids or comets hit a planet or moon."],["Earth’s hidden craters","About 200 impact craters are confirmed on Earth."],["Shaping history","The Chicxulub impact helped end the age of dinosaurs."]],
l:"An impact crater is a roughly circular depression formed when a meteoroid, asteroid or comet strikes the surface of a planet, moon or other solid body at high speed. Because impactors travel at tens of kilometers per second, they release enormous energy, vaporizing rock and blasting out a crater typically about 10 to 20 times wider than the impactor itself. Small craters are bowl-shaped, while large ones have central peaks or rings. Craters cover the Moon, Mercury and many moons, which have little or no air, water or geological activity to erase them. On Earth, erosion, plate tectonics and plants hide most craters, but about 200 have been confirmed. The largest known, Vredefort in South Africa, formed about 2 billion years ago and was originally perhaps 160 to 300 kilometers wide. The Chicxulub crater in Mexico, about 180 kilometers wide, formed 66 million years ago by the impact that helped wipe out the dinosaurs. Arizona’s Meteor Crater, about 1.2 kilometers wide, is one of the best preserved.",
s:[["How craters form","On impact, a shock wave compresses and melts rock and the impactor, then rebounds, throwing material outward as ejecta that forms a blanket and rays around the crater. In large impacts the crater floor springs back upward, forming a central peak. Bright rays from the Moon’s crater Tycho stretch for more than 1,000 kilometers."],["Recognizing impact craters","Geologists identify ancient impact sites by features that only extreme shock pressures create, such as shocked quartz, shatter cones and high-pressure minerals like coesite. In 1960, Eugene Shoemaker found coesite at Meteor Crater, proving that it was made by an impact rather than a volcano."],["Famous craters","Meteor Crater in Arizona formed about 50,000 years ago. Popigai in Siberia contains impact diamonds. In 2018, scientists announced a 31-kilometer crater hidden beneath the Greenland ice sheet, named Hiawatha. Manicouagan in Canada forms a ring-shaped lake visible from space."],["Craters elsewhere","Counting craters lets scientists estimate the ages of surfaces on other worlds: heavily cratered areas are old, smooth ones young. The Moon’s dark maria are huge ancient impact basins later flooded with lava. Valhalla on Jupiter’s moon Callisto is a multi-ring basin about 3,800 kilometers across."]],
k:[["Confirmed on Earth","About 200"],["Largest on Earth","Vredefort, South Africa (about 2 billion years old)"],["Dinosaur-era impact","Chicxulub, Mexico (66 million years ago)"],["Best preserved","Meteor Crater, Arizona"],["Proof of impact","Shocked quartz, shatter cones, coesite"]],
d:[["c. 2 billion years ago","Vredefort impact in South Africa"],["66 million years ago","Chicxulub impact"],["c. 50,000 years ago","Meteor Crater forms in Arizona"],["1960","Shoemaker proves Meteor Crater was made by an impact"],["1991","Chicxulub identified as the dinosaur-killing crater"],["2018","Hiawatha crater found under Greenland’s ice"]],
s2:[["Chicxulub","In 1980, Luis and Walter Alvarez proposed that an asteroid impact caused the extinction of the dinosaurs, based on a layer rich in the rare metal iridium. In 1991, scientists linked the idea to a buried crater at Chicxulub on Mexico’s Yucatán Peninsula, first noticed by oil company geophysicists."],["Crater lakes and resources","Many impact craters hold lakes, such as Pingualuit in Canada. Some craters contain valuable resources: the Sudbury basin in Canada is a major source of nickel, and oil and gas are found in buried craters."]],
sp:["Eugene Shoemaker","Geologist Eugene Shoemaker founded the science of astrogeology and helped train Apollo astronauts. He hoped to go to the Moon himself; after his death in 1997, some of his ashes were carried to the Moon aboard the Lunar Prospector spacecraft, making him the only person buried there."],
g:[["Impactor","An object that hits another"],["Ejecta","Material thrown out of a crater"],["Shatter cone","A cone-shaped rock fracture made by shock waves"],["Maria","Dark plains on the Moon"]],
f:["About 200 impact craters are confirmed on Earth.","Craters are usually 10–20 times wider than the impactor.","Eugene Shoemaker is the only person with ashes on the Moon."],
fun:"Meteor Crater in Arizona was used to train Apollo astronauts in geology before they went to the Moon."});

ADDART({id:"starship",t:"SpaceX Starship",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🚀",w:"SpaceX Starship",r:["reusablerockets","rocket","artemis","mars","spaceports"],
p:[["The biggest rocket","Starship is the largest and most powerful rocket ever flown."],["Fully reusable design","Both stages are designed to be caught and flown again."],["Moon and Mars","NASA chose a version of Starship to land astronauts on the Moon."]],
l:"Starship is a super heavy-lift launch system developed by the American company SpaceX, designed to be fully reusable. It consists of two stages: the Super Heavy booster, powered by 33 Raptor engines burning liquid methane and liquid oxygen, and the Starship spacecraft, or Ship, on top, which has its own six engines. Stacked together, the system stands about 124 meters tall, making it the largest and most powerful rocket ever flown. SpaceX began full test flights from its Starbase site in southern Texas in April 2023; early flights ended in explosions, but the company improved the design with each test. On 13 October 2024, the launch tower’s mechanical arms caught a returning Super Heavy booster for the first time. The Ship reached orbit for the first time on 28 September 2026, and on 14 November 2026 the tower caught a Ship returning from orbit. NASA selected a Starship lunar lander for its Artemis program, and SpaceX hopes Starship will eventually carry people to Mars.",
s:[["Design","Starship is built mainly of stainless steel, which is cheaper than carbon composites and handles both very cold fuel and the heat of reentry. Thousands of hexagonal heat-shield tiles protect the Ship as it reenters the atmosphere belly-first, then flips upright for landing."],["Testing by flying","SpaceX uses an approach of rapid testing, building and flying prototypes quickly and learning from failures. Several early test flights exploded or broke apart, and debris from some flights fell near the Caribbean, leading to investigations by US aviation authorities."],["Moon lander","In 2021, NASA chose a version of Starship as the Human Landing System for Artemis. To reach the Moon, it must be refueled in Earth orbit by several tanker flights, a technique that has not yet been demonstrated. NASA plans a 2027 test in Earth orbit before a crewed lunar landing."],["Satellites and Mars","Starship is designed to carry more than 100 metric tons to orbit and is used to launch larger Starlink satellites. SpaceX founder Elon Musk has said he wants Starship to build a city on Mars, though no firm schedule exists for crewed Mars missions."]],
k:[["Builder","SpaceX"],["Height","About 124 m"],["Booster engines","33 Raptor engines"],["Fuel","Liquid methane and liquid oxygen"],["First Ship catch","14 November 2026"]],
d:[["2019","Starhopper prototype makes test hops"],["2021","NASA selects Starship as a lunar lander"],["2023","First full Starship test flight"],["2024","First Super Heavy booster caught by tower arms"],["2026","Ship reaches orbit (28 September)"],["2026","Ship caught by tower after orbital flight (14 November)"]],
s2:[["Why methane?","Raptor engines burn methane rather than kerosene because it burns cleaner, leaving fewer deposits in reused engines, and because methane could in principle be made on Mars from carbon dioxide and water ice, providing fuel for return journeys."],["Environmental concerns","Starship launches from Starbase near wildlife refuges on the Texas coast. Environmental groups have raised concerns about noise, debris and road closures, while supporters point to jobs and technological progress."]],
sp:["Chopsticks","SpaceX’s launch towers, nicknamed ‘Mechazilla’, have two huge arms called chopsticks. They swing together to grab a descending booster or ship by small pins, avoiding the weight of landing legs and allowing quick reuse."],
g:[["Super heavy-lift","Able to carry very heavy loads to orbit"],["Stage","A section of a rocket with its own engines"],["Raptor","SpaceX’s methane-fueled rocket engine"],["Refueling in orbit","Transferring fuel between spacecraft in space"]],
f:["Starship is about 124 meters tall.","Its booster has 33 engines.","A tower first caught a returning Ship in November 2026."],
fun:"Starship’s Super Heavy booster produces about twice the thrust of the Saturn V rocket that carried astronauts to the Moon."});

ADDART({id:"megaconstellations",t:"Satellite Megaconstellations",c:"Space Exploration",i:"📡",w:"Satellite constellation",r:["sat","internet","spacejunk","lightpollution","reusablerockets","satellites"],
p:[["Swarms of satellites","Megaconstellations are networks of thousands of satellites."],["Internet from space","Starlink and others provide internet almost anywhere."],["Concerns","They raise worries about debris, astronomy and control of space."]],
l:"A satellite megaconstellation is a network of hundreds or thousands of satellites in low Earth orbit working together, mainly to provide broadband internet around the world. Because they orbit only a few hundred kilometers up, much closer than traditional communications satellites in geostationary orbit about 36,000 kilometers away, they can deliver fast connections with little delay, but many satellites are needed to cover the whole planet. SpaceX’s Starlink, which began launching in 2019, is by far the largest, with more than 10,000 satellites in orbit by 2026, serving millions of users, including ships, aircraft, remote communities and disaster areas; it also became important in the war in Ukraine. Other systems include Eutelsat OneWeb, Amazon’s Project Kuiper and Chinese constellations such as Qianfan and Guowang. Megaconstellations have helped connect remote areas, but astronomers warn that bright satellite trails interfere with telescope images, and experts worry about collisions, space debris and the power of a few companies over global communications.",
s:[["How they work","Users connect with a small dish antenna that tracks satellites as they pass overhead. Satellites relay data to ground stations connected to the internet, and newer satellites pass data between each other using lasers. Each satellite lasts about five years before being lowered to burn up in the atmosphere."],["Starlink","SpaceX launched its first batch of 60 Starlink satellites in May 2019, using its reusable Falcon 9 rockets, and passed 10,000 satellites in orbit in March 2026. Starlink now provides service in more than 100 countries, and some newer satellites can connect directly to ordinary mobile phones."],["Effects on astronomy","Sunlight reflecting off satellites creates streaks across telescope images, especially near dawn and dusk. The Vera C. Rubin Observatory, which began its survey in 2025, is expected to find satellite trails in many images. Companies have darkened satellites, and astronomers develop software to remove streaks, but radio interference is also a concern."],["Debris and traffic","Thousands of satellites in similar orbits must constantly maneuver to avoid collisions. Starlink satellites performed tens of thousands of avoidance maneuvers in recent years. Scientists also study whether metals and other materials from satellites burning up affect the upper atmosphere."]],
k:[["Orbit","Low Earth orbit, a few hundred km up"],["Largest","SpaceX Starlink"],["Starlink first launch","May 2019"],["Starlink satellites in orbit","More than 10,000 (2026)"],["Others","OneWeb, Kuiper, Qianfan, Guowang"]],
d:[["1998","Iridium phone satellite network begins service"],["2019","First 60 Starlink satellites launched"],["2020","Eutelsat OneWeb resumes launches after bankruptcy"],["2022","Starlink terminals sent to Ukraine"],["2025","Amazon launches first Kuiper production satellites"],["2026","Starlink passes 10,000 satellites in orbit"]],
s2:[["Iridium","The Iridium network, completed in 1998, was an early constellation of 66 satellites for satellite phones. The company went bankrupt in 1999 but was revived, and its sun-reflecting antennas once produced brief bright ‘Iridium flares’ in the night sky."],["Satellites and war","After Russia’s invasion of Ukraine in 2022, Starlink terminals helped Ukraine keep communications working. The role of a private company in a war raised debates about who controls important infrastructure in space."]],
sp:["Satellite trains","Shortly after launch, Starlink satellites can be seen as a line of bright moving dots, like a ‘train’, crossing the night sky before they spread out and rise to their working orbits. Many people have mistaken them for UFOs."],
g:[["Constellation (satellites)","A group of satellites working together"],["Low Earth orbit","Orbits up to about 2,000 km above Earth"],["Latency","Delay in sending data"],["Ground station","A station on Earth that communicates with satellites"]],
f:["Starlink has more than 10,000 satellites in orbit.","Megaconstellation satellites burn up after about five years.","Satellite streaks affect telescope images."],
fun:"Some Starlink satellites have been fitted with dark visors or special mirror coatings to make them less bright in the night sky."});

ADDART({id:"spacetourism",t:"Space Tourism",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🧑‍🚀",w:"Space tourism",r:["commspace","iss","edgeofspace","reusablerockets","tourism"],
p:[["Paying to fly to space","Space tourism lets private people travel to space for fun."],["Suborbital and orbital","Some trips last minutes; others spend days in orbit."],["Expensive and uncertain","Tickets cost hundreds of thousands to millions of dollars."]],
l:"Space tourism is spaceflight by private individuals for recreation or adventure rather than as professional astronauts. The first space tourist, American businessman Dennis Tito, paid about 20 million dollars to fly to the International Space Station on a Russian Soyuz in April 2001, and several others followed. Suborbital trips, which go just above the boundary of space for a few minutes of weightlessness and views of Earth before returning, began in 2021, when Richard Branson flew on Virgin Galactic’s rocket plane on 11 July and Jeff Bezos on Blue Origin’s New Shepard on 20 July. In September 2021, SpaceX’s Inspiration4 carried the first all-civilian crew into orbit for three days, and in 2024 the private Polaris Dawn mission included the first commercial spacewalk. However, demand and schedules have proved uncertain: Blue Origin paused New Shepard flights in January 2026, and Virgin Galactic has not flown customers since 2024 while it develops new spaceplanes. Critics question the environmental impact and value of space trips for the very rich.",
s:[["Suborbital flights","New Shepard is a reusable rocket and capsule that launches vertically from Texas, carrying six passengers above 100 kilometers, the Kármán line, before parachuting back. Virgin Galactic’s spaceplanes are carried by a mothership aircraft and then fire a rocket to climb to about 85 kilometers, which the United States counts as space."],["Orbital trips","Orbital space tourism costs tens of millions of dollars per seat. Private astronauts have flown to the ISS on Russian Soyuz and SpaceX Crew Dragon spacecraft, including missions organized by the company Axiom Space. Japanese billionaire Yusaku Maezawa spent 12 days on the ISS in December 2021."],["Who goes?","Most space tourists have been wealthy businesspeople, but some flights carried guests chosen through competitions or charities, such as a physician assistant, a teacher and an 82-year-old aviation pioneer, Wally Funk, who had trained as an astronaut in the 1960s. William Shatner, who played Captain Kirk in Star Trek, flew in 2021 at age 90."],["Debates","Supporters say space tourism helps fund reusable rockets and inspires interest in science. Critics point to emissions from rocket launches, safety risks and the cost of flights for a tiny number of people. In 2014, a Virgin Galactic test flight crashed, killing one pilot."]],
k:[["First space tourist","Dennis Tito, 2001"],["First suborbital tourist flights","July 2021"],["First all-civilian orbital crew","Inspiration4, 2021"],["First commercial spacewalk","Polaris Dawn, 2024"],["New Shepard status","Paused since January 2026"]],
d:[["2001","Dennis Tito visits the ISS"],["2004","SpaceShipOne wins the Ansari X Prize"],["2014","Virgin Galactic test flight crash"],["2021","Branson and Bezos fly to space; Inspiration4 orbits Earth"],["2024","Polaris Dawn includes a commercial spacewalk"],["2026","Blue Origin pauses New Shepard flights"]],
s2:[["The X Prize","In 2004, SpaceShipOne, designed by Burt Rutan and funded by Paul Allen, became the first private crewed spacecraft to reach space twice within two weeks, winning the 10 million dollar Ansari X Prize. Its technology led to Virgin Galactic’s spaceplanes."],["Space hotels","Several companies have proposed private space stations and hotels, and NASA is funding commercial stations to replace the ISS, which is planned to be retired around 2030. None had hosted tourists as of 2026."]],
sp:["The overview effect","Many people who see Earth from space report a powerful change in perspective, sensing the planet’s fragility and unity. Writer Frank White called this the ‘overview effect’ in 1987."],
g:[["Suborbital","Reaching space but not completing an orbit"],["Kármán line","A boundary of space at 100 km altitude"],["Weightlessness","The feeling of floating in free fall"],["Private astronaut","A non-professional traveler to space"]],
f:["The first space tourist flew in 2001.","Branson and Bezos flew to space in July 2021.","William Shatner flew to space at age 90."],
fun:"Dennis Tito, the first space tourist, has booked a seat on a future SpaceX flight around the Moon."});

ADDART({id:"moonbases",t:"Moon Bases",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🌕",w:"Moonbase",r:["moon","artemis","chandrayaan","starship","apollo","spacemining"],
p:[["Living on the Moon","Space agencies plan permanent bases on the Moon."],["Ice at the poles","Water ice near the lunar south pole could supply water, air and fuel."],["A new race","The United States and China both aim to land astronauts and build bases."]],
l:"A Moon base is a planned permanent or long-term human outpost on the Moon. No humans have been to the Moon since Apollo 17 in 1972, but the United States, China and other countries now plan to return astronauts and eventually build bases. Interest focuses on the lunar south pole, where craters in permanent shadow contain water ice, first confirmed by spacecraft in 2008 and 2009. Ice could be turned into drinking water, breathable oxygen and hydrogen rocket fuel, making long stays and travel deeper into space easier. NASA’s Artemis program aims to land astronauts near the south pole, with a landing currently targeted for 2028 using a commercial lander, followed by a ‘base camp’. China plans to land astronauts before 2030 and, with Russia and other partners, to build an International Lunar Research Station in the 2030s. Challenges include radiation, extreme temperatures, moonquakes, sharp lunar dust and the cost of supplying a base from Earth.",
s:[["Why the south pole?","Near the lunar poles, the Sun stays low on the horizon. Some crater floors never see sunlight and stay colder than −200°C, trapping ice for billions of years, while nearby ridges are lit for most of the year, ideal for solar power. India’s Chandrayaan-3 became the first spacecraft to land near the south pole in August 2023."],["Building on the Moon","Bases could be partly buried under lunar soil, called regolith, to shield astronauts from radiation and micrometeorites. Engineers are testing ways to 3D-print structures from regolith and to extract oxygen from lunar rocks. Lava tubes, natural tunnels left by ancient lava flows, could also provide shelter."],["Plans","NASA’s plans include Artemis landings with SpaceX’s Starship or Blue Origin’s Blue Moon landers, pressurized rovers and surface habitats. China plans a crewed landing with its Mengzhou spacecraft and Lanyue lander before 2030. Both countries have discussed small nuclear reactors to provide power during the two-week lunar night."],["Challenges","Lunar dust is fine and jagged, sticks to everything and can damage equipment and lungs. Temperatures swing from about 120°C in daylight to −130°C at night. Without a magnetic field or atmosphere, astronauts would face radiation from the Sun and deep space, and resupplying a base from Earth is very costly."]],
k:[["Last crewed landing","Apollo 17, 1972"],["Target region","Lunar south pole"],["Key resource","Water ice"],["NASA crewed landing target","2028 (Artemis)"],["China’s target","Before 2030"]],
d:[["1972","Apollo 17, the last crewed Moon landing"],["2008","Chandrayaan-1 detects signs of water on the Moon"],["2009","LCROSS impact confirms ice in a polar crater"],["2017","Artemis program begins"],["2021","China and Russia announce the International Lunar Research Station"],["2023","Chandrayaan-3 lands near the south pole"]],
s2:[["The Outer Space Treaty","The Outer Space Treaty of 1967 says that no country can claim the Moon. The US-led Artemis Accords, signed by dozens of countries since 2020, set principles for using lunar resources, while China and Russia have their own partnership. Experts debate how resources and ‘safety zones’ around bases should be managed."],["Moon bases in fiction","Moon bases have long appeared in science fiction, from H. G. Wells’s The First Men in the Moon (1901) to the 1970s television series Space: 1999 and the film Moon (2009)."]],
sp:["LCROSS","In October 2009, NASA deliberately crashed a spent rocket stage into Cabeus crater near the lunar south pole. The LCROSS spacecraft flew through the plume of debris and detected water, confirming ice in permanently shadowed craters."],
g:[["Regolith","Loose soil and rock on the Moon’s surface"],["Permanently shadowed crater","A crater floor that never receives sunlight"],["Lava tube","A natural tunnel formed by flowing lava"],["Lunar night","About two weeks of darkness on the Moon"]],
f:["No one has walked on the Moon since 1972.","Ice near the lunar south pole could make rocket fuel.","Chandrayaan-3 landed near the south pole in 2023."],
fun:"A day-night cycle on the Moon lasts about 29.5 Earth days, so a lunar base would have about two weeks of sunlight followed by two weeks of darkness."});

;
/* data/modern33.js */
// New articles, 18 November 2026 (part 3): animals and plants.
ADDART({id:"moose",t:"Moose",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🫎",w:"Moose",r:["deer","reindeer","wolf","conservation","swedencountry"],
p:[["The biggest deer","The moose is the largest member of the deer family."],["Northern forests","Moose live in the cold forests of North America, Europe and Asia."],["Called elk in Europe","In Britain and Europe, the same animal is usually called an elk."]],
l:"The moose is the largest living member of the deer family, found in the boreal and mixed forests of northern North America, Scandinavia, the Baltic states, Russia and northern China. A large bull can stand more than 2 meters tall at the shoulder and weigh up to about 700 kilograms. Males grow huge, flat, palm-shaped antlers that can span about 1.8 meters, which they shed each winter and regrow in spring. Moose have long legs for wading through snow and deep water, a drooping nose and a flap of skin called a bell hanging from the throat. They eat leaves, twigs, bark and water plants, which they reach by diving several meters underwater. Moose usually live alone, except for mothers with calves. In British English the animal is called an elk, while in North America ‘elk’ means a different deer, the wapiti. Moose are important to Indigenous peoples and hunters, but collisions with cars are dangerous, and warming winters have increased deadly tick infestations.",
s:[["Body","A moose’s long legs let it step over fallen logs and move through snow up to about a meter deep. Its hollow hairs insulate it from the cold, but moose overheat easily above about 14°C in winter and 20°C in summer, so they rest in shade or water on warm days."],["Antlers","Only males grow antlers. They grow at up to about 2.5 centimeters a day in summer, covered in soft velvet, and are used in autumn to impress females and fight rivals. A large set can weigh about 30 kilograms."],["Moose and water","Moose are strong swimmers and can cross wide lakes and rivers. They feed on water lilies and other aquatic plants rich in sodium and can stay underwater for about a minute, closing their nostrils."],["Threats","Winter ticks, which survive better in milder winters, can number tens of thousands on a single moose, causing many calves to die in parts of New England. Brain worms carried by white-tailed deer, wolves and bears also affect moose populations."]],
k:[["Family","Deer"],["Height","Up to about 2.1 m at the shoulder"],["Weight","Up to about 700 kg"],["Antler span","Up to about 1.8 m"],["European name","Elk"]],
d:[["1600s","English settlers adopt the Algonquian word ‘moose’"],["1900s","Moose reintroduced to parts of the northeastern United States"],["1997","Swedish ‘moose test’ tips over a new car"],["2010s","Winter ticks cause heavy moose losses in New England"]],
s2:[["The moose test","Sweden tests how cars handle sudden swerves to avoid a moose on the road, called the älgtest. In 1997, a new small car rolled over during the test, forcing its maker to add stability systems. Moose collisions are a major road danger in Scandinavia and Canada."],["Moose and wolves","On Isle Royale in Lake Superior, scientists have studied moose and wolves together since 1958, one of the longest predator–prey studies in the world. When wolf numbers fell, moose multiplied and overgrazed the forest, until new wolves were brought in from 2018."]],
sp:["The word moose","The English word ‘moose’ comes from an Algonquian language word, often translated as ‘twig eater’ or ‘he strips off bark’, referring to how moose feed in winter."],
g:[["Boreal forest","The cold northern forest of conifers"],["Antler","A bony growth shed and regrown each year"],["Velvet","Soft skin covering growing antlers"],["Bell","A flap of skin under a moose’s throat"]],
f:["The moose is the largest deer.","Moose can dive underwater to feed.","In Britain the moose is called an elk."],
fun:"A moose’s antlers are among the fastest-growing tissues of any animal, adding up to about 2.5 centimeters a day."});

ADDART({id:"jaguars",t:"Jaguars",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐆",w:"Jaguar",r:["leopards","tiger","amazon","maya","conservation"],
p:[["Big cat of the Americas","The jaguar is the largest cat in the Americas."],["Powerful bite","Jaguars kill prey by biting through skulls and turtle shells."],["Loves water","Unlike most cats, jaguars swim well and hunt in rivers."]],
l:"The jaguar is a large wild cat native to the Americas, from the southwestern United States and Mexico through Central America to northern Argentina, with its stronghold in the Amazon rainforest and the Pantanal wetlands of Brazil. It is the third-largest cat in the world, after the tiger and lion, and the largest in the Americas, weighing up to about 150 kilograms. Jaguars have a golden coat covered in rosettes, rings with spots inside, which distinguish them from leopards; some are almost black. They are solitary, ambush predators with an exceptionally strong bite for their size, often killing prey by biting directly through the skull, and can crack turtle shells. Jaguars eat more than 80 kinds of prey, including capybaras, deer, peccaries, caimans and fish, and are excellent swimmers. They were sacred to the Olmec, Maya and Aztec peoples. Jaguars are listed as near threatened because of deforestation, conflict with ranchers and illegal trade in their body parts; scientists estimated about 173,000 jaguars remained in 2016.",
s:[["Jaguar or leopard?","Jaguars and leopards look similar, but jaguars are stockier, with bigger heads and shorter tails, and their rosettes usually have spots in the middle. Leopards live in Africa and Asia, so a spotted big cat in the wild in the Americas is a jaguar."],["Hunting","Jaguars hunt mostly at dawn and dusk, stalking and ambushing prey. In the Pantanal they regularly catch caimans in rivers, diving after them. Their name may come from a Tupi–Guarani word, yaguara, often said to mean ‘one who kills with one leap’."],["Jaguars and people","The Olmec carved were-jaguar figures, Maya kings wore jaguar skins and took names such as Jaguar Paw, and Aztec elite warriors were called jaguar warriors. The jaguar remains a national symbol in Brazil, Mexico and other countries."],["Conservation","Jaguars have lost about half of their historic range. Wildlife corridors, such as the Jaguar Corridor Initiative, aim to connect populations from Mexico to Argentina. A few jaguars have been seen in Arizona since the 1990s, crossing from Mexico."]],
k:[["Range","Americas, from Mexico to Argentina"],["Rank","Third-largest cat"],["Weight","Up to about 150 kg"],["Markings","Rosettes with central spots"],["Status","Near threatened"]],
d:[["c. 1200 BC","Olmec culture reveres the jaguar"],["1758","Linnaeus names the jaguar Felis onca"],["1960s–1970s","Hundreds of thousands killed for the fur trade"],["1975","International trade in jaguar skins banned (CITES)"],["2016","Scientists estimate about 173,000 jaguars"]],
s2:[["The fur trade","In the 1960s and early 1970s, tens of thousands of jaguar skins were exported each year for fashion. A ban on international trade under CITES in 1975 helped stop the slaughter, but jaguar teeth and body parts are still trafficked."],["Black jaguars","Black jaguars have a gene that produces extra dark pigment, but their rosettes are still faintly visible. They are sometimes called black panthers, a name also used for black leopards."]],
sp:["The Pantanal","The Pantanal, the world’s largest tropical wetland, has one of the highest densities of jaguars. Jaguar-watching tourism there has become worth more to local communities than cattle ranching in some areas."],
g:[["Rosette","A rose-shaped spot pattern"],["Ambush predator","An animal that hides and attacks suddenly"],["Wildlife corridor","Habitat that connects separated populations"],["CITES","An international treaty controlling wildlife trade"]],
f:["The jaguar is the largest cat in the Americas.","Jaguars can bite through turtle shells.","Jaguars are strong swimmers."],
fun:"A jaguar’s bite is strong enough that it often kills prey with a single bite to the back of the skull, something few other big cats do."});

ADDART({id:"squirrels",t:"Squirrels",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐿️",w:"Squirrel",r:["rodents","trees","oaks","invasiveplants","animalbehavior"],
p:[["Busy rodents","Squirrels are a family of rodents that includes tree squirrels, chipmunks and marmots."],["Planting forests","Forgotten nuts buried by squirrels grow into new trees."],["Gliders","Flying squirrels glide between trees using skin flaps."]],
l:"Squirrels are a family of about 280 species of rodents found on every continent except Australia and Antarctica. The family includes tree squirrels with bushy tails, ground squirrels, chipmunks, prairie dogs and marmots, and flying squirrels that glide between trees. Squirrels range in size from the African pygmy squirrel, about 10 centimeters long, to the Alpine marmot and Indian giant squirrel, which can be about a meter long including the tail. Most eat nuts, seeds, fruit and fungi, and some eat insects, eggs and small animals. Many squirrels store food for winter: some hide thousands of nuts in separate places and find them later using memory and smell, but forgotten nuts often sprout, helping forests spread. Squirrels have excellent vision and agility and can rotate their back feet to climb headfirst down trees. In Britain, introduced North American gray squirrels have largely replaced native red squirrels.",
s:[["Caching food","Eastern gray squirrels bury nuts one at a time in scattered places, called scatter-hoarding, and can remember the locations of many of them. Studies suggest that squirrels may organize their caches by type of nut. Red squirrels in North America instead store cones in large central piles called middens."],["Flying squirrels","Flying squirrels have a furry membrane, the patagium, stretching between their front and back legs. By spreading it, they can glide up to about 90 meters between trees, steering with their tails. In 2019, scientists found that some flying squirrels glow pink under ultraviolet light."],["Red versus gray","Gray squirrels were introduced to Britain from North America from 1876. They are larger, better at digesting acorns and carry a squirrelpox virus that kills red squirrels. Red squirrels now survive mainly in Scotland, islands and protected forests, helped by the return of pine martens, which prey more on grays."],["Ground squirrels","Prairie dogs live in huge underground ‘towns’ with complex warning calls. Marmots hibernate for up to eight months. The groundhog, a marmot, is the focus of Groundhog Day in the United States, celebrated each 2 February since 1887 in Punxsutawney, Pennsylvania."]],
k:[["Family","Sciuridae"],["Species","About 280"],["Absent from","Australia and Antarctica"],["Smallest","African pygmy squirrel"],["Gliding distance","Up to about 90 m"]],
d:[["1876","Gray squirrels introduced to Britain"],["1887","First Groundhog Day in Punxsutawney"],["1930s","Gray squirrels declared pests in Britain"],["2019","Flying squirrels found to glow pink under ultraviolet light"]],
s2:[["Squirrel-proof feeders","Squirrels are famous for raiding bird feeders, solving obstacles and leaping long distances. In a 2021 study, squirrels performed parkour-like leaps, adjusting their jumps based on how bendy branches were."],["Squirrels and trees","By burying acorns and other seeds far from the parent tree, squirrels help oaks, walnuts and pines spread. Scientists estimate that a single gray squirrel may bury thousands of nuts each autumn."]],
sp:["Indian giant squirrel","The Indian giant squirrel, or Malabar giant squirrel, has a brilliantly colored coat of maroon, orange, cream and black and can leap about 6 meters between trees in the forests of India."],
g:[["Rodent","A mammal with constantly growing front teeth"],["Cache","A hidden store of food"],["Patagium","The skin membrane used by gliding animals"],["Hibernate","To spend winter in a deep, sleep-like state"]],
f:["Forgotten squirrel nuts can grow into trees.","Flying squirrels glide, not fly.","Squirrels can climb down trees headfirst."],
fun:"A squirrel’s front teeth never stop growing, about 15 centimeters a year, so it must keep gnawing to wear them down."});

ADDART({id:"tapirs",t:"Tapirs",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐗",w:"Tapir",r:["rhino","horses","amazon","conservation","malaysiacountry"],
p:[["Living fossils","Tapirs have changed little in tens of millions of years."],["A short trunk","Their flexible snout works like a small trunk."],["Gardeners of the forest","Tapirs spread seeds across forests."]],
l:"Tapirs are large, plant-eating mammals with stout bodies, short legs and a flexible, trunk-like snout. They are related to horses and rhinoceroses. There are four widely recognized living species: three in Central and South America, the Brazilian or lowland tapir, the mountain tapir and Baird’s tapir, and one in Southeast Asia, the black-and-white Malayan tapir. Tapirs live in forests, grasslands and mountain areas near water and are good swimmers, often escaping predators by diving into rivers. They eat leaves, fruit and water plants and help forests by spreading seeds in their droppings over long distances. Tapirs have existed in similar form for about 20 million years or more. Baby tapirs are born with striped and spotted coats, like watermelons, that provide camouflage. All four species are threatened by habitat loss and hunting, with the mountain, Baird’s and Malayan tapirs listed as endangered.",
s:[["The snout","A tapir’s snout, or proboscis, is formed from the nose and upper lip. It can stretch, grab leaves and fruit, and act as a snorkel when swimming. Tapirs have an excellent sense of smell but poor eyesight."],["Seed dispersers","Lowland tapirs swallow large fruits and travel long distances, dropping seeds far from parent trees. Scientists call them ‘gardeners of the forest’, and studies in Brazil found that tapirs help regrow damaged rainforest."],["The Malayan tapir","The Malayan tapir’s black front and back and white middle break up its outline in moonlit forests, making it hard for tigers to see. It lives in Myanmar, Thailand, Malaysia and Sumatra and is endangered by deforestation for palm oil plantations."],["Ancient animals","Tapir-like animals once lived across North America, Europe and Asia. Fossils show their basic body plan has changed little for millions of years, earning them the label ‘living fossils’."]],
k:[["Relatives","Horses and rhinoceroses"],["Living species","Four widely recognized"],["Range","Central and South America, Southeast Asia"],["Special feature","Trunk-like snout"],["Baby coat","Stripes and spots"]],
d:[["c. 20 million years ago","Tapirs of modern form appear"],["1819","Malayan tapir described by Western scientists"],["1865","Baird’s tapir named"],["1981","Baird’s tapir becomes the national animal of Belize"],["2008","International Tapir Day first held (27 April)"]],
s2:[["Mountain tapir","The mountain tapir of the Andes has thick, woolly fur to survive cold cloud forests and páramo grasslands at up to about 4,500 meters. Fewer than 2,500 adults are thought to remain."],["The dream eater","In Japanese folklore, the baku is a creature that eats nightmares. The modern Japanese word for tapir is also baku, because the mythical creature was often drawn with a tapir-like trunk."]],
sp:["Watermelon babies","Tapir calves are born with reddish-brown coats covered in white stripes and spots, which camouflage them in dappled forest light. The pattern fades by about six months of age."],
g:[["Proboscis","A long, flexible snout"],["Seed dispersal","Spreading seeds to new places"],["Living fossil","A species little changed over millions of years"],["Páramo","High, cold grasslands of the Andes"]],
f:["Tapirs are related to horses and rhinos.","Baby tapirs look like watermelons.","Tapirs use their snouts as snorkels."],
fun:"The Malayan tapir’s black-and-white pattern makes it look like a black animal wearing a white blanket."});

ADDART({id:"iguanas",t:"Iguanas",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦎",w:"Iguana",r:["lizard","galapagos","invasiveplants","darwin","pets"],
p:[["Big plant-eating lizards","Iguanas are large lizards of the Americas and Caribbean."],["Sea-going iguanas","Galápagos marine iguanas are the only lizards that feed in the sea."],["A third eye","Iguanas have a light-sensing ‘third eye’ on top of the head."]],
l:"Iguanas are large lizards of tropical Central and South America and the Caribbean. The best known is the green iguana, which can grow to about 1.5 to 2 meters long including its tail and lives in trees near water. Iguanas have a row of spines down the back, a flap of skin called a dewlap under the throat, and sharp claws for climbing. Adults eat mainly leaves, flowers and fruit. Like many lizards, they have a pale scale on top of the head called the parietal eye, which senses light and shadow and may help them detect birds of prey. The Galápagos Islands are home to marine iguanas, the only lizards that forage in the sea, diving to eat algae, and to land iguanas. Rock iguanas of the Caribbean are among the most endangered lizards in the world. Green iguanas are popular pets and have become invasive in Florida, Puerto Rico and elsewhere, where cold snaps sometimes leave stunned iguanas falling from trees.",
s:[["Green iguanas","Green iguanas bask in the sun to warm up and can drop from branches into water from heights of 12 meters or more without injury. They are excellent swimmers and can stay underwater for nearly half an hour. Males defend territories by bobbing their heads and extending their dewlaps."],["Marine iguanas","Charles Darwin called marine iguanas ‘imps of darkness’. They dive in cold Galápagos waters to graze algae from rocks, then warm up on black lava. They sneeze out excess salt through glands near their nostrils, and during El Niño food shortages, they can shrink in body length to survive."],["Rock iguanas","Caribbean rock iguanas, such as the Grand Cayman blue iguana, were driven near extinction by cats, dogs, habitat loss and road traffic. Captive breeding raised the blue iguana from about 25 wild animals in 2002 to more than 1,000."],["Falling iguanas","Iguanas are cold-blooded and become sluggish when temperatures drop below about 10°C. During cold snaps in South Florida, such as in January 2020 and February 2025, weather forecasters warned residents of iguanas falling from trees."]],
k:[["Best-known species","Green iguana"],["Length","Up to about 2 m"],["Diet","Mainly leaves, flowers and fruit"],["Sea-feeding species","Galápagos marine iguana"],["Invasive in","Florida, Puerto Rico and elsewhere"]],
d:[["1835","Darwin studies marine iguanas in the Galápagos"],["1960s","Green iguanas established in Florida"],["2002","Only about 25 wild blue iguanas remain"],["2012","Blue iguana downgraded from critically endangered"],["2020","Cold snap brings ‘falling iguana’ warnings in Florida"]],
s2:[["Iguanas as food","In parts of Central America, iguana meat and eggs are traditionally eaten and nicknamed ‘chicken of the trees’. Iguana farming has been promoted to reduce hunting of wild animals."],["Pink iguana","A pink land iguana was discovered on Wolf Volcano in the Galápagos in 1986 and recognized as a separate species in 2009. Only a few hundred survive, all on that one volcano."]],
sp:["The third eye","The parietal eye has a simple lens and retina but cannot form images. It detects changes in light and may help iguanas sense predators from above and regulate daily and seasonal rhythms."],
g:[["Dewlap","A flap of skin under the throat"],["Parietal eye","A light-sensing organ on top of the head"],["Cold-blooded","Relying on outside heat for body temperature"],["Bask","To lie in the sun to warm up"]],
f:["Marine iguanas are the only sea-feeding lizards.","Iguanas have a light-sensing third eye.","Cold iguanas can fall from trees."],
fun:"Marine iguanas can shrink their skeletons by up to about 20 percent when food is scarce, then grow back when times improve."});

ADDART({id:"pelicans",t:"Pelicans",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦤",w:"Pelican",r:["birds","puffins","fish","pollution","albatross"],
p:[["Built-in fishing nets","Pelicans scoop fish with huge stretchy throat pouches."],["Diving and herding","Brown pelicans plunge-dive, while white pelicans fish in teams."],["A comeback","Brown pelicans recovered after the pesticide DDT was banned."]],
l:"Pelicans are large water birds with long bills and enormous, stretchy throat pouches that they use to catch fish. There are eight species, living on every continent except Antarctica, along coasts, lakes and rivers. The Dalmatian pelican, one of the largest flying birds, can have a wingspan of more than 3 meters. A pelican’s pouch can hold about 11 liters of water; after scooping up fish, the bird tilts its head to drain the water before swallowing. The brown and Peruvian pelicans plunge-dive from the air into the sea, while other species, such as the American white pelican, swim in groups to herd fish into shallow water. Pelicans are strong fliers that soar on rising air and often fly in lines or V-shaped formations. In the mid-20th century, the pesticide DDT made pelican eggshells thin and fragile, and brown pelicans nearly vanished from parts of the United States; after DDT was banned in 1972, they recovered and were removed from the US endangered species list in 2009.",
s:[["The pouch","The pouch is made of stretchy skin hanging from the lower bill. Besides catching fish, pelicans flutter it to cool down on hot days. Pelicans do not store fish in their pouches; they swallow them quickly or carry them in the throat to their chicks."],["Fishing styles","Brown pelicans dive from heights of up to about 20 meters, folding their wings back just before hitting the water. Air sacs under their skin cushion the impact and help them bob back up. Great white pelicans in Africa fish cooperatively, forming a semicircle and beating their wings to drive fish toward the shore."],["Breeding","Pelicans nest in colonies on islands, in trees or on the ground. Parents feed chicks partly digested fish, and older chicks reach deep into their parents’ pouches and throats. Some colonies contain thousands of birds."],["Threats","Besides past pesticide poisoning, pelicans are harmed by oil spills, fishing lines and hooks, and disturbance of nesting sites. The 2010 Deepwater Horizon oil spill in the Gulf of Mexico coated many brown pelicans in oil. In 2022, bird flu killed many Dalmatian pelicans in Greece."]],
k:[["Species","Eight"],["Pouch capacity","About 11 liters"],["Largest","Dalmatian pelican, wingspan over 3 m"],["Diving species","Brown and Peruvian pelicans"],["Recovery","Brown pelican delisted in the US, 2009"]],
d:[["1903","Pelican Island becomes the first US national wildlife refuge"],["1970","Brown pelican listed as endangered in the US"],["1972","DDT banned in the United States"],["2009","Brown pelican removed from the US endangered list"],["2010","Deepwater Horizon spill harms pelicans"],["2022","Bird flu kills many Dalmatian pelicans in Greece"]],
s2:[["Pelican Island","In 1903, US President Theodore Roosevelt made Pelican Island in Florida the country’s first national wildlife refuge, to protect pelicans and other birds from hunters seeking feathers for fashionable hats."],["The pelican in Christianity","A medieval legend said that a mother pelican fed her chicks with her own blood by piercing her breast. The ‘pelican in her piety’ became a Christian symbol of sacrifice and appears in church art and heraldry."]],
sp:["Prehistoric pelicans","A 30-million-year-old fossil from France shows that pelicans with almost the same bill and pouch existed long ago, suggesting the design has changed little since then."],
g:[["Throat pouch","A stretchy skin bag under the bill"],["Plunge-dive","Diving into water from flight"],["DDT","A pesticide that thinned bird eggshells"],["Colony","A group of birds nesting together"]],
f:["A pelican’s pouch holds about 11 liters of water.","Brown pelicans recovered after DDT was banned.","Some pelicans fish in cooperative teams."],
fun:"Pelicans sometimes try to swallow surprising things, and one in a London park was filmed swallowing a pigeon whole, though it later let it go."});

ADDART({id:"toucans",t:"Toucans",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐦",w:"Toucan",r:["birds","amazon","parrot","hornbill","seeds"],
p:[["Giant colorful bills","Toucans are tropical birds with huge, bright bills."],["Light but large","The bill is light because it is mostly hollow inside."],["Natural radiators","Toucans use their bills to release heat."]],
l:"Toucans are birds of the rainforests and woodlands of Central and South America, famous for their enormous, brightly colored bills. There are about 40 species, including smaller toucanets and aracaris. The largest, the toco toucan, has an orange bill about 20 centimeters long, about a third of its total length. Despite its size, a toucan’s bill is light: it is made of keratin, the material of fingernails, over a honeycomb of bone and air spaces. Toucans use their bills to reach and peel fruit at the ends of branches, and also eat insects, eggs and small animals. In 2009, scientists found that toucans control blood flow to their bills to release body heat, using the bill like a radiator. Toucans nest in tree holes, often old woodpecker holes, and sleep with their bills tucked over their backs. They help forests by spreading seeds. Some species are threatened by deforestation and capture for the pet trade.",
s:[["The bill","Scientists have long debated why toucan bills are so large. The bill may help attract mates, scare other birds away from fruit trees and reach food. Its serrated edges help grip fruit, and a long, narrow tongue helps move food to the throat."],["Keeping cool","Using infrared cameras, researchers in Brazil showed that toco toucans can quickly increase blood flow to the bill to shed heat when warm and reduce it to save heat when they sleep. The bill can release up to about 60 percent of body heat."],["Social life","Toucans live in pairs or small flocks and call loudly with croaks and yelps. They toss fruit to each other and fence playfully with their bills, behavior that may strengthen social bonds."],["Toucans and forests","Because toucans swallow fruits whole and later regurgitate or pass the seeds, they carry seeds of many trees across the forest. When large toucans disappear, trees with large seeds may struggle to spread."]],
k:[["Range","Central and South America"],["Species","About 40"],["Largest","Toco toucan"],["Bill made of","Keratin over bony honeycomb"],["Bill use","Feeding, display and cooling"]],
d:[["1550s","Toucans described in Europe after Spanish and Portuguese voyages"],["1758","Linnaeus names the toco toucan"],["2009","Study shows toucans use bills to shed heat"],["2015","Toucan with broken bill gets a 3D-printed replacement in Costa Rica"]],
s2:[["Grecia’s new beak","In 2015, a chestnut-mandibled toucan in Costa Rica named Grecia lost half of its upper bill after being attacked. Volunteers designed a 3D-printed prosthetic bill, which helped the bird eat and became famous worldwide."],["Toucans and hornbills","Hornbills of Africa and Asia look similar to toucans, with large bills, but they are not closely related. Their resemblance is an example of convergent evolution, in which unrelated animals evolve similar features."]],
sp:["Name","The word toucan comes from the Tupi language of Brazil, possibly imitating the bird’s call."],
g:[["Keratin","The tough protein in hair, nails and bills"],["Regurgitate","To bring swallowed food back up"],["Convergent evolution","Unrelated species evolving similar features"],["Aracari","A smaller kind of toucan"]],
f:["A toucan’s bill is mostly hollow.","Toucans use their bills to cool down.","Toucans and hornbills are not close relatives."],
fun:"When sleeping, a toucan turns its head and lays its bill along its back, then folds its tail forward to cover it, becoming a feathery ball."});

ADDART({id:"tardigrades",t:"Tardigrades",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐻",w:"Tardigrade",r:["microscopes","extremeheat","astrobio","ferns","animalbehavior"],
p:[["Water bears","Tardigrades are tiny eight-legged animals nicknamed water bears."],["Almost indestructible","They survive drying out, freezing, radiation and even the vacuum of space."],["Found everywhere","Tardigrades live from mountaintops to the deep sea."]],
l:"Tardigrades, also called water bears or moss piglets, are microscopic animals, usually less than a millimeter long, with plump bodies and eight stubby legs ending in claws. About 1,300 species have been described, living in moss, lichen, soil, leaf litter, fresh water and the ocean, on every continent including Antarctica. They eat plant cells, algae, bacteria and sometimes other tiny animals, piercing them with needle-like mouthparts. Tardigrades are famous for surviving extreme conditions by entering a state called cryptobiosis: they dry out, curl into a barrel shape called a tun and almost stop their metabolism. In this state they have survived temperatures close to absolute zero and up to about 150°C for a short time, pressures greater than in the deepest ocean, high radiation and, in a 2007 experiment, exposure to the vacuum of space. They revive when water returns. Scientists study tardigrade proteins, such as Dsup, for ways to protect cells and preserve medicines.",
s:[["Discovery","German pastor Johann August Ephraim Goeze first described tardigrades in 1773, calling them ‘little water bears’. Three years later, Italian biologist Lazzaro Spallanzani named them tardigrada, meaning ‘slow steppers’."],["Cryptobiosis","As a tardigrade dries out, it replaces water in its cells with protective sugars and special proteins that form glass-like structures, protecting its molecules. It can stay in this state for years, perhaps decades, and revive within hours when wet."],["Tardigrades in space","In 2007, the European FOTON-M3 mission exposed dried tardigrades to space for 10 days. Many survived the vacuum, and some survived the Sun’s ultraviolet radiation too. In 2019, the Israeli Beresheet lander crashed on the Moon carrying dehydrated tardigrades, though scientists believe they could not survive there long."],["Dsup","In 2016, Japanese scientists found a protein in one tardigrade species, which they named Dsup, for ‘damage suppressor’, that protects DNA from radiation. Human cells given the protein in the laboratory suffered less damage from X-rays."]],
k:[["Nicknames","Water bears, moss piglets"],["Size","Usually under 1 mm"],["Species","About 1,300"],["Survival state","Cryptobiosis (tun)"],["Space test","FOTON-M3, 2007"]],
d:[["1773","Goeze describes ‘little water bears’"],["1776","Spallanzani names them tardigrades"],["2007","Tardigrades survive exposure to space"],["2016","Dsup protein discovered"],["2019","Tardigrades crash on the Moon aboard Beresheet"]],
s2:[["Not quite indestructible","Tardigrades are tough only when dried out. Active tardigrades are much more fragile, and a 2020 study found that they could not survive long in water warmer than about 37°C. Climate change could therefore affect them."],["How to find one","Tardigrades are easy to find: soak a clump of moss in water overnight, then examine drops of the water under a simple microscope at about 30 to 40 times magnification. They often look like slow, plump bears waddling through the water."]],
sp:["Eight legs","Tardigrades have four pairs of legs, each ending in claws or sticky discs. They walk with a gait similar to insects, despite their very different body plan and tiny brains."],
g:[["Microscopic","Too small to see without a microscope"],["Cryptobiosis","A state in which life processes almost stop"],["Tun","The curled, dried state of a tardigrade"],["Metabolism","The chemical processes of life"]],
f:["Tardigrades have survived the vacuum of space.","Tardigrades have eight legs.","Dried tardigrades can revive when wet."],
fun:"Tardigrades crash-landed on the Moon in 2019, making them some of the first animals to reach its surface, though they almost certainly did not survive."});

ADDART({id:"chickens",t:"Chickens",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐔",w:"Chicken",r:["birds","domestication","agri","dino","pets"],
p:[["The most common bird","There are more chickens than any other bird on Earth."],["From the jungle","Chickens descend from the red junglefowl of Southeast Asia."],["Eggs and meat","Chickens are the world’s main source of eggs and a leading meat."]],
l:"The chicken is a domesticated bird kept for eggs and meat, and the most numerous bird in the world, with more than 25 billion alive at any time. Chickens descend mainly from the red junglefowl of the forests of Southeast Asia. A 2022 study suggested they were domesticated only about 3,500 years ago, probably in Thailand or nearby, after rice and millet farming attracted junglefowl to fields; at first they may have been kept for cockfighting and ceremonies rather than food. Chickens spread across Asia, Africa, Europe and the Pacific, and later around the world. Today hundreds of breeds exist, from tiny bantams to large meat breeds. Modern egg-laying hens can lay about 300 eggs a year, while broiler chickens are bred to reach market weight in about six weeks. Most are raised in large industrial farms, raising concerns about animal welfare, antibiotics and disease, especially bird flu, which has killed or forced the culling of hundreds of millions of poultry since 2020.",
s:[["Behavior","Chickens are social and form a ‘pecking order’, a hierarchy first described by Norwegian scientist Thorleif Schjelderup-Ebbe in 1921. They communicate with about 24 different calls, including alarms for predators from the air and the ground. Hens make special calls to their chicks and cluck to them while still in the egg."],["Eggs","A hen’s egg forms over about 25 hours. Hens lay eggs whether or not a rooster is present, but only fertilized eggs can hatch. Eggshell color depends on the breed; it does not affect taste or nutrition."],["Chickens and dinosaurs","Birds evolved from dinosaurs, and in 2007 scientists found that proteins from a Tyrannosaurus rex bone were similar to those of chickens. Chickens are not descended from T. rex, but they share a distant ancestor."],["Welfare and farming","Many countries have banned or are phasing out small ‘battery cages’ for hens; the European Union banned them in 2012. Fast-growing broiler breeds can suffer leg and heart problems. Campaigns push for cage-free eggs and slower-growing breeds."]],
k:[["Wild ancestor","Red junglefowl"],["Domesticated","About 3,500 years ago (2022 study)"],["Number alive","More than 25 billion"],["Eggs per hen","Up to about 300 a year"],["Social structure","Pecking order"]],
d:[["c. 1500 BC","Chickens domesticated in Southeast Asia"],["1921","Pecking order described"],["1948","‘Chicken of Tomorrow’ contest begins industrial breeding in the US"],["2007","T. rex proteins compared with chickens"],["2012","EU bans battery cages"],["2022","Study dates chicken domestication"]],
s2:[["Chickens in culture","The rooster is a symbol of France and one of the 12 animals of the Chinese zodiac. Ancient Romans consulted sacred chickens before battles, judging omens by how eagerly they ate. Cockfighting, still practiced in some countries, is banned in many others."],["Backyard chickens","Keeping a few hens in gardens has become popular in many cities. Owners collect fresh eggs and use chickens to eat pests and kitchen scraps, though they must protect them from foxes and follow local rules."]],
sp:["Bird flu","Highly pathogenic avian influenza H5N1 spread worldwide after 2020, killing wild birds and poultry. In 2024, it was found in dairy cattle in the United States, raising concern about spread to mammals and people."],
g:[["Domesticated","Bred and kept by people"],["Broiler","A chicken raised for meat"],["Pecking order","A social ranking among animals"],["Avian influenza","A virus that infects birds"]],
f:["Chickens are the world’s most numerous bird.","Chickens descend from the red junglefowl.","Eggshell color does not affect taste."],
fun:"There are about three chickens for every person on Earth."});

ADDART({id:"cattle",t:"Cattle",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐄",w:"Cattle",r:["domestication","agri","bison","climchange","indiacountry","sheepgoats","pigs"],
p:[["Ancient partners","People domesticated cattle from wild aurochs about 10,500 years ago."],["Milk, meat and muscle","Cattle provide milk, beef, leather and pulling power."],["Climate impact","Cattle produce methane, a powerful greenhouse gas."]],
l:"Cattle are large domesticated hoofed mammals raised for meat (beef and veal), milk, leather and as draft animals for pulling plows and carts. There are about 1.5 billion cattle in the world. They descend from the aurochs, a huge wild ox that roamed Europe, Asia and North Africa; people domesticated aurochs about 10,500 years ago in the Near East, producing humpless taurine cattle, and separately in South Asia, producing humped zebu cattle. The last aurochs died in Poland in 1627. Cattle are ruminants with four-chambered stomachs, which let microbes break down grass and other tough plants; they bring food back up to chew as cud. This digestion produces methane, a powerful greenhouse gas, making cattle a major source of agricultural emissions. Brazil, India, the United States and China have the largest cattle herds. In Hinduism, cows are sacred, and their slaughter is banned in most Indian states. Cattle ranching is a leading driver of deforestation in the Amazon.",
s:[["Digestion","A cow’s stomach has four compartments: the rumen, reticulum, omasum and abomasum. Microbes in the rumen ferment plant material, and the cow regurgitates and rechews it. A dairy cow may spend about eight hours a day chewing cud."],["Breeds","There are more than 1,000 cattle breeds, including Holstein-Friesians, the black-and-white dairy cows that produce the most milk; beef breeds such as Angus and Hereford; and zebu breeds such as Brahman, adapted to heat and insects. Japan’s Wagyu cattle produce heavily marbled beef."],["Cattle and climate","Livestock produce about 12 percent of human-caused greenhouse gas emissions, according to the Food and Agriculture Organization, with cattle the largest share. Methane from cows mostly comes from burping. Feed additives such as seaweed and the chemical 3-NOP can reduce methane emissions, and some people eat less beef for environmental reasons."],["Sacred cows","In India, cows are revered as symbols of life and motherhood, and many Hindus do not eat beef. In parts of East Africa, cattle are central to wealth, marriage and identity for peoples such as the Maasai and Dinka."]],
k:[["Wild ancestor","Aurochs (extinct 1627)"],["Domesticated","About 10,500 years ago"],["World population","About 1.5 billion"],["Stomach","Four chambers (ruminant)"],["Main greenhouse gas","Methane"]],
d:[["c. 8500 BC","Aurochs domesticated in the Near East"],["c. 6000 BC","Zebu cattle domesticated in South Asia"],["1627","The last aurochs dies in Poland"],["1860s–1880s","Great cattle drives in the American West"],["2024","Bird flu found in US dairy cattle"]],
s2:[["Cattle drives","After the American Civil War, cowboys drove millions of Texas longhorn cattle north along trails such as the Chisholm Trail to railroads in Kansas, giving rise to the image of the cowboy. Railways and barbed wire ended the great drives by the late 1880s."],["Bringing back the aurochs","Breeders in Europe are trying to recreate aurochs-like cattle by crossing old breeds, to graze wild landscapes as the aurochs once did. In the 1930s, German brothers bred Heck cattle with the same aim."]],
sp:["Cows have best friends","Studies suggest that cows form close bonds with particular herd members, and their heart rates and stress levels fall when they are with a preferred companion."],
g:[["Ruminant","An animal that chews cud and has a multi-chambered stomach"],["Aurochs","The extinct wild ancestor of cattle"],["Zebu","Humped cattle from South Asia"],["Methane","A powerful greenhouse gas"]],
f:["Cattle descend from the extinct aurochs.","A cow’s stomach has four chambers.","Most cow methane comes from burping."],
fun:"Cows can recognize more than 50 individual members of their herd."});

ADDART({id:"mangoes",t:"Mangoes",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🥭",w:"Mango",r:["fruits","indiacountry","trade","coconut","culturalrev"],
p:[["King of fruits","Mangoes are sweet tropical fruits loved across Asia and beyond."],["From South Asia","Mangoes were domesticated in India thousands of years ago."],["Cashew cousins","Mangoes belong to the same family as cashews and poison ivy."]],
l:"The mango is the fruit of Mangifera indica, a large evergreen tree of the cashew family native to South Asia, where it has been cultivated for at least 4,000 years. Mango trees can grow 30 meters tall and live for centuries. The fruit is a drupe, with a single large flat seed surrounded by sweet, juicy flesh, and comes in hundreds of varieties that differ in color, size, flavor and texture, such as Alphonso and Kesar from India, Carabao from the Philippines, Ataulfo from Mexico and Kent and Tommy Atkins grown widely for export. India grows more mangoes than any other country, about 40 percent of the world’s supply, followed by Indonesia, China and Pakistan. Mangoes are eaten fresh, dried, in juices, chutneys and desserts, and green mangoes are used in pickles and salads. The mango is the national fruit of India, Pakistan and the Philippines. Mango skin and sap contain urushiol, the same irritant as in poison ivy, which can cause rashes in sensitive people.",
s:[["Spread around the world","Buddhist monks are said to have carried mangoes across Asia, and Persian and Arab traders brought them to East Africa. Portuguese traders introduced mangoes to Brazil and West Africa in the 16th century, and they spread to the Caribbean and Mexico. Today mangoes grow throughout the tropics."],["Varieties","There are more than 1,000 named varieties. Indian varieties are often sweet and fiberless, while many export varieties were bred in Florida in the 20th century for color and shelf life. ‘Polyembryonic’ Southeast Asian mangoes produce several seedlings from one seed, identical to the parent."],["Mangoes in culture","Mango leaves decorate doorways at Hindu weddings and festivals, and the mango motif inspired the paisley pattern. The emperor Akbar planted an orchard of 100,000 mango trees in the 16th century. In India, summer mango season is celebrated with festivals."],["Mao’s mangoes","In 1968, during China’s Cultural Revolution, Mao Zedong gave a gift of mangoes from a Pakistani visitor to workers. The fruit, unfamiliar to most Chinese, became an object of worship; wax replicas were made and paraded in a short-lived ‘mango cult’."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Mangifera indica"],["Family","Cashew family"],["Origin","South Asia"],["Largest producer","India (about 40%)"],["National fruit of","India, Pakistan, the Philippines"]],
d:[["c. 2000 BC","Mangoes cultivated in India"],["1500s","Portuguese spread mangoes to Africa and Brazil"],["1570s","Akbar plants a vast mango orchard"],["1968","Mango cult in China during the Cultural Revolution"],["2007","India resumes mango exports to the United States"]],
s2:[["Mango diplomacy","Pakistan and India have long sent boxes of mangoes to foreign leaders as goodwill gifts. In 2007, after a 17-year ban, the United States agreed to import Indian mangoes in exchange for allowing Indian imports of American motorcycles."],["Why mango sap stings","Mango trees contain urushiol, especially in the sap near the stem and the skin. Workers harvesting mangoes may get ‘mango dermatitis’, and people allergic to poison ivy may react to mango skin."]],
sp:["The Philippine mango","The Carabao mango, known for its sweetness, was once listed by Guinness World Records as the sweetest mango. Dried Philippine mangoes are a popular export."],
g:[["Drupe","A fruit with a single hard stone, like a peach"],["Urushiol","An oily irritant found in mango skin and poison ivy"],["Chutney","A spicy sauce made with fruit"],["Variety","A distinct type of a plant"]],
f:["India grows about 40 percent of the world’s mangoes.","Mangoes are related to cashews.","Mango skin can cause rashes."],
fun:"The paisley pattern, popular in clothing, is thought to be based on the shape of a mango or a teardrop."});

ADDART({id:"strawberries",t:"Strawberries",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🍓",w:"Strawberry",r:["fruits","roses","apples","francecountry","chilecountry"],
p:[["Not a true berry","Botanically, a strawberry is not a berry."],["Seeds on the outside","Each ‘seed’ on a strawberry is actually a tiny fruit."],["A French-made hybrid","The garden strawberry was created by accident in 18th-century France."]],
l:"The garden strawberry is a widely grown plant of the rose family, cultivated for its sweet, red, fragrant fruit. Botanically, a strawberry is not a berry but an ‘accessory fruit’: the red flesh grows from the base of the flower, and the true fruits are the tiny yellow ‘seeds’, called achenes, on the outside, about 200 on each strawberry. Wild strawberries have been eaten since ancient times, but they were small. The modern garden strawberry arose in France in the 1750s as an accidental hybrid between two American species: the large-fruited Chilean strawberry, brought from Chile by French officer Amédée-François Frézier in 1714, and the flavorful North American Virginia strawberry. Strawberries now grow worldwide; China is the largest producer, followed by the United States, where California grows most of the crop. Strawberries spread by runners, horizontal stems that root to form new plants. They are rich in vitamin C. Strawberry farming relies heavily on pesticides and labor-intensive picking.",
s:[["An accidental hybrid","Frézier’s Chilean plants were all female and produced little fruit in France until they were planted near Virginia strawberries, which pollinated them. In 1766, botanist Antoine Nicolas Duchesne showed that the garden strawberry was a hybrid of the two species."],["Growing strawberries","Strawberries are perennial plants that grow low to the ground. Farmers often grow them on raised beds covered with plastic, and increasingly in greenhouses and vertical farms. ‘Day-neutral’ varieties fruit throughout the season, while others fruit once in early summer."],["Strawberries in culture","In Britain, strawberries and cream are a tradition at the Wimbledon tennis championships, where about 38 metric tons of strawberries are eaten each year. Strawberries have symbolized love and purity in European art."],["Wild strawberries","Wild woodland strawberries, or fraises des bois, are tiny but intensely flavored. Unlike garden strawberries, they are native to Europe and were gathered by the ancient Romans."]],
k:[["Family","Rose family"],["Fruit type","Accessory fruit"],["Achenes per fruit","About 200"],["Hybrid of","Chilean and Virginia strawberries"],["Largest producer","China"]],
d:[["1714","Frézier brings Chilean strawberries to France"],["1750s","Garden strawberry arises as a hybrid in France"],["1766","Duchesne explains the hybrid’s origin"],["1877","First Wimbledon championships, later famous for strawberries and cream"],["2010s","Vertical and greenhouse strawberry farming expands"]],
s2:[["The name","The origin of the word ‘strawberry’ is uncertain. It may come from the runners that ‘strew’ across the ground, or from the straw farmers place under the plants to keep fruit clean."],["The strawberry moon","The full moon in June is sometimes called the strawberry moon, a name used by some Native American peoples because it came at strawberry harvest time."]],
sp:["Plougastel","The town of Plougastel in Brittany, France, near where the first garden strawberries were grown, became famous for strawberry farming and has a strawberry museum."],
g:[["Accessory fruit","A fruit whose flesh comes from parts other than the ovary"],["Achene","A small dry fruit containing one seed"],["Runner","A horizontal stem that roots to form a new plant"],["Hybrid","The offspring of two different species"]],
f:["Strawberries are not true berries.","A strawberry has about 200 seeds on the outside.","The garden strawberry is a French-made hybrid of two American species."],
fun:"Strawberries are the only fruit that wears its seeds on the outside."});

ADDART({id:"avocados",t:"Avocados",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🥑",w:"Avocado",r:["fruits","mexicocountry","drugtrade","waterscarcity","droughts"],
p:[["A fatty fruit","Avocados are fruits rich in healthy fats."],["From Mexico","Avocados were domesticated in Mexico and Central America."],["Green gold","Rising demand has brought wealth but also water and crime problems."]],
l:"The avocado is the fruit of Persea americana, a tree of the laurel family native to south-central Mexico and Central America, where people have eaten avocados for about 10,000 years and cultivated them for thousands. Avocados are unusual fruits: instead of sugar, their creamy flesh is rich in oils, mostly healthy monounsaturated fats, along with fiber and potassium. The fruit ripens only after it is picked. The Hass variety, with dark, bumpy skin, makes up about 80 percent of avocados sold worldwide; all Hass trees descend from a single tree grown by California postal worker Rudolph Hass, who patented it in 1935. Mexico is by far the largest producer, especially the state of Michoacán, followed by Colombia, Peru and the Dominican Republic. Avocado consumption has soared since the 2000s with the popularity of guacamole and avocado toast. Avocado farming needs a lot of water and has been linked to deforestation, and in Michoacán criminal cartels have extorted avocado growers.",
s:[["Ghost of the megafauna","Avocado seeds are large, and scientists think the fruit evolved to be eaten by giant animals such as ground sloths and mastodons, which swallowed them whole and spread the seeds. After these animals died out, humans became the main spreaders of avocados."],["The Hass avocado","Rudolph Hass bought a seedling in the 1920s and noticed its rich fruit. He patented it in 1935, but earned little from it. The mother tree in La Habra Heights, California, died of root rot in 2002."],["Ripening","Avocados do not ripen on the tree; they can hang for months and soften only after picking. Ripening is triggered by ethylene gas, so placing avocados in a paper bag with a banana speeds it up. Cut avocados brown when exposed to oxygen."],["Problems","An avocado may take about 300–600 liters of water to grow, depending on the region, and plantations in Chile and Mexico have strained water supplies. In Michoacán, cartels have demanded protection money from farmers. In 2022, the United States briefly suspended Mexican avocado imports after an inspector was threatened."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Persea americana"],["Family","Laurel family"],["Origin","Mexico and Central America"],["Main variety","Hass (about 80%)"],["Largest producer","Mexico"]],
d:[["c. 8000 BC","Avocados eaten in Mexico"],["1519","Spanish conquerors encounter avocados"],["1935","Rudolph Hass patents the Hass avocado"],["1997","US lifts its ban on Mexican avocados"],["2002","The Hass mother tree dies"],["2022","US briefly halts Mexican avocado imports"]],
s2:[["The name","The word avocado comes from the Nahuatl word āhuacatl. Spanish speakers turned it into aguacate, and English speakers into ‘avocado’ and the old name ‘alligator pear’, after its bumpy skin."],["Avocados and pets","Avocado leaves, skin and seeds contain persin, a substance harmful to birds, horses and some other animals. Dogs and cats are less sensitive, but the large seed can block their intestines."]],
sp:["Guacamole","Guacamole, mashed avocado with lime, onion, chili and cilantro, was made by the Aztecs, whose name for it, ahuacamolli, meant ‘avocado sauce’."],
g:[["Monounsaturated fat","A kind of fat considered healthy"],["Ethylene","A gas that makes fruits ripen"],["Megafauna","Very large animals"],["Extortion","Getting money by threats"]],
f:["Avocados ripen only after picking.","All Hass avocados come from one tree.","Avocados are rich in fats rather than sugar."],
fun:"Botanically, an avocado is a large berry with a single seed."});

ADDART({id:"pumpkins",t:"Pumpkins and Squash",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🎃",w:"Cucurbita",r:["fruits","mexicocountry","festivals","agri","tomatoes"],
p:[["Ancient American crops","Squash was among the first plants domesticated in the Americas."],["The Three Sisters","Squash was grown with corn and beans by Indigenous farmers."],["Giant pumpkins","The record pumpkin, grown in 2026, weighed about 1,432 kilograms."]],
l:"Pumpkins and squash are the fruits of plants of the genus Cucurbita, in the gourd family, which also includes cucumbers and melons. They include pumpkins, zucchini (courgettes), butternut squash, acorn squash and spaghetti squash. Squash was domesticated in Mexico about 10,000 years ago, earlier than corn, making it one of the first crops in the Americas. Indigenous farmers across North America grew squash together with corn and beans in a system called the Three Sisters: corn provides a pole for beans to climb, beans add nitrogen to the soil, and squash leaves shade the ground to keep it moist and block weeds. Squash spread to Europe after 1492. Today China and India are the largest producers. Pumpkins are carved into jack-o’-lanterns at Halloween, a custom that began in Ireland and Britain with turnips. Competitive growers raise giant pumpkins; in October 2026, a pumpkin grown in Rhode Island set a world record of about 1,432 kilograms (3,156 pounds).",
s:[["Summer and winter squash","Summer squash, such as zucchini, is picked young when the skin is soft. Winter squash, such as pumpkins and butternut squash, is harvested when mature with a hard rind and can be stored for months. Squash flowers and seeds are also eaten."],["The Three Sisters","The Haudenosaunee (Iroquois) and many other Native American peoples planted corn, beans and squash together in mounds. The combination provides a balanced diet: carbohydrates from corn, protein from beans and vitamins from squash. Many gardeners still use the method today."],["Jack-o’-lanterns","In Ireland and Scotland, people carved turnips and potatoes into lanterns for the festival of Samhain. Irish immigrants in North America adopted pumpkins, which were larger and easier to carve. The name comes from a folk tale about ‘Stingy Jack’, who wandered with a lantern after tricking the Devil."],["Giant pumpkins","Giant pumpkin growers use the variety Atlantic Giant and feed plants carefully, and a big pumpkin can gain more than 20 kilograms a day at its peak. The world record rose from about 480 kilograms in 1996 to over 1,400 kilograms by 2026. Giant pumpkins are sometimes hollowed out and paddled as boats in races."]],
k:[["Genus","Cucurbita"],["Family","Gourd family"],["Domesticated","About 10,000 years ago in Mexico"],["Growing system","Three Sisters (corn, beans, squash)"],["Record pumpkin","About 1,432 kg (2026)"]],
d:[["c. 8000 BC","Squash domesticated in Mexico"],["c. 1000","Three Sisters farming widespread in North America"],["1490s","Squash reaches Europe"],["1800s","Pumpkin jack-o’-lanterns spread in North America"],["2026","World-record pumpkin of about 1,432 kg"]],
s2:[["Pumpkin pie","Pumpkin pie became a traditional dessert at Thanksgiving in the United States in the 19th century, after writer Sarah Josepha Hale campaigned for the holiday. Most canned pumpkin is actually made from a type of squash that looks less like a classic pumpkin."],["Is it a fruit?","Botanically, pumpkins and squash are fruits, specifically a type of berry called a pepo, because they develop from flowers and contain seeds. In cooking they are treated as vegetables."]],
sp:["Pumpkin regattas","In several towns in North America and Europe, people hollow out giant pumpkins weighing hundreds of kilograms, climb in and paddle them across lakes in pumpkin regattas."],
g:[["Gourd family","A plant family including pumpkins, cucumbers and melons"],["Three Sisters","Corn, beans and squash grown together"],["Pepo","A berry with a hard rind, like a pumpkin"],["Jack-o’-lantern","A carved lantern, usually a pumpkin"]],
f:["Squash was domesticated before corn.","Jack-o’-lanterns were first carved from turnips.","The record pumpkin weighed about 1,432 kg."],
fun:"Giant pumpkins grow so quickly near their peak that they can gain more than 20 kilograms in a single day."});

ADDART({id:"brassicas",t:"Cabbage, Broccoli and Kale",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🥦",w:"Brassica oleracea",r:["plantbased","vitamins","domestication","evolution","agri"],
p:[["One plant, many vegetables","Cabbage, broccoli, cauliflower, kale and Brussels sprouts are all one species."],["Bred by farmers","Farmers created them by selecting different parts of a wild cabbage."],["Bitter taste","Some people taste these vegetables as more bitter because of their genes."]],
l:"Cabbage, broccoli, cauliflower, kale, Brussels sprouts, kohlrabi and collard greens look very different, but they are all varieties of a single species, Brassica oleracea, descended from a wild cabbage that grows on coastal cliffs of Europe. Over thousands of years, farmers selected plants with particular features: large leaves produced kale and collards, a tight bud at the top produced cabbage, swollen side buds produced Brussels sprouts, enlarged flower clusters produced broccoli and cauliflower, and a thickened stem produced kohlrabi. They belong to the mustard family, which also includes turnips, radishes, mustard, bok choy, rapeseed (canola) and wasabi. These vegetables contain sulfur compounds called glucosinolates, which give them their sharp or bitter flavor and have been studied for possible health benefits. People with certain versions of the gene TAS2R38 taste some of these compounds as much more bitter, which may explain why some children and adults dislike Brussels sprouts.",
s:[["Artificial selection","The varieties of Brassica oleracea are a classic example of artificial selection, which Charles Darwin used to explain natural selection. Cabbage was grown by the ancient Greeks and Romans; broccoli was developed in Italy, cauliflower in the Mediterranean and Brussels sprouts probably near Brussels in the 1600s."],["Nutrition","These vegetables are rich in vitamins C and K, folate and fiber. Kale became a ‘superfood’ trend in the 2010s. Boiling for too long releases sulfur compounds that cause a strong smell, so steaming or roasting keeps flavor and nutrients."],["The mustard family","Relatives in the mustard family include turnips, radishes, watercress, horseradish and wasabi, whose heat comes from similar sulfur compounds. Rapeseed oil was bred in Canada in the 1970s into canola, low in bitter compounds."],["Fermentation","Cabbage is fermented into sauerkraut in Germany and kimchi in Korea, where napa cabbage, another brassica, is the main ingredient. Fermentation preserved vegetables for winter, and sailors ate sauerkraut to help prevent scurvy."]],
k:[["Species","Brassica oleracea"],["Ancestor","Wild cabbage of European coasts"],["Family","Mustard family"],["Flavor compounds","Glucosinolates"],["Bitter-taste gene","TAS2R38"]],
d:[["c. 600 BC","Cabbage grown in ancient Greece"],["1500s","Broccoli developed in Italy"],["1600s","Brussels sprouts grown near Brussels"],["1970s","Canada breeds canola from rapeseed"],["2010s","Kale becomes a ‘superfood’ trend"]],
s2:[["Sprouts are getting sweeter","Many people today find Brussels sprouts less bitter than in the past. In the 1990s, Dutch breeders identified compounds causing bitterness and crossed old varieties to produce milder sprouts, helping their popularity grow."],["Romanesco","Romanesco broccoli has a striking spiral pattern in which each bud is made of smaller buds, a natural example of a fractal. The number of spirals often follows the Fibonacci sequence."]],
sp:["Wild cabbage","Wild cabbage still grows on chalk and limestone cliffs in Britain, France and other parts of Europe. It is a tough plant with thick, waxy leaves that store water and resist salt spray."],
g:[["Artificial selection","Breeding by choosing plants with desired traits"],["Glucosinolate","A sulfur compound in mustard-family plants"],["Fermentation","Changing food using microbes"],["Fractal","A pattern that repeats at smaller scales"]],
f:["Broccoli and cabbage are the same species.","Brussels sprouts have become less bitter through breeding.","Kimchi is made mainly from napa cabbage."],
fun:"Broccoli, cauliflower and kohlrabi were all bred from the same wild cabbage plant, which you can still find growing on European sea cliffs."});

ADDART({id:"oaks",t:"Oak Trees",c:"Plants",i:"🌳",w:"Oak",r:["trees","squirrels","deforestation","ships","grapes","corkoak"],
p:[["Mighty trees","Oaks are long-lived trees and shrubs that produce acorns."],["Homes for wildlife","A single oak can support thousands of other species."],["Wood for ships and wine","Oak wood built navies and ages wine and whisky."]],
l:"Oaks are trees and shrubs of the genus Quercus, in the beech family, with about 500 species found across the Northern Hemisphere, from Europe and Asia to North and Central America. They range from small shrubs to giant trees more than 40 meters tall, and many live for hundreds of years; some exceed 1,000 years. Oaks produce acorns, nuts held in cup-shaped bases, which feed squirrels, jays, deer, wild boars and many other animals. In some years, called mast years, oaks over a large area produce huge crops of acorns at once, overwhelming seed-eaters so some acorns survive. Oaks support remarkable biodiversity: in Britain, a 2019 study found that more than 2,300 species of insects, fungi, birds, lichens and other organisms use native oaks. Oak wood is hard, strong and resistant to decay and was used to build houses, cathedrals and sailing warships; oak barrels give flavor to wine and whisky. Oaks are symbols of strength in many cultures and national trees of countries including the United States, England and Germany.",
s:[["Acorns","Acorns contain bitter tannins, which animals and people must remove before eating them in large amounts. Native Americans in California leached acorns in water to make flour, a major food source for thousands of years. Jays bury thousands of acorns each autumn, planting new oak forests."],["Oak and ships","A large sailing warship such as HMS Victory, launched in 1765, needed about 6,000 trees, mostly oak. Britain and France planted oak forests to supply future navies, though by the time they matured, ships were built of iron."],["Wine and whisky barrels","Oak barrels let small amounts of oxygen in and add flavors such as vanilla and spice to wine, whisky and brandy. French and American oaks give different flavors, and coopers char the insides of whisky barrels."],["Threats","Diseases such as sudden oak death, caused by a fungus-like organism discovered in California in the 1990s, have killed millions of oaks. Acute oak decline, oak wilt and pests such as the oak processionary moth also threaten trees, and climate change may shift where oaks can grow."]],
k:[["Genus","Quercus"],["Species","About 500"],["Fruit","Acorn"],["Lifespan","Often hundreds of years"],["Species supported (UK)","More than 2,300"]],
d:[["c. 1000","The Major Oak in Sherwood Forest begins to grow (estimated)"],["1765","HMS Victory launched, built from about 6,000 trees"],["1995","Sudden oak death identified in California"],["2004","The oak becomes the national tree of the United States"],["2019","Study counts species that depend on British oaks"]],
s2:[["Famous oaks","The Major Oak in Sherwood Forest, England, linked in legend to Robin Hood, is about 800–1,000 years old. The Royal Oak, in which the future King Charles II hid after a battle in 1651, became a national symbol, and many pubs are named after it."],["Iron gall ink","For more than 1,000 years, writers used ink made from oak galls, growths caused by wasps, mixed with iron salts. Iron gall ink was used for many famous documents, including the United States Constitution and Leonardo da Vinci’s notebooks."]],
sp:["Mast years","Scientists think oaks synchronize mast years using weather cues, such as warm, dry springs. In mast years, mice and deer populations boom the following year, which can increase ticks and Lyme disease."],
g:[["Acorn","The nut of an oak tree"],["Mast year","A year with a huge seed crop"],["Tannin","A bitter substance in oak bark and acorns"],["Cooper","A person who makes barrels"]],
f:["There are about 500 species of oak.","British oaks support more than 2,300 species.","HMS Victory used about 6,000 trees."],
fun:"An oak may not produce acorns until it is about 20 to 50 years old, and its biggest crops come when it is about 80 to 120 years old."});

ADDART({id:"legumes",t:"Beans and Legumes",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🫘",w:"Legume",r:["plantbased","agri","mendel","nutri","soil"],
p:[["Seeds in pods","Legumes include beans, peas, lentils, chickpeas and peanuts."],["Natural fertilizer","Legumes work with bacteria to add nitrogen to the soil."],["Protein-rich","They are an important source of protein around the world."]],
l:"Legumes are plants of the pea family, Fabaceae, one of the largest plant families, with about 20,000 species including trees, shrubs and herbs. Their fruits are pods containing seeds. The dried edible seeds, called pulses, include beans, lentils, chickpeas and dry peas, while soybeans and peanuts are grown mainly for oil and protein. Legumes were among the first crops domesticated: lentils, peas and chickpeas in the Fertile Crescent about 10,000 years ago, and beans in the Americas. A special feature of legumes is their partnership with soil bacteria called rhizobia, which live in nodules on their roots and convert nitrogen from the air into a form plants can use. This enriches the soil, so farmers rotate legumes with other crops. Legumes are rich in protein, fiber and minerals and are central to diets in South Asia, Latin America, Africa and the Middle East. Gregor Mendel used pea plants to discover the laws of heredity. Brazil and the United States are the largest soybean producers.",
s:[["Nitrogen fixation","Rhizobia bacteria enter legume roots and form nodules, pinkish lumps containing a protein like hemoglobin. The plant supplies sugars, and the bacteria supply nitrogen compounds. A legume crop can add tens of kilograms of nitrogen per hectare, reducing the need for chemical fertilizer."],["Pulses around the world","Lentils and chickpeas are the basis of Indian dal and Middle Eastern hummus and falafel. Black beans and rice are staples in Latin America, and soybeans are made into tofu, soy sauce and miso in East Asia. The United Nations declared 2016 the International Year of Pulses."],["Soybeans","Soybeans were domesticated in China several thousand years ago and are now one of the world’s most important crops, mostly used for animal feed and vegetable oil. Expanding soybean farming in Brazil has contributed to deforestation in the Amazon and Cerrado."],["Mendel’s peas","Between 1856 and 1863, Gregor Mendel grew about 28,000 pea plants in his monastery garden, tracking traits such as flower color and seed shape. He discovered patterns of inheritance that founded the science of genetics."]],
k:[["Family","Fabaceae (pea family)"],["Species","About 20,000"],["Dried seeds called","Pulses"],["Root partners","Rhizobia bacteria"],["Largest soybean producer","Brazil"]],
d:[["c. 8000 BC","Lentils, peas and chickpeas domesticated"],["c. 1000 BC","Soybeans widely farmed in China"],["1856–1863","Mendel’s pea experiments"],["1888","Nitrogen-fixing root bacteria discovered"],["2016","International Year of Pulses"]],
s2:[["Beans and wind","Beans contain sugars called oligosaccharides that humans cannot digest. Bacteria in the large intestine ferment them, producing gas. Soaking and cooking beans reduces the effect."],["Peanuts","Peanuts are legumes, not nuts. Their flowers grow above ground, but after pollination the stalks bend down and push the developing pods into the soil, where they mature. Peanut allergies are among the most common food allergies."]],
sp:["Favism","Some people, especially in Mediterranean, African and Asian populations, have an inherited condition called G6PD deficiency. Eating fava beans can cause a dangerous breakdown of their red blood cells, a reaction called favism."],
g:[["Pulse","A dried edible legume seed"],["Rhizobia","Bacteria that fix nitrogen in legume roots"],["Nitrogen fixation","Turning nitrogen gas into usable compounds"],["Crop rotation","Growing different crops in turn on the same land"]],
f:["Legumes add nitrogen to the soil.","Peanuts are legumes, not nuts.","Mendel discovered heredity using peas."],
fun:"Peanut pods ripen underground after the flower stalk bends down and plants them in the soil."});

;
/* data/modern34.js */
// New articles, 18 November 2026 (part 4): technology and space.
ADDART({id:"telegraph",t:"The Telegraph and Morse Code",c:"Technology",i:"📠",w:"Electrical telegraph",r:["phone","radio","elec","subseacables","indrev","morsecode"],
p:[["Messages by wire","The electric telegraph sent messages along wires using electrical pulses."],["Dots and dashes","Morse code turned letters into short and long signals."],["The Victorian internet","Telegraph cables linked the world in the 19th century."]],
l:"The electric telegraph was the first technology to send messages almost instantly over long distances, using electrical signals along wires. Before it, news traveled only as fast as a horse, ship or train. Several inventors developed telegraphs in the 1830s, including William Cooke and Charles Wheatstone in Britain, whose system went into use on a railway in 1838, and Samuel Morse in the United States. Morse and Alfred Vail created Morse code, which represents letters and numbers as combinations of short and long signals, dots and dashes. On 24 May 1844, Morse sent the message ‘What hath God wrought’ from Washington, D.C., to Baltimore. Telegraph lines spread rapidly along railways, and a lasting transatlantic cable was laid in 1866. Telegrams carried news, business deals, diplomatic messages and personal greetings, and telegraph networks have been called the ‘Victorian internet’. The telephone, radio and later the internet replaced the telegraph; Western Union sent its last telegram in 2006. Morse code is still used by some radio amateurs.",
s:[["How it worked","Pressing a telegraph key closed an electric circuit, sending current down the wire. At the other end, an electromagnet moved a lever that clicked or marked paper tape. Skilled operators could send and read about 30 to 40 words a minute by ear."],["Morse code","Morse code gives the most common letters the shortest codes: E is a single dot and T a single dash. International Morse code, adopted in 1865, became standard worldwide. The distress signal SOS, three dots, three dashes, three dots, was adopted in 1906 because it was easy to send and recognize."],["Changing the world","The telegraph let newspapers report events from distant places on the same day, helped railways run safely, allowed businesses to trade across continents and changed warfare and diplomacy. Time zones and standardized time were partly driven by telegraph timing signals."],["Wireless telegraphy","In the 1890s, Guglielmo Marconi developed wireless telegraphy using radio waves, and in 1901 he received signals across the Atlantic. Ships carried wireless operators, and the Titanic’s distress calls in 1912 brought rescuers. Ships stopped officially using Morse code for distress in 1999."]],
k:[["Key inventors","Cooke and Wheatstone; Morse and Vail"],["First Morse message","24 May 1844"],["Code","Morse code (dots and dashes)"],["Lasting transatlantic cable","1866"],["Last Western Union telegram","2006"]],
d:[["1837","Cooke and Wheatstone patent an electric telegraph"],["1844","Morse sends ‘What hath God wrought’"],["1858","First transatlantic telegraph cable works briefly"],["1866","Lasting transatlantic cable laid"],["1906","SOS adopted as a distress signal"],["2006","Western Union ends its telegram service"]],
s2:[["Telegram style","Because telegrams were charged by the word, people wrote short, clipped messages, leaving out small words and using STOP instead of a period. This ‘telegraphese’ influenced newspaper headlines and later text messaging."],["The Pony Express","The Pony Express carried mail by horse relay across the western United States for only 18 months before the transcontinental telegraph was completed in October 1861, making it obsolete."]],
sp:["What hath God wrought","The first official Morse message, a Bible verse chosen by Annie Ellsworth, the daughter of a patent official, was sent from the US Capitol to Alfred Vail in Baltimore on 24 May 1844."],
g:[["Telegraph","A system for sending messages by electric signals"],["Morse code","A code of short and long signals"],["Telegram","A message sent by telegraph"],["Telegraph key","A switch used to send Morse code"]],
f:["Morse code’s shortest letter is E, a single dot.","SOS became the distress signal in 1906.","Western Union sent its last telegram in 2006."],
fun:"Skilled Morse code operators can recognize each other’s ‘fist’, the personal rhythm of their sending, like a handwriting style."});

ADDART({id:"email",t:"Email",c:"Technology",i:"📧",w:"Email",r:["internet","comp","passwords","cybersecurity","socialmedia"],
p:[["Electronic letters","Email sends messages between computers over networks."],["The @ sign","Ray Tomlinson chose @ for email addresses in 1971."],["Billions of users","More than 4 billion people use email, and spam is a constant problem."]],
l:"Email, short for electronic mail, is a method of sending messages from one person to another over computer networks. Early forms of electronic messaging existed on shared computers in the 1960s, but in 1971 American engineer Ray Tomlinson sent the first email between two computers on ARPANET, the forerunner of the internet, and chose the @ symbol to separate the user’s name from the computer’s name. Email became the most popular use of ARPANET, spread through universities and businesses in the 1980s and reached the public in the 1990s with services such as Hotmail (1996) and later Gmail (2004). Today more than 4 billion people use email, sending hundreds of billions of messages a day. Email remains essential for work, online accounts and official communication, even as messaging apps have replaced it for many personal conversations. Unwanted bulk messages, called spam, make up a large share of email traffic, and phishing emails are a major way criminals steal passwords and spread malware.",
s:[["How email works","An email is sent from an email program or website to a mail server, which looks up the recipient’s domain, the part after the @, and passes the message to the recipient’s server using a standard called SMTP. The recipient then downloads or views it using standards such as IMAP or POP."],["Spam","The first spam email was sent in 1978 by Gary Thuerk, a marketing manager who advertised computers to about 400 ARPANET users, causing complaints. The word ‘spam’ comes from a Monty Python comedy sketch in which the canned meat Spam is endlessly repeated. Filters now block most spam automatically."],["Phishing","Phishing emails pretend to come from banks, companies or colleagues to trick people into clicking links, opening attachments or revealing passwords. Signs include urgent requests, unusual sender addresses and links that do not match the claimed website."],["Email etiquette","Email developed conventions such as subject lines, signatures and the ‘reply all’ button, which has caused embarrassing mistakes. Writing in capital letters is considered shouting. Many workplaces now try to limit after-hours email."]],
k:[["First network email","Ray Tomlinson, 1971"],["Symbol","@ separates user and server"],["First spam","1978"],["Users","More than 4 billion"],["Main sending standard","SMTP"]],
d:[["1965","Messages sent between users of one computer at MIT"],["1971","Ray Tomlinson sends the first network email"],["1978","First spam email"],["1982","SMTP email standard published"],["1996","Hotmail launches free web email"],["2004","Gmail launches"]],
s2:[["The @ sign","Before email, the @ symbol was used mainly in accounting to mean ‘at the rate of’. Tomlinson chose it because it was on the keyboard and unlikely to appear in names. It now has many nicknames, such as ‘snail’ in Italian and ‘little monkey’ in several languages."],["Email’s carbon footprint","Sending and storing emails uses electricity in data centers. A single email has a tiny footprint, but the vast number sent, especially with large attachments, adds up, which has led to campaigns to delete old messages and unsubscribe from newsletters."]],
sp:["Queen Elizabeth’s email","In 1976, Queen Elizabeth II sent an email from a British army research center using ARPANET, making her one of the first heads of state to send one."],
g:[["Email address","A name and domain separated by @"],["Spam","Unwanted bulk email"],["Phishing","Tricking people with fake messages"],["Server","A computer that stores and delivers data"]],
f:["Ray Tomlinson chose @ for email addresses.","The first spam was sent in 1978.","More than 4 billion people use email."],
fun:"Ray Tomlinson said he could not remember the text of the first email, which was probably a test such as ‘QWERTYUIOP’."});

ADDART({id:"fasteners",t:"Zippers and Velcro",c:"Technology",i:"🤐",w:"Zipper",r:["clothing","sewingmachine","plastics","spaceagencies","astronauts"],
p:[["Closing clothes","Zippers and hook-and-loop fasteners close clothes, bags and shoes."],["A slow start","The zipper took decades to become popular."],["Inspired by burrs","Velcro was invented after a Swiss engineer studied burrs stuck to his dog."]],
l:"Zippers and hook-and-loop fasteners are two of the most widely used ways of fastening clothing, bags, shoes and other items. A zipper has two strips of interlocking teeth that are joined or separated by a slider. Early versions were patented by Elias Howe in 1851 and Whitcomb Judson, whose ‘clasp locker’ appeared at the 1893 Chicago World’s Fair, but they often jammed. Swedish-American engineer Gideon Sundback designed the modern zipper in 1913, and the name ‘zipper’ came from a brand of rubber boots in 1923. Zippers became common on trousers and jackets in the 1930s. Hook-and-loop fasteners were invented by Swiss engineer George de Mestral, who noticed burdock burrs sticking to his dog’s fur after a walk in 1941 and saw under a microscope that they had tiny hooks. He developed a fabric with hooks on one side and loops on the other, patented in 1955 and sold under the brand Velcro. It is a famous example of biomimicry, inventions inspired by nature, and was used by NASA astronauts.",
s:[["How a zipper works","The slider has a Y-shaped channel. Moving it up forces the teeth on each side together at an angle so that each tooth’s hook fits into the hollow of a tooth opposite. Moving it down wedges them apart. Zippers can be made of metal, molded plastic or plastic coils."],["Hook and loop","One strip has thousands of tiny stiff hooks, and the other has soft loops. Pressed together, the hooks catch the loops; pulling them apart makes the familiar ripping sound. Modern versions can be opened and closed thousands of times."],["Zippers take over","The US military used zippers on flight suits and boots in World War I. In the 1930s, fashion designers such as Elsa Schiaparelli used them prominently, and they replaced buttons on men’s trousers. Japan’s YKK became the world’s largest zipper maker."],["Velcro in space","NASA used hook-and-loop fasteners in the Apollo program to keep objects from floating away in weightlessness, attaching pens, food packets and equipment to walls. Astronauts even used a patch inside their helmets to scratch their noses."]],
k:[["Modern zipper","Gideon Sundback, 1913"],["Name ‘zipper’","1923"],["Hook-and-loop inventor","George de Mestral"],["Inspiration","Burdock burrs"],["Velcro patent","1955"]],
d:[["1851","Elias Howe patents an ‘automatic continuous clothing closure’"],["1893","Judson’s clasp locker shown in Chicago"],["1913","Sundback designs the modern zipper"],["1923","The name ‘zipper’ is used for boots"],["1941","De Mestral studies burrs on his dog"],["1955","Hook-and-loop fastener patented"]],
s2:[["The name Velcro","The name Velcro combines the French words velours, ‘velvet’, and crochet, ‘hook’. The company has asked people to say ‘hook and loop’ rather than using its brand name for all such fasteners, even releasing a humorous song about it in 2017."],["Zipper mishaps","Doctors treat a number of zipper injuries each year, mostly from trousers. Engineers have designed zippers with safety features, and YKK makes zippers resistant to water for space suits and diving gear."]],
sp:["Biomimicry","Biomimicry means copying designs from nature. Besides Velcro, examples include swimsuits inspired by sharkskin, self-cleaning surfaces inspired by lotus leaves and train noses inspired by kingfisher beaks."],
g:[["Fastener","A device that joins two things"],["Slider","The part that opens and closes a zipper"],["Burr","A seed head with hooks that sticks to fur"],["Biomimicry","Designs inspired by nature"]],
f:["The modern zipper was designed in 1913.","Velcro was inspired by burrs on a dog.","Astronauts used Velcro to scratch their noses."],
fun:"George de Mestral’s idea took about ten years to perfect, because making tiny, strong hooks from fabric was very difficult until he used nylon."});

ADDART({id:"subseacables",t:"Undersea Internet Cables",c:"Technology",i:"🌊",w:"Submarine communications cable",r:["internet","fiberoptics","telegraph","trench","cybersecurity"],
p:[["The internet’s backbone","Most data between continents travels through cables on the seabed."],["Hundreds of cables","About 600 cable systems are in service around the world."],["Vulnerable","Ship anchors, earthquakes and sabotage can cut cables."]],
l:"Undersea, or submarine, communications cables are cables laid on the seabed to carry telecommunications signals between continents and islands. Although many people imagine the internet travels by satellite, about 99 percent of international data, including web pages, video calls and financial transactions, flows through these cables. Modern cables contain optical fibers that carry data as pulses of laser light, with repeaters every 50 to 100 kilometers to boost the signal, and are only about as thick as a garden hose in deep water. As of 2026, the research firm TeleGeography counted about 600 cable systems in service and about 100 planned, stretching well over a million kilometers. Undersea cables began with telegraph cables: the first crossed the English Channel in 1850, and a lasting transatlantic cable was laid in 1866. Today large technology companies such as Google, Meta, Amazon and Microsoft own or fund many new cables. Cables are damaged about 150 to 200 times a year, mostly by fishing gear and ship anchors, and suspected sabotage has raised security concerns.",
s:[["Laying cables","Special cable ships carry thousands of kilometers of cable and lay it as they sail, following routes carefully surveyed to avoid steep slopes and hazards. Near shore, cables are armored and buried in the seabed to protect them from anchors and trawlers. In deep water, they rest on the ocean floor."],["How fast?","A single modern cable can carry hundreds of terabits of data per second, enough to stream millions of high-definition videos at once. The first fiber-optic transatlantic cable, TAT-8, opened in 1988."],["Breaks and repairs","When a cable breaks, traffic is rerouted through other cables, and repair ships use grapnels to lift the cable from the seabed and splice it. A 2006 earthquake off Taiwan broke several cables and disrupted internet service across East Asia. In 2024, cables in the Red Sea were cut after a ship damaged by an attack dragged its anchor."],["Security","Because cables are essential to economies, governments worry about sabotage and spying. Several incidents of cut cables in the Baltic Sea in 2023–2025 led NATO to increase patrols. Countries are building more routes to make networks resilient."]],
k:[["Share of international data","About 99%"],["Cable systems in service","About 600 (2026)"],["Technology","Optical fibers"],["First undersea cable","English Channel, 1850"],["First fiber-optic transatlantic cable","TAT-8, 1988"]],
d:[["1850","First undersea telegraph cable crosses the English Channel"],["1866","Lasting transatlantic telegraph cable"],["1956","First transatlantic telephone cable, TAT-1"],["1988","First fiber-optic transatlantic cable, TAT-8"],["2006","Earthquake off Taiwan cuts several cables"],["2024","Red Sea cable cuts disrupt traffic"]],
s2:[["Sharks and cables","In the 1980s, sharks were found to have bitten some fiber-optic cables, possibly attracted by electrical fields. Cable makers added protective layers, and shark bites are now a very minor cause of damage."],["The Great Eastern","The huge steamship Great Eastern, the only ship big enough to carry an entire transatlantic cable, laid the successful 1866 cable and recovered and completed a lost 1865 cable."]],
sp:["Tonga cut off","In January 2022, the eruption of the Hunga Tonga–Hunga Haʻapai volcano severed Tonga’s only international cable, cutting the country off from the internet for about five weeks until it was repaired."],
g:[["Optical fiber","A thin glass strand that carries light"],["Repeater","A device that boosts a signal along a cable"],["Grapnel","A hook used to lift cables from the seabed"],["Bandwidth","The amount of data that can be carried"]],
f:["About 99% of international data travels through undersea cables.","About 600 cable systems are in service.","Most cable damage is caused by fishing and anchors."],
fun:"In deep water, an internet cable crossing an ocean may be only about as thick as a garden hose."});

ADDART({id:"searchengines",t:"Search Engines",c:"Technology",i:"🔍",w:"Search engine",r:["internet","ai","dataprivacy","recommender","misinformation","sorting","webcookies"],
p:[["Finding information","Search engines help people find pages on the web."],["Crawl, index, rank","They copy the web, organize it and rank the results."],["One giant","Google handles about 90 percent of searches worldwide."]],
l:"A search engine is a software system that helps people find information on the World Wide Web. When a user types a query, the search engine returns a ranked list of web pages, images, videos, news and other results, usually in a fraction of a second. Search engines work in three stages: automated programs called crawlers or spiders visit and copy billions of web pages; the pages are stored and organized in a giant index; and ranking algorithms decide which results are most relevant to each query, considering hundreds of factors such as words on the page, links from other sites and the user’s location. Early search tools included Archie (1990), Yahoo’s directory (1994) and AltaVista (1995). Google, founded in 1998, became dominant with its PageRank system, which ranked pages by the links pointing to them, and now handles about 90 percent of searches worldwide. Other search engines include Microsoft’s Bing, Baidu in China, Yandex in Russia and Naver in South Korea. Since 2023, search engines have increasingly added AI-generated answers.",
s:[["PageRank","Larry Page and Sergey Brin, PhD students at Stanford University, developed PageRank, which treated a link from one page to another as a vote, giving more weight to votes from important pages. Combined with a simple, fast interface, it made Google’s results much better than rivals’."],["Advertising","Most search engines are free to use and earn money by showing advertisements linked to search terms. Google’s parent company, Alphabet, earns most of its revenue from advertising. Businesses use search engine optimization (SEO) to try to rank higher in results."],["Antitrust","In August 2024, a US federal judge ruled that Google had illegally maintained a monopoly in search, partly by paying billions of dollars to be the default search engine on phones and browsers. In 2025 the judge ordered remedies including sharing some data with competitors but did not force Google to sell its Chrome browser; both sides appealed. The European Union has also fined Google."],["AI and search","Since 2023, search engines have added AI chatbots and summaries that answer questions directly. Supporters say this saves time; critics worry about errors, reduced traffic to websites that create content, and the concentration of information in a few companies."]],
k:[["Stages","Crawl, index, rank"],["Google founded","1998"],["Google’s share","About 90% of global searches"],["Other major engines","Bing, Baidu, Yandex, Naver"],["Key ranking idea","PageRank (links as votes)"]],
d:[["1990","Archie, the first internet search tool"],["1994","Yahoo’s web directory launches"],["1995","AltaVista launches"],["1998","Google founded"],["2009","Microsoft launches Bing"],["2024","US court rules Google illegally maintained a search monopoly"]],
s2:[["To google","The verb ‘to google’ was added to the Oxford English Dictionary and Merriam-Webster in 2006. Google has asked people to use it only for searches made with Google."],["Filter bubbles","Personalized search results based on location and history can show different people different results. Some researchers worry this could create ‘filter bubbles’ that reinforce existing beliefs, though studies suggest the effect in search is smaller than often claimed."]],
sp:["The name Google","Google’s name is a play on ‘googol’, the number 1 followed by 100 zeros, reflecting the founders’ goal of organizing a huge amount of information."],
g:[["Crawler","A program that visits and copies web pages"],["Index","An organized database of web pages"],["Algorithm","A set of rules a computer follows"],["SEO","Search engine optimization"]],
f:["Google handles about 90 percent of searches worldwide.","Search engines work by crawling, indexing and ranking.","Google’s name comes from ‘googol’."],
fun:"Google receives billions of searches every day, and about 15 percent of searches each day have never been searched before."});

ADDART({id:"datacenters",t:"Data Centers",c:"Technology",i:"🏢",w:"Data center",r:["cloud","ai","internet","elec","waterscarcity"],
p:[["Where the internet lives","Data centers are buildings full of computers that store and process data."],["Power-hungry","They use about 1.5 percent of the world’s electricity, and demand is rising fast."],["Driven by AI","Artificial intelligence is fueling a boom in data center building."]],
l:"A data center is a building or group of buildings housing computer servers, data storage and networking equipment that store, process and deliver digital information. Every web search, video stream, email, online purchase and AI chatbot response relies on data centers. Large ‘hyperscale’ data centers run by companies such as Amazon, Microsoft, Google and Meta can contain hundreds of thousands of servers and cover areas the size of several football fields. Data centers need huge amounts of electricity to run and cool their equipment: the International Energy Agency estimated that they used about 415 terawatt-hours in 2024, about 1.5 percent of global electricity, that demand grew 17 percent in 2025, and that it could roughly double by 2030, driven largely by artificial intelligence. Many also use large amounts of water for cooling. The United States has the most data centers, followed by China and Europe. Their growth has brought investment and jobs but also concerns about electricity prices, grid strain, emissions, water use and local land.",
s:[["Inside a data center","Servers are stacked in tall racks arranged in rows, connected by fiber-optic cables. Backup batteries and generators keep them running during power cuts. Security guards, cameras and access controls protect the equipment, and the most important data is copied to several sites."],["Keeping cool","Computers turn electricity into heat. Data centers use fans, air conditioning, chilled water and increasingly liquid cooling, in which coolant flows directly over hot chips. Some are built in cold places such as Scandinavia, and a few reuse waste heat to warm nearby homes."],["AI and power","Training and running large AI models uses powerful chips called GPUs, which consume much more electricity than ordinary servers. Technology companies have signed deals for solar, wind, geothermal and nuclear power, including plans to restart closed nuclear plants, while some new data centers rely on natural gas."],["History","Early computers of the 1940s and 1950s filled whole rooms. Businesses later kept servers in their own ‘server rooms’. In the 2000s, cloud computing, led by Amazon Web Services from 2006, moved computing into giant shared data centers."]],
k:[["Electricity use (2024)","About 415 TWh, about 1.5% of the world total"],["Growth in 2025","17% (IEA)"],["Outlook","Roughly double by 2030"],["Largest market","United States"],["Main driver","Artificial intelligence"]],
d:[["1946","ENIAC fills a large room"],["1990s","Internet boom drives early data centers"],["2006","Amazon Web Services launches cloud computing"],["2018","Microsoft tests an underwater data center"],["2025","Data center electricity use rises 17%"]],
s2:[["Underwater data center","In 2018, Microsoft sank a sealed data center the size of a shipping container off Scotland’s Orkney Islands. After two years, its servers had failed less often than those on land, though the company later said it would not build more."],["Local debates","In places such as northern Virginia, the world’s largest data center hub, Ireland and the Netherlands, rapid data center growth has led to debates about electricity supplies and land use. Ireland’s data centers used about a fifth of the country’s electricity by the mid-2020s."]],
sp:["Waste heat","In Stockholm, Helsinki and other northern cities, heat from data centers is piped into district heating networks, warming thousands of homes."],
g:[["Server","A computer that provides data or services to others"],["Hyperscale","Extremely large-scale computing facilities"],["Terawatt-hour","A billion kilowatt-hours of electricity"],["GPU","A powerful processor used for graphics and AI"]],
f:["Data centers use about 1.5% of the world’s electricity.","AI is driving rapid growth in data center power use.","Some data centers heat nearby homes."],
fun:"Northern Virginia, near Washington, D.C., has so many data centers that a large share of the world’s internet traffic is thought to pass through the area."});

ADDART({id:"thermometers",t:"Thermometers",c:"Technology",i:"🌡️",w:"Thermometer",r:["thermo","fever","climate","galileo","mercury"],
p:[["Measuring temperature","Thermometers measure how hot or cold something is."],["Three famous scales","Fahrenheit, Celsius and Kelvin are the main temperature scales."],["Leaving mercury behind","Mercury thermometers are being phased out because mercury is toxic."]],
l:"A thermometer is an instrument for measuring temperature. Around 1593, Galileo Galilei built a thermoscope, a glass bulb and tube in which water rose and fell as air expanded and contracted, but it had no scale. Sealed liquid-in-glass thermometers were developed in Italy in the 1650s. German-Dutch instrument maker Daniel Gabriel Fahrenheit made the first reliable mercury thermometer in 1714 and created his temperature scale in 1724. Swedish astronomer Anders Celsius proposed a scale based on water’s freezing and boiling points in 1742, and Lord Kelvin introduced an absolute scale starting at absolute zero in 1848. Thermometers are used in medicine to check for fever, in weather forecasting, cooking, industry and science. Modern types include digital thermometers, infrared thermometers that measure heat radiation without contact, and thermocouples. Because mercury is toxic, many countries have phased out mercury thermometers under the Minamata Convention, which entered into force in 2017.",
s:[["How they work","Liquid thermometers rely on the fact that liquids expand when heated. Bimetallic strips, made of two metals that expand at different rates, bend as temperature changes. Digital thermometers use sensors whose electrical resistance changes with temperature. Infrared thermometers detect the heat energy that all objects radiate."],["Temperature scales","On the Celsius scale, water freezes at 0° and boils at 100° at sea level. On the Fahrenheit scale, used mainly in the United States, water freezes at 32° and boils at 212°. The Kelvin scale, used in science, begins at absolute zero, −273.15°C, the coldest possible temperature. The scales meet at −40°, where Celsius and Fahrenheit are equal."],["Medical thermometers","In 1867, English doctor Thomas Clifford Allbutt invented a short clinical thermometer that took a temperature in five minutes instead of 20. Normal body temperature is around 37°C, though it varies between people and through the day. Ear and forehead thermometers became common in the 2000s and during the COVID-19 pandemic."],["Weather records","Standard weather thermometers are kept in shaded, ventilated boxes about 1.5 meters above the ground. Long thermometer records, such as the Central England Temperature series starting in 1659, help scientists track climate change."]],
k:[["Early thermoscope","Galileo, about 1593"],["Mercury thermometer","Fahrenheit, 1714"],["Celsius scale","1742"],["Kelvin scale","1848"],["Mercury phase-out","Minamata Convention (in force 2017)"]],
d:[["c. 1593","Galileo’s thermoscope"],["1654","Sealed liquid thermometer made in Florence"],["1714","Fahrenheit’s mercury thermometer"],["1742","Celsius proposes his scale"],["1848","Kelvin proposes an absolute scale"],["1867","Allbutt’s clinical thermometer"],["2017","Minamata Convention on mercury enters into force"]],
s2:[["Celsius upside down","Anders Celsius’s original scale was reversed: water boiled at 0° and froze at 100°. After his death, it was flipped to the familiar form, possibly by Carl Linnaeus or others."],["Galileo thermometers","The decorative ‘Galileo thermometer’, with colored glass bulbs floating in liquid, was not invented by Galileo but named after him because it uses a principle he studied: the density of liquids changes with temperature."]],
sp:["Hottest and coldest","The highest air temperature accepted by the World Meteorological Organization is 56.7°C, recorded in Death Valley, California, in 1913, though some scientists doubt it. The lowest is −89.2°C at Vostok Station in Antarctica in 1983."],
g:[["Temperature","How hot or cold something is"],["Absolute zero","The lowest possible temperature, −273.15°C"],["Infrared","Heat radiation invisible to the eye"],["Thermocouple","A temperature sensor made of two metals"]],
f:["Celsius and Fahrenheit are equal at −40°.","Celsius’s original scale was upside down.","Mercury thermometers are being phased out."],
fun:"Daniel Fahrenheit set 0° on his scale using a mixture of ice, water and salt, the coldest temperature he could reliably make."});

ADDART({id:"browndwarfs",t:"Brown Dwarfs",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🟤",w:"Brown dwarf",r:["stars","jupiter","exoplanet","jwst","fusion","rogueplanets"],
p:[["Failed stars","Brown dwarfs are too massive to be planets but too small to be true stars."],["Not quite shining","They cannot sustain hydrogen fusion, so they slowly cool and fade."],["Discovered in 1995","The first brown dwarfs were confirmed only in 1995."]],
l:"Brown dwarfs are objects in space that are bigger than giant planets but too small to become true stars, often called ‘failed stars’. They form like stars, from collapsing clouds of gas, but have between about 13 and 80 times the mass of Jupiter, not enough to sustain the fusion of ordinary hydrogen in their cores that powers stars like the Sun. Brown dwarfs can briefly fuse a heavy form of hydrogen called deuterium, then gradually cool and fade over billions of years. Despite their name, they appear deep red, magenta or even dark to the human eye, and give off most of their light as infrared. Their existence was predicted by Indian-American astronomer Shiv Kumar in 1962, but they were so faint that the first were confirmed only in 1995: Teide 1 in the Pleiades star cluster and Gliese 229B, a companion to a nearby red dwarf. Thousands are now known. The coldest, such as WISE 0855, are about as cold as the North Pole, and the James Webb Space Telescope has studied their weather of clouds made of hot sand and iron droplets.",
s:[["Between planets and stars","Astronomers usually define a brown dwarf as having between about 13 and 80 Jupiter masses. Below 13, objects cannot fuse even deuterium and are considered planets; above about 80, they become red dwarf stars. Curiously, most brown dwarfs are roughly the same size as Jupiter, because extra mass squeezes them tighter."],["Spectral classes","Astronomers classify brown dwarfs as L, T and Y types as they get cooler. Y dwarfs, the coldest, discovered by NASA’s WISE telescope in 2011, can be colder than 0°C. Methane and water are found in their atmospheres, much like in giant planets."],["Weather","Brown dwarfs rotate quickly and have bands of winds and storms. Observations show they change in brightness as patchy clouds rotate into view. JWST has detected clouds of silicate dust in some of them, which would rain down as molten rock."],["Gliese 229B","Gliese 229B, one of the first brown dwarfs discovered, puzzled astronomers for decades because it seemed too faint for its mass. In 2024, astronomers found that it is actually two brown dwarfs orbiting each other every 12 days."]],
k:[["Mass","About 13–80 Jupiter masses"],["Size","About the size of Jupiter"],["Predicted","Shiv Kumar, 1962"],["First confirmed","1995 (Teide 1, Gliese 229B)"],["Classes","L, T and Y"]],
d:[["1962","Shiv Kumar predicts brown dwarfs"],["1975","The name ‘brown dwarf’ suggested by Jill Tarter"],["1995","First brown dwarfs confirmed"],["2011","WISE discovers Y-type brown dwarfs"],["2014","Coldest known brown dwarf, WISE 0855, found"],["2024","Gliese 229B found to be a pair"]],
s2:[["Why ‘brown’?","Astronomer Jill Tarter suggested the name ‘brown dwarf’ in 1975 for objects between red dwarfs and planets. Brown is not a true color of light, and real brown dwarfs would look dull red or magenta, but the name stuck."],["Free-floating","Many brown dwarfs drift through space alone, not orbiting any star. Some astronomers think the galaxy may contain tens of billions of them, though they are hard to count because they are so faint."]],
sp:["Luhman 16","Luhman 16, discovered in 2013, is a pair of brown dwarfs only about 6.5 light-years away, the closest brown dwarfs to the Sun. Weather maps of one of them show cloud bands like Jupiter’s."],
g:[["Fusion","Joining atomic nuclei, releasing energy"],["Deuterium","A heavy form of hydrogen"],["Infrared","Light with longer wavelengths than red"],["Red dwarf","A small, cool star"]],
f:["Brown dwarfs are called failed stars.","Most brown dwarfs are about the size of Jupiter.","The first brown dwarfs were confirmed in 1995."],
fun:"The coldest brown dwarf known, WISE 0855, is colder than −20°C, cold enough to have water ice clouds."});

ADDART({id:"redgiants",t:"Red Giants",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🔴",w:"Red giant",r:["sun","stars","whitedwarfs","betelgeuse","nebula","starlifecycle","hrdiagram","hypergiants","planetarynebulae"],
p:[["Swollen old stars","Red giants are stars that have swollen hugely near the end of their lives."],["The Sun’s future","In about 5 billion years, the Sun will become a red giant."],["Making elements","Red giants create carbon and other elements later spread into space."]],
l:"A red giant is a star in a late stage of its life that has expanded to tens or hundreds of times its original size and cooled on the surface, giving it an orange-red color. Stars like the Sun spend most of their lives fusing hydrogen into helium in their cores. When the core runs out of hydrogen, it contracts and heats up, while hydrogen continues fusing in a shell around it, causing the outer layers to swell enormously. Later the core becomes hot enough to fuse helium into carbon and oxygen. Well-known red giants include Arcturus and Aldebaran, among the brightest stars in the night sky; much larger stars become red supergiants, such as Betelgeuse and Antares. In about 5 billion years, the Sun will become a red giant, swelling to perhaps more than 200 times its present size, engulfing Mercury and Venus and possibly Earth. Eventually, red giants shed their outer layers into colorful planetary nebulae, leaving behind white dwarfs.",
s:[["How a star becomes a red giant","As the core shrinks and heats, the energy from shell burning pushes the outer layers outward. Although the star becomes far more luminous, its huge surface is cooler, about 3,000 to 4,500°C, so it glows red-orange. Its outer layers become thin, with a density far less than air."],["The helium flash","In stars like the Sun, helium fusion begins suddenly in a ‘helium flash’, which releases enormous energy deep in the core for a few minutes, though it is not visible from outside. After that, the star settles down to fuse helium more steadily."],["Factories of elements","Red giants produce carbon, nitrogen and heavier elements through slow neutron capture. Their strong winds carry these elements into space, where they become part of new stars and planets. Much of the carbon in living things was made inside red giant stars."],["The Sun’s fate","When the Sun becomes a red giant, its outer layers may reach Earth’s orbit. Whether Earth is swallowed is uncertain, because the Sun will also lose mass, letting the planets move outward. Long before then, in about 1 billion years, the Sun’s growing brightness will boil away Earth’s oceans."]],
k:[["Stage","Late in a star’s life"],["Surface temperature","About 3,000–4,500°C"],["Examples","Arcturus, Aldebaran"],["Supergiant examples","Betelgeuse, Antares"],["Sun becomes one","In about 5 billion years"]],
d:[["1905–1913","Hertzsprung and Russell discover giant and dwarf stars"],["1920","First star size measured, Betelgeuse"],["1950s","Astronomers explain how red giants form"],["1957","Paper explains how stars make elements"],["2023","Scientists observe a star swallowing a planet"]],
s2:[["The Hertzsprung–Russell diagram","Around 1910, Ejnar Hertzsprung and Henry Norris Russell plotted stars’ brightness against their color and found that they fall into groups, including the ‘main sequence’ where stars spend most of their lives and the region of red giants. The diagram became one of the most important tools in astronomy."],["A star eats a planet","In 2023, astronomers reported a burst of light from a star about 12,000 light-years away, which they interpreted as the star swallowing a planet as it expanded into a red giant, a preview of what may happen to some planets in our solar system."]],
sp:["Arcturus","Arcturus, in the constellation Boötes, is a red giant about 25 times the Sun’s diameter and one of the brightest stars visible from Earth. Light from Arcturus was used to switch on the lights of the 1933 Chicago World’s Fair."],
g:[["Red giant","A large, cool, very bright old star"],["Planetary nebula","A glowing shell of gas thrown off by a dying star"],["White dwarf","The hot, dense core left after a red giant"],["Main sequence","The stage when stars fuse hydrogen in their cores"]],
f:["The Sun will become a red giant in about 5 billion years.","Much of the carbon in living things was made in red giants.","Arcturus is a red giant."],
fun:"Betelgeuse is so large that if it replaced the Sun, its surface would extend beyond the orbit of Mars."});

ADDART({id:"phobosdeimos",t:"Phobos and Deimos",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🥔",w:"Moons of Mars",r:["mars","moons","marsrovers","asteroidbelt","impactcraters"],
p:[["The moons of Mars","Mars has two small, potato-shaped moons, Phobos and Deimos."],["Fear and dread","They are named after sons of Ares, the Greek god of war."],["Phobos is doomed","Phobos is slowly spiraling toward Mars and will break apart."]],
l:"Phobos and Deimos are the two small moons of Mars, discovered by American astronomer Asaph Hall in August 1877. They are named after the twin sons of Ares, the Greek god of war: Phobos means ‘fear’ and Deimos ‘dread’. Both are irregular, lumpy bodies, more like potatoes than spheres. Phobos, the larger, is about 27 kilometers across at its widest; Deimos is about 15 kilometers. Phobos orbits only about 6,000 kilometers above the Martian surface, closer than any other moon to its planet, circling Mars three times a day, so that from the surface it rises in the west and sets in the east. Deimos orbits farther out, taking about 30 hours. Their origin is debated: they may be captured asteroids or debris from a giant impact on Mars. Phobos is slowly spiraling inward and will be torn apart by Mars’s gravity in roughly 30 to 50 million years, possibly forming a ring. Japan’s Martian Moons eXploration (MMX) mission, launched in October 2026, aims to land on Phobos and return samples to Earth in 2031.",
s:[["Phobos","Phobos is covered in craters and grooves. Its largest crater, Stickney, is about 9 kilometers wide, almost a third of the moon’s size; the impact that formed it nearly shattered Phobos. Long grooves across its surface may be stretch marks from Mars’s tidal pull or chains of craters from debris."],["Deimos","Deimos is smoother, with fewer visible craters because they are partly buried by loose dust. From Mars, it looks like a bright star and takes about two and a half days to cross the sky because it orbits almost as fast as Mars rotates."],["Origins","The moons’ dark color and composition resemble some asteroids, suggesting capture, but their nearly circular orbits near Mars’s equator are hard to explain that way. Many scientists now favor the idea that they formed from debris thrown into orbit when a large object struck Mars. MMX samples may settle the question."],["Missions","Soviet Phobos 1 and 2 probes in 1988 failed before completing their missions, and Russia’s Phobos-Grunt sample-return mission in 2011 never left Earth orbit. Spacecraft orbiting Mars have photographed both moons in detail, and rovers have watched Phobos pass in front of the Sun."]],
k:[["Discovered","Asaph Hall, August 1877"],["Phobos size","About 27 km across"],["Deimos size","About 15 km across"],["Phobos orbit","About 6,000 km above Mars, three times a day"],["Sample mission","MMX (launched 2026)"]],
d:[["1726","Gulliver’s Travels imagines two moons of Mars"],["1877","Asaph Hall discovers Phobos and Deimos"],["1971","Mariner 9 photographs the moons up close"],["1988","Soviet Phobos probes fail"],["2011","Phobos-Grunt fails in Earth orbit"],["2026","Japan launches MMX"]],
s2:[["Swift’s prediction","In Gulliver’s Travels (1726), Jonathan Swift described astronomers of Laputa who had discovered two moons of Mars, more than 150 years before they were found. It was a lucky guess, possibly based on an idea of Johannes Kepler."],["Eclipses on Mars","NASA’s rovers have filmed Phobos crossing the Sun, creating a partial eclipse shaped like a potato. Because Phobos is too small to cover the Sun completely, Mars never has total solar eclipses."]],
sp:["Asaph Hall’s discovery","Asaph Hall searched for Martian moons with the US Naval Observatory’s new telescope in 1877. He almost gave up, but his wife, Angeline Stickney, encouraged him to continue. He found Deimos on 12 August and Phobos on 18 August. Phobos’s largest crater is named Stickney in her honor."],
g:[["Captured asteroid","An asteroid pulled into orbit by a planet’s gravity"],["Tidal force","The stretching effect of gravity"],["Groove","A long, narrow channel"],["Sample return","Bringing material from space to Earth"]],
f:["Phobos orbits Mars three times a day.","Phobos will break apart in tens of millions of years.","Mars never has total solar eclipses."],
fun:"From the surface of Mars, Phobos rises in the west and sets in the east, crossing the sky in about four and a half hours."});

ADDART({id:"gammaraybursts",t:"Gamma-Ray Bursts",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"💥",w:"Gamma-ray burst",r:["supernova","bh","pulsar","gravwaves","radioastronomy"],
p:[["The brightest explosions","Gamma-ray bursts are the most powerful explosions known in the universe."],["Found by spy satellites","Satellites watching for nuclear tests discovered them in 1967."],["Dying stars and colliding stars","They come from collapsing giant stars and merging neutron stars."]],
l:"Gamma-ray bursts (GRBs) are extremely powerful flashes of gamma rays, the most energetic form of light, coming from distant galaxies. In a few seconds, a gamma-ray burst can release more energy than the Sun will produce in its entire 10-billion-year life. They were discovered by accident in 1967 by American Vela satellites designed to detect secret nuclear weapons tests, and the discovery was announced in 1973. For decades their source was a mystery, until in 1997 the Italian–Dutch BeppoSAX satellite detected an afterglow that allowed astronomers to show they come from billions of light-years away. Long gamma-ray bursts, lasting more than about two seconds, occur when massive stars collapse to form black holes, launching narrow jets of matter at nearly the speed of light. Short bursts come from the merger of two neutron stars, as confirmed in 2017 when one was detected together with gravitational waves. On 9 October 2022, astronomers observed GRB 221009A, nicknamed the BOAT, ‘brightest of all time’.",
s:[["Long and short bursts","Long bursts are linked to supernovae of very massive, rapidly spinning stars, in an event called a collapsar. Short bursts, lasting less than two seconds, come from colliding neutron stars or a neutron star and a black hole. In both cases, jets aimed toward Earth make the bursts look especially bright."],["Afterglows","After the gamma rays fade, the jets crash into surrounding gas, producing afterglows in X-rays, visible light and radio waves that can last for days or weeks. NASA’s Swift satellite, launched in 2004, quickly points its telescopes at new bursts to catch afterglows."],["GW170817","On 17 August 2017, gravitational-wave detectors recorded two neutron stars merging about 130 million light-years away, and less than two seconds later a short gamma-ray burst was detected. Telescopes worldwide then observed a ‘kilonova’ that created heavy elements such as gold and platinum."],["The BOAT","GRB 221009A, seen in October 2022, was so bright that it disturbed Earth’s upper atmosphere and blinded many gamma-ray detectors. Astronomers estimate a burst that bright happens only about once every 10,000 years."]],
k:[["Light type","Gamma rays"],["Discovered","1967 by Vela satellites (announced 1973)"],["Long bursts","Collapsing massive stars"],["Short bursts","Merging neutron stars"],["Brightest seen","GRB 221009A, 2022"]],
d:[["1967","Vela satellites detect the first gamma-ray burst"],["1973","Discovery announced"],["1997","BeppoSAX finds an afterglow, proving bursts are distant"],["2004","Swift satellite launched"],["2017","Short burst seen with gravitational waves"],["2022","The BOAT, the brightest burst ever seen"]],
s2:[["A danger to life?","If a gamma-ray burst occurred within a few thousand light-years and pointed at Earth, its radiation could destroy much of the ozone layer, exposing life to harmful ultraviolet light. Some scientists have suggested a burst might have contributed to a mass extinction about 440 million years ago, though there is no direct evidence."],["Distant beacons","Because they are so bright, gamma-ray bursts can be seen across almost the whole observable universe. GRB 090423, detected in 2009, exploded when the universe was only about 630 million years old."]],
sp:["Spy satellites","The Vela satellites were launched by the United States after the 1963 treaty banning nuclear tests in the atmosphere and space. Their detectors recorded unexpected gamma-ray flashes that did not match nuclear explosions; scientists kept the discovery secret until they were sure the flashes came from space."],
g:[["Gamma ray","The most energetic form of light"],["Afterglow","Fading light after a burst"],["Kilonova","An explosion from merging neutron stars"],["Jet","A narrow, fast stream of matter"]],
f:["Gamma-ray bursts are the most powerful explosions known.","They were found by satellites watching for nuclear tests.","The brightest burst ever seen was in 2022."],
fun:"A single gamma-ray burst can briefly outshine all the other stars in the universe put together, as seen in gamma rays."});

ADDART({id:"cosmicrays",t:"Cosmic Rays",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"⚛️",w:"Cosmic ray",r:["radioact","sun","atom","carbondating","supernova"],
p:[["Particles from space","Cosmic rays are fast-moving particles that rain on Earth from space."],["Discovered by balloon","Victor Hess discovered them on a balloon flight in 1912."],["Mysterious origins","The most energetic cosmic rays come from unknown sources."]],
l:"Cosmic rays are high-energy particles, mostly protons and the nuclei of atoms, that travel through space at nearly the speed of light and constantly strike Earth’s atmosphere. Despite the name, they are not rays of light. Austrian physicist Victor Hess discovered them in 1912, when he carried radiation detectors on balloon flights and found that radiation increased with altitude, showing that it came from space; he shared the 1936 Nobel Prize in Physics. Cosmic rays come from the Sun, from supernovae and other violent events in our galaxy, and the most energetic come from beyond the Milky Way. When they hit atoms in the upper atmosphere, they create showers of secondary particles, such as muons, millions of which pass through each of us every day. Cosmic rays produce the carbon-14 used in radiocarbon dating, can flip bits in computer memory, and are a radiation risk for astronauts and airline crews. The ‘Oh-My-God particle’ detected in 1991 and the ‘Amaterasu particle’ announced in 2023 had energies millions of times greater than any particle accelerator can produce.",
s:[["Air showers","A single cosmic ray hitting the atmosphere can create a cascade of billions of particles spreading over many square kilometers. Large observatories, such as the Pierre Auger Observatory in Argentina, completed in 2008, use detectors spread over 3,000 square kilometers to measure these showers."],["Where they come from","Lower-energy cosmic rays come from the Sun, especially during solar flares. Most others are thought to be accelerated by shock waves from supernova explosions. The origins of the highest-energy ‘ultra-high-energy’ cosmic rays, which may come from distant active galaxies, remain uncertain because magnetic fields bend their paths."],["Effects on Earth","Earth’s magnetic field and atmosphere shield us from most cosmic radiation. Air crews on long high-altitude flights receive higher doses than people on the ground. Cosmic rays occasionally cause errors in computer chips, which matters for aircraft and spacecraft electronics."],["Muon imaging","Muons from cosmic rays can pass through rock. Scientists use them like X-rays to image the insides of volcanoes and pyramids. In 2017, muon imaging revealed a large hidden void inside the Great Pyramid of Giza."]],
k:[["Made of","Mostly protons and atomic nuclei"],["Discovered","Victor Hess, 1912"],["Nobel Prize","1936"],["Most famous particle","Oh-My-God particle, 1991"],["Uses","Carbon-14 dating, muon imaging"]],
d:[["1912","Victor Hess discovers cosmic rays by balloon"],["1936","Hess shares the Nobel Prize in Physics"],["1991","Oh-My-God particle detected in Utah"],["2008","Pierre Auger Observatory completed"],["2017","Muons reveal a void in the Great Pyramid"],["2023","Amaterasu particle announced"]],
s2:[["The Oh-My-God particle","On 15 October 1991, the Fly’s Eye detector in Utah recorded a cosmic ray with an energy of about 3 × 10²⁰ electron volts, equal to a baseball thrown at about 90 kilometers per hour, packed into a single subatomic particle. Scientists still do not know what produced it."],["Cosmic rays and astronauts","Beyond Earth’s magnetic field, astronauts are exposed to far more cosmic radiation, which can damage DNA and increase cancer risk. Protecting crews on long missions to Mars is one of the biggest challenges of space travel."]],
sp:["Seeing cosmic rays","Apollo astronauts reported seeing flashes of light with their eyes closed. Scientists concluded that these were caused by cosmic rays passing through their eyes and striking the retina."],
g:[["Cosmic ray","A high-energy particle from space"],["Muon","A heavier cousin of the electron created in air showers"],["Air shower","A cascade of particles from a cosmic ray impact"],["Electron volt","A unit of energy used for particles"]],
f:["Cosmic rays are particles, not rays of light.","Millions of muons pass through you every day.","Cosmic rays make carbon-14."],
fun:"About one muon created by cosmic rays passes through every square centimeter of Earth’s surface each minute."});

ADDART({id:"ionengines",t:"Ion Engines",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🔵",w:"Ion thruster",r:["rocket","ceres","asteroidbelt","megaconstellations","solarsails"],
p:[["Electric rockets","Ion engines push spacecraft using electrically charged atoms."],["Gentle but tireless","Their thrust is tiny, but they can run for years."],["Deep-space travel","Ion engines carried spacecraft such as Dawn to the asteroid belt."]],
l:"An ion engine, or ion thruster, is a type of electric propulsion that moves spacecraft by accelerating charged atoms, called ions, to very high speeds using electricity. Most ion engines use xenon gas: electrons are knocked off the xenon atoms, giving them a positive charge, and charged grids or magnetic fields shoot them out of the engine at up to about 40 kilometers per second, more than ten times faster than exhaust from chemical rockets. The push is extremely gentle, about the weight of a sheet of paper on a hand, so ion engines cannot lift rockets off the ground. But in space, they can fire for months or years using little fuel, gradually reaching very high speeds. NASA’s Deep Space 1 tested an ion engine on an interplanetary mission in 1998, and the Dawn spacecraft used ion propulsion to orbit two worlds, Vesta and Ceres. Japan’s Hayabusa probes, Europe’s BepiColombo to Mercury and NASA’s Psyche, heading to a metal-rich asteroid, all use electric propulsion, as do thousands of satellites, including many Starlink satellites.",
s:[["How it works","Electricity, usually from solar panels, powers the engine. In a gridded ion engine, xenon is ionized and the ions are pulled through electrically charged grids, which accelerate them. A Hall-effect thruster uses a magnetic field to trap electrons that ionize and accelerate the gas. Electrons are added back to the exhaust so the spacecraft does not build up an electric charge."],["Efficiency","Rocket engineers measure fuel efficiency by ‘specific impulse’. Chemical rockets reach about 450 seconds at best, while ion engines achieve 3,000 seconds or more, meaning they get far more change in speed from each kilogram of propellant."],["Dawn","Dawn, launched in 2007, orbited the giant asteroid Vesta in 2011–2012 and then flew on to the dwarf planet Ceres, which it orbited from 2015 until 2018. It was the first spacecraft to orbit two different bodies beyond Earth, something possible only with its ion engines, which fired for a total of almost six years."],["The future","Ion engines are increasingly used to move satellites into their final orbits and keep them in place. Engineers are developing more powerful electric thrusters, and some cheaper ones use krypton or iodine instead of rare xenon. Nuclear-electric propulsion could one day power crewed missions."]],
k:[["Propellant","Usually xenon"],["Exhaust speed","Up to about 40 km/s"],["Thrust","Very gentle"],["First interplanetary test","Deep Space 1, 1998"],["Famous user","Dawn (Vesta and Ceres)"]],
d:[["1911","Konstantin Tsiolkovsky writes about electric propulsion"],["1959","Harold Kaufman builds an early ion engine at NASA"],["1998","Deep Space 1 launches with an ion engine"],["2003","Japan’s Hayabusa launches with ion engines"],["2007","Dawn launches"],["2023","Psyche launches to a metal-rich asteroid"]],
s2:[["Science fiction","Ion engines appeared in science fiction long before they flew. In Star Wars, the TIE fighters’ name stands for ‘Twin Ion Engines’, and the glowing blue exhaust of real ion engines looks remarkably like the films."],["Psyche","NASA’s Psyche spacecraft, launched in October 2023, uses Hall-effect thrusters to travel to the asteroid 16 Psyche, which may be the exposed metal core of an early planet. It is due to arrive in 2029."]],
sp:["The blue glow","Ion engines produce a ghostly blue glow from excited xenon ions. Photos of the engines being tested in vacuum chambers show beams of soft blue light."],
g:[["Ion","An atom with an electric charge"],["Xenon","A heavy, unreactive gas"],["Specific impulse","A measure of rocket fuel efficiency"],["Hall-effect thruster","An ion engine using magnetic fields"]],
f:["Ion engines push with about the force of a sheet of paper.","Dawn orbited both Vesta and Ceres.","Ion engines usually use xenon gas."],
fun:"Over its mission, Dawn’s ion engines increased its speed by about 11.5 kilometers per second, more than any other spacecraft’s own propulsion at the time."});

;
/* data/modern35.js */
// New articles, 18 November 2026 (part 5): animals and plants.
ADDART({id:"skunks",t:"Skunks",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦨",w:"Skunk",r:["raccoons","foxes","animalbehavior","rabies","owl"],
p:[["Smelly defenders","Skunks spray a foul-smelling liquid to drive off predators."],["Warning stripes","Their black and white coats warn animals to stay away."],["Mostly American","Most skunks live in the Americas."]],
l:"Skunks are small to medium-sized mammals famous for their ability to spray a strong-smelling liquid from glands under their tails. There are about a dozen species, most in North and South America; two species of stink badgers in Indonesia and the Philippines are also in the skunk family. The best known, the striped skunk, ranges across much of North America. Skunks have bold black-and-white coats, a kind of warning coloration that tells predators they are dangerous to attack. When threatened, a skunk stamps its feet, raises its tail and may perform a handstand before spraying; the spray, made of sulfur compounds called thiols, can reach about 3 meters and can be smelled more than a kilometer away. Skunks are omnivores, eating insects, grubs, small animals, eggs, fruit and garbage, and help farmers by eating pests. Great horned owls, which have a poor sense of smell, are among their few regular predators. Skunks can carry rabies.",
s:[["The spray","A skunk has two glands that can each hold enough spray for several shots, and it can aim accurately. Refilling takes about ten days, so skunks spray only as a last resort. The spray can cause temporary blindness and nausea if it hits the eyes."],["Getting rid of the smell","Tomato juice baths are a popular myth; they only mask the smell. Chemist Paul Krebaum developed a mixture of hydrogen peroxide, baking soda and dish soap in 1993 that breaks down the thiols. It must be mixed fresh and used carefully."],["Life","Skunks are mostly active at night and shelter in burrows, hollow logs or under buildings. In cold regions they become inactive for long periods in winter. Females give birth to litters of about four to seven kits in spring."],["Skunks and people","Skunks often live near people, digging lawns for grubs. Some are kept as pets with their scent glands removed, though this is illegal or discouraged in many places. In parts of the United States, skunks are among the main carriers of rabies."]],
k:[["Species","About 12 (plus stink badgers)"],["Range","Mostly the Americas"],["Spray range","About 3 m"],["Smell chemicals","Thiols"],["Main predator","Great horned owl"]],
d:[["1630s","European settlers adopt the Algonquian word ‘skunk’"],["1945","Cartoon skunk Pepé Le Pew first appears"],["1993","Krebaum publishes a skunk-odor remedy"],["1997","Genetic studies place skunks in their own family"]],
s2:[["A family of their own","Skunks were once classified with weasels, but genetic studies in the 1990s showed they belong to their own family, Mephitidae. Their closest relatives include the stink badgers of Southeast Asia."],["Spotted skunks","Spotted skunks do a handstand on their front paws, raising their back legs and tail toward a threat before spraying, a dramatic warning display."]],
sp:["Owls and skunks","Great horned owls hunt skunks because birds of prey have a poor sense of smell. Their nests sometimes reek of skunk spray."],
g:[["Warning coloration","Bold colors that warn predators"],["Thiol","A sulfur compound with a strong smell"],["Omnivore","An animal that eats plants and animals"],["Kit","A young skunk"]],
f:["Skunk spray can be smelled over a kilometer away.","Tomato juice does not remove skunk smell.","Great horned owls hunt skunks."],
fun:"Skunks give plenty of warning before spraying, stamping their feet and hissing, because their spray takes days to refill."});

ADDART({id:"porcupines",t:"Porcupines",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦔",w:"Porcupine",r:["rodents","hedgehogs","trees","animalbehavior","kenyacountry"],
p:[["Spiky rodents","Porcupines are rodents covered in sharp quills."],["They cannot shoot quills","Quills detach easily but are not thrown."],["Two families","Old World and New World porcupines evolved quills separately."]],
l:"Porcupines are large rodents protected by quills, stiff hairs modified into sharp spines. There are two groups: Old World porcupines of Africa, southern Europe and Asia, which live on the ground, and New World porcupines of the Americas, many of which climb trees. Although they look alike, the two groups evolved quills independently. The North American porcupine may have about 30,000 quills, each with microscopic backward-facing barbs that make it hard to remove once stuck in skin. Porcupines cannot shoot their quills, as an old myth claims, but quills detach easily when touched, and porcupines may swing their tails or charge backward at attackers. Before attacking, they warn predators by raising quills, rattling them and releasing a strong smell. Porcupines are plant-eaters, feeding on bark, leaves, roots, fruit and crops. Predators such as fishers, mountain lions and leopards have learned to flip porcupines or attack their unprotected faces and bellies.",
s:[["Quills","Quills grow back after they are lost. Scientists studying North American porcupine quills found that their barbs help them penetrate tissue with less force and grip strongly, inspiring designs for medical staples and needles. Quills also contain a natural antibiotic coating, possibly because porcupines sometimes fall from trees and stab themselves."],["Old World porcupines","The crested porcupine of Africa and Italy is one of the largest rodents, weighing up to about 27 kilograms, with quills up to 35 centimeters long. It lives in burrows and forages at night, sometimes raiding crops."],["New World porcupines","North American porcupines climb trees to eat bark in winter and leaves in summer. South American prehensile-tailed porcupines use their tails to grip branches. New World porcupines are more closely related to guinea pigs and chinchillas than to Old World porcupines."],["Porcupines and people","In some regions, porcupines damage trees and crops. Quills have been used in Native American quillwork, dyed and sewn into designs on clothing and bags. Porcupine meat is eaten in parts of Africa and Asia."]],
k:[["Group","Rodents"],["Quills (North American)","About 30,000"],["Shoots quills?","No"],["Largest","Crested porcupine, up to about 27 kg"],["Two families","Old World and New World"]],
d:[["c. 30 million years ago","New World porcupines’ ancestors reach South America"],["1758","Linnaeus names the crested porcupine"],["2012","Study explains how quill barbs grip skin"]],
s2:[["Porcupine love","Female North American porcupines are ready to mate for only about 8 to 12 hours a year, so males compete fiercely. Babies, called porcupettes, are born with soft quills that harden within an hour."],["Quillwork","Before glass beads became available through trade, many Plains and Great Lakes peoples decorated clothing, moccasins and bags with flattened, dyed porcupine quills, an art still practiced today."]],
sp:["Fishers","The fisher, a forest weasel of North America, is one of the few predators that regularly kills porcupines, circling them to attack the face repeatedly until the porcupine is exhausted."],
g:[["Quill","A sharp spine made from modified hair"],["Barb","A backward-pointing hook"],["Prehensile","Able to grasp"],["Porcupette","A baby porcupine"]],
f:["Porcupines cannot shoot their quills.","A North American porcupine has about 30,000 quills.","Porcupine quills have tiny barbs."],
fun:"The word porcupine comes from Latin words meaning ‘spiny pig’, though porcupines are rodents, not pigs."});

ADDART({id:"opossums",t:"Opossums",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐀",w:"Opossum",r:["kangaroo","koalas","snake","rabies","animalbehavior"],
p:[["America’s marsupials","Opossums are the only marsupials north of Mexico."],["Playing dead","When frightened, opossums may faint and appear dead."],["Venom resistant","Many opossums are resistant to snake venom."]],
l:"Opossums are marsupials, mammals that carry their young in a pouch, found in the Americas. There are more than 100 species, most in Central and South America, but the Virginia opossum, about the size of a house cat, has spread across much of the United States and southern Canada and is the only marsupial living north of Mexico. Opossums have pointed faces, hairless tails that can grip branches, and 50 teeth, more than any other land mammal in North America. Females give birth after only about 13 days of pregnancy to tiny young the size of honeybees, which crawl into the pouch and later ride on their mother’s back. Opossums are famous for ‘playing possum’: when badly frightened, they may fall into an involuntary state resembling death, lying stiff with their mouths open and releasing a foul smell. They eat almost anything, including insects, snails, fruit, carrion and ticks. Many opossums are resistant to snake venom, and they rarely carry rabies because of their low body temperature.",
s:[["Playing possum","Playing dead, or thanatosis, is an automatic response, not a deliberate trick. An opossum may stay ‘dead’ for minutes to hours, its heart rate slowing. Many predators lose interest in prey that seems dead and smells rotten."],["Venom resistance","Opossums have a protein in their blood that neutralizes venom from rattlesnakes and other pit vipers. Scientists have studied it as a possible basis for a universal antivenom."],["Ticks","A 2009 study suggested opossums groom off and eat many ticks, which might reduce tick-borne diseases. Later research found the effect was likely smaller than first reported, though opossums do eat ticks."],["Ancient survivors","Opossum-like marsupials have lived in the Americas for tens of millions of years. Australian marsupials such as kangaroos and koalas are thought to have descended from South American ancestors that crossed via Antarctica when the continents were joined."]],
k:[["Type","Marsupial"],["Species","More than 100"],["Northernmost","Virginia opossum"],["Teeth","50"],["Famous behavior","Playing dead"]],
d:[["1608","John Smith records the Algonquian word ‘opassom’"],["1700s","Opossum described by European naturalists"],["1900s","Virginia opossum spreads north into Canada"],["2009","Study suggests opossums eat many ticks"]],
s2:[["Possum or opossum?","In North America, ‘possum’ is a common short form of opossum. In Australia, ‘possum’ refers to different marsupials, such as brushtail possums, which are only distantly related."],["Possum Pie and folk culture","Opossums appear in Native American and African American folk tales, often as tricksters. Opossum meat was traditionally eaten in parts of the southern United States."]],
sp:["Short lives","Despite their survival skills, Virginia opossums live only about two to four years in the wild, a very short life for a mammal of their size."],
g:[["Marsupial","A mammal that carries its young in a pouch"],["Thanatosis","Playing dead"],["Carrion","Dead animals eaten as food"],["Antivenom","A treatment for venomous bites"]],
f:["Opossums are North America’s only marsupials.","Opossums have 50 teeth.","Many opossums resist snake venom."],
fun:"A newborn opossum is so small that an entire litter could fit in a teaspoon."});

ADDART({id:"badgers",t:"Badgers",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦡",w:"Badger",r:["otters","foxes","ukcountry","cattle","animalbehavior"],
p:[["Champion diggers","Badgers are stocky, powerful diggers of the weasel family."],["Underground homes","European badgers live in tunnel systems called setts."],["The fearless honey badger","The honey badger is famous for its toughness."]],
l:"Badgers are short-legged, heavily built mammals of the weasel family, known for their strong claws and digging skills. Several kinds live in Europe, Asia, Africa and North America. The European badger, with its black-and-white striped face, lives in family groups in large underground burrow systems called setts, which may be used for decades or even centuries and can have dozens of entrances. The American badger is a more solitary hunter of ground squirrels and prairie dogs, sometimes teaming up with coyotes. The honey badger, or ratel, of Africa and Asia, is not a close relative of other badgers but is famous for its toughness, thick loose skin and habit of raiding beehives and fighting venomous snakes. Badgers are omnivores, eating earthworms, insects, small mammals, roots and fruit. In Britain and Ireland, badgers can carry bovine tuberculosis, and government culls to protect cattle have been highly controversial.",
s:[["Setts","A large European badger sett can have tunnels hundreds of meters long and many chambers. Badgers carry fresh bedding of grass and leaves into their setts and remove old bedding. Some setts in Britain are thought to be hundreds of years old."],["Diet","European badgers eat mostly earthworms, sometimes several hundred in a night. They dig up wasp and bee nests, eat fruit and bulbs, and occasionally take hedgehogs."],["Honey badgers","Honey badgers have skin so thick and loose that they can twist around to bite an attacker holding them. They eat snakes, rodents, insects and the larvae and honey of bees. A long-told story that they follow honeyguide birds to hives is debated by scientists."],["Badgers and cattle","Bovine tuberculosis infects cattle in Britain and Ireland, and badgers can spread it. From 2013, England allowed large-scale badger culls, killing more than 230,000 badgers. Many scientists and campaigners opposed the culls, and the government announced plans to end them in favor of vaccination."]],
k:[["Family","Weasels (Mustelidae)"],["European home","Sett"],["Main European food","Earthworms"],["Famously tough","Honey badger"],["Controversy","Culling to control bovine TB"]],
d:[["1835","Badger baiting banned in Britain"],["1973","Badgers Act protects badgers in Britain"],["1992","Protection of Badgers Act"],["2013","Badger culling begins in England"],["2025","England announces plans to end culling"]],
s2:[["Badgers in stories","Badgers appear in many children’s stories, including Mr. Badger in The Wind in the Willows (1908), a wise and gruff character. In Japanese folklore, the tanuki, a raccoon dog, is sometimes translated as ‘badger’."],["Badger baiting","Badger baiting, in which dogs were set on captured badgers, was a cruel blood sport banned in Britain in 1835, though illegal cases still occur. Badgers are now strongly protected by law in the United Kingdom."]],
sp:["Coyote partners","In North America, coyotes and American badgers sometimes hunt together. The badger digs after ground squirrels while the coyote catches those that run out; studies show both catch more prey when working together."],
g:[["Sett","A European badger’s burrow system"],["Mustelid","A member of the weasel family"],["Bovine tuberculosis","A bacterial disease of cattle"],["Cull","Killing animals to reduce their numbers"]],
f:["Badgers can eat hundreds of earthworms in a night.","Some badger setts are centuries old.","Coyotes and badgers sometimes hunt together."],
fun:"A shaving brush made from badger hair was once considered the finest kind, though synthetic brushes are now common."});

ADDART({id:"waterfowl",t:"Ducks, Geese and Swans",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦢",w:"Anatidae",r:["birds","birdmigr","wetlands","domestication","chickens"],
p:[["Waterbirds","Ducks, geese and swans are waterbirds with webbed feet and flat bills."],["Great migrations","Many fly thousands of kilometers between breeding and wintering grounds."],["Domesticated long ago","Ducks and geese were domesticated for meat, eggs and feathers."]],
l:"Ducks, geese and swans make up the family Anatidae, a group of about 170 species of waterbirds found on every continent except Antarctica. They have webbed feet for swimming, waterproof feathers kept oiled by a gland near the tail, and broad, flat bills, many with comb-like edges for filtering food from water. Ducks are generally the smallest, with males often brightly colored; geese are larger, graze on land and often fly in V-shaped formations; and swans are the largest, with long necks for reaching underwater plants. The trumpeter swan of North America is the heaviest flying bird native to that continent. Many species migrate long distances: some geese fly from the Arctic to wintering grounds thousands of kilometers south, and bar-headed geese fly over the Himalayas. Ducklings and goslings can swim soon after hatching and famously imprint on the first moving object they see. Ducks and geese were domesticated thousands of years ago, and wild waterfowl are important to hunting and conservation.",
s:[["Flying in V formation","Geese flying in a V save energy by riding in the rising air created by the wingtips of the bird ahead, and they take turns leading. A 2014 study of ibises with GPS loggers confirmed that birds carefully time their wingbeats to gain this benefit."],["Imprinting","Austrian scientist Konrad Lorenz showed in the 1930s that newly hatched goslings would follow the first moving object they saw, even Lorenz himself. This rapid learning, called imprinting, helps young birds stay with their mothers. Lorenz shared the 1973 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine."],["Domestic ducks and geese","Most domestic ducks descend from the mallard, except the Muscovy duck from South America. Domestic geese descend from the graylag goose and the swan goose. Geese were kept as guards in ancient Rome, where, according to legend, sacred geese warned the city of a night attack in 390 BC."],["Conservation","Wetland loss, hunting and lead poisoning from shot have threatened waterfowl, but conservation programs have helped many recover. In North America, hunters’ fees for ‘duck stamps’ since 1934 have funded the protection of millions of acres of wetlands. Bird flu has killed large numbers of wild geese and swans since 2020."]],
k:[["Family","Anatidae"],["Species","About 170"],["Feet","Webbed"],["Famous behavior","Imprinting"],["Domestic duck ancestor","Mallard (mostly)"]],
d:[["390 BC","Sacred geese warn Rome of an attack, by legend"],["c. 500 BC","Ducks domesticated in China (estimated)"],["1934","US federal duck stamps begin funding wetlands"],["1935","Konrad Lorenz describes imprinting"],["1973","Lorenz shares a Nobel Prize"]],
s2:[["Mute swans and the Crown","In England, unmarked mute swans on open water have traditionally been claimed by the monarch. Every July, ‘Swan Upping’ on the River Thames counts and checks cygnets, a custom dating back to the 12th century."],["Why ducks’ feet don’t freeze","Ducks standing on ice keep their feet just above freezing using a ‘countercurrent’ heat exchange: warm blood flowing down to the feet passes cold blood returning to the body, transferring heat back before it is lost."]],
sp:["Bar-headed geese","Bar-headed geese migrate between Central Asia and India, crossing the Himalayas at altitudes of up to about 7,000 meters, where oxygen is scarce. Their blood and lungs are specially adapted to thin air."],
g:[["Waterfowl","Ducks, geese and swans"],["Imprinting","Rapid learning to follow a parent soon after hatching"],["Cygnet","A young swan"],["Gosling","A young goose"]],
f:["Geese save energy by flying in a V.","Most domestic ducks come from mallards.","Bar-headed geese fly over the Himalayas."],
fun:"A duck’s quack does echo, despite a popular myth that it does not."});

ADDART({id:"storks",t:"Storks",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🪿",w:"Stork",r:["birds","birdmigr","wetlands","pelicans","waterfowl","herons"],
p:[["Long-legged wading birds","Storks are large birds with long legs, necks and bills."],["Great travelers","White storks migrate between Europe and Africa every year."],["Bringers of babies","European folklore says storks deliver babies."]],
l:"Storks are large, long-legged wading birds with long necks and long, stout bills, found in warm and temperate regions around the world. There are about 20 species, including the white stork of Europe, Africa and Asia, the marabou stork of Africa, the jabiru of the Americas and the shoebill-like saddle-billed stork. Storks feed on fish, frogs, insects, small mammals and reptiles, which they catch while walking through wetlands and fields. Most storks have no voice box and communicate by clattering their bills loudly. White storks are famous for nesting on rooftops, chimneys and church towers in Europe and for their long migrations: each autumn, hundreds of thousands fly to Africa, avoiding long sea crossings by funneling over the Strait of Gibraltar and through Israel, and return in spring. Because they return around the time of spring births, European folklore made storks the bringers of babies. White storks declined in the 20th century but have recovered through reintroduction and wetland protection.",
s:[["Migration routes","Storks are soaring birds that ride thermals, rising columns of warm air, which form over land but not over the sea. Western populations cross at Gibraltar, eastern ones over the Bosporus and Israel. Some storks now winter in Spain and Portugal, feeding at landfill sites instead of crossing to Africa."],["The Pfeilstorch","In 1822, a white stork was shot in Germany with a 75-centimeter African spear through its neck. This ‘arrow stork’, or Pfeilstorch, provided proof that birds migrate to Africa, at a time when some people believed birds hibernated in winter or turned into other animals."],["Rooftop nests","White storks build huge nests of sticks, sometimes more than 2 meters across, reused and added to year after year. Many towns in Europe provide nesting platforms, and storks are considered lucky. Poland has about a quarter of the world’s white storks."],["Marabou storks","The marabou stork of Africa, one of the largest flying birds, with a wingspan of up to about 3 meters, often scavenges with vultures and gathers at rubbish dumps. Its bald head and throat pouch give it the nickname ‘undertaker bird’."]],
k:[["Species","About 20"],["Communication","Bill clattering"],["Famous migrant","White stork"],["Folklore","Bringing babies"],["Proof of migration","The 1822 ‘arrow stork’"]],
d:[["1822","An ‘arrow stork’ proves migration to Africa"],["1838","Hans Christian Andersen publishes ‘The Storks’"],["1980s","White storks reach their lowest numbers in western Europe"],["2016","Storks returned to England after centuries"],["2020","First wild white stork chicks hatch in England in over 600 years"]],
s2:[["Storks and babies","The legend that storks deliver babies spread through German and Dutch folklore and Hans Christian Andersen’s tale ‘The Storks’ (1838). It became a popular image on birth announcements and cards."],["Return to Britain","White storks had not bred wild in Britain since 1416. A reintroduction project at the Knepp estate in Sussex began in 2016, and in 2020 the first wild-born chicks hatched there."]],
sp:["Storks and statistics","Statisticians use storks to explain that correlation does not equal causation: in some regions, the number of storks and the number of human births have risen and fallen together, but storks do not bring babies."],
g:[["Wading bird","A bird that feeds by walking in shallow water"],["Thermal","A rising column of warm air"],["Migration","Seasonal travel between regions"],["Correlation","A link between two changing things"]],
f:["Most storks have no voice and clatter their bills.","A stork shot with an African spear proved migration.","Storks returned to breed in England in 2020."],
fun:"White storks can travel more than 10,000 kilometers each way between Europe and southern Africa."});

ADDART({id:"cuttlefish",t:"Cuttlefish",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦑",w:"Cuttlefish",r:["octopus","squid","mollusc","eye","animalbehavior"],
p:[["Masters of disguise","Cuttlefish change color and texture in an instant."],["Not fish","Cuttlefish are mollusks related to squid and octopuses."],["Clever animals","They can pass self-control tests similar to children’s."]],
l:"Cuttlefish are marine mollusks belonging to the cephalopods, the group that includes squid and octopuses. There are about 120 species, living in shallow seas of Europe, Africa, Asia and Australia, but none in the Americas. A cuttlefish has eight arms and two longer feeding tentacles, a fin running around its body, and an internal shell called a cuttlebone, which is filled with gas and liquid to control buoyancy; dried cuttlebones are sold as calcium supplements for pet birds. Cuttlefish are famous for camouflage: their skin contains millions of pigment cells called chromatophores, controlled by the brain, that let them change color and pattern in a fraction of a second, and muscles that raise bumps to change skin texture. They hunt crabs, shrimp and fish, sometimes hypnotizing prey with rippling patterns. Cuttlefish have W-shaped pupils, three hearts and blue-green blood. Experiments show they are intelligent, with good memory and self-control.",
s:[["Camouflage and display","Cuttlefish blend into rocks, sand and seaweed and use dramatic flashing patterns to court mates and warn rivals. Strangely, they are probably colorblind; scientists are still studying how they match colors so well, perhaps using their unusual pupils."],["Clever tricks","Small male cuttlefish sometimes disguise themselves as females to sneak past larger males and mate. A 2021 study found that cuttlefish could wait for a preferred snack rather than eat a less-liked one immediately, a version of the famous ‘marshmallow test’ of self-control."],["The giant cuttlefish","Each winter, thousands of giant cuttlefish, up to about 50 centimeters long, gather to breed in shallow water near Whyalla in South Australia, one of the largest gatherings of cephalopods in the world and a popular site for divers."],["Sepia","Cuttlefish ink, called sepia, was used for writing and drawing, and its brown color gave its name to sepia-toned photographs. Cuttlefish are eaten in Mediterranean and Asian cuisines, and their ink colors pasta and rice black."]],
k:[["Group","Cephalopods (mollusks)"],["Species","About 120"],["Internal shell","Cuttlebone"],["Hearts","Three"],["Pupils","W-shaped"]],
d:[["c. 350 BC","Aristotle describes cuttlefish behavior"],["1700s","Sepia ink widely used for drawing"],["2008","Study shows cuttlefish copy backgrounds with patterns"],["2021","Cuttlefish pass a self-control test"]],
s2:[["The flamboyant cuttlefish","The small flamboyant cuttlefish of the Indo-Pacific walks along the seafloor on its arms, flashing purple, yellow and red. Its flesh contains a toxin, and its bright colors warn predators."],["Short lives","Despite their intelligence, most cuttlefish live only one to two years, dying soon after breeding."]],
sp:["W-shaped pupils","A cuttlefish’s W-shaped pupil may help control light coming from above and below, letting it see contrasts in the uneven lighting of shallow seas."],
g:[["Cephalopod","A mollusk with arms, like an octopus"],["Chromatophore","A color-changing skin cell"],["Cuttlebone","The internal shell of a cuttlefish"],["Sepia","Brown ink made from cuttlefish"]],
f:["Cuttlefish have three hearts.","Cuttlefish are not fish.","Cuttlefish passed a version of the marshmallow test."],
fun:"Cuttlefish can display a different pattern on each side of their body, courting a female on one side while warning a male on the other."});

ADDART({id:"snowleopards",t:"Snow Leopards",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐆",w:"Snow leopard",r:["leopards","himal","tiger","conservation","climchange"],
p:[["Ghost of the mountains","Snow leopards live high in the mountains of Central and South Asia."],["Built for cold","Thick fur, wide paws and a long tail help them survive."],["Rarely seen","Only about 4,000 to 6,500 remain in the wild."]],
l:"The snow leopard is a large wild cat that lives in the high mountains of Central and South Asia, including the Himalayas, the Tibetan Plateau, the Pamirs, the Tian Shan and the Altai, across 12 countries from Afghanistan to Mongolia and China. It usually lives between about 3,000 and 5,500 meters above sea level. Snow leopards have thick, smoky-gray fur with dark rosettes, which camouflages them among rocks and snow; wide, furry paws that act like snowshoes; and an extremely long, thick tail that helps them balance on steep slopes and that they wrap around themselves for warmth. They hunt wild sheep and goats such as blue sheep and ibex, sometimes leaping several meters, but also kill livestock, which brings them into conflict with herders. Snow leopards cannot roar. They are so elusive that they are called ‘ghosts of the mountains’. Scientists estimate that about 4,000 to 6,500 remain in the wild, threatened by poaching, retaliatory killing, loss of prey and climate change.",
s:[["Adaptations","Snow leopards have large nasal cavities that warm cold air and big lungs for thin air. Their tails can be nearly as long as their bodies. They are agile climbers and can leap about 9 meters horizontally across ravines."],["Hunting","A snow leopard typically kills a large animal every week or two and may spend several days eating it. In some areas, livestock make up a significant part of its diet, especially where wild prey has declined."],["Counting snow leopards","Because snow leopards are so hard to see, scientists count them using camera traps, which identify individuals by their unique spot patterns, and DNA from droppings. Recent surveys estimated about 400 in Nepal and more than 700 in India."],["Conservation","Community programs pay herders for livestock lost to snow leopards, insure herds and build predator-proof corrals, reducing revenge killings. In 2017, the snow leopard was moved from endangered to vulnerable on the IUCN Red List, though some scientists argued this was premature. Warming may shrink its high-mountain habitat."]],
k:[["Range","Mountains of Central and South Asia"],["Altitude","About 3,000–5,500 m"],["Wild population","About 4,000–6,500"],["Status","Vulnerable"],["Can it roar?","No"]],
d:[["1775","Snow leopard described by Western scientists"],["1970s","George Schaller’s studies bring attention to snow leopards"],["1978","Peter Matthiessen publishes The Snow Leopard"],["2013","Bishkek Declaration by 12 range countries"],["2017","Status changed from endangered to vulnerable"]],
s2:[["The Snow Leopard","In 1973, biologist George Schaller and writer Peter Matthiessen trekked into the Nepalese Himalayas hoping to see a snow leopard. Matthiessen’s book The Snow Leopard (1978) became a classic, although he never saw one."],["A cat that cannot roar","Unlike lions and tigers, snow leopards have a vocal structure that does not allow roaring. They communicate with mews, hisses, growls and a puffing sound called a chuff."]],
sp:["Ghosts on camera","Wildlife filmmakers spent years trying to film snow leopards. Footage of a snow leopard hunting a markhor on a cliff, filmed for the BBC series Planet Earth in 2006, was among the first close-up views of the cats hunting in the wild."],
g:[["Camera trap","A camera triggered by passing animals"],["Rosette","A rose-shaped spot pattern"],["Retaliatory killing","Killing an animal in revenge for livestock losses"],["Vulnerable","At high risk of becoming endangered"]],
f:["Snow leopards cannot roar.","A snow leopard’s tail can be almost as long as its body.","About 4,000 to 6,500 snow leopards remain in the wild."],
fun:"Snow leopards sometimes wrap their long, furry tails around their faces like scarves while sleeping."});

ADDART({id:"redpandas",t:"Red Pandas",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐼",w:"Red panda",r:["panda","himal","bamboo","conservation","chinacountry"],
p:[["The first panda","The red panda was named a ‘panda’ before the giant panda."],["Bamboo eater","Like the giant panda, it eats mostly bamboo."],["Endangered","Fewer than 10,000 remain in the wild."]],
l:"The red panda is a small mammal, about the size of a large house cat, with reddish-brown fur, a white-marked face and a long, bushy, ringed tail. It lives in the cool temperate forests of the eastern Himalayas and southwestern China, in Nepal, India, Bhutan, Myanmar and China, at altitudes of about 2,200 to 4,800 meters. Red pandas spend most of their time in trees, using their tails for balance and wrapping them around themselves for warmth. Although they belong to the order of carnivores, they eat mainly bamboo leaves and shoots, as well as fruit, acorns and eggs, and they have a ‘false thumb’, an enlarged wrist bone that helps them grip bamboo. The red panda was described by Western scientists in 1825, almost 50 years before the giant panda, and was the original ‘panda’. Despite their similar names and diets, red pandas are not closely related to giant pandas; they belong to their own family, Ailuridae. Red pandas are endangered, with fewer than 10,000 thought to remain, because of habitat loss, poaching and the pet trade.",
s:[["Two species?","A 2020 genetic study suggested that there are two species: the Himalayan red panda and the Chinese red panda, divided by a river in eastern Tibet. The Chinese form has a redder face and more distinct tail rings. Not all scientists agree."],["Living on bamboo","Bamboo is low in nutrients, so red pandas eat large amounts, up to about 20 to 30 percent of their body weight a day, and spend much of their time resting to save energy. They can lower their metabolism in cold weather, curling up in trees."],["Not giant pandas’ cousins","Red pandas were once classified with raccoons, then with bears. Genetic studies show they are in their own family, most closely related to skunks, raccoons and weasels. Both pandas evolved false thumbs separately to handle bamboo."],["Conservation","Red pandas are threatened by forest clearing for farming, roads and grazing, and by traps set for other animals. Community forest guardians in Nepal monitor red pandas and protect habitat. Zoos run breeding programs, and the first International Red Panda Day was held in 2010."]],
k:[["Range","Eastern Himalayas and southwestern China"],["Main food","Bamboo"],["Family","Ailuridae (its own family)"],["Described","1825"],["Status","Endangered"]],
d:[["1825","Red panda described by Frédéric Cuvier"],["1869","Giant panda described, taking the ‘panda’ name"],["2010","First International Red Panda Day"],["2015","Red panda listed as endangered"],["2020","Study suggests two species"]],
s2:[["The name panda","The word panda may come from a Nepali word, sometimes said to mean ‘bamboo eater’. When the giant panda was described in 1869, it was named after the red panda, and the red panda later became known as the ‘lesser panda’."],["Firefox","Red pandas are sometimes called ‘firefoxes’, a name that may translate a Chinese name for them. The Firefox web browser’s logo, though widely thought to show a fox, was inspired by the red panda’s nickname."]],
sp:["Rusty the escape artist","In 2013, a red panda named Rusty escaped from the National Zoo in Washington, D.C., and was found a day later in a nearby neighborhood, becoming a social media sensation."],
g:[["False thumb","An enlarged wrist bone used for gripping"],["Metabolism","The body’s energy use"],["Carnivoran","A member of the order Carnivora"],["Endangered","At high risk of extinction"]],
f:["The red panda was called a panda first.","Red pandas are not related closely to giant pandas.","Red pandas eat mostly bamboo."],
fun:"When threatened, red pandas stand up on their hind legs and raise their front paws, trying to look bigger."});

ADDART({id:"cassowaries",t:"Cassowaries",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦤",w:"Cassowary",r:["ostrich","australiacountry","kiwis","dino","amazon"],
p:[["Giant forest birds","Cassowaries are huge flightless birds of New Guinea and northern Australia."],["Dangerous claws","Each foot has a dagger-like claw up to 12 centimeters long."],["Rainforest gardeners","They spread the seeds of many rainforest trees."]],
l:"Cassowaries are large flightless birds native to the tropical forests of New Guinea, nearby islands and northeastern Australia. There are three species; the southern cassowary, which can stand about 1.8 meters tall and weigh up to about 70 kilograms, is the second-heaviest bird in the world after the ostrich. Cassowaries have glossy black, hair-like feathers, bright blue and red skin on their heads and necks, and a tall, helmet-like crest called a casque on top of their heads. Their powerful legs end in three-toed feet, the inner toe bearing a sharp claw up to about 12 centimeters long, which they can use to kick in self-defense. Cassowaries eat mostly fallen fruit, swallowing it whole and spreading seeds of more than 200 plant species, so they are important to rainforest health. Males incubate the eggs and raise the striped chicks alone. Cassowaries are often described as the world’s most dangerous birds, but attacks are rare and usually occur when people feed or corner them.",
s:[["The casque","The casque is made of a spongy core covered with keratin. Scientists have suggested it is used for display, to push through vegetation or to make and sense low-frequency sounds. A 2019 study suggested it may also help shed heat."],["Booming calls","Cassowaries make very deep booming calls, some at the lowest frequencies of any bird, close to the limit of human hearing. Such sounds travel well through dense forest."],["Dangerous birds?","A cassowary can run at about 50 kilometers per hour and jump about 1.5 meters high. In 2019, a man in Florida was killed by a captive cassowary. In Australia, most injuries happen when people feed wild cassowaries, which makes them bold."],["Conservation","In Australia, the southern cassowary is endangered, threatened by habitat clearing, road traffic and dog attacks, with only a few thousand left. Cyclones that destroy fruit trees also cause starvation. Road signs warn drivers in cassowary areas."]],
k:[["Range","New Guinea and northeastern Australia"],["Species","Three"],["Rank","Second-heaviest bird"],["Claw length","Up to about 12 cm"],["Who incubates","Males"]],
d:[["1758","Linnaeus names the southern cassowary"],["1926","A cassowary kills a teenager in Australia"],["2006","Cyclone Larry devastates cassowary habitat"],["2019","Captive cassowary kills its owner in Florida"]],
s2:[["Living dinosaurs","Cassowaries are often said to look like dinosaurs. All birds are descended from dinosaurs, and cassowaries’ large size, scaly legs and crests make the link easy to imagine. Their closest relatives are emus."],["Raised in New Guinea","In New Guinea, people have captured and raised cassowary chicks for perhaps 18,000 years, possibly the earliest known example of humans raising birds. Cassowary feathers and bones are used in ceremonies and tools."]],
sp:["Seed spreaders","Some rainforest seeds germinate much better after passing through a cassowary’s gut. Fruits such as the cassowary plum depend largely on the birds to spread them."],
g:[["Casque","A helmet-like crest"],["Flightless","Unable to fly"],["Incubate","To keep eggs warm until hatching"],["Germinate","To begin growing from a seed"]],
f:["Cassowaries are the second-heaviest birds.","Male cassowaries raise the chicks alone.","Cassowaries make some of the deepest bird calls."],
fun:"A cassowary’s eggs are bright green, among the most colorful eggs of any bird."});

ADDART({id:"pineapples",t:"Pineapples",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🍍",w:"Pineapple",r:["fruits","bananas","hawaii","explor","colonialism"],
p:[["From South America","Pineapples were domesticated in South America."],["Many fruits in one","A pineapple forms from dozens of flowers fused together."],["A luxury symbol","In 18th-century Europe, pineapples were a sign of wealth."]],
l:"The pineapple is a tropical plant of the bromeliad family whose fruit is one of the most popular in the world. It originated in South America, probably in the region of southern Brazil and Paraguay, and was spread by Indigenous peoples to the Caribbean, where Christopher Columbus encountered it in 1493. A pineapple is a multiple fruit: about 100 to 200 small flowers on a single stalk each produce a fruit, and these fuse into one juicy fruit with the familiar crisscross pattern. A plant usually produces one pineapple at a time, which takes about 18 to 24 months to grow. Because pineapples were difficult to grow in Europe, they became symbols of luxury and hospitality in the 18th century, grown in heated greenhouses called pineries or even rented for display at parties. Today the largest producers include Costa Rica, the Philippines, Indonesia, Brazil and India. Pineapples contain bromelain, an enzyme that breaks down proteins, which is why fresh pineapple can make the mouth tingle and why it is used to tenderize meat.",
s:[["Growing pineapples","Pineapples are grown from the leafy crown on top of the fruit or from side shoots. Farmers often treat plants with ethylene gas to make them flower at the same time, so that whole fields can be harvested together. Pineapples do not ripen further after picking."],["Pineapples and luxury","King Charles II of England was painted around 1675 receiving a pineapple, then extremely rare. Wealthy Europeans built heated pineapple houses, and pineapple shapes appeared on gateposts, furniture and buildings, such as the Dunmore Pineapple in Scotland, built in 1761."],["Hawaii","Pineapples were grown commercially in Hawaii from the late 19th century. James Dole founded the Hawaiian Pineapple Company in 1901, and the island of Lānaʻi became a giant pineapple plantation. Hawaii once produced most of the world’s canned pineapple, but production moved to cheaper countries from the 1960s."],["Bromelain","Bromelain breaks down proteins, including those on the tongue, causing a tingling feeling. It is destroyed by heat, so canned pineapple does not have this effect. Fresh pineapple also prevents gelatin from setting."]],
k:[["Family","Bromeliads"],["Origin","South America"],["Fruit type","Multiple fruit"],["Growing time","About 18–24 months"],["Enzyme","Bromelain"]],
d:[["1493","Columbus encounters pineapples in the Caribbean"],["c. 1675","Charles II painted receiving a pineapple"],["1761","The Dunmore Pineapple is built in Scotland"],["1901","James Dole founds the Hawaiian Pineapple Company"],["1996","Extra-sweet MD-2 variety released"]],
s2:[["Why ‘pineapple’?","European explorers named the fruit ‘pineapple’ because it resembled a pine cone. In most other languages it is called ananas, from a Tupi word meaning ‘excellent fruit’."],["Pineapple on pizza","Hawaiian pizza, topped with ham and pineapple, was invented in 1962 not in Hawaii but in Canada, by Greek-Canadian restaurant owner Sam Panopoulos. Whether pineapple belongs on pizza remains a popular debate."]],
sp:["Piña cloth","In the Philippines, fibers from pineapple leaves are woven into a delicate, translucent fabric called piña, used for traditional formal shirts such as the barong Tagalog."],
g:[["Multiple fruit","A fruit formed from many flowers fused together"],["Bromeliad","A family of tropical plants"],["Bromelain","An enzyme that breaks down proteins"],["Crown","The leafy top of a pineapple"]],
f:["A pineapple forms from up to 200 fused flowers.","Pineapples take up to two years to grow.","Hawaiian pizza was invented in Canada."],
fun:"In 18th-century Britain, pineapples were so rare that people rented them to show off at dinner parties, then returned them uneaten."});

ADDART({id:"maples",t:"Maple Trees",c:"Plants",i:"🍁",w:"Maple",r:["trees","canadacountry","japan","sugar","oaks","seeddispersal","autumncolors"],
p:[["Autumn colors","Maples are famous for their brilliant red, orange and yellow autumn leaves."],["Maple syrup","Sugar maple sap is boiled down to make maple syrup."],["Helicopter seeds","Their winged seeds spin as they fall."]],
l:"Maples are trees and shrubs of the genus Acer, with about 130 species found mostly in Asia, but also in Europe, North Africa and North America. Most have leaves with several pointed lobes arranged opposite each other on the twigs, and fruits called samaras, pairs of winged seeds that spin like helicopter blades as they fall, carrying them away on the wind. Maples are famous for their autumn colors, especially in eastern North America and Japan: as days shorten, the green chlorophyll in leaves breaks down, revealing yellow and orange pigments, while some maples also produce red pigments called anthocyanins. The sugar maple of eastern North America is tapped in early spring for its sweet sap, which is boiled down to make maple syrup; about 40 liters of sap make 1 liter of syrup. Canada produces about three-quarters of the world’s maple syrup, mostly in Quebec, and the maple leaf is Canada’s national symbol, appearing on its flag since 1965. Maple wood is used for furniture, floors, violins and baseball bats.",
s:[["Maple syrup","Indigenous peoples of northeastern North America made maple sugar long before Europeans arrived. Sap flows best when nights are below freezing and days are above freezing, usually in March and April. Modern producers use plastic tubing and vacuum pumps to collect sap, and evaporators to boil it."],["The great syrup heist","Quebec’s maple syrup producers keep a strategic reserve of syrup to stabilize prices. Between 2011 and 2012, thieves stole about 2,700 metric tons of syrup worth about 18 million Canadian dollars from a warehouse, one of Canada’s largest thefts."],["Japanese maples","Japanese maples, with delicate, finely cut leaves, have been cultivated in Japan for centuries, with hundreds of varieties. Viewing autumn leaves, called momijigari, is a popular tradition, and maple leaves are even fried as a snack in Osaka."],["Maple wood","Hard maple is dense and pale, used for basketball courts, bowling alleys and butcher blocks. Figured maple, with rippling patterns called flame or quilt, is prized for the backs of violins and guitars."]],
k:[["Genus","Acer"],["Species","About 130"],["Seeds","Winged samaras"],["Sap for 1 liter of syrup","About 40 liters"],["Largest syrup producer","Canada (Quebec)"]],
d:[["1600s","Europeans learn maple sugaring from Indigenous peoples"],["1965","Maple leaf flag adopted by Canada"],["2000","Quebec creates a maple syrup reserve"],["2012","Great maple syrup heist discovered"]],
s2:[["Canada’s flag","Canada adopted its red-and-white maple leaf flag on 15 February 1965, replacing a flag based on the British Red Ensign. The 11-pointed leaf is stylized and does not represent a particular species."],["Climate change and syrup","Maple syrup production depends on freeze-thaw cycles in spring. Warmer winters are shifting the sugaring season earlier and could reduce sap quality in southern areas while expanding production northward."]],
sp:["Helicopter seeds","Maple seeds create a small whirlwind above their wing as they spin, which slows their fall and lets the wind carry them farther. Engineers have studied them to design small drones and wind turbines."],
g:[["Samara","A winged seed"],["Sap","The fluid that moves through a plant"],["Anthocyanin","A red or purple plant pigment"],["Chlorophyll","The green pigment that captures light"]],
f:["About 40 liters of sap make 1 liter of maple syrup.","Canada makes about three-quarters of the world’s maple syrup.","Maple seeds spin like helicopters."],
fun:"Thieves stole about 18 million Canadian dollars’ worth of maple syrup from a Quebec warehouse in 2011–2012."});

ADDART({id:"vanilla",t:"Vanilla",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🍦",w:"Vanilla",r:["spice","choco","mexicocountry","madag","icecream"],
p:[["An orchid spice","Vanilla comes from the pods of a climbing orchid."],["Pollinated by hand","Outside Mexico, vanilla flowers must be pollinated by hand."],["Second most expensive spice","Only saffron costs more."]],
l:"Vanilla is a flavoring made from the cured seed pods of orchids of the genus Vanilla, mainly Vanilla planifolia, a climbing vine native to Mexico and Central America. The Totonac people of Mexico were the first to cultivate vanilla, and the Aztecs used it to flavor chocolate drinks; Spanish conquerors brought it to Europe in the 16th century. Outside its native range, vanilla vines would flower but rarely produced pods, because their natural pollinators were absent. In 1841, Edmond Albius, a 12-year-old enslaved boy on the island of Réunion, invented a quick method of hand-pollinating vanilla flowers with a stick and his thumb, which is still used today. After harvest, the green pods are cured for months, sweating and drying until they turn dark brown and develop vanillin and other flavor compounds. Madagascar produces most of the world’s natural vanilla. Because curing is so labor-intensive, vanilla is the second most expensive spice after saffron, and most vanilla flavoring is artificial vanillin made from other sources.",
s:[["Hand pollination","Each vanilla flower opens for only one day. Workers lift a flap separating the male and female parts and press them together. A skilled worker can pollinate more than 1,000 flowers a day. Pods take about nine months to mature."],["Curing","Curing can include blanching the pods in hot water, sweating them in boxes, drying them in the sun and conditioning them for months. The process develops vanillin and hundreds of other aromatic compounds that give natural vanilla its complex flavor."],["Price swings","Vanilla prices are volatile. After a cyclone hit Madagascar in 2017, prices soared above the price of silver by weight, leading to theft from fields; farmers began branding pods with their own marks. Prices later fell sharply."],["Artificial vanilla","Vanillin was first synthesized in 1874 from pine bark compounds and is now made mostly from petrochemicals or from lignin, a byproduct of papermaking. Synthetic vanillin is far cheaper than real vanilla and is used in most ice cream, cakes and perfumes."]],
k:[["Source","Pods of vanilla orchids"],["Origin","Mexico and Central America"],["Largest producer","Madagascar"],["Hand-pollination inventor","Edmond Albius, 1841"],["Key flavor compound","Vanillin"]],
d:[["c. 1400s","Totonac people cultivate vanilla"],["1520s","Spanish bring vanilla to Europe"],["1841","Edmond Albius invents hand pollination"],["1874","Vanillin first synthesized"],["2017","Vanilla prices reach record highs"]],
s2:[["Edmond Albius","Edmond Albius’s method made vanilla farming possible in Réunion, Madagascar and elsewhere, creating great wealth for plantation owners. Albius himself received little reward and died in poverty in 1880. Today he is honored with a statue on Réunion."],["Not so plain","Calling something ‘vanilla’ to mean plain or ordinary is ironic, given that natural vanilla is complex, costly and difficult to produce."]],
sp:["Vanilla and the Totonac","According to a Totonac legend, the vanilla vine grew from the blood of a princess and her lover, who were killed for falling in love. The town of Papantla in Veracruz is known as the city that perfumed the world."],
g:[["Orchid","A large family of flowering plants"],["Curing","Treating a crop to develop flavor"],["Vanillin","The main flavor compound in vanilla"],["Pollination","Moving pollen to fertilize a flower"]],
f:["Vanilla comes from an orchid.","A boy on Réunion invented vanilla hand pollination.","Most vanilla flavoring is artificial."],
fun:"Vanilla pods take about nine months to grow after pollination and several more months to cure, so producing one vanilla bean can take more than a year."});

ADDART({id:"cherryblossoms",t:"Cherry Blossoms",c:"Plants",i:"🌸",w:"Cherry blossom",r:["japan","japancountry","flower","festivals","climchange"],
p:[["Spring flowers","Cherry blossoms are the flowers of ornamental cherry trees."],["Hanami","In Japan, people gather to view the blossoms in spring."],["A climate record","Kyoto’s blossom dates have been recorded for over 1,200 years."]],
l:"Cherry blossoms, called sakura in Japanese, are the flowers of several species of cherry trees in the genus Prunus, most of them grown for their blossoms rather than their fruit. In spring, the trees burst into clouds of pink or white flowers that last only about one or two weeks. In Japan, cherry blossoms are a national symbol, and hanami, the custom of picnicking under the blossoms with friends and family, dates back more than a thousand years. Because they bloom so briefly, cherry blossoms have long symbolized the beauty and fleeting nature of life in Japanese art and poetry. Japan’s weather agency and media track the ‘cherry blossom front’ as it moves north through the country each spring. Japan has given cherry trees to many countries; in 1912, Tokyo gave about 3,000 trees to Washington, D.C., which now holds a National Cherry Blossom Festival. Records of blossom dates in Kyoto, going back to the 9th century, show that the trees now bloom earlier than at any time in the record, because of warming.",
s:[["Varieties","The most common variety in Japan, Somei Yoshino, has pale pink flowers that appear before the leaves. All Somei Yoshino trees are clones descended from trees bred in Edo, now Tokyo, in the 19th century, so they bloom at about the same time in the same place. Other varieties have double flowers or weeping branches."],["Hanami","Hanami began among Japanese aristocrats in the Nara and Heian periods and spread to all classes in the Edo period. Today, parks such as Ueno Park in Tokyo and Maruyama Park in Kyoto fill with crowds, and some trees are lit up at night for yozakura, ‘night sakura’."],["A long climate record","Scientists have compiled dates of peak blossoming in Kyoto from diaries and court records going back to about 812 AD. In 2021, Kyoto’s cherry trees reached peak bloom on 26 March, the earliest in the record. Researchers link the trend to climate change and urban heat."],["Cherry blossoms around the world","Cherry blossom festivals are held in Washington, D.C., Vancouver, South Korea, China, Germany and elsewhere. In South Korea, the Jinhae festival is one of the largest. The trees are also used to celebrate Japanese-American friendship."]],
k:[["Japanese name","Sakura"],["Genus","Prunus"],["Bloom period","About 1–2 weeks"],["Viewing custom","Hanami"],["Earliest Kyoto peak on record","26 March 2021"]],
d:[["c. 812","Earliest recorded Kyoto blossom date"],["1800s","Somei Yoshino variety bred in Edo"],["1912","Tokyo gives cherry trees to Washington, D.C."],["1935","First National Cherry Blossom Festival in Washington"],["2021","Kyoto records its earliest peak bloom in over 1,200 years"]],
s2:[["Mono no aware","Cherry blossoms are linked to mono no aware, a Japanese idea of a gentle sadness at the passing of things. Their beauty is valued partly because it does not last."],["Darker symbolism","In World War II, the Japanese military used falling cherry blossoms as a symbol for young soldiers, including kamikaze pilots, who died for the emperor. After the war, the blossoms returned to being mainly symbols of spring and renewal."]],
sp:["Washington’s cherry trees","The first gift of trees to Washington in 1910 had to be burned because they carried insects and disease. A healthy shipment of about 3,000 trees arrived in 1912, and First Lady Helen Taft and the wife of Japan’s ambassador planted the first two by the Tidal Basin."],
g:[["Sakura","Japanese word for cherry blossom"],["Hanami","Flower viewing, especially of cherry blossoms"],["Clone","A genetically identical copy"],["Peak bloom","When most flowers are open"]],
f:["Kyoto’s blossom records go back about 1,200 years.","Somei Yoshino cherry trees are all clones.","Tokyo gave Washington about 3,000 cherry trees in 1912."],
fun:"Cherry blossom petals and leaves are pickled in salt and used to flavor sweets and tea in Japan."});

ADDART({id:"cassava",t:"Cassava",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🥔",w:"Cassava",r:["potato","nigeriacountry","brazilcountry","agri","poisonousplants"],
p:[["A staple root","Cassava is a starchy root eaten by about 800 million people."],["From the Amazon","It was domesticated in South America about 10,000 years ago."],["Must be prepared","Raw cassava contains compounds that can release cyanide."]],
l:"Cassava, also called manioc or yuca, is a woody shrub grown for its starchy, tuberous roots, one of the most important food crops in the tropics. It was domesticated in the southwestern Amazon basin of Brazil about 10,000 years ago and was taken to Africa by Portuguese traders in the 16th century and later to Asia. Today Nigeria is the largest producer, followed by the Democratic Republic of the Congo and Thailand, and cassava feeds roughly 800 million people, especially in Africa. Cassava is valued because it grows in poor soils, tolerates drought and can be left in the ground for months until needed, making it a reliable food in hard times. However, its roots and leaves contain compounds called cyanogenic glucosides, which release poisonous cyanide; bitter varieties must be soaked, grated, fermented, dried or cooked to remove them. Cassava is made into flour, flatbreads, porridges, fufu and garri, and its starch becomes tapioca, used in puddings and the pearls in bubble tea.",
s:[["Preparing cassava","Traditional methods remove most toxins: grating and squeezing out juice, soaking for days, fermenting, sun-drying or roasting. Sweet varieties have fewer toxins and can be boiled and eaten. Poorly processed bitter cassava, especially during droughts and food shortages, can cause a paralytic disease called konzo."],["Tapioca","Tapioca is starch extracted from cassava roots. It is made into pearls for puddings and bubble tea, flour for gluten-free baking and thickeners for sauces. Brazil’s pão de queijo, cheese bread, uses tapioca flour."],["Diseases","Cassava mosaic disease and cassava brown streak disease, spread by whiteflies and infected cuttings, can destroy harvests in Africa. Scientists have bred resistant varieties, and projects distribute clean planting material to farmers."],["Biofortification","Scientists have bred yellow cassava rich in vitamin A, released in Nigeria from 2011, to help reduce vitamin A deficiency, a major cause of childhood blindness in parts of Africa."]],
k:[["Other names","Manioc, yuca"],["Domesticated","About 10,000 years ago in the Amazon"],["Largest producer","Nigeria"],["People fed","About 800 million"],["Toxins","Cyanogenic glucosides"]],
d:[["c. 8000 BC","Cassava domesticated in the Amazon"],["1500s","Portuguese bring cassava to Africa"],["1800s","Cassava spreads widely in Asia"],["2011","Vitamin A cassava released in Nigeria"]],
s2:[["Bubble tea pearls","The chewy black ‘boba’ pearls in bubble tea, invented in Taiwan in the 1980s, are made from cassava starch, often colored with brown sugar."],["Food security","Because cassava can stay in the ground for up to two years, it acts as a living food store for families. The UN Food and Agriculture Organization has called it a crop for the 21st century because of its tolerance to heat and drought."]],
sp:["Konzo","Konzo is a sudden, permanent paralysis of the legs caused by eating insufficiently processed bitter cassava combined with a low-protein diet, mostly in poor rural areas of Africa during droughts or conflict."],
g:[["Tuberous root","A swollen root that stores food"],["Cyanide","A fast-acting poison"],["Tapioca","Starch made from cassava"],["Biofortification","Breeding crops to be more nutritious"]],
f:["Cassava feeds about 800 million people.","Raw cassava can release cyanide.","Bubble tea pearls are made from cassava."],
fun:"Cassava roots can be left in the ground for up to two years and harvested only when needed."});

ADDART({id:"eucalyptus",t:"Eucalyptus",c:"Plants",i:"🌿",w:"Eucalyptus",r:["trees","koalas","australiacountry","firefighters","invasiveplants"],
p:[["Australia’s trees","Eucalyptus trees dominate most Australian forests."],["Fragrant oil","Their leaves contain aromatic oils."],["Fire-adapted","Many eucalypts survive and even need bushfires."]],
l:"Eucalyptus is a genus of more than 700 species of flowering trees and shrubs in the myrtle family, nearly all native to Australia, where they dominate about three-quarters of the forests; they are often called gum trees. They range from small shrubs called mallees to the mountain ash of Victoria and Tasmania, the tallest flowering plants in the world, reaching about 100 meters. Eucalyptus leaves contain oil glands that release a strong, fresh scent; eucalyptus oil, rich in a compound called cineole, is used in medicines, cough drops, cleaning products and perfumes. The oils make leaves toxic to most animals, but koalas and some possums and insects can eat them. Many eucalypts are adapted to fire: they resprout from buds under their thick bark or from swollen woody bases, and some species need fire to release their seeds. Their oils are flammable, which can make bushfires intense. Eucalypts have been planted worldwide for timber, paper pulp and firewood, but in some places they have become invasive and use a lot of water.",
s:[["Surviving fire","After a bushfire, blackened eucalyptus trunks quickly sprout green shoots from buds protected under the bark, called epicormic buds. Woody swellings at the base, called lignotubers, store food and buds so the plant can regrow even if the top is destroyed. The heat of fire opens seed capsules, and seedlings grow in the ash."],["Blue haze","The Blue Mountains near Sydney get their name from a blue haze caused partly by tiny droplets of eucalyptus oil released into the air, which scatter blue light."],["Eucalyptus around the world","Eucalypts grow fast and are the most widely planted hardwood trees in the world, with large plantations in Brazil, India, China, Portugal and South Africa, mainly for paper pulp. In Portugal and California, plantations and escaped trees have been blamed for fueling fires, and they can lower water tables."],["The rainbow eucalyptus","The rainbow eucalyptus, one of the few species native outside Australia, grows in the Philippines, Indonesia and Papua New Guinea. As its bark peels in strips, it reveals patches of green, blue, purple, orange and red."]],
k:[["Species","More than 700"],["Native to","Australia (almost all)"],["Common name","Gum trees"],["Tallest","Mountain ash, about 100 m"],["Oil compound","Cineole"]],
d:[["1770","Joseph Banks collects eucalyptus on Cook’s voyage"],["1788","Eucalyptus named by French botanist L’Héritier"],["1850s","Eucalyptus planted in California and elsewhere"],["2019–20","Black Summer bushfires burn vast eucalyptus forests"]],
s2:[["Centurion","A mountain ash in Tasmania named Centurion, about 100 meters tall, is the tallest known flowering plant. It survived bushfires in 2019."],["Gold in leaves","In 2013, Australian scientists found tiny particles of gold in eucalyptus leaves growing above gold deposits. The trees’ deep roots draw up water carrying gold, suggesting leaves could help prospectors find ore."]],
sp:["Koalas and eucalyptus","Koalas eat only a few eucalyptus species and can sniff out which leaves are least toxic and most nutritious. Gut bacteria help them break down the leaves’ oils and tannins."],
g:[["Lignotuber","A woody swelling at the base of a plant that stores buds"],["Epicormic bud","A dormant bud under bark"],["Cineole","The main compound in eucalyptus oil"],["Mallee","A shrubby eucalypt with several stems"]],
f:["Eucalypts dominate three-quarters of Australian forests.","Mountain ash are the tallest flowering plants.","Eucalyptus leaves can contain tiny traces of gold."],
fun:"Eucalyptus trees can grow very fast, with some plantation trees growing more than 10 meters in their first three years."});

ADDART({id:"ginger",t:"Ginger and Turmeric",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🫚",w:"Ginger",r:["spice","indiacountry","medicinalplants","china","trade"],
p:[["Spicy roots","Ginger and turmeric are spices made from underground stems called rhizomes."],["Ancient trade","Ginger was one of the first spices traded from Asia to Europe."],["Traditional medicine","Both have long been used in Asian medicine."]],
l:"Ginger and turmeric are related tropical plants of the ginger family whose underground stems, called rhizomes, are used as spices and medicines. Ginger, Zingiber officinale, has a pungent, warming flavor from compounds called gingerols, which turn into shogaols when dried and into zingerone when cooked. Turmeric, Curcuma longa, gives a bright yellow-orange color and earthy flavor to curries, rice dishes and mustards, coming from its main pigment, curcumin. Both plants originated in tropical Asia and were cultivated in India and Southeast Asia thousands of years ago. Ginger was one of the first spices traded along routes to the Roman Empire and medieval Europe, where it was used in gingerbread and medicines. India is by far the largest producer of both. In traditional Ayurvedic and Chinese medicine, ginger is used for digestion and turmeric for inflammation and wounds. Studies suggest ginger can help reduce nausea, while evidence for many health claims about curcumin is weak because the body absorbs it poorly.",
s:[["Growing","Ginger and turmeric are grown from pieces of rhizome planted in warm, moist soil and harvested after about eight to ten months. The leafy stems grow up to about a meter tall, and turmeric produces striking white and pink flowers."],["Ginger in the kitchen","Fresh ginger flavors stir-fries, curries and teas across Asia. Dried ginger flavors gingerbread, ginger ale and ginger beer. Pickled ginger accompanies sushi in Japan, and crystallized ginger is eaten as a sweet."],["Turmeric’s color","Turmeric has been used as a dye for cloth, including some Buddhist monks’ robes, and as a natural food coloring. It plays a role in Hindu ceremonies, including the haldi ceremony before weddings, where a turmeric paste is applied to the bride and groom."],["Health claims","Clinical trials suggest ginger can help with nausea from pregnancy and chemotherapy. Curcumin shows anti-inflammatory effects in laboratory studies, but large reviews have found little reliable evidence that turmeric supplements treat diseases. High doses of supplements have been linked to rare liver problems."]],
k:[["Family","Ginger family (Zingiberaceae)"],["Part used","Rhizome"],["Ginger’s flavor","Gingerols"],["Turmeric’s color","Curcumin"],["Largest producer","India"]],
d:[["c. 500 BC","Ginger used in Indian and Chinese medicine"],["1st century","Ginger traded to the Roman Empire"],["1500s","Spanish grow ginger in the Caribbean"],["2017","Review finds weak evidence for curcumin as a medicine"]],
s2:[["Gingerbread","Gingerbread became popular in medieval Europe, and Queen Elizabeth I is credited with having gingerbread figures made in the likeness of guests. Gingerbread houses became popular in Germany after the Grimm brothers’ story Hansel and Gretel."],["Turmeric lattes","Since the 2010s, ‘golden milk’ or turmeric lattes, based on an Indian drink of turmeric and warm milk called haldi doodh, have become popular in cafés worldwide."]],
sp:["Spice adulteration","Because turmeric is valuable, some suppliers have illegally added lead chromate, a toxic yellow pigment, to brighten its color. Studies in Bangladesh found it was a major source of lead poisoning, prompting crackdowns from 2019."],
g:[["Rhizome","An underground stem that grows sideways"],["Gingerol","The compound that gives ginger its bite"],["Curcumin","The yellow pigment in turmeric"],["Adulteration","Adding cheaper or harmful substances to food"]],
f:["Ginger and turmeric are rhizomes, not roots.","India produces most of the world’s ginger and turmeric.","Ginger can help reduce nausea."],
fun:"Cardamom, another popular spice, also belongs to the ginger family."});

;
