// New articles, 18 November 2026 (part 11): technology.
ADDART({id:"malware",t:"Computer Viruses and Malware",c:"Technology",i:"🦠",w:"Malware",r:["cybersecurity","hacking","passwords","operatingsystems","email"],
p:[["Harmful software","Malware is software designed to damage computers, steal data or demand money."],["Many kinds","Viruses, worms, trojans, spyware and ransomware are types of malware."],["Protection","Updates, backups and caution with links and attachments help protect computers."]],
l:"Malware, short for malicious software, is any program designed to harm computers, steal information, spy on users or take control of devices. Computer viruses attach themselves to other files and spread when those files are shared; worms spread on their own across networks; trojans disguise themselves as useful programs; spyware secretly collects information; and ransomware locks or encrypts files and demands payment to restore them. The idea of self-copying programs was explored in the 1940s by mathematician John von Neumann. Early examples include the experimental Creeper program in 1971 and the Elk Cloner virus, written by a 15-year-old for Apple II computers in 1982. The Morris worm of 1988 infected about a tenth of computers on the early internet. Email worms such as ILOVEYOU in 2000 spread to millions of computers in days. Ransomware attacks, such as WannaCry in 2017, have disrupted hospitals, businesses and governments, and the 2010 Stuxnet worm sabotaged Iranian nuclear equipment. Security experts recommend keeping software updated, using antivirus protection, backing up files and being careful with email attachments and links.",
s:[["Types of malware","Besides viruses and worms, malware includes keyloggers that record keystrokes, botnets that turn infected computers into networks controlled by criminals, adware that floods users with advertisements and cryptojackers that secretly use computers to mine cryptocurrency."],["Ransomware","Ransomware gangs encrypt victims’ data and demand payment, usually in cryptocurrency, and often threaten to publish stolen data. The 2021 attack on the Colonial Pipeline caused fuel shortages along the US East Coast. Governments discourage paying ransoms, which can fund further crime."],["ILOVEYOU","The ILOVEYOU worm, released from the Philippines in May 2000, arrived as an email with the subject ‘ILOVEYOU’ and an attachment that appeared to be a love letter. When opened, it overwrote files and emailed itself to everyone in the victim’s address book, causing billions of dollars in damage worldwide."],["Defenses","Antivirus programs detect malware by recognizing known code and suspicious behavior. Operating system updates fix security holes. Organizations train staff to spot phishing, use multi-factor authentication and keep offline backups so they can recover without paying ransoms."]],
k:[["Main types","Viruses, worms, trojans, spyware, ransomware"],["Early virus","Elk Cloner, 1982"],["First major internet worm","Morris worm, 1988"],["Famous email worm","ILOVEYOU, 2000"],["Major ransomware attack","WannaCry, 2017"]],
d:[["1971","Creeper, an experimental self-copying program"],["1982","Elk Cloner virus for the Apple II"],["1988","Morris worm infects the early internet"],["2000","ILOVEYOU worm spreads worldwide"],["2010","Stuxnet worm discovered"],["2017","WannaCry ransomware attack"],["2021","Colonial Pipeline ransomware attack"]],
s2:[["Stuxnet","Stuxnet, discovered in 2010, was a highly sophisticated worm that damaged centrifuges used to enrich uranium in Iran by making them spin out of control while showing normal readings to operators. It is widely believed to have been created by the United States and Israel."],["The Morris worm","Robert Tappan Morris, a graduate student, released his worm in 1988 to measure the size of the internet, but a programming error made it copy itself repeatedly, slowing thousands of computers. He became the first person convicted under the US Computer Fraud and Abuse Act and later became a professor at MIT."]],
sp:["Why ‘virus’?","Computer scientist Fred Cohen popularized the term ‘computer virus’ in 1983, comparing self-copying programs to biological viruses that infect cells and use them to make copies of themselves."],
g:[["Malware","Software designed to cause harm"],["Ransomware","Malware that locks data and demands payment"],["Worm","Malware that spreads by itself across networks"],["Trojan","Malware disguised as a useful program"]],
f:["Elk Cloner was written by a 15-year-old in 1982.","The ILOVEYOU worm spread to millions of computers.","Backups help victims recover from ransomware."],
fun:"Elk Cloner, one of the first computer viruses, displayed a short poem on infected Apple II computers every 50th time they started up."});

ADDART({id:"captcha",t:"CAPTCHAs",c:"Technology",i:"🤖",w:"CAPTCHA",r:["ai","cybersecurity","compvision","worldwideweb","passwords"],
p:[["Are you human?","CAPTCHAs are tests that websites use to tell people from bots."],["Wavy letters and traffic lights","They ask users to type distorted text or pick images."],["An AI arms race","As AI improves, CAPTCHAs must become harder or invisible."]],
l:"A CAPTCHA, which stands for ‘Completely Automated Public Turing test to tell Computers and Humans Apart’, is a challenge that websites use to check whether a user is a human rather than an automated program, or bot. CAPTCHAs help stop bots from creating fake accounts, posting spam, buying up concert tickets or guessing passwords. The term was coined in 2003 by Luis von Ahn, Manuel Blum and colleagues at Carnegie Mellon University. Early CAPTCHAs showed distorted letters and numbers that were easy for people to read but hard for computers. In reCAPTCHA, launched by von Ahn in 2007 and bought by Google in 2009, users typed words from scanned old books and newspapers that computers could not read, helping to digitize millions of texts. Later versions asked users to click images containing traffic lights, buses or crosswalks, which also helped train AI for self-driving cars. As artificial intelligence became able to solve many CAPTCHAs, newer systems analyze mouse movements, browsing behavior and device information invisibly, often asking users only to tick a box saying ‘I’m not a robot’.",
s:[["The Turing test","The name refers to the Turing test, proposed by Alan Turing in 1950 to judge whether a machine can imitate human conversation. A CAPTCHA is a reverse Turing test, administered by a computer to judge whether the user is human."],["Digitizing books","The original reCAPTCHA showed two words: one the computer knew, to check the user, and one from a scanned book that optical character recognition had failed to read. By combining many users’ answers, it helped digitize the archives of The New York Times and millions of Google Books."],["Accessibility","Visual CAPTCHAs can be difficult for people who are blind or have low vision, dyslexia or other disabilities. Audio CAPTCHAs offer an alternative but are often hard to understand. Accessibility advocates push for less burdensome methods."],["Bots fight back","Criminals sometimes pay people in low-wage ‘CAPTCHA farms’ to solve challenges, and modern AI can read distorted text and identify images better than many humans. This has pushed websites toward invisible risk scoring and passkeys."]],
k:[["Stands for","Completely Automated Public Turing test to tell Computers and Humans Apart"],["Coined","2003, Carnegie Mellon University"],["reCAPTCHA","2007 (bought by Google in 2009)"],["Purpose","Block bots"],["Modern approach","Invisible behavior analysis"]],
d:[["1950","Alan Turing proposes the Turing test"],["1997","Early text-distortion test used by AltaVista"],["2003","The term CAPTCHA is coined"],["2007","reCAPTCHA launched"],["2009","Google acquires reCAPTCHA"],["2014","‘I’m not a robot’ checkbox introduced"]],
s2:[["Luis von Ahn","Luis von Ahn, a Guatemalan-born computer scientist, also co-founded the language-learning app Duolingo. He has said he was inspired by the idea of using the time millions of people spent solving CAPTCHAs for something useful."],["Annoying but useful","Studies estimated that people worldwide spent hundreds of thousands of hours a day solving CAPTCHAs. Researchers continue to look for ways to keep out bots without wasting users’ time."]],
sp:["‘I’m not a robot’","The simple checkbox introduced in 2014 works by analyzing how the user moves the mouse and interacts with the page before clicking. Bots tend to move in perfectly straight lines or too quickly."],
g:[["Bot","An automated software program"],["Turing test","A test of whether a machine can seem human"],["Optical character recognition","Software that reads printed text from images"],["Accessibility","Ease of use for people with disabilities"]],
f:["CAPTCHA was coined in 2003.","reCAPTCHA helped digitize old books.","Clicking traffic lights helped train AI."],
fun:"By clicking on images of traffic lights and buses, millions of people unknowingly helped train artificial intelligence systems for self-driving cars."});

ADDART({id:"laptops",t:"Laptops and Tablets",c:"Technology",i:"💻",w:"Laptop",r:["comp","smartphone","touchscreens","battery","operatingsystems"],
p:[["Computers on the go","Laptops and tablets are portable computers with built-in screens and batteries."],["From luggables to ultrabooks","Early portables weighed more than 10 kilograms."],["Tablets with touch","The iPad made touchscreen tablets popular in 2010."]],
l:"Laptops, also called notebook computers, are portable personal computers that combine a screen, keyboard, pointing device and battery in one folding case. Tablets are flat, touchscreen computers without built-in keyboards. Early portable computers, such as the Osborne 1 of 1981, weighed about 11 kilograms and were called ‘luggables’. The clamshell design, with a screen folding over the keyboard, appeared in the GRiD Compass of 1982, which was used by NASA on Space Shuttle missions. Through the 1990s, laptops became lighter and more powerful, adding color screens, trackpads and wireless networking. By 2008, laptops outsold desktop computers in the United States. Apple’s MacBook Air (2008) popularized very thin laptops, and inexpensive Chromebooks became common in schools. Earlier tablets and handheld computers existed, such as the Apple Newton and Microsoft Tablet PC, but Apple’s iPad, released in April 2010, made tablets a mass market. Today, laptops are the main computers for many students and workers, while tablets are used for reading, video, drawing and education, and many devices convert between the two.",
s:[["Early portables","The Osborne 1 had a 5-inch screen and no battery; it had to be plugged in. The Compaq Portable of 1983 was nicknamed a ‘luggable’. The Epson HX-20 of 1981, about the size of a sheet of paper, is often called the first true laptop, running on rechargeable batteries."],["Key technologies","Laptops depended on improvements in flat LCD screens, rechargeable lithium-ion batteries, low-power processors and, later, solid-state drives. Wi-Fi, built into most laptops from the early 2000s, made them truly mobile."],["The iPad","Steve Jobs introduced the iPad in January 2010. Apple sold 3 million in its first 80 days. Tablets became popular with children, older people and businesses, and drawing tablets with styluses are used by artists and designers."],["Laptops in education","Projects such as One Laptop per Child, launched in 2005, aimed to provide low-cost laptops to children in developing countries. During the COVID-19 pandemic, laptops and tablets became essential for remote learning, though many students lacked access."]],
k:[["First portable computer","Osborne 1, 1981"],["First clamshell laptop","GRiD Compass, 1982"],["Laptops outsold desktops (US)","2008"],["iPad released","April 2010"],["Key battery","Lithium-ion"]],
d:[["1981","Osborne 1 and Epson HX-20 released"],["1982","GRiD Compass clamshell laptop"],["1991","Apple PowerBook introduces a palm rest and trackball"],["2005","One Laptop per Child project announced"],["2008","MacBook Air released"],["2010","Apple iPad released"]],
s2:[["Alan Kay’s Dynabook","In 1972, computer scientist Alan Kay described the Dynabook, a book-sized personal computer for children with a flat screen and wireless connection, decades before laptops and tablets made the idea real."],["Two-in-ones","Many modern devices combine laptop and tablet, with detachable keyboards or screens that fold back. Microsoft’s Surface line, launched in 2012, popularized this design."]],
sp:["Laptops in space","The GRiD Compass flew on the Space Shuttle in 1983. Today, astronauts on the International Space Station use dozens of laptops to control experiments, check procedures and talk with family."],
g:[["Laptop","A portable folding computer"],["Tablet","A flat touchscreen computer"],["Clamshell","A design with a hinged lid"],["Trackpad","A touch-sensitive pointing surface"]],
f:["The Osborne 1 weighed about 11 kilograms.","Laptops outsold desktops in the US in 2008.","The iPad was released in 2010."],
fun:"The first laptop to go to space, the GRiD Compass, cost about 8,000 dollars in 1982."});

ADDART({id:"arcadegames",t:"Arcade Games and Pinball",c:"Technology",i:"🕹️",w:"Arcade video game",r:["videogames","esports","toys","japancountry","comp","emulators"],
p:[["Coin-operated fun","Arcade games are coin-operated machines in public places."],["Pong and Pac-Man","Arcade hits launched the video game industry."],["Pinball’s comeback","Pinball, once banned in some cities, has enjoyed a revival."]],
l:"Arcade games are coin-operated entertainment machines found in amusement arcades, bars, restaurants and malls, including video games, pinball machines and redemption games. Pinball developed from the 18th-century table game bagatelle; coin-operated pinball machines spread in the 1930s, and flippers were added in 1947. In the 1940s, some US cities, including New York, banned pinball as a form of gambling; New York’s ban lasted until 1976. Video arcade games began with Computer Space in 1971 and Atari’s Pong in 1972, a simple table-tennis game that became a huge hit. The ‘golden age’ of arcade video games followed in the late 1970s and early 1980s, with Space Invaders (1978), Asteroids (1979), Pac-Man (1980) and Donkey Kong (1981), which introduced the character later called Mario. Fighting games such as Street Fighter II (1991) revived arcades in the 1990s. Home consoles and computers eventually reduced arcade attendance in many countries, but arcades remain popular in Japan, and ‘barcades’ and retro gaming have brought them back elsewhere.",
s:[["Pinball","A pinball machine uses flippers, bumpers and ramps to keep a steel ball in play, scoring points. Mechanical machines gave way to electronic ones in the 1970s. In 1976, Roger Sharpe convinced the New York City Council that pinball was a game of skill by calling a shot and making it, helping overturn the ban."],["The golden age","Space Invaders was so popular in Japan that it was said to cause a shortage of 100-yen coins, though this may be a myth. Pac-Man, designed by Toru Iwatani, became one of the highest-grossing video games ever and appealed to people who had never played games before."],["High scores","Arcade games recorded the best players’ initials, inspiring competition. The 2007 documentary The King of Kong followed players competing for the Donkey Kong record. Competitive gaming in arcades was a forerunner of e-sports."],["Arcades in Japan","Japanese game centers remain popular, with rhythm games, crane games, photo booths called purikura and large multiplayer cabinets. Many are in busy entertainment districts such as Akihabara in Tokyo."]],
k:[["Pinball flippers added","1947"],["First commercial video arcade game","Computer Space, 1971"],["Breakthrough hit","Pong, 1972"],["Golden age","Late 1970s–early 1980s"],["New York pinball ban","1942–1976"]],
d:[["1931","Coin-operated pinball spreads"],["1942","New York City bans pinball"],["1947","Flippers added to pinball"],["1972","Atari’s Pong released"],["1978","Space Invaders released"],["1980","Pac-Man released"],["1991","Street Fighter II revives arcades"]],
s2:[["Pac-Man’s design","Toru Iwatani said Pac-Man’s shape was inspired by a pizza with a slice missing. Each of the four ghosts has its own pattern of chasing Pac-Man, giving the game strategy."],["Donkey Kong","Nintendo’s Donkey Kong (1981), designed by Shigeru Miyamoto, introduced a carpenter called Jumpman, later renamed Mario, who became the most famous video game character."]],
sp:["Pong’s first test","Atari placed its first Pong machine in Andy Capp’s Tavern in Sunnyvale, California, in 1972. Days later the bar owner called to say it had broken; it was so full of coins that the coin box had jammed."],
g:[["Arcade","A place with coin-operated games"],["Pinball","A game of keeping a ball in play with flippers"],["Cabinet","The box housing an arcade game"],["High score","The best score recorded on a game"]],
f:["Pinball was banned in New York until 1976.","Pong was released in 1972.","Mario first appeared in Donkey Kong."],
fun:"The first Pong machine stopped working because so many coins were stuffed into it that the coin box overflowed."});

ADDART({id:"rubikscube",t:"The Rubik’s Cube",c:"Technology",i:"🧊",w:"Rubik's Cube",r:["toys","alg","robot","boardgames","esports"],
p:[["A famous puzzle","The Rubik’s Cube is a colorful 3D puzzle invented in Hungary in 1974."],["Quintillions of positions","It has about 43 quintillion possible arrangements."],["Speedcubing","The fastest solvers finish in under three seconds."]],
l:"The Rubik’s Cube is a three-dimensional combination puzzle invented in 1974 by Hungarian architecture professor Ernő Rubik, who created it to help explain three-dimensional movement to his students. The classic cube has six faces, each with nine colored squares; each face can be turned, mixing up the colors, and the goal is to return each face to a single color. Rubik needed a month to solve his own cube for the first time. Launched internationally in 1980, it became a worldwide craze, selling about 100 million cubes in its first few years, and it remains one of the best-selling toys ever, with more than 500 million sold. The standard cube has about 43 quintillion (43,252,003,274,489,856,000) possible arrangements, yet in 2010 mathematicians and computer scientists proved that any position can be solved in 20 moves or fewer, a number nicknamed ‘God’s number’. Speedcubing competitions are held worldwide under the World Cube Association, and in 2026 a solver in Poland recorded the first official solve under three seconds. Variations include larger cubes and other shapes.",
s:[["How it works","A clever internal mechanism lets the pieces turn without falling apart. The cube has 26 visible pieces: 6 centers that stay fixed relative to each other, 8 corners and 12 edges. Solvers learn sequences of moves called algorithms to move pieces without disturbing others."],["Speedcubing","Speedcubers use special lubricated cubes and memorize many algorithms. Popular methods solve the cube in layers. Competitions include events such as one-handed solving, blindfolded solving after memorizing the cube, and larger cubes up to 7×7×7."],["God’s number","Researchers used about 35 years of computer processing time, donated by Google, to show in 2010 that every position can be solved in at most 20 moves. Finding the shortest solution for a given position is still computationally difficult."],["Cubes and robots","Robots have solved Rubik’s Cubes in a fraction of a second; in 2024, a Japanese robot solved one in about 0.3 seconds. In 2019, an AI system learned to solve the cube without human knowledge."]],
k:[["Inventor","Ernő Rubik, 1974"],["Country","Hungary"],["Possible positions","About 43 quintillion"],["God’s number","20 moves"],["Cubes sold","More than 500 million"]],
d:[["1974","Ernő Rubik invents the cube"],["1977","Cube first sold in Hungary"],["1980","Cube launched internationally"],["1982","First world championship in Budapest"],["2010","God’s number proved to be 20"],["2026","First official solve under three seconds"]],
s2:[["The 1980s craze","At the height of the craze, books explaining how to solve the cube topped bestseller lists, and there was even a cartoon series, Rubik, the Amazing Cube. Many people peeled off and rearranged the stickers in frustration."],["Ernő Rubik","Rubik did not originally intend to make a toy, calling his invention the ‘Magic Cube’. He became one of the first self-made millionaires in communist Hungary."]],
sp:["Blindfolded solving","In blindfolded speedcubing, solvers inspect the cube, memorize its pattern, put on a blindfold and then solve it. Top competitors complete the whole process in less than 15 seconds."],
g:[["Algorithm","A sequence of moves with a specific result"],["Speedcubing","Solving cubes as fast as possible"],["God’s number","The maximum moves needed to solve any position"],["Combination puzzle","A puzzle solved by arranging pieces"]],
f:["The Rubik’s Cube was invented in 1974.","Any position can be solved in 20 moves or fewer.","The first official sub-3-second solve came in 2026."],
fun:"If you turned a Rubik’s Cube once every second, it would take you about 1.4 trillion years to go through all of its positions."});

ADDART({id:"lego",t:"LEGO",c:"Technology",i:"🧱",w:"Lego",r:["toys","plastics","printing3d","robot","boardgames"],
p:[["Building bricks","LEGO bricks are interlocking plastic toys from Denmark."],["Still compatible","Bricks made today still fit bricks made in 1958."],["More than toys","LEGO is used in education, robotics and art."]],
l:"LEGO is a line of plastic construction toys made by the Danish company the LEGO Group, best known for its interlocking bricks that can be assembled and taken apart to build almost anything. The company was founded in 1932 in Billund, Denmark, by carpenter Ole Kirk Christiansen, who made wooden toys; the name comes from the Danish words leg godt, meaning ‘play well’. The company began making plastic bricks in 1949, and in 1958 it patented the modern brick design with hollow tubes inside, which gives bricks their strong ‘clutch power’. Bricks made today are still compatible with those made in 1958. LEGO introduced the minifigure in 1978 and themed sets such as space, castles and pirates, and later licensed sets based on films such as Star Wars, which began in 1999. LEGO Mindstorms (1998) brought programmable robots to classrooms, and LEGO competitions teach engineering. LEGO bricks are made to tolerances as fine as a few thousandths of a millimeter. The LEGO Group became the world’s largest toy company by revenue and is working to make bricks from more sustainable materials.",
s:[["The brick","A standard 2×4 brick has eight studs on top and three tubes underneath. Six 2×4 bricks of the same color can be combined in more than 915 million ways. Bricks are made from ABS plastic in molds so precise that only about 18 in a million are rejected."],["Minifigures","The LEGO minifigure, introduced in 1978, has movable arms, legs and head. Billions have been made, and minifigures are sometimes said to outnumber the human population."],["LEGO in education","LEGO Education and the FIRST LEGO League competitions teach children engineering, programming and teamwork using robots built from LEGO parts. Researchers have also used LEGO to build prototypes and scientific instruments."],["Sustainability","Most LEGO bricks are made from oil-based plastic. The company has made some pieces, such as trees and leaves, from plant-based plastic from sugarcane since 2018 and continues to search for sustainable materials that keep the same quality."]],
k:[["Founded","1932, Billund, Denmark"],["Name meaning","Leg godt, ‘play well’"],["Modern brick patented","1958"],["Minifigure introduced","1978"],["Combinations of six 2×4 bricks","More than 915 million"]],
d:[["1932","Ole Kirk Christiansen starts making wooden toys"],["1949","First plastic bricks"],["1958","Modern LEGO brick patented"],["1968","Legoland Billund opens"],["1978","Minifigure introduced"],["1998","LEGO Mindstorms launched"],["2014","The LEGO Movie released"]],
s2:[["Legoland","The first Legoland theme park opened in Billund in 1968. Legoland parks now operate in several countries, featuring huge models of cities, landmarks and characters built from millions of bricks."],["The LEGO Movie","The LEGO Movie (2014), with its song ‘Everything Is Awesome’, was a box-office hit and led to sequels and spin-offs, introducing LEGO to new generations."]],
sp:["Stepping on LEGO","Stepping on a LEGO brick in bare feet is famously painful because bricks are hard and distribute weight onto small points. The company once made slippers designed to protect feet from bricks."],
g:[["Stud","A bump on top of a brick"],["Clutch power","How firmly bricks hold together"],["Minifigure","A small LEGO person"],["ABS plastic","A tough plastic used for bricks"]],
f:["LEGO comes from Danish words meaning ‘play well’.","Bricks from 1958 still fit today’s bricks.","Six 2×4 bricks can combine in over 915 million ways."],
fun:"A tower of about 375,000 LEGO bricks would be needed before the bottom brick broke under the weight, according to a test by scientists."});

ADDART({id:"pens",t:"Pens and Pencils",c:"Technology",i:"✏️",w:"Pen",r:["writing","paper","print","typewriter","calligraphy"],
p:[["Writing tools","Pens and pencils are among the most common tools ever made."],["The ballpoint revolution","The ballpoint pen made writing cheap and easy."],["Pencils use graphite","‘Lead’ pencils actually contain graphite and clay."]],
l:"Pens and pencils are handheld tools for writing and drawing. Ancient Egyptians wrote with reed pens dipped in ink, and in medieval Europe people used quill pens made from bird feathers. Steel dip pens became common in the 19th century, and fountain pens with internal ink reservoirs, perfected by inventors such as Lewis Waterman in 1884, became popular. The ballpoint pen, which uses a tiny rotating ball to roll quick-drying ink onto paper, was patented by Hungarian journalist László Bíró in 1938; mass production after World War II, notably by Marcel Bich’s BIC company from 1950, made pens cheap enough for everyone. Felt-tip pens and markers spread in the 1960s, and gel pens in the 1980s. Pencils use a core of graphite, a form of carbon, mixed with clay; a large deposit of pure graphite discovered in Borrowdale, England, in the 16th century started the pencil industry. The core is called ‘lead’ only because graphite was once mistaken for lead ore. Pencil hardness is graded from hard (H) to soft and black (B). Despite keyboards and touchscreens, billions of pens and pencils are still made each year.",
s:[["The ballpoint","László Bíró noticed that newspaper ink dried quickly and designed a pen with a ball bearing in its tip to spread thick ink. He fled to Argentina during World War II, where the pen was produced; in Argentina and some other countries, ballpoints are still called ‘biromes’ or ‘biros’. The BIC Cristal, introduced in 1950, has sold more than 100 billion units."],["The pencil","Wooden pencils hold a core glued between two grooved strips of wood. In 1795, French scientist Nicolas-Jacques Conté developed a method of mixing graphite with clay and baking it, allowing pencils of different hardness. The common US school pencil, the No. 2, is equivalent to HB."],["Erasers","Rubber was named in 1770 by English scientist Joseph Priestley, who noticed it could rub out pencil marks. Hymen Lipman patented a pencil with an attached eraser in 1858."],["Pens in space","A popular story says NASA spent millions developing a space pen while the Soviets used pencils. In fact, both used pencils at first, but pencil tips and graphite dust are hazards in spacecraft. The Fisher Space Pen, developed privately with a pressurized ink cartridge, was bought by both NASA and the Soviet program."]],
k:[["Ballpoint inventor","László Bíró, 1938"],["Fountain pen improver","Lewis Waterman, 1884"],["Pencil core","Graphite and clay"],["Graphite deposit","Borrowdale, England (1500s)"],["Pencil grades","From H (hard) to B (soft)"]],
d:[["c. 3000 BC","Egyptians write with reed pens"],["c. 600","Quill pens come into use"],["1565","Graphite pencil described by Conrad Gessner"],["1795","Conté invents the modern pencil lead process"],["1884","Waterman’s fountain pen patented"],["1938","Bíró patents the ballpoint pen"],["1950","BIC Cristal introduced"]],
s2:[["How long can a pencil write?","A typical pencil can draw a line about 56 kilometers long or write about 45,000 words, according to popular estimates."],["Pens and history","Many famous documents were signed with dip pens or quills, including the US Declaration of Independence. Some national leaders still sign laws with several pens and give them away as souvenirs."]],
sp:["No lead in pencils","Pencils have never contained lead. Early users thought the newly discovered graphite was a type of lead ore and called it ‘plumbago’, meaning lead-like, and the name stuck."],
g:[["Graphite","A soft form of carbon"],["Ballpoint","A pen that writes with a rolling ball"],["Fountain pen","A pen with an internal ink reservoir"],["Quill","A pen made from a feather"]],
f:["Pencils contain graphite, not lead.","The ballpoint pen was patented in 1938.","The BIC Cristal has sold over 100 billion units."],
fun:"The BIC Cristal pen is one of the best-selling consumer products in history, with more than 100 billion sold."});

ADDART({id:"eyeglasses",t:"Eyeglasses and Contact Lenses",c:"Health and the Body",i:"👓",w:"Glasses",r:["eye","light","glass","illusions","scope"],
p:[["Seeing clearly","Eyeglasses and contact lenses correct blurry vision."],["Medieval invention","Eyeglasses were invented in Italy around 1286."],["Billions need them","Billions of people need glasses, but many cannot get them."]],
l:"Eyeglasses, or spectacles, are frames holding lenses in front of the eyes to correct vision problems, while contact lenses sit directly on the eye’s surface. The most common problems are nearsightedness (myopia), in which distant objects look blurry; farsightedness (hyperopia); astigmatism; and presbyopia, the age-related loss of near focus that makes reading difficult after about age 40. Eyeglasses were invented in northern Italy around 1286, probably by glassmakers near Pisa or Venice; early versions were convex lenses for reading held on the nose. Concave lenses for nearsightedness followed in the 15th century, and Benjamin Franklin invented bifocals in the 1780s. Contact lenses were first made of glass in the 1880s; soft hydrogel lenses, introduced in 1971, made them comfortable and popular. Today, laser eye surgery can also reshape the cornea. The World Health Organization estimates that at least a billion people have vision impairment that could have been prevented or corrected, often because they lack access to glasses. Myopia is becoming much more common worldwide, especially among children in East Asia.",
s:[["How lenses work","A nearsighted eye focuses light in front of the retina, so concave lenses spread light slightly before it enters the eye. Farsighted eyes focus behind the retina, corrected by convex lenses. Lens strength is measured in diopters."],["The myopia boom","In some East Asian cities, more than 80 percent of young adults are nearsighted. Researchers think spending more time indoors and doing close work contributes; studies show that more time outdoors in daylight reduces children’s risk of developing myopia."],["Contact lenses","Contact lenses float on the tear film covering the cornea. Rigid gas-permeable lenses let oxygen through, while soft lenses are comfortable and may be disposable daily. Poor hygiene can cause serious eye infections, so users must follow cleaning instructions."],["Access to glasses","Simple reading glasses can cost very little, yet hundreds of millions of people lack them, affecting education and work. Programs train local workers to test eyes and supply affordable glasses."]],
k:[["Invented","Italy, about 1286"],["Bifocals","Benjamin Franklin, 1780s"],["Soft contact lenses","1971"],["Most common problem","Myopia (nearsightedness)"],["People with preventable vision impairment","At least 1 billion (WHO)"]],
d:[["c. 1286","Eyeglasses invented in Italy"],["1400s","Concave lenses for nearsightedness"],["1780s","Franklin invents bifocals"],["1888","First glass contact lenses"],["1971","Soft contact lenses approved in the US"],["1990s","Laser eye surgery becomes widespread"]],
s2:[["A sermon about glasses","In 1306, Friar Giordano da Pisa said in a sermon in Florence that eyeglasses had been invented ‘not yet twenty years’ earlier, the main evidence for their invention around 1286."],["Sunglasses","Inuit peoples carved snow goggles with narrow slits to reduce glare long before sunglasses. Modern sunglasses spread in the 1930s, and good ones block harmful ultraviolet light."]],
sp:["Glasses in art","One of the earliest paintings showing eyeglasses is a 1352 fresco by Tommaso da Modena in Treviso, Italy, depicting a cardinal reading with spectacles."],
g:[["Myopia","Nearsightedness"],["Presbyopia","Age-related loss of near focus"],["Diopter","A unit of lens strength"],["Cornea","The clear front surface of the eye"]],
f:["Eyeglasses were invented around 1286.","Benjamin Franklin invented bifocals.","More time outdoors may reduce myopia in children."],
fun:"Early eyeglasses had no arms; people balanced them on their noses or held them up, and arms that rested on the ears became common only in the 18th century."});

ADDART({id:"firesafety",t:"Fire Extinguishers and Sprinklers",c:"Technology",i:"🧯",w:"Fire extinguisher",r:["firefighters","smokedetectors","thermo","reactions","skyscrapers"],
p:[["Fighting fires early","Fire extinguishers and sprinklers put out fires before they spread."],["Different fires, different extinguishers","Water, foam, powder and carbon dioxide suit different fires."],["Sprinklers save lives","Automatic sprinklers greatly reduce deaths in building fires."]],
l:"Fire extinguishers and automatic sprinkler systems are devices for putting out or controlling fires in their early stages. Fire needs heat, fuel and oxygen, known as the fire triangle, and extinguishers work by removing one or more of these, cooling the fire, smothering it or interrupting the chemical reaction. The first portable pressurized fire extinguisher was invented by British army officer George William Manby in 1818, using a copper vessel of potassium carbonate solution and compressed air. Today’s extinguishers use water, foam, dry chemical powder, carbon dioxide or wet chemicals, each suited to particular kinds of fire; using water on burning oil or electrical equipment can be dangerous. Automatic fire sprinklers, developed in the 19th century and perfected by Henry Parmelee in 1874 and Frederick Grinnell, spray water when heat breaks a glass bulb or melts a link in a sprinkler head, usually only above the fire. Studies by the US National Fire Protection Association show sprinklers sharply reduce deaths and damage in building fires. Building codes require sprinklers in many new buildings.",
s:[["Classes of fire","Fires are classified by fuel: ordinary materials such as wood and paper, flammable liquids, gases, electrical equipment, metals and cooking oils. Labels on extinguishers show which types they can fight. Multipurpose dry powder extinguishers work on several classes."],["Using an extinguisher","Many fire services teach the PASS method: Pull the pin, Aim at the base of the fire, Squeeze the handle, Sweep from side to side. People should use extinguishers only on small fires and leave immediately if a fire grows."],["How sprinklers work","Each sprinkler head has a heat-sensitive glass bulb filled with liquid that expands and shatters at about 68°C, or a metal link that melts. Only heads near the fire activate, contrary to the movie image of every sprinkler going off at once."],["Fire blankets and kitchens","Kitchen fires, often from cooking oil, should never be put out with water, which can cause an explosive fireball. Fire blankets and wet chemical extinguishers smother the fire. Turning off the heat and covering the pan with a lid can also help."]],
k:[["Fire triangle","Heat, fuel and oxygen"],["First portable extinguisher","George Manby, 1818"],["Automatic sprinkler","Henry Parmelee, 1874"],["Common extinguishing agents","Water, foam, powder, carbon dioxide"],["Sprinkler trigger","Heat-sensitive bulb or link"]],
d:[["1723","Ambrose Godfrey patents an early fire extinguisher"],["1818","Manby invents the portable pressurized extinguisher"],["1874","Parmelee patents an automatic sprinkler"],["1881","Grinnell improves the sprinkler head"],["1911","Triangle Shirtwaist Factory fire leads to safety reforms"]],
s2:[["The Triangle fire","On 25 March 1911, a fire at the Triangle Shirtwaist Factory in New York killed 146 garment workers, mostly young immigrant women, many trapped by locked doors. The disaster led to new laws requiring sprinklers, fire drills and better exits."],["Halon","Halon gas was once widely used to fight fires in computer rooms and aircraft because it leaves no residue. It was phased out under the Montreal Protocol because it damages the ozone layer, though some aircraft still use it."]],
sp:["Fire safety at home","Fire services recommend keeping a small extinguisher and fire blanket in the kitchen, installing smoke alarms and planning escape routes. In a fire, people should get out, stay out and call emergency services."],
g:[["Fire triangle","Heat, fuel and oxygen needed for fire"],["Extinguisher","A device that puts out fires"],["Sprinkler","A device that sprays water when heated"],["Smother","To cut off a fire’s oxygen"]],
f:["Fire needs heat, fuel and oxygen.","Only sprinklers near a fire go off.","Never put water on an oil fire."],
fun:"In films, every sprinkler in a building often goes off at once, but in reality each sprinkler head activates only when heat reaches it."});

ADDART({id:"carsafety",t:"Seat Belts and Airbags",c:"Technology",i:"🚗",w:"Automobile safety",r:["cars","trafficlights","selfdriving","roads","newton"],
p:[["Saving lives on the road","Seat belts and airbags protect people in car crashes."],["The three-point belt","Volvo engineer Nils Bohlin invented the modern seat belt in 1959."],["Fewer road deaths","Safety features have made cars far safer than in the past."]],
l:"Car safety features protect people in vehicles from injury during crashes. The most important is the seat belt: the modern three-point seat belt, crossing the lap and chest, was invented by Swedish engineer Nils Bohlin at Volvo in 1959, and Volvo made its patent freely available so that all carmakers could use it. Seat belts reduce the risk of death for front-seat passengers by about 45 percent in cars. Airbags, which inflate in a fraction of a second during a crash, were developed in the 1950s to 1970s and became common in the 1990s; they work together with seat belts and are not a replacement. Other features include crumple zones that absorb crash energy, safety glass, child car seats, anti-lock brakes and electronic stability control. Newer cars have automatic emergency braking, lane-keeping assistance and blind-spot warnings. Road safety campaigns, laws requiring seat belts and crash tests have greatly reduced deaths in many countries, yet the World Health Organization estimates that about 1.2 million people still die in road crashes each year, most in low- and middle-income countries.",
s:[["How seat belts work","In a crash, a car stops suddenly, but unbelted occupants keep moving until they hit the dashboard, windshield or other passengers. Seat belts spread the force across the strong bones of the pelvis and chest. Pretensioners tighten belts instantly in a crash, and load limiters prevent excessive force."],["Airbags","Sensors detect a crash and trigger a chemical reaction that fills the airbag with gas in about 30 milliseconds. The bag then deflates as the occupant presses into it, cushioning the impact. Faulty airbag inflators made by the Japanese company Takata led to the largest automotive recall in history from 2013."],["Crash tests","Organizations such as Euro NCAP and the US Insurance Institute for Highway Safety crash cars into barriers using instrumented dummies and rate their safety. Crash test dummies have mostly been based on average male bodies; newer dummies represent women and children more accurately."],["Ralph Nader","American lawyer Ralph Nader’s 1965 book Unsafe at Any Speed criticized carmakers for neglecting safety. It helped lead to the creation of the US National Highway Traffic Safety Administration in 1970 and mandatory safety standards."]],
k:[["Three-point seat belt","Nils Bohlin, Volvo, 1959"],["Risk reduction from seat belts","About 45% for front-seat occupants"],["Airbags common","1990s"],["Airbag inflation time","About 30 milliseconds"],["Global road deaths","About 1.2 million a year (WHO)"]],
d:[["1959","Volvo introduces the three-point seat belt"],["1965","Unsafe at Any Speed published"],["1970","Australia’s state of Victoria makes seat belts compulsory"],["1973","First cars with airbags sold in the US"],["1983","UK makes front seat belts compulsory"],["2013","Takata airbag recalls begin"]],
s2:[["Volvo’s gift","Volvo kept its patent on the three-point belt open for any carmaker to use, saying it was too important to keep to itself. Experts estimate it has saved more than a million lives."],["Self-driving cars and safety","Most crashes involve human error, such as distraction, speeding or drunk driving. Supporters of self-driving cars argue they could prevent many crashes, though the technology has its own risks."]],
sp:["Crash test dummies","The first crash test dummy, Sierra Sam, was built in 1949 to test aircraft ejection seats. Modern dummies contain dozens of sensors measuring forces on the head, neck, chest and legs."],
g:[["Three-point belt","A seat belt crossing the lap and chest"],["Crumple zone","A part of a car designed to absorb crash energy"],["Airbag","A cushion that inflates in a crash"],["Recall","When a maker asks owners to return products for repair"]],
f:["The three-point seat belt was invented in 1959.","Airbags inflate in about 30 milliseconds.","About 1.2 million people die in road crashes each year."],
fun:"Volvo gave away the patent for its life-saving three-point seat belt so that every carmaker could use it."});

ADDART({id:"creditcards",t:"Credit and Debit Cards",c:"Technology",i:"💳",w:"Payment card",r:["money","digitalpay","rfid","atms","cybersecurity"],
p:[["Plastic money","Credit and debit cards let people pay without cash."],["Born in the 1950s","Diners Club launched the first general charge card in 1950."],["Chips and taps","Cards now use chips and contactless payment."]],
l:"Credit and debit cards are plastic or metal cards that let people pay for goods and services without cash. A credit card lets the holder borrow money from a bank up to a limit and pay it back later, usually with interest if the balance is not paid in full; a debit card takes money directly from the holder’s bank account. Store credit plates and charge cards existed in the early 20th century, but the first general-purpose charge card, Diners Club, was launched in 1950 after businessman Frank McNamara reportedly forgot his wallet at a restaurant. American Express followed in 1958, and BankAmericard, later Visa, began in 1958, letting customers carry a balance. Magnetic stripes were added in the 1960s and 1970s, and chips (EMV), which make cards harder to copy, spread from the 1990s. Contactless cards, using NFC radio technology, let people pay with a tap, and phone wallets now often replace physical cards. Visa and Mastercard run the largest networks. Card payments brought convenience but also debt, fees and fraud, and many people worldwide still lack bank accounts.",
s:[["How a card payment works","When a card is used, the shop’s terminal sends payment details through the card network to the cardholder’s bank, which approves or declines the payment within seconds. The shop pays a fee on each transaction, which is part of how card companies make money."],["Chip and PIN","Chips generate a unique code for each transaction, making cloned cards much less useful. Many countries combine chips with a PIN. The United States adopted chip cards widely only around 2015, later than Europe."],["Debt","Credit cards can help people manage spending and build credit histories but can also lead to debt because of high interest rates. Financial advisers recommend paying the full balance each month when possible."],["Fraud","Criminals steal card details using skimmers, data breaches and phishing. Banks use fraud detection systems that look for unusual spending and can block suspicious payments. Customers are usually protected from liability for fraud they report quickly."]],
k:[["First general charge card","Diners Club, 1950"],["BankAmericard (Visa)","1958"],["Security chip","EMV chip"],["Contactless technology","NFC"],["Largest networks","Visa and Mastercard"]],
d:[["1950","Diners Club card launched"],["1958","American Express card and BankAmericard launched"],["1966","Interbank, later Mastercard, formed"],["1970s","Magnetic stripes become standard"],["1990s","Chip cards introduced in Europe"],["2007","Contactless cards spread"]],
s2:[["The ‘first supper’","According to Diners Club, Frank McNamara forgot his wallet at a New York restaurant in 1949 and paid with a card the following year, an event the company called the ‘First Supper’. Some historians consider the story partly marketing legend."],["Card sizes","Payment cards follow an international standard size, 85.6 by 54 millimeters, also used for driver’s licenses and ID cards in many countries."]],
sp:["The magnetic stripe","IBM engineer Forrest Parry developed the magnetic stripe card in the 1960s. He reportedly got the idea when his wife suggested using a hot iron to attach a strip of magnetic tape to a plastic card."],
g:[["Credit card","A card for borrowing money to pay"],["Debit card","A card that takes money directly from a bank account"],["Interest","The cost of borrowing money"],["EMV chip","A security chip in payment cards"]],
f:["Diners Club was the first general charge card.","Chip cards are harder to copy than magnetic stripes.","Contactless cards use NFC."],
fun:"The magnetic stripe on cards was attached using a household iron in its inventor’s first experiments."});

ADDART({id:"machinetools",t:"Machine Tools",c:"Technology",i:"⚙️",w:"Machine tool",r:["indrev","steel","printing3d","robot","metals"],
p:[["Machines that make machines","Machine tools cut and shape metal parts precisely."],["Lathes and mills","Lathes spin the workpiece; milling machines spin the cutter."],["Computer control","CNC machines are guided by computer programs."]],
l:"Machine tools are powered machines used to cut, drill, grind and shape metal and other rigid materials into precise parts, making them the foundation of manufacturing; they are sometimes called the ‘machines that make machines’. The most important types are the lathe, which spins a workpiece against a cutting tool to make round parts such as shafts and screws, and the milling machine, which uses a rotating cutter to shape flat and complex surfaces. Others include drill presses, grinders and saws. Simple lathes existed in ancient Egypt, but precision machine tools emerged during the Industrial Revolution: John Wilkinson’s boring machine of 1775 allowed accurate cylinders for James Watt’s steam engines, and Henry Maudslay’s screw-cutting lathe of about 1800 made standardized screws possible. Interchangeable parts, produced by machine tools, enabled mass production of guns, sewing machines, bicycles and cars. Since the 1950s, numerical control, and later computer numerical control (CNC), has let computers guide machine tools with extreme precision. Today, CNC machining works alongside 3D printing and robotics in factories worldwide.",
s:[["The lathe","On a lathe, the workpiece spins while a sharp tool removes material. Woodworkers use lathes to make bowls and table legs; metal lathes make precise cylindrical parts. Henry Maudslay’s lathe used a lead screw to move the cutting tool precisely, allowing identical screw threads."],["Precision and standards","Machine tools allowed parts to be made to precise measurements, so that parts from one machine fit another. British engineer Joseph Whitworth standardized screw threads in 1841, and precise measuring tools such as micrometers developed alongside machine tools."],["CNC","Numerical control was developed in the late 1940s and 1950s, partly by John T. Parsons and MIT, to make complex aircraft parts. CNC machines read instructions called G-code from computer-aided design files and can cut parts accurate to a few thousandths of a millimeter."],["Subtractive vs additive","Machine tools are ‘subtractive’, removing material to make parts, while 3D printers are ‘additive’, building parts layer by layer. Many factories combine both, printing complex shapes and finishing them on CNC machines."]],
k:[["Main types","Lathes, milling machines, drills, grinders"],["Boring machine","John Wilkinson, 1775"],["Screw-cutting lathe","Henry Maudslay, about 1800"],["Standard screw threads","Joseph Whitworth, 1841"],["Computer control","CNC"]],
d:[["c. 1300 BC","Lathes used in ancient Egypt"],["1775","Wilkinson’s boring machine"],["c. 1800","Maudslay’s screw-cutting lathe"],["1818","Eli Whitney credited with an early milling machine"],["1841","Whitworth standard screw threads"],["1952","First numerically controlled milling machine at MIT"]],
s2:[["Interchangeable parts","The idea of interchangeable parts, demonstrated with muskets in France and the United States around 1800, was called the ‘American system of manufacturing’. It depended on machine tools and gauges and made mass production possible."],["The importance of machine tools","Countries consider machine tools strategically important because they are needed to make weapons, aircraft and other machines. China, Germany and Japan are the largest producers."]],
sp:["Maudslay’s micrometer","Henry Maudslay built a bench micrometer he called the ‘Lord Chancellor’ because its judgments were final; it could measure to about a ten-thousandth of an inch."],
g:[["Lathe","A machine that spins a workpiece against a tool"],["Milling machine","A machine with a rotating cutter"],["CNC","Computer numerical control"],["Interchangeable parts","Identical parts that fit any product"]],
f:["Machine tools are called the machines that make machines.","Maudslay’s lathe made standard screws possible.","CNC machines follow computer code."],
fun:"Henry Maudslay’s workshop trained many of the great engineers of the 19th century, including Joseph Whitworth and James Nasmyth, inventor of the steam hammer."});

ADDART({id:"aluminum",t:"Aluminum",c:"Technology",i:"🥫",w:"Aluminium",r:["metals","periodictable","recycling","flight","hydropower"],
p:[["Once more precious than gold","Aluminum was once rare and expensive."],["Light and strong","It is used in planes, cans, cars and buildings."],["Endlessly recyclable","Recycling aluminum saves about 95 percent of the energy."]],
l:"Aluminum is a lightweight, silvery metal and the most abundant metal in Earth’s crust, about 8 percent by weight. It is never found pure in nature but in minerals, mainly bauxite ore. Because extracting it was extremely difficult, aluminum was once more valuable than gold: Napoleon III reportedly served his most honored guests with aluminum cutlery, and a small aluminum pyramid was placed on top of the Washington Monument in 1884 as a precious metal. In 1886, Charles Martin Hall in the United States and Paul Héroult in France independently invented the electrolytic process for producing aluminum cheaply, and the price collapsed. Aluminum is light, about a third of the weight of steel, resists corrosion because it forms a protective oxide layer, conducts electricity and heat well and is easily shaped. It is used in aircraft, cars, drink cans, foil, window frames, power lines, smartphones and cooking pans. Producing new aluminum uses huge amounts of electricity, but recycling it uses about 5 percent of the energy, and it can be recycled endlessly. China produces more than half of the world’s aluminum.",
s:[["The Hall–Héroult process","Alumina, refined from bauxite, is dissolved in molten cryolite at about 950°C, and a powerful electric current separates the aluminum, which collects at the bottom of the cell. Smelters are often built near cheap electricity, such as hydroelectric dams in Iceland, Canada and Norway."],["Aluminum or aluminium?","Chemist Humphry Davy named the metal ‘alumium’ and then ‘aluminum’ around 1808–1812. British scientists later preferred ‘aluminium’ to match elements like sodium and potassium. Americans kept ‘aluminum’, and both spellings are accepted."],["Aluminum in flight","The Wright brothers’ 1903 engine used an aluminum crankcase to save weight. Aluminum alloys made modern aircraft possible; about 80 percent of a typical airliner’s structure has been aluminum, though newer planes use more carbon composites."],["Recycling","Aluminum cans can be recycled and back on store shelves in about 60 days. About three-quarters of all aluminum ever produced is still in use. Recycling saves energy, reduces mining and cuts greenhouse gas emissions."]],
k:[["Share of Earth’s crust","About 8%"],["Main ore","Bauxite"],["Cheap production process","Hall–Héroult, 1886"],["Weight vs steel","About one-third"],["Energy saved by recycling","About 95%"]],
d:[["1825","Hans Christian Ørsted first produces aluminum"],["1855","Aluminum bars shown at the Paris Exhibition"],["1884","Aluminum cap placed on the Washington Monument"],["1886","Hall and Héroult invent cheap electrolysis"],["1959","First aluminum drink cans"]],
s2:[["Napoleon III’s cutlery","Napoleon III is said to have used aluminum plates and cutlery for his most important guests, while lesser guests used gold. The story shows how valuable aluminum once was."],["Charles Martin Hall","Charles Martin Hall made his discovery in a woodshed laboratory at his family home in Ohio, aged 22, with help from his sister Julia. The company he co-founded became Alcoa."]],
sp:["The Washington Monument’s tip","The 2.8-kilogram aluminum pyramid placed atop the Washington Monument in 1884 was one of the largest pieces of aluminum made at the time and was displayed at Tiffany’s jewelry store before installation."],
g:[["Bauxite","The main ore of aluminum"],["Electrolysis","Splitting compounds using electricity"],["Alloy","A mixture of metals"],["Oxide layer","A thin protective surface coating"]],
f:["Aluminum is the most abundant metal in Earth’s crust.","Aluminum was once more valuable than gold.","Recycling aluminum saves about 95% of the energy."],
fun:"About three-quarters of all the aluminum ever produced is still in use today, thanks to recycling."});

ADDART({id:"adhesives",t:"Glues and Adhesives",c:"Technology",i:"🧴",w:"Adhesive",r:["plastics","molecules","geckos","polymers","constructionmachines"],
p:[["Sticking things together","Adhesives bond materials together."],["Ancient glues","Neanderthals made glue from birch tar over 200,000 years ago."],["Superglue and sticky notes","Some famous adhesives were discovered by accident."]],
l:"Adhesives, or glues, are substances that bond surfaces together. They are among humanity’s oldest technologies: Neanderthals in Europe heated birch bark to produce sticky tar to attach stone points to wooden handles more than 200,000 years ago. Ancient Egyptians used animal glues made from boiled hides and bones in furniture, and natural glues were made from plant starches, tree resins and milk proteins. The 20th century brought synthetic adhesives such as epoxies, which harden by chemical reaction into very strong bonds; cyanoacrylate ‘superglue’, which sets almost instantly by reacting with moisture; hot-melt glues; and pressure-sensitive adhesives used in tapes and labels. Adhesives are used in nearly everything: books, shoes, furniture, cars, aircraft, electronics, medical bandages and construction. Modern aircraft and cars use structural adhesives in place of some rivets and welds to save weight. Scientists study natural adhesives, such as the sticky feet of geckos and the glue of mussels that sticks underwater, to design new materials.",
s:[["How glues stick","Adhesives stick by flowing into tiny gaps in surfaces and hardening, by chemical bonds and by attractive forces between molecules. Glues harden by drying as solvents evaporate, by cooling, or by chemical reactions such as those in two-part epoxies."],["Superglue","Cyanoacrylate was discovered in 1942 by Harry Coover, who was trying to make clear plastic gun sights and rejected it because it stuck to everything. He rediscovered its value in 1951. It was used in the Vietnam War to close wounds, and medical versions are still used in place of stitches."],["Sticky notes","In 1968, 3M scientist Spencer Silver created a weak, reusable adhesive. His colleague Art Fry used it to make bookmarks that would not fall out of his hymn book, leading to Post-it Notes, launched in 1980."],["Gecko-inspired adhesives","Geckos climb walls using millions of tiny hairs on their toes that stick through weak molecular forces. Researchers have created gecko-inspired dry adhesives that can grip and release repeatedly, used in robots and grippers."]],
k:[["Oldest known glue","Neanderthal birch tar, over 200,000 years ago"],["Superglue discovered","Harry Coover, 1942"],["Post-it Notes launched","1980"],["Strong structural glue","Epoxy"],["Inspired by nature","Gecko feet, mussel glue"]],
d:[["c. 200,000 BC","Neanderthals make birch tar glue"],["c. 1500 BC","Egyptians use animal glues in furniture"],["1845","First rubber-based surgical tape"],["1925","Masking tape invented by Richard Drew at 3M"],["1942","Superglue discovered"],["1980","Post-it Notes launched"]],
s2:[["Duct tape","Duct tape, a cloth-backed waterproof tape, was developed during World War II to seal ammunition boxes. Its strength and versatility made it famous, and astronauts used it to repair the Apollo 17 lunar rover’s fender on the Moon."],["Glue-free history","Before modern glues, Japanese woodworkers developed intricate joints that fit together without nails or glue, used in temples and furniture."]],
sp:["Mussel glue","Mussels attach to rocks using threads with a protein-based glue that sticks strongly underwater. Scientists are developing similar adhesives for surgery, where wet surfaces make ordinary glues fail."],
g:[["Adhesive","A substance that sticks things together"],["Epoxy","A strong two-part glue"],["Cyanoacrylate","The chemical in superglue"],["Pressure-sensitive adhesive","A sticky material used on tapes and labels"]],
f:["Neanderthals made glue from birch tar.","Superglue was discovered by accident.","Post-it Notes use a weak, reusable adhesive."],
fun:"Astronauts on Apollo 17 repaired a broken fender on their Moon buggy using duct tape and maps."});

ADDART({id:"teflon",t:"Teflon and Nonstick Coatings",c:"Technology",i:"🍳",w:"Polytetrafluoroethylene",r:["plastics","carbonchem","pollution","spacesuits","metals"],
p:[["Slippery plastic","Teflon is a super-slippery plastic discovered by accident in 1938."],["Nonstick pans","It made eggs slide off frying pans."],["Forever chemicals","Chemicals used to make it have raised serious health and pollution concerns."]],
l:"Polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE), best known by the brand name Teflon, is a plastic made of carbon and fluorine atoms that is extremely slippery, heat-resistant and chemically inert, so almost nothing sticks to it and few chemicals attack it. It was discovered by accident on 6 April 1938 by Roy Plunkett, a chemist at DuPont, when a cylinder of refrigerant gas he expected to use seemed empty but turned out to contain a waxy white powder. PTFE was used during World War II in the Manhattan Project to resist corrosive uranium compounds. In 1954, French engineer Marc Grégoire, encouraged by his wife Colette, found a way to bond PTFE to aluminum, creating the first nonstick frying pans, sold under the Tefal brand. PTFE is also used in electrical insulation, seals, medical implants, waterproof fabrics such as Gore-Tex and spacecraft. However, chemicals called PFAS, including PFOA, once used to manufacture PTFE, persist in the environment and the human body for decades, earning the nickname ‘forever chemicals’; they have been linked to health problems, leading to lawsuits, bans and phase-outs.",
s:[["Why nothing sticks","The fluorine atoms in PTFE form a tight shell around the carbon chain, and the strong carbon–fluorine bonds make the surface unreactive and very low in friction. PTFE has one of the lowest friction coefficients of any solid."],["Nonstick cooking","Nonstick pans are safe at normal cooking temperatures, but overheating an empty pan above about 260°C can break down the coating and release fumes that can cause flu-like symptoms in people and can kill pet birds. Manufacturers advise cooking on low to medium heat and avoiding metal utensils."],["Forever chemicals","PFOA, used to make PTFE for decades, contaminated water supplies near factories. Lawsuits in West Virginia, dramatized in the 2019 film Dark Waters, led to large settlements. PFOA was phased out of US production by 2015, and many countries are restricting other PFAS chemicals."],["The space myth","Teflon is often said to have been invented for the space program, but it was discovered in 1938, long before. It has, however, been used in spacesuits, spacecraft and the Statue of Liberty’s restoration."]],
k:[["Chemical name","Polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE)"],["Discovered","Roy Plunkett, 6 April 1938"],["Nonstick pan","Marc Grégoire, 1954"],["Key property","Very low friction"],["Concern","PFAS ‘forever chemicals’"]],
d:[["1938","Roy Plunkett discovers PTFE"],["1945","DuPont registers the Teflon trademark"],["1954","First nonstick pan made"],["1969","Gore-Tex invented using stretched PTFE"],["2015","PFOA phased out of US production"]],
s2:[["The Teflon president","US Representative Patricia Schroeder called President Ronald Reagan the ‘Teflon president’ in 1983 because scandals seemed not to stick to him. The term has since been applied to other politicians."],["Gore-Tex","In 1969, Bob Gore discovered that rapidly stretching PTFE created a material full of tiny pores that let water vapor escape but kept rain out. Gore-Tex became widely used in outdoor clothing."]],
sp:["How it sticks to the pan","Since nothing sticks to Teflon, attaching it to pans was a challenge. Grégoire roughened aluminum surfaces with acid so that PTFE could grip them mechanically."],
g:[["PTFE","Polytetrafluoroethylene, the chemical in Teflon"],["Inert","Not chemically reactive"],["PFAS","A group of long-lasting fluorinated chemicals"],["Friction","Resistance when surfaces rub"]],
f:["Teflon was discovered by accident in 1938.","The first nonstick pan was made in 1954.","PFAS are called forever chemicals."],
fun:"Teflon is so slippery that geckos, which can climb glass, cannot cling to it."});

ADDART({id:"youtube",t:"YouTube",c:"Technology",i:"▶️",w:"YouTube",r:["streaming","socialmedia","shortvideo","worldwideweb","recommender","recalgorithms","copyrightonline","creatoreconomy"],
p:[["Video for everyone","YouTube lets anyone upload and watch videos."],["Founded in 2005","The first video, ‘Me at the zoo’, was uploaded in April 2005."],["Billions of viewers","YouTube is one of the most visited websites in the world."]],
l:"YouTube is a video-sharing platform where users upload, watch, comment on and share videos. It was founded in February 2005 by three former PayPal employees, Chad Hurley, Steve Chen and Jawed Karim; the first video, ‘Me at the zoo’, an 18-second clip of Karim at the San Diego Zoo, was uploaded on 23 April 2005. YouTube grew explosively, and Google bought it in 2006 for 1.65 billion dollars. Today it has more than 2.5 billion monthly users, and hundreds of hours of video are uploaded every minute, in more than 100 countries and 80 languages. YouTube changed entertainment, music, education and news, allowing ordinary people to become creators with millions of subscribers and earn money from advertising. It hosts music videos, tutorials, lectures, gaming streams, vlogs and ‘Shorts’, short vertical videos introduced in 2020. YouTube’s recommendation algorithm, which suggests videos to watch next, drives most viewing; critics say it can spread misinformation and extreme content, and YouTube has faced criticism over content moderation, copyright and children’s safety.",
s:[["Early growth","YouTube’s easy uploading and embedding of videos on other websites helped it spread. A 2005 Nike advertisement featuring footballer Ronaldinho became one of the first videos to reach a million views. In 2012, ‘Gangnam Style’ by PSY became the first video to pass one billion views."],["Creators","YouTube creators earn money from advertising, memberships, sponsorships and merchandise. Some, such as MrBeast, have hundreds of millions of subscribers. Being a YouTuber has become a popular career aspiration among children."],["Learning","YouTube is widely used for learning, from cooking and repairs to mathematics and languages. Channels such as Khan Academy and Crash Course have reached millions of students."],["Problems","YouTube removes videos that break its rules on violence, hate speech and misinformation, using AI and human reviewers, but critics say harmful content still spreads. In 2019, YouTube paid a 170 million dollar settlement over collecting data from children without parental consent."]],
k:[["Founded","February 2005"],["First video","‘Me at the zoo’, 23 April 2005"],["Bought by Google","2006, for 1.65 billion dollars"],["Monthly users","More than 2.5 billion"],["Short videos","YouTube Shorts (2020)"]],
d:[["2005","YouTube founded; first video uploaded"],["2006","Google buys YouTube"],["2007","YouTube Partner Program shares ad revenue"],["2012","Gangnam Style passes 1 billion views"],["2020","YouTube Shorts launched"]],
s2:[["Baby Shark","‘Baby Shark Dance’, a children’s song video from South Korea, became the most-viewed video on YouTube in 2020, eventually passing 15 billion views."],["YouTube and music","YouTube became the world’s most popular music streaming service by number of users. Many artists, such as Justin Bieber, were discovered through YouTube videos."]],
sp:["‘Me at the zoo’","The first YouTube video shows Jawed Karim in front of elephants, commenting that they have ‘really, really, really long trunks’. It has been viewed more than 300 million times."],
g:[["Upload","To send a file to a website"],["Creator","A person who makes online content"],["Algorithm","Rules a computer uses to make decisions"],["Vlog","A video blog"]],
f:["YouTube’s first video was uploaded in 2005.","Google bought YouTube in 2006.","Baby Shark is YouTube’s most-viewed video."],
fun:"YouTube was originally planned as a video dating site called ‘Tune In Hook Up’ before its founders changed the idea."});

ADDART({id:"wikipedia",t:"Wikipedia",c:"Technology",i:"📚",w:"Wikipedia",r:["encyclopedias","worldwideweb","opensource","searchengines","misinformation","onlinecommunities"],
p:[["The free encyclopedia","Wikipedia is a free online encyclopedia written by volunteers."],["Launched in 2001","It began on 15 January 2001."],["Hundreds of languages","It has millions of articles in more than 300 languages."]],
l:"Wikipedia is a free online encyclopedia written and edited collaboratively by volunteers around the world. It was launched on 15 January 2001 by Jimmy Wales and Larry Sanger, growing out of Nupedia, an earlier expert-written encyclopedia that had produced few articles. Wikipedia uses wiki software, which lets anyone edit pages directly in a web browser, and its content is freely licensed so that anyone can reuse it. It grew rapidly and became one of the most visited websites in the world. By late 2025, the English Wikipedia had about 7 million articles, and Wikipedia editions in more than 340 languages together held more than 66 million articles. Wikipedia is run by the Wikimedia Foundation, a nonprofit organization funded mainly by donations, and carries no advertising. Its articles are expected to follow a neutral point of view and cite reliable published sources. Early critics doubted that an encyclopedia anyone could edit could be accurate, but studies found its quality comparable to traditional encyclopedias in many areas, though vandalism, bias and uneven coverage remain challenges. Wikipedia’s content is widely used to train artificial intelligence and power search results.",
s:[["How it works","Every article has a history page showing all changes and a talk page for discussion. Volunteer editors watch for vandalism, and automated programs called bots revert many obvious problems within seconds. Disputes are resolved through discussion and policies, with experienced volunteers acting as administrators."],["Accuracy","A 2005 study in the journal Nature compared science articles in Wikipedia and Encyclopaedia Britannica and found a similar number of serious errors, though Britannica disputed the study. Wikipedia advises readers to check sources, especially for controversial topics."],["Gaps and bias","Most Wikipedia editors are men from wealthier countries, which affects coverage. Projects such as Women in Red have created hundreds of thousands of biographies of women to address this imbalance, and editors work to improve coverage of Africa, Asia and other regions."],["Wikipedia and AI","Wikipedia is one of the most important sources of text used to train large language models, and its information appears in search engine panels and voice assistants. The Wikimedia Foundation has asked AI companies to credit and support Wikipedia."]],
k:[["Launched","15 January 2001"],["Founders","Jimmy Wales and Larry Sanger"],["English articles","About 7 million (late 2025)"],["Languages","More than 340"],["Run by","Wikimedia Foundation (nonprofit)"]],
d:[["2000","Nupedia launched"],["2001","Wikipedia launched"],["2003","Wikimedia Foundation founded"],["2005","Nature study compares Wikipedia and Britannica"],["2012","English Wikipedia blacks out in protest against proposed US laws"],["2026","Wikipedia celebrates its 25th anniversary"]],
s2:[["The blackout","On 18 January 2012, the English Wikipedia blacked out for 24 hours, along with other websites, to protest proposed US laws, SOPA and PIPA, that critics said threatened free expression online. The bills were shelved soon afterward."],["Britannica’s print end","In 2012, Encyclopaedia Britannica announced it would stop printing its encyclopedia after 244 years, a change often linked to the rise of Wikipedia and online information."]],
sp:["Wiki","The word ‘wiki’ comes from the Hawaiian word for ‘quick’. Ward Cunningham created the first wiki software, WikiWikiWeb, in 1995, naming it after the Wiki Wiki shuttle buses at Honolulu airport."],
g:[["Wiki","A website anyone can edit"],["Vandalism","Deliberately damaging content"],["Neutral point of view","Presenting all significant views fairly"],["Free license","Permission for anyone to reuse content"]],
f:["Wikipedia was launched on 15 January 2001.","English Wikipedia has about 7 million articles.","Wiki means ‘quick’ in Hawaiian."],
fun:"The first edit on Wikipedia, made by Jimmy Wales on its launch day, was simply the text ‘Hello, World!’."});

ADDART({id:"textmessaging",t:"Text Messaging",c:"Technology",i:"💬",w:"SMS",r:["phone","smartphone","emoji","socialmedia","email"],
p:[["Short messages","Text messages are short written messages sent between phones."],["The first text","The first SMS, ‘Merry Christmas’, was sent in 1992."],["Messaging apps","Apps such as WhatsApp now carry most messages."]],
l:"Text messaging is the sending of short written messages between mobile phones and other devices. The Short Message Service (SMS), developed as part of the GSM mobile phone standard in the 1980s, limited messages to 160 characters, a length chosen by engineer Friedhelm Hillebrand after counting the characters in typical postcards and telex messages. The first SMS, ‘Merry Christmas’, was sent from a computer to a phone by British engineer Neil Papworth on 3 December 1992. Texting grew slowly at first but became hugely popular in the late 1990s and 2000s, especially among young people, despite typing letters on number keypads, aided by predictive text such as T9. The Philippines was once called the ‘texting capital of the world’ because of how much its people used SMS. Texting created its own language of abbreviations, such as ‘LOL’ and ‘BRB’, and later emoji. In the 2010s, internet-based messaging apps such as WhatsApp, WeChat, iMessage, Messenger, Telegram and Signal overtook SMS, adding photos, voice messages, video calls and group chats. RCS, a modern successor to SMS, is now supported by Android and Apple phones.",
s:[["160 characters","SMS messages fit into the spare capacity of signaling channels used by mobile networks, which limited their length. Hillebrand typed sentences on a typewriter and found that 160 characters were enough for most short messages. Twitter originally limited posts to 140 characters to fit into an SMS with room for a username."],["Texting language","Texters abbreviated words to save effort and characters: ‘C U L8R’ for ‘see you later’ and ‘GR8’ for ‘great’. Linguists found that such texting did not harm young people’s literacy and often showed creative play with language."],["Messaging apps","WhatsApp, founded in 2009 and bought by Facebook in 2014, offers free encrypted messaging over the internet and has more than 2 billion users. WeChat in China combines messaging with payments and other services. These apps reduced SMS use and costs."],["Texting and safety","Texting while driving greatly increases crash risk, and many countries ban using handheld phones while driving. SMS is also used for emergency alerts, bank verification codes and public health messages."]],
k:[["SMS limit","160 characters"],["First SMS","‘Merry Christmas’, 3 December 1992"],["Sender","Neil Papworth"],["Predictive text","T9 (1990s)"],["Major messaging apps","WhatsApp, WeChat, iMessage, Messenger"]],
d:[["1984","Friedhelm Hillebrand sets the 160-character limit"],["1992","First SMS sent"],["1995","T9 predictive text invented"],["2009","WhatsApp founded"],["2011","Apple introduces iMessage"],["2024","Apple adopts RCS messaging"]],
s2:[["The texting capital","In the early 2000s, Filipinos sent hundreds of millions of texts a day thanks to cheap SMS and prepaid phones. Text messages helped organize the protests that led to the fall of President Joseph Estrada in 2001."],["Thumb generation","Researchers noticed young texters using their thumbs more skillfully than older people, sometimes pressing doorbells with their thumbs, and called them the ‘thumb generation’."]],
sp:["The first text","Neil Papworth sent the first text message from a computer because phones could not yet type messages. The recipient, Vodafone director Richard Jarvis, was at a Christmas party and could not reply. The text was sold as an NFT in 2021."],
g:[["SMS","Short Message Service"],["Predictive text","Software that guesses the word being typed"],["Encryption","Scrambling messages so only recipients can read them"],["RCS","Rich Communication Services, a successor to SMS"]],
f:["The first text message was ‘Merry Christmas’.","SMS messages are limited to 160 characters.","WhatsApp has more than 2 billion users."],
fun:"The first-ever text message was sold as a digital collectible in 2021 for about 107,000 euros."});

ADDART({id:"lithium",t:"Lithium",c:"Technology",i:"🔋",w:"Lithium",r:["battery","ev","periodictable","chilecountry","australiacountry","saltflats"],
p:[["The lightest metal","Lithium is the lightest metal and the third element in the periodic table."],["Battery power","Lithium-ion batteries power phones, laptops and electric cars."],["White gold","Demand for lithium has soared with electric vehicles."]],
l:"Lithium is a soft, silvery-white metal and the lightest of all metals, with atomic number 3. It is so reactive that it is never found pure in nature and must be stored in oil. Swedish chemist Johan August Arfwedson discovered it in 1817 in a mineral called petalite. Lithium is used in lubricating greases, glass and ceramics, medicines and, most importantly today, rechargeable lithium-ion batteries, which power smartphones, laptops, electric vehicles and grid energy storage. Lithium-ion batteries were developed by John Goodenough, Stanley Whittingham and Akira Yoshino, who shared the 2019 Nobel Prize in Chemistry, and Sony sold the first commercial ones in 1991. Lithium is mined from hard rock, mainly in Australia, and extracted from salty brines in the high deserts of Chile, Argentina and Bolivia, the ‘Lithium Triangle’; China refines most of the world’s lithium. Growing demand for electric vehicles has made lithium a strategically important resource, sometimes called ‘white gold’, and prices have swung wildly. Mining raises concerns about water use, pollution and the rights of local and Indigenous communities.",
s:[["Lithium-ion batteries","In a lithium-ion battery, lithium ions move from the negative electrode to the positive electrode through an electrolyte as the battery discharges, and back again when charging. Lithium’s lightness and high energy make these batteries far lighter than earlier types. Damaged batteries can overheat and catch fire, so they need careful design."],["Brines and mines","In Chile’s Atacama Desert, brine is pumped into huge evaporation ponds, where the sun concentrates the lithium over many months. Hard-rock mines in Australia dig a mineral called spodumene. New methods aim to extract lithium directly from brines with less water."],["Lithium in medicine","Lithium salts have been used since 1949 to treat bipolar disorder, after Australian psychiatrist John Cade discovered their calming effect. Lithium remains one of the most effective mood-stabilizing medicines, though doses must be carefully monitored."],["Recycling","Recycling lithium from used batteries is becoming important to reduce mining. Companies recover lithium, cobalt, nickel and other metals from old batteries, and some countries now require battery recycling."]],
k:[["Atomic number","3"],["Discovered","Johan August Arfwedson, 1817"],["Main use","Lithium-ion batteries"],["Major producers","Australia, Chile, China, Argentina"],["Nobel Prize","2019 (lithium-ion batteries)"]],
d:[["1817","Lithium discovered"],["1949","John Cade discovers lithium’s effect on bipolar disorder"],["1970s","Whittingham develops the first lithium battery concepts"],["1991","Sony sells the first lithium-ion batteries"],["2019","Nobel Prize for lithium-ion batteries"]],
s2:[["The Lithium Triangle","Chile, Argentina and Bolivia hold more than half of the world’s known lithium resources in their salt flats. Bolivia’s Salar de Uyuni, the world’s largest salt flat, contains huge reserves that have been little exploited."],["Lithium from the Big Bang","Lithium is one of only three elements, with hydrogen and helium, made in significant amounts in the Big Bang. Astronomers measure lithium in old stars to test theories of the early universe."]],
sp:["Lighter than water","Lithium is so light that it floats on water, but it reacts with it, fizzing and producing hydrogen gas. It is the least dense metal."],
g:[["Lithium-ion battery","A rechargeable battery using lithium ions"],["Brine","Very salty water"],["Electrolyte","A substance that lets ions move"],["Spodumene","A lithium-bearing mineral"]],
f:["Lithium is the lightest metal.","Lithium-ion batteries won the 2019 Nobel Prize.","Lithium floats on water."],
fun:"Lithium is soft enough to cut with a knife and light enough to float on oil."});

ADDART({id:"faxmachines",t:"Fax Machines",c:"Technology",i:"📠",w:"Fax",r:["telegraph","phone","printers","email","japancountry"],
p:[["Sending pages by phone","Fax machines send copies of documents over telephone lines."],["Older than the telephone","The first fax patent dates from 1843."],["Still in use","Faxes remain common in some offices and hospitals."]],
l:"A fax machine, short for facsimile, scans a paper document, converts the image into signals and sends it over a telephone line to another fax machine, which prints a copy. Surprisingly, the idea is older than the telephone: Scottish inventor Alexander Bain patented a method of transmitting images over telegraph wires in 1843, and Italian priest Giovanni Caselli’s pantelegraph provided a commercial fax service between Paris and Lyon from 1865. In the 20th century, newspapers and police used wirephoto and fax systems to send photographs. Fax machines became common in offices in the 1970s and 1980s after international standards made machines compatible and prices fell, especially in Japan, where faxing handwritten Japanese characters was easier than typing them. By the 1990s, fax machines were everywhere, but email, scanning and electronic signatures largely replaced them. Faxes are still used in some places, including Japanese offices, legal firms and hospitals, where they are trusted for security and legal records, although governments have tried to phase them out.",
s:[["How a fax works","The sending machine scans the page line by line, dividing it into tiny dots that are black or white. These are encoded as sounds and sent through the phone line; the receiving machine decodes them and prints the dots. The screeching sounds of fax machines connecting are the machines negotiating speed and settings."],["Early faxes","Bain’s device used pendulums synchronized at each end. Caselli’s pantelegraph sent handwriting and drawings, mainly for checking signatures on bank documents. In the 1920s, newspapers sent photos across oceans by radio fax."],["The fax boom","International standards from the 1970s and 1980s let machines from different makers communicate. Thermal paper faxes, which faded over time, were common. By the late 1980s, millions of businesses relied on faxes for orders, contracts and memos."],["Japan and faxes","Japan was a center of fax manufacturing, and faxes remained in wide use there long after other countries switched to email. During the COVID-19 pandemic, reports of health data being faxed prompted the government to push digital reforms."]],
k:[["First patent","Alexander Bain, 1843"],["First commercial service","Pantelegraph, Paris–Lyon, 1865"],["Office boom","1970s–1990s"],["Transmission","Over telephone lines"],["Still used in","Some offices, hospitals and law firms"]],
d:[["1843","Alexander Bain patents a fax device"],["1865","Pantelegraph service between Paris and Lyon"],["1924","Wirephotos sent by newspapers"],["1964","Xerox introduces a fax machine for telephone lines"],["1980","International fax standard adopted"],["1990s","Fax use peaks"]],
s2:[["Faxes and the fall of communism","During the 1989 Tiananmen Square protests and the collapse of communist regimes in Eastern Europe, fax machines helped activists send news and information past censors."],["Fax in medicine","In the United Kingdom, the National Health Service was told in 2018 to stop using fax machines by 2020, as it was one of the world’s largest buyers of them."]],
sp:["A sound of the past","The modem sounds of fax machines and dial-up internet, a series of beeps, hisses and screeches, are remembered fondly by many people as the soundtrack of early digital communication."],
g:[["Facsimile","An exact copy"],["Scanner","A device that converts paper images into digital data"],["Thermal paper","Paper that darkens when heated"],["Modem","A device that converts data into sound signals"]],
f:["The first fax patent came in 1843.","Fax machines predate the telephone.","Faxes are still used in some hospitals and offices."],
fun:"The fax machine was invented 33 years before the telephone."});

;
/* data/modern42.js */
// New articles, 18 November 2026 (part 12): space.
ADDART({id:"spitzer",t:"The Spitzer Space Telescope",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🔭",w:"Spitzer Space Telescope",r:["hubblest","jwst","scope","trappist1","starformation"],
p:[["Infrared eye","Spitzer studied the universe in infrared light from 2003 to 2020."],["Following Earth","It trailed behind Earth in its own orbit around the Sun."],["Seven planets","It helped reveal the seven Earth-sized planets of TRAPPIST-1."]],
l:"The Spitzer Space Telescope was a NASA space telescope that observed the universe in infrared light, which is invisible to human eyes and can pass through dust that blocks visible light. Launched on 25 August 2003, it was the last of NASA’s four ‘Great Observatories’, alongside the Hubble Space Telescope, the Compton Gamma Ray Observatory and the Chandra X-ray Observatory. It was named after American astrophysicist Lyman Spitzer, who proposed putting telescopes in space in the 1940s. Rather than circling Earth, Spitzer orbited the Sun, slowly drifting behind Earth, which kept it away from Earth’s heat. Its 85-centimeter mirror and instruments were cooled with liquid helium to near absolute zero; when the coolant ran out in 2009, it continued a ‘warm mission’ with two of its cameras for more than a decade. Spitzer saw stars forming inside dusty clouds, studied distant galaxies, measured the temperatures of planets around other stars and, in 2017, helped confirm that the star TRAPPIST-1 has seven Earth-sized planets. NASA switched it off on 30 January 2020, and its work continues with the James Webb Space Telescope.",
s:[["Why infrared?","Warm objects give off infrared light. Cool stars, dust clouds, newborn stars and planets glow brightly in infrared, and the light of very distant galaxies is stretched into infrared by the expansion of the universe. Because Earth’s atmosphere absorbs much infrared light, telescopes in space see it best."],["Keeping cold","An infrared telescope must be colder than the objects it studies, or its own heat would swamp the signal. Spitzer carried 360 liters of liquid helium and a sunshield, and its Earth-trailing orbit helped it cool down by radiating heat into space."],["Discoveries","Spitzer discovered a huge, nearly invisible ring around Saturn made of dust and ice in 2009, made the first direct measurements of light from exoplanets in 2005 and mapped weather on a hot gas giant. It also helped find some of the most distant galaxies known at the time."],["Drifting away","Because Spitzer drifted farther behind Earth each year, its antenna had to point ever more steeply toward Earth, which forced its solar panels away from the Sun. This, rather than broken equipment, was a key reason the mission ended in 2020."]],
k:[["Launched","25 August 2003"],["Ended","30 January 2020"],["Type of light","Infrared"],["Mirror","85 centimeters"],["Orbit","Earth-trailing orbit around the Sun"]],
d:[["2003","Spitzer launched"],["2005","First direct detection of light from exoplanets"],["2009","Liquid helium runs out; warm mission begins"],["2009","Giant ring around Saturn discovered"],["2017","Helps reveal TRAPPIST-1’s seven planets"],["2020","Mission ends"]],
s2:[["The Great Observatories","NASA’s four Great Observatories each studied a different kind of light: Compton (gamma rays), Chandra (X-rays), Hubble (mainly visible and ultraviolet) and Spitzer (infrared). Together they gave astronomers a fuller picture of objects than any single telescope could."],["Lyman Spitzer","In 1946, Lyman Spitzer wrote a paper explaining the advantages of a telescope above Earth’s atmosphere. He campaigned for decades for space telescopes and is often called the father of the Hubble Space Telescope."]],
sp:["Phoebe ring","The enormous ring Spitzer found around Saturn is so wide that about a billion Earths could fit inside it, but its material is so thin that it is almost impossible to see in visible light."],
g:[["Infrared","Light with longer wavelengths than red light"],["Cryogenic","Kept extremely cold"],["Great Observatories","NASA’s four large space telescopes"],["Exoplanet","A planet orbiting another star"]],
f:["Spitzer was launched in 2003.","It saw the universe in infrared light.","It helped reveal TRAPPIST-1’s seven planets."],
fun:"Spitzer’s Saturn ring is so large that it would appear about twice as wide as the full Moon in our sky, if we could see it."});

ADDART({id:"tess",t:"TESS: The Planet Hunter",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🪐",w:"Transiting Exoplanet Survey Satellite",r:["exoplanet","keplertelescope","habitablezone","reddwarfs","jwst","exodetect"],
p:[["Searching nearby stars","TESS looks for planets around bright, nearby stars."],["Watching for dips","It spots tiny dips in starlight when planets pass in front."],["Thousands of candidates","It has found thousands of possible planets."]],
l:"The Transiting Exoplanet Survey Satellite (TESS) is a NASA space telescope that searches for planets orbiting other stars. Launched on a SpaceX Falcon 9 rocket on 18 April 2018 and led by the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, TESS succeeded the Kepler Space Telescope. While Kepler stared deeply at one small patch of sky, TESS surveys almost the entire sky, focusing on bright, nearby stars whose planets are easier to study further. It uses four wide-field cameras to watch for transits, the slight dimming of a star when a planet passes in front of it. TESS travels on an unusual, highly stretched orbit around Earth that takes about 13.7 days, exactly half the Moon’s orbital period, which keeps it stable. By 2026, it had found more than 7,000 planet candidates and helped confirm hundreds of planets, including Earth-sized worlds in the habitable zones of small red dwarf stars, such as TOI-700 d. Many TESS planets are studied in detail by other telescopes, including the James Webb Space Telescope, which can analyze their atmospheres.",
s:[["The transit method","When a planet passes between its star and us, it blocks a tiny fraction of the starlight, often less than 1 percent. Repeated dips at regular intervals reveal a planet’s size and orbit. Ground-based telescopes then measure the star’s wobble to confirm the planet and find its mass."],["Surveying the sky","TESS divides the sky into sectors and watches each for about 27 days before moving on. In its first two years it covered about three-quarters of the sky, and extended missions have filled in gaps and revisited regions to find planets with longer orbits."],["Citizen scientists","Volunteers in projects such as Planet Hunters TESS inspect TESS data by eye and have helped discover planets that computer searches missed, including a planet orbiting two stars found by a high school student in 2019."],["Beyond planets","TESS data are also used to study exploding stars, eclipsing binary stars, comets, asteroids and the vibrations of stars, which reveal their insides."]],
k:[["Launched","18 April 2018"],["Led by","MIT, for NASA"],["Cameras","Four wide-field cameras"],["Orbit period","About 13.7 days"],["Candidates found","More than 7,000"]],
d:[["2018","TESS launched on a Falcon 9"],["2018","First planet discovery announced"],["2019","Student helps find a planet orbiting two stars"],["2020","TOI-700 d, an Earth-sized habitable-zone planet, announced"],["2020","Primary mission ends; extended mission begins"]],
s2:[["TOI-700","The red dwarf TOI-700, about 100 light-years away, has at least four planets, two of them Earth-sized worlds in the habitable zone, where liquid water could exist on the surface."],["Why bright stars?","Planets around bright, nearby stars are easier for other telescopes to study. Webb can split the starlight passing through their atmospheres into spectra to search for water, carbon dioxide and other gases."]],
sp:["Moon-locked orbit","TESS’s orbit is in resonance with the Moon: it goes around Earth exactly twice for every lunar orbit. The Moon’s gravity keeps nudging it in a balanced way, so the orbit can stay stable for decades without much fuel."],
g:[["Transit","A planet passing in front of its star"],["Candidate","A possible planet not yet confirmed"],["Habitable zone","The region where liquid water could exist"],["Resonance","A regular repeating relationship between orbits"]],
f:["TESS launched in 2018.","It watches for dips in starlight.","It has found more than 7,000 planet candidates."],
fun:"A 17-year-old intern at NASA discovered a TESS planet orbiting two stars just three days into his internship."});

ADDART({id:"planck",t:"Planck and WMAP",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🌌",w:"Planck (spacecraft)",r:["cmb","bigbang","darkmatter","fateuniverse","lagrange"],
p:[["Baby pictures of the universe","WMAP and Planck mapped the oldest light in the universe."],["Age of the universe","They measured the universe’s age at about 13.8 billion years."],["Mostly dark","They showed only about 5 percent of the universe is ordinary matter."]],
l:"WMAP and Planck were space telescopes that mapped the cosmic microwave background, the faint afterglow of the Big Bang, released about 380,000 years after the universe began. Tiny temperature differences in this glow, only millionths of a degree, are the seeds from which galaxies grew, and their patterns reveal the universe’s age, shape and contents. NASA’s Wilkinson Microwave Anisotropy Probe (WMAP), launched on 30 June 2001, operated until 2010 and produced the first precise full-sky maps, measuring the age of the universe at about 13.77 billion years. The European Space Agency’s Planck, launched with the Herschel Space Observatory on 14 May 2009, mapped the background in even finer detail until 2013. Planck’s results showed that the universe is about 13.8 billion years old and is made of about 5 percent ordinary matter, about 27 percent dark matter and about 68 percent dark energy, and that space is very nearly flat. Both spacecraft observed from near the L2 Lagrange point, 1.5 million kilometers from Earth. Their measurements underpin the standard model of cosmology, though a puzzle called the ‘Hubble tension’ remains.",
s:[["COBE first","NASA’s COBE satellite, launched in 1989, first detected the tiny ripples in the cosmic microwave background. Its leaders, John Mather and George Smoot, received the 2006 Nobel Prize in Physics. WMAP and Planck mapped those ripples in far greater detail."],["Reading the ripples","The sizes of the hot and cold spots depend on how much ordinary matter, dark matter and dark energy the universe contains. By comparing the maps with theory, scientists calculated these amounts with an accuracy of about 1 percent."],["The Hubble tension","Planck’s data predict that the universe is expanding at about 67 kilometers per second per megaparsec, but measurements using nearby stars and supernovae give about 73. This disagreement, the Hubble tension, may point to new physics or unknown errors."],["Cold instruments","Planck’s most sensitive detectors were cooled to just 0.1 degrees above absolute zero, making them among the coldest objects in space. Its mission ended when its coolant ran out."]],
k:[["WMAP launched","30 June 2001 (NASA)"],["Planck launched","14 May 2009 (ESA)"],["Age of universe","About 13.8 billion years"],["Ordinary matter","About 5%"],["Dark matter / dark energy","About 27% / 68%"]],
d:[["1989","COBE launched"],["2001","WMAP launched"],["2003","WMAP’s first full-sky results"],["2009","Planck launched"],["2013","Planck releases its first cosmological results"],["2018","Final Planck results published"]],
s2:[["David Wilkinson","WMAP was renamed in 2003 in honor of Princeton physicist David Wilkinson, a pioneer of cosmic microwave background research and a member of the mission team, who died in 2002."],["Max Planck","The Planck mission is named after German physicist Max Planck, who founded quantum theory and described the radiation given off by warm objects, the kind of radiation the cosmic microwave background is."]],
sp:["TV static","Part of the ‘snow’ seen on old analog televisions between channels came from the cosmic microwave background, a tiny fraction of the static that the TV antenna picked up."],
g:[["Cosmic microwave background","The afterglow of the Big Bang"],["Dark energy","The mysterious cause of the universe’s accelerating expansion"],["Anisotropy","Small differences in different directions"],["Megaparsec","About 3.26 million light-years"]],
f:["WMAP launched in 2001 and Planck in 2009.","The universe is about 13.8 billion years old.","Only about 5% of the universe is ordinary matter."],
fun:"The light Planck detected had been traveling for about 13.8 billion years, making its maps pictures of the universe when it was a tiny fraction of its current age."});

ADDART({id:"solarobservatories",t:"SOHO and Solar Orbiter",c:"Space Exploration",i:"☀️",w:"Solar and Heliospheric Observatory",r:["sun","spaceweather","sunspots","parkerprobe","lagrange"],
p:[["Watching the Sun","Spacecraft such as SOHO and Solar Orbiter watch the Sun constantly."],["A comet hunter","SOHO has found more than 5,000 comets."],["Seeing the poles","Solar Orbiter took the first pictures of the Sun’s poles."]],
l:"Solar observatories in space study the Sun without the blurring of Earth’s atmosphere, watching sunspots, solar flares and coronal mass ejections that can affect Earth. The Solar and Heliospheric Observatory (SOHO), a joint mission of the European Space Agency and NASA, was launched on 2 December 1995 and positioned near the L1 Lagrange point, 1.5 million kilometers toward the Sun, where it has an uninterrupted view. Planned to last two years, SOHO has worked for three decades, providing warnings of space weather and revealing the Sun’s interior through vibrations of its surface. Volunteers searching its images have discovered more than 5,000 comets, making it the most prolific comet finder in history. NASA’s Solar Dynamics Observatory, launched in 2010, takes high-resolution images of the Sun every few seconds. Solar Orbiter, an ESA-led mission with NASA launched in February 2020, travels on a tilted orbit that lifts it out of the plane of the planets, and in 2025 it returned the first images of the Sun’s south pole. Together with NASA’s Parker Solar Probe, which flies through the Sun’s outer atmosphere, these missions help scientists understand and forecast solar activity.",
s:[["Space weather warnings","Coronal mass ejections are huge clouds of charged gas that can reach Earth in one to three days. SOHO’s coronagraph, which blocks the bright disk of the Sun to see its faint outer atmosphere, gives forecasters early warning so that satellite operators and power companies can prepare."],["Lost and found","In June 1998, SOHO lost contact with Earth and began spinning out of control. Engineers found it with the giant Arecibo radio telescope and restored it over several months, an extraordinary rescue."],["Solar Orbiter","Solar Orbiter uses repeated flybys of Venus to tilt its orbit gradually, reaching more than 17 degrees above the Sun’s equator in 2025 and higher later. Seeing the poles helps scientists understand the Sun’s magnetic field, which flips about every 11 years."],["Telescopes on the ground","The Daniel K. Inouye Solar Telescope in Hawaii, with a 4-meter mirror, is the world’s largest solar telescope and shows features on the Sun as small as about 30 kilometers across."]],
k:[["SOHO launched","2 December 1995"],["SOHO location","Near the L1 point"],["Comets found by SOHO","More than 5,000"],["Solar Orbiter launched","February 2020"],["First Sun pole images","2025 (Solar Orbiter)"]],
d:[["1995","SOHO launched"],["1998","SOHO lost and recovered"],["2010","Solar Dynamics Observatory launched"],["2020","Solar Orbiter launched"],["2024","SOHO discovers its 5,000th comet"],["2025","First images of the Sun’s south pole"]],
s2:[["Sungrazing comets","Most of SOHO’s comets are tiny ‘sungrazers’ that pass extremely close to the Sun and usually evaporate. Many belong to the Kreutz family, fragments of one giant comet that broke apart centuries ago."],["India’s Aditya-L1","India’s Aditya-L1 solar observatory reached the L1 point in January 2024, joining SOHO and other spacecraft that watch the Sun from there."]],
sp:["Amateur discoveries","Anyone can look at SOHO images online. Many of its comets have been discovered by amateur astronomers and students around the world, some of whom have found hundreds."],
g:[["Coronagraph","An instrument that blocks the Sun’s disk to see its outer atmosphere"],["Coronal mass ejection","A huge cloud of charged gas thrown out by the Sun"],["L1 point","A balance point between Earth and the Sun"],["Sungrazer","A comet that passes very close to the Sun"]],
f:["SOHO was launched in 1995.","SOHO has found more than 5,000 comets.","Solar Orbiter photographed the Sun’s south pole in 2025."],
fun:"SOHO was designed to last two years but has kept working for more than 30 years."});

ADDART({id:"galileoprobe",t:"The Galileo Mission to Jupiter",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🛰️",w:"Galileo (spacecraft)",r:["jupiter","europa","io","ganymede","junoprobe"],
p:[["First Jupiter orbiter","Galileo was the first spacecraft to orbit Jupiter."],["A probe into the clouds","It dropped a probe into Jupiter’s atmosphere in 1995."],["Hidden oceans","It found evidence of an ocean beneath Europa’s ice."]],
l:"Galileo was a NASA spacecraft that became the first to orbit Jupiter, studying the giant planet and its moons from 1995 to 2003. Named after Galileo Galilei, who discovered Jupiter’s four large moons in 1610, it was launched from the Space Shuttle Atlantis on 18 October 1989. On its long, looping journey it flew past Venus and twice past Earth to gain speed, and it made the first close flyby of an asteroid, Gaspra, in 1991; in 1993 it discovered Dactyl, the first known moon of an asteroid, orbiting Ida. In 1994 it had a direct view of the fragments of Comet Shoemaker–Levy 9 striking Jupiter. On 7 December 1995, Galileo’s probe plunged into Jupiter’s atmosphere, measuring winds and chemistry for 58 minutes before being destroyed, while the orbiter began circling the planet. Despite a main antenna that failed to open, forcing engineers to send data slowly through a small backup antenna, Galileo found evidence of a salty ocean beneath the icy surface of Europa, saw volcanic eruptions on Io and discovered that Ganymede has its own magnetic field. On 21 September 2003 it was deliberately crashed into Jupiter to avoid any chance of contaminating Europa.",
s:[["The atmospheric probe","The probe hit Jupiter’s atmosphere at about 170,000 kilometers per hour and slowed with a heat shield and parachute. It found less water and stronger winds than expected; scientists later realized it had entered an unusually dry spot, like a desert in Jupiter’s clouds."],["The stuck antenna","Galileo’s umbrella-like high-gain antenna jammed partly open in 1991. Engineers reprogrammed the spacecraft and used data compression so that the low-gain antenna, which was thousands of times slower, could still send back most of the mission’s planned science."],["Europa’s ocean","Galileo’s magnetometer detected that Europa disturbs Jupiter’s magnetic field in a way best explained by a layer of salty, electrically conducting water beneath its ice. This made Europa a top target in the search for life and led to the Europa Clipper mission."],["Volcanic Io","Galileo watched Io’s volcanoes erupt, spewing lava fountains and plumes hundreds of kilometers high. Io is the most volcanically active body in the solar system, heated by tides raised by Jupiter."]],
k:[["Launched","18 October 1989 (Space Shuttle Atlantis)"],["Reached Jupiter","7 December 1995"],["Probe descent","58 minutes"],["First asteroid moon found","Dactyl, orbiting Ida (1993)"],["Mission end","21 September 2003"]],
d:[["1989","Galileo launched from the Space Shuttle"],["1991","First close flyby of an asteroid, Gaspra"],["1993","Discovers Dactyl, moon of asteroid Ida"],["1994","Views Shoemaker–Levy 9 impacts"],["1995","Probe enters Jupiter’s atmosphere; orbiter arrives"],["2003","Galileo crashes into Jupiter"]],
s2:[["Planetary protection","NASA ended Galileo by sending it into Jupiter because Europa might harbor life. Without fuel to steer, the spacecraft could someday have crashed into Europa and contaminated it with Earth microbes."],["Successors","NASA’s Juno has orbited Jupiter since 2016, and Europa Clipper and ESA’s JUICE are traveling to study the moons, building on Galileo’s discoveries."]],
sp:["Plutonium power","Galileo was too far from the Sun for solar panels, so it was powered by radioisotope thermoelectric generators, which convert heat from decaying plutonium into electricity."],
g:[["Orbiter","A spacecraft that circles a planet"],["Atmospheric probe","A device that falls into a planet’s atmosphere"],["High-gain antenna","A large dish antenna for fast communication"],["Planetary protection","Preventing contamination of other worlds"]],
f:["Galileo was the first spacecraft to orbit Jupiter.","Its probe fell into Jupiter’s clouds in 1995.","It found evidence of an ocean beneath Europa’s ice."],
fun:"Galileo found the first known moon of an asteroid, a tiny 1.4-kilometer moonlet named Dactyl."});

ADDART({id:"cometmissions",t:"Missions to Comets",c:"Space Exploration",i:"☄️",w:"Stardust (spacecraft)",r:["comet","halley","rosetta","hayabusa","osirisrex"],
p:[["Visiting comets","Spacecraft have flown past, crashed into and landed on comets."],["Comet dust on Earth","Stardust brought comet dust back to Earth in 2006."],["A Fourth of July impact","Deep Impact smashed into a comet on 4 July 2005."]],
l:"Comets are icy leftovers from the birth of the solar system, and space missions have visited several to learn what they are made of. In 1986, an international fleet of spacecraft met Halley’s Comet; the European Space Agency’s Giotto flew within about 600 kilometers of its nucleus on 14 March 1986, revealing a dark, potato-shaped core. NASA’s Deep Space 1 flew past Comet Borrelly in 2001. NASA’s Stardust, launched on 7 February 1999, flew through the dust around Comet Wild 2 in January 2004, trapping particles in an ultralight material called aerogel, and returned them to Earth in a capsule that landed in Utah on 15 January 2006; scientists later found the amino acid glycine in the samples. NASA’s Deep Impact, launched on 12 January 2005, released a 370-kilogram copper impactor that struck Comet Tempel 1 on 4 July 2005, blasting out material from beneath its surface. ESA’s Rosetta orbited Comet 67P/Churyumov–Gerasimenko from 2014 to 2016 and placed the Philae lander on it. Future missions such as ESA’s Comet Interceptor aim to visit a pristine comet entering the inner solar system for the first time.",
s:[["The Halley Armada","Five spacecraft from the Soviet Union, Japan and Europe visited Halley’s Comet in 1986. The Soviet Vega probes helped guide Giotto to its close approach, an early example of international cooperation in space."],["Catching dust","Stardust’s aerogel, a foam that is more than 99 percent air, slowed comet particles traveling at about 6 kilometers per second without destroying them. Volunteers in the Stardust@home project searched microscope images to find tiny particle tracks."],["Hitting a comet","Deep Impact’s impactor steered itself into Tempel 1 while the main spacecraft watched. The crash threw up a bright cloud of fine powder, showing the comet’s surface was dusty and fragile. Stardust later revisited Tempel 1 and photographed the crater in 2011."],["What comets tell us","Comets contain water ice, dust and carbon-rich compounds. Studies suggest some may have delivered water and the building blocks of life to the early Earth, though the exact contribution is debated."]],
k:[["Giotto at Halley","14 March 1986"],["Stardust launched","7 February 1999"],["Stardust sample landing","15 January 2006, Utah"],["Deep Impact collision","4 July 2005, Comet Tempel 1"],["Rosetta at 67P","2014–2016"]],
d:[["1986","Giotto and others fly past Halley’s Comet"],["1999","Stardust launched"],["2004","Stardust collects dust from Comet Wild 2"],["2005","Deep Impact strikes Comet Tempel 1"],["2006","Stardust sample capsule lands"],["2014","Rosetta reaches Comet 67P; Philae lands"]],
s2:[["Glycine","In 2009, NASA scientists reported glycine, the simplest amino acid used by living things, in Stardust’s samples. Rosetta later also detected glycine around Comet 67P, supporting the idea that comets carry life’s ingredients."],["A comet surprise","Stardust’s samples contained minerals that formed at very high temperatures near the young Sun, showing that material from the inner solar system was carried far out to where comets formed."]],
sp:["Comet Interceptor","ESA’s Comet Interceptor, planned for launch around 2029, will wait in space until a suitable fresh comet is discovered and then fly to meet it, possibly even a visitor from another star system."],
g:[["Nucleus","The solid icy core of a comet"],["Aerogel","An extremely light, porous material"],["Impactor","An object designed to hit a target"],["Amino acid","A building block of proteins"]],
f:["Giotto flew past Halley’s Comet in 1986.","Stardust returned comet dust to Earth in 2006.","Deep Impact hit a comet on 4 July 2005."],
fun:"Deep Impact’s crash into a comet was timed for the Fourth of July, giving the United States a cosmic fireworks show."});

ADDART({id:"messenger",t:"MESSENGER at Mercury",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🌑",w:"MESSENGER",r:["mercury","bepicolombo","orbits","sun","marinerprogram"],
p:[["First Mercury orbiter","MESSENGER was the first spacecraft to orbit Mercury."],["Ice near the Sun","It found water ice in Mercury’s shadowed craters."],["A long road","It took more than six years and six planetary flybys to get there."]],
l:"MESSENGER (MErcury Surface, Space ENvironment, GEochemistry and Ranging) was a NASA spacecraft that became the first to orbit Mercury, the planet closest to the Sun. Launched on 3 August 2004, it needed a long, complicated journey because a spacecraft heading toward the Sun speeds up and must lose a great deal of energy to be captured by small Mercury. It flew past Earth once, Venus twice and Mercury three times before entering orbit around Mercury on 18 March 2011 (17 March in US time). Only one earlier spacecraft, Mariner 10, had visited Mercury, in 1974 and 1975, seeing less than half its surface. MESSENGER mapped the entire planet, discovered that Mercury’s surface is rich in volatile elements such as sulfur and potassium, found that its magnetic field is offset to the north and confirmed water ice and dark organic material in permanently shadowed craters near its poles. It also found strange hollows, shallow pits that may form as materials evaporate. A sunshade made of ceramic cloth protected it from intense heat. After running out of fuel, MESSENGER crashed into Mercury on 30 April 2015, creating a new crater.",
s:[["Getting to Mercury","Reaching Mercury requires more energy in some ways than escaping the solar system. MESSENGER used gravity assists from planets to slow down, traveling about 7.9 billion kilometers over six and a half years to reach a planet that is, at its closest, about 77 million kilometers from Earth."],["Ice on a hot planet","Mercury’s daytime temperature reaches about 430°C, yet the floors of some polar craters never see sunlight and stay below −170°C. MESSENGER’s instruments confirmed that these craters hold water ice, perhaps delivered by comets and asteroids."],["Shrinking planet","MESSENGER found long cliffs called scarps, formed as Mercury’s interior cooled and the planet shrank by up to about 7 kilometers in radius over billions of years. Small, young scarps suggest Mercury may still be shrinking."],["Mercury’s large core","Mercury has a huge iron core, making up about 85 percent of its radius. MESSENGER measured its gravity field to learn about the core’s structure, which helps explain its magnetic field."]],
k:[["Launched","3 August 2004"],["Entered Mercury orbit","18 March 2011 (UTC)"],["Flybys","Earth once, Venus twice, Mercury three times"],["Key discovery","Water ice in polar craters"],["Mission end","30 April 2015"]],
d:[["1974","Mariner 10 makes the first Mercury flyby"],["2004","MESSENGER launched"],["2008","First MESSENGER flyby of Mercury"],["2011","MESSENGER enters orbit around Mercury"],["2012","Water ice confirmed at Mercury’s poles"],["2015","MESSENGER crashes into Mercury"]],
s2:[["After MESSENGER","The European–Japanese BepiColombo mission entered orbit around Mercury in November 2026 with two orbiters to study the planet in even more detail, building on MESSENGER’s maps and puzzles."],["The final impact","MESSENGER hit Mercury at about 14,000 kilometers per hour, on the side facing away from Earth, probably making a crater about 16 meters wide. Scientists hope future missions can photograph it."]],
sp:["Naming craters","Craters on Mercury are named after famous artists, musicians and writers. MESSENGER’s images led to many new names, such as craters honoring Ernest Hemingway, Dr. Seuss and Pablo Picasso."],
g:[["Gravity assist","Using a planet’s gravity to change a spacecraft’s speed"],["Scarp","A long cliff"],["Permanently shadowed crater","A crater floor that never receives sunlight"],["Volatile","A substance that evaporates easily"]],
f:["MESSENGER was the first spacecraft to orbit Mercury.","It found water ice on Mercury.","It crashed into Mercury in 2015."],
fun:"Mercury, the closest planet to the Sun, has ice in its polar craters that MESSENGER detected."});

ADDART({id:"marinerprogram",t:"The Mariner Program",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🛰️",w:"Mariner program",r:["venusmissions","mars","mercury","space","vikinglanders"],
p:[["First to the planets","NASA’s Mariner probes were the first to visit other planets."],["Venus, Mars and Mercury","Mariners explored three planets between 1962 and 1975."],["First planetary orbiter","Mariner 9 became the first spacecraft to orbit another planet."]],
l:"The Mariner program was a series of ten NASA robotic spacecraft launched between 1962 and 1973 to explore Venus, Mars and Mercury, making many historic firsts. Mariner 1 was destroyed shortly after launch in July 1962 because of a guidance problem, partly blamed on a missing symbol in its software, but Mariner 2 flew past Venus on 14 December 1962, becoming the first spacecraft to successfully visit another planet; it found that Venus’s surface is extremely hot. Mariner 4 made the first successful flyby of Mars on 15 July 1965, sending back 21 photos that showed a cratered, Moon-like surface and ending hopes of finding canals or abundant life. Mariners 6 and 7 photographed more of Mars in 1969. On 14 November 1971, Mariner 9 became the first spacecraft to orbit another planet, waiting out a global dust storm and then mapping Mars, revealing giant volcanoes such as Olympus Mons, the huge canyon system Valles Marineris, named after the mission, and dry riverbeds. Mariner 10 became the first spacecraft to use a gravity assist, swinging past Venus in 1974 to reach Mercury, which it flew past three times. The Mariner designs led to the Viking, Voyager and Magellan spacecraft.",
s:[["Mariner 2","Mariner 2 measured Venus’s temperature with microwave radiometers, showing the surface was about 425°C, far too hot for life. It also measured the solar wind for the first time, confirming that the Sun constantly blows charged particles into space."],["Mariner 4’s pictures","Mariner 4’s images were sent back at just 8.3 bits per second, so each picture took hours. Impatient engineers hand-colored a printout of the first image’s numbers with pastels before the computer-processed version was ready."],["Mariner 9","Mariner 9 arrived to find Mars covered by a planet-wide dust storm. Only the tops of four giant volcanoes poked through. When the dust cleared, it photographed about 85 percent of the planet, plus close-ups of the moons Phobos and Deimos."],["Mariner 10","Mariner 10’s gravity assist at Venus, suggested by Italian scientist Giuseppe Colombo, opened up a new way to travel through the solar system, later used by Voyager, Galileo, Cassini and many others."]],
k:[["Spacecraft","10 (1962–1973)"],["First successful planetary flyby","Mariner 2, Venus, 1962"],["First Mars photos","Mariner 4, 1965"],["First planetary orbiter","Mariner 9, Mars, 1971"],["First gravity assist","Mariner 10, 1974"]],
d:[["1962","Mariner 2 flies past Venus"],["1965","Mariner 4 flies past Mars"],["1969","Mariners 6 and 7 fly past Mars"],["1971","Mariner 9 enters Mars orbit"],["1974","Mariner 10 flies past Venus and Mercury"]],
s2:[["The ‘most expensive hyphen’","Writer Arthur C. Clarke called the error that doomed Mariner 1 ‘the most expensive hyphen in history’. The real problem was a missing bar over a symbol in the guidance equations combined with a hardware fault, but the nickname stuck."],["Mariner’s legacy","Voyager 1 and 2 were originally planned as Mariner 11 and 12. Valles Marineris, a canyon system about 4,000 kilometers long, carries the program’s name on Mars."]],
sp:["The pastel picture","The hand-colored first image of Mars from Mariner 4, made with pastels from an art store, is now displayed at NASA’s Jet Propulsion Laboratory."],
g:[["Flyby","A mission that passes a planet without stopping"],["Orbiter","A spacecraft that circles a planet"],["Radiometer","An instrument that measures radiation"],["Solar wind","A stream of charged particles from the Sun"]],
f:["Mariner 2 was the first successful planetary flyby.","Mariner 4 sent the first close-up photos of Mars.","Mariner 9 was the first planetary orbiter."],
fun:"Valles Marineris, a Martian canyon as long as the United States is wide, is named after the Mariner 9 spacecraft that discovered it."});

ADDART({id:"lro",t:"The Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🌕",w:"Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter",r:["moon","apollo","artemis","moonbases","chandrayaan"],
p:[["Mapping the Moon","LRO has mapped the Moon in detail since 2009."],["Apollo footprints","It photographed Apollo landing sites, rovers and tracks."],["Hunting water","Its partner LCROSS found water ice at the Moon’s south pole."]],
l:"The Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter (LRO) is a NASA spacecraft that has been orbiting and mapping the Moon since 2009. Launched on 18 June 2009 together with LCROSS, a companion mission, LRO was designed to scout safe landing sites and resources for future human missions. Its cameras can see objects less than a meter across, and it has photographed the Apollo landing sites, showing the descent stages of lunar modules, rover tracks and even the paths left by astronauts’ footsteps. It also photographed the crash sites of other spacecraft and found the Soviet Lunokhod rovers. LRO has produced the most detailed maps of the Moon ever made, measured temperatures as low as about −250°C in permanently shadowed craters near the poles, among the coldest places known in the solar system, and studied radiation that astronauts would face. On 9 October 2009, LCROSS sent a spent rocket stage crashing into the crater Cabeus near the Moon’s south pole and flew through the plume, detecting water ice. LRO’s maps are used to plan the Artemis missions and other countries’ landings near the lunar south pole.",
s:[["Apollo sites","LRO’s images show Apollo hardware such as the lunar modules’ lower stages, experiment packages and flags, most of which still cast shadows, though their colors have probably been bleached white by sunlight."],["LCROSS","LCROSS guided the 2,300-kilogram Centaur upper stage into Cabeus crater and followed it four minutes later, measuring the debris before crashing itself. Scientists estimated the plume contained about 155 kilograms of water vapor and ice."],["Changing Moon","By comparing images taken years apart, LRO has found hundreds of new craters formed by recent impacts, showing that the Moon’s surface is still being reshaped."],["A long mission","LRO was planned for one year of exploration followed by science missions. It has kept working for more than 15 years and has photographed landers from India, China, Japan, Israel and private companies."]],
k:[["Launched","18 June 2009"],["Camera resolution","About 0.5 meters"],["Companion","LCROSS"],["LCROSS impact","9 October 2009, Cabeus crater"],["Coldest temperature measured","About −250°C"]],
d:[["2009","LRO and LCROSS launched"],["2009","LCROSS impact finds water ice"],["2009","First LRO images of Apollo landing sites"],["2011","Low-altitude images show astronauts’ footpaths"],["2023","LRO photographs Chandrayaan-3’s landing site"]],
s2:[["Coldest places","Some craters near the Moon’s poles have not seen sunlight for billions of years. LRO’s Diviner instrument measured temperatures colder than the surface of Pluto, cold enough to trap water and other substances for ages."],["Finding lost landers","LRO helped locate the lost Soviet Luna and Lunokhod spacecraft and the crash site of India’s Vikram lander in 2019, with help from an amateur who examined its images."]],
sp:["Lunokhod reflector","After LRO found the Soviet Lunokhod 1 rover, scientists aimed lasers at its retroreflector and received a bright return signal, using it to measure the distance to the Moon precisely."],
g:[["Reconnaissance","Exploration to gather information"],["Permanently shadowed region","An area that never receives sunlight"],["Plume","A cloud of material thrown up by an impact"],["Retroreflector","A device that reflects light back to its source"]],
f:["LRO has orbited the Moon since 2009.","It photographed Apollo landing sites.","LCROSS found water ice near the lunar south pole."],
fun:"LRO measured temperatures in the Moon’s shadowed craters that are colder than the surface of Pluto."});

ADDART({id:"insight",t:"InSight: Listening to Mars",c:"Space Exploration",i:"📡",w:"InSight",r:["mars","marsrovers","quake","vikinglanders","phobosdeimos"],
p:[["A Martian seismometer","InSight listened for marsquakes from 2018 to 2022."],["Inside Mars","It measured Mars’s crust, mantle and liquid core."],["More than 1,300 quakes","It detected more than 1,300 marsquakes."]],
l:"InSight (Interior Exploration using Seismic Investigations, Geodesy and Heat Transport) was a NASA lander that studied the deep interior of Mars. Launched on 5 May 2018, it landed on 26 November 2018 on Elysium Planitia, a flat plain near the Martian equator. Unlike rovers, InSight stayed in one place. Its robotic arm placed a French-built seismometer called SEIS on the ground and covered it with a dome to protect it from wind and temperature changes. Over four years, SEIS detected more than 1,300 marsquakes, including a magnitude 4.7 quake in May 2022, the largest ever detected on another planet, and recorded vibrations from meteorite impacts. By studying how seismic waves traveled through the planet, scientists measured the thickness of Mars’s crust, the structure of its mantle and the size of its core, confirming that the core is liquid. InSight’s German heat probe, nicknamed ‘the mole’, was meant to hammer five meters into the ground but could not dig deep because the soil clumped together. Dust gradually covered InSight’s solar panels, and NASA declared the mission over in December 2022.",
s:[["Marsquakes","Mars has no moving tectonic plates like Earth, but it still has quakes as its interior cools and its crust cracks. Many of InSight’s quakes came from a region called Cerberus Fossae, a set of young faults where lava may have flowed recently."],["The mole","The heat probe hammered itself into the ground but kept bouncing back because the soil did not provide enough friction. Engineers tried for almost two years, even pressing on it with the robotic arm, before giving up in January 2021."],["Mars’s core","InSight’s data showed that Mars’s core is liquid and larger and less dense than expected, suggesting it contains light elements such as sulfur. Later analyses suggested a solid inner core may also exist; scientists are still debating the details."],["MarCO","Two briefcase-sized satellites called MarCO-A and MarCO-B flew to Mars with InSight and relayed its landing signals to Earth, becoming the first small CubeSats to travel into deep space."]],
k:[["Launched","5 May 2018"],["Landed","26 November 2018, Elysium Planitia"],["Marsquakes detected","More than 1,300"],["Largest marsquake","Magnitude 4.7 (May 2022)"],["Mission ended","December 2022"]],
d:[["2018","InSight launched"],["2018","InSight lands on Mars"],["2019","First marsquake detected"],["2021","Heat probe efforts end"],["2022","Largest marsquake recorded"],["2022","Mission ends as dust covers the solar panels"]],
s2:[["Listening to Mars","Scientists converted InSight’s seismic recordings into sounds that people can hear, along with the rumble of Martian wind. Its microphone-like instruments also detected the pressure of passing dust devils."],["Impacts heard","InSight detected the vibrations of several meteorite strikes, and orbiters later photographed the fresh craters, including one large impact in December 2021 that threw up blocks of ice."]],
sp:["Selfie with dust","InSight took ‘selfies’ with its arm camera throughout its mission. Comparing them shows its once-shiny solar panels slowly turning reddish brown with Martian dust."],
g:[["Seismometer","An instrument that detects ground vibrations"],["Marsquake","An earthquake on Mars"],["Mantle","The rocky layer between a planet’s crust and core"],["CubeSat","A small, standardized satellite"]],
f:["InSight landed on Mars in 2018.","It detected more than 1,300 marsquakes.","It showed Mars has a liquid core."],
fun:"InSight’s final message, posted shortly before the mission ended, was a photo captioned that it might be its last."});

ADDART({id:"hopeprobe",t:"The Hope Mars Mission",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🔴",w:"Emirates Mars Mission",r:["mars","uaecountry","spaceagencies","tianwen","phobosdeimos"],
p:[["The Arab world’s first Mars mission","Hope is the United Arab Emirates’ Mars orbiter."],["Weather on Mars","It studies Mars’s atmosphere and weather around the clock."],["Arrived in 2021","It reached Mars orbit in February 2021."]],
l:"The Emirates Mars Mission, known as Hope or Al-Amal in Arabic, is a spacecraft from the United Arab Emirates that orbits Mars to study its atmosphere and weather. It was the first interplanetary mission by an Arab country. Hope was built by the Mohammed bin Rashid Space Centre in Dubai with partner universities in the United States, a model that helped Emirati engineers gain skills quickly. It was launched on a Japanese H-IIA rocket from Tanegashima Space Center on 20 July 2020 (Japan time) and entered Mars orbit on 9 February 2021, making the UAE the fifth space agency to reach Mars orbit, and the second to do so on its first attempt. Hope’s large, stretched orbit, which takes about 55 hours, lets it see nearly the whole planet at every time of day, giving the first complete picture of Martian weather through the day and seasons. It has studied dust storms, auroras and how hydrogen and oxygen escape from Mars’s upper atmosphere into space, which helps explain how Mars lost its water. In 2023, Hope made the closest flyby of the moon Deimos in decades. The mission was planned to coincide with the UAE’s 50th anniversary in 2021.",
s:[["Building fast","Hope went from design to launch in about six years. Emirati engineers, most of them young, worked alongside experts at the University of Colorado Boulder, Arizona State University and the University of California, Berkeley."],["A global weather satellite","Most Mars orbiters pass over places at the same local time each day. Hope’s orbit lets it watch each region at different times, so scientists can see how temperatures, dust and ice clouds change through a Martian day."],["Mars’s auroras","Hope captured images of ‘discrete auroras’ across Mars’s night side, glowing patterns linked to patches of magnetism in Mars’s crust and to the solar wind."],["Launch in 2020","Hope was one of three Mars missions launched in July 2020, along with China’s Tianwen-1 and NASA’s Perseverance rover, taking advantage of a window when Earth and Mars line up every 26 months."]],
k:[["Country","United Arab Emirates"],["Launched","20 July 2020 (Japan time), H-IIA rocket"],["Arrived at Mars","9 February 2021"],["Orbit period","About 55 hours"],["Main goal","Study Mars’s atmosphere and weather"]],
d:[["2014","UAE announces its Mars mission"],["2020","Hope launched from Japan"],["2021","Hope enters Mars orbit"],["2021","First science data released"],["2023","Close flyby of Deimos"]],
s2:[["The UAE in space","The UAE also sent its first astronaut, Hazzaa Al Mansouri, to the International Space Station in 2019, and astronaut Sultan Al Neyadi spent six months there in 2023, making the first spacewalk by an Arab astronaut. Its Rashid rover flew on a Japanese lander that crashed on the Moon in April 2023."],["Sharing data","All of Hope’s science data are released freely online for scientists and the public around the world."]],
sp:["Deimos up close","In 2023, Hope flew within about 100 kilometers of Deimos, the smaller of Mars’s two moons, and found evidence that Deimos may have formed from material blasted off Mars rather than being a captured asteroid."],
g:[["Interplanetary","Traveling between planets"],["Orbit insertion","Slowing down to be captured into orbit"],["Atmospheric escape","Gas leaking from a planet into space"],["Launch window","The best period to launch toward a target"]],
f:["Hope is the Arab world’s first interplanetary mission.","It reached Mars orbit in 2021.","It studies Martian weather through the day."],
fun:"Hope’s arrival at Mars was timed for 2021, the 50th anniversary year of the founding of the United Arab Emirates."});

ADDART({id:"isromissions",t:"India’s Space Missions",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🇮🇳",w:"Indian Space Research Organisation",r:["chandrayaan","indiacountry","spaceagencies","mars","sun"],
p:[["Mars on the first try","India reached Mars orbit on its first attempt in 2014."],["Watching the Sun","Aditya-L1 studies the Sun from a point 1.5 million kilometers away."],["Astronauts next","India is preparing to launch its own astronauts with Gaganyaan."]],
l:"India’s space program, run by the Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO), founded in 1969, is known for ambitious missions achieved at low cost. Led in its early years by physicist Vikram Sarabhai, it launched India’s first satellite, Aryabhata, on a Soviet rocket in 1975 and its own satellite launcher in 1980. India’s Mars Orbiter Mission, nicknamed Mangalyaan, launched on 5 November 2013 and entered orbit around Mars on 24 September 2014, making India the first Asian country to reach Mars orbit and the first nation to do so on its first attempt, at a cost of about 74 million dollars, less than many Hollywood films. It operated for about eight years. The Chandrayaan missions explored the Moon, and in August 2023, Chandrayaan-3 made India the first country to land near the lunar south pole. Aditya-L1, India’s first solar observatory, launched on 2 September 2023 and reached the L1 point in January 2024. Gaganyaan is India’s human spaceflight program; after uncrewed test flights, the first planned for late 2026 carrying a humanoid robot called Vyommitra, India plans to launch its own astronauts in a crew capsule. In 2025, Indian Air Force pilot Shubhanshu Shukla became the second Indian in space, flying to the International Space Station.",
s:[["Mangalyaan","Mangalyaan was built in about 15 months. Because India’s rocket was not powerful enough to send it straight to Mars, it spent nearly a month raising its orbit around Earth step by step before heading off. It photographed Mars and its moon Phobos and studied the atmosphere."],["Launching satellites","ISRO’s PSLV rocket is known for reliability and launching many satellites at once; in February 2017 it carried 104 satellites on one launch, a record at the time. ISRO also launches satellites for other countries."],["Aditya-L1","Aditya, meaning ‘Sun’ in Sanskrit, carries seven instruments to study the Sun’s outer atmosphere, solar flares and the solar wind from a halo orbit around the L1 point, where it has a constant view of the Sun."],["Gaganyaan","Four Indian Air Force pilots were named in 2024 as astronaut-designates for Gaganyaan, including Shubhanshu Shukla. The capsule is designed to carry up to three astronauts into low Earth orbit for several days."]],
k:[["ISRO founded","1969"],["First satellite","Aryabhata, 1975"],["Mars Orbiter Mission","Launched 2013; reached Mars 24 September 2014"],["Aditya-L1","Launched 2 September 2023"],["Human spaceflight","Gaganyaan (in preparation)"]],
d:[["1975","Aryabhata launched"],["1984","Rakesh Sharma becomes the first Indian in space"],["2008","Chandrayaan-1 launched"],["2014","Mangalyaan enters Mars orbit"],["2023","Chandrayaan-3 lands; Aditya-L1 launched"],["2025","Shubhanshu Shukla flies to the ISS"]],
s2:[["A bicycle and a bullock cart","In ISRO’s early days, rocket parts were carried to the launch site on bicycles, and in 1981 a satellite was moved on a bullock cart, images often used to show how far the program has come."],["Rakesh Sharma","In 1984, Rakesh Sharma flew on a Soviet spacecraft. Asked by Prime Minister Indira Gandhi how India looked from space, he replied, quoting a famous poem, that it was ‘better than the whole world’."]],
sp:["Mars on a banknote","Mangalyaan was so celebrated in India that it appeared on the country’s 2,000-rupee banknote, introduced in 2016."],
g:[["ISRO","Indian Space Research Organisation"],["Mangalyaan","India’s Mars Orbiter Mission (mangal means Mars)"],["Halo orbit","A looping orbit around a Lagrange point"],["Astronaut-designate","A person selected and training for spaceflight"]],
f:["India reached Mars orbit on its first try in 2014.","Aditya-L1 watches the Sun.","Chandrayaan-3 landed near the Moon’s south pole in 2023."],
fun:"India’s Mars mission cost less than the Hollywood space film Gravity."});

ADDART({id:"spacex",t:"SpaceX",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🚀",w:"SpaceX",r:["reusablerockets","starship","commspace","megaconstellations","iss"],
p:[["A rocket company","SpaceX is an American company that builds rockets and spacecraft."],["Landing boosters","It made reusable rocket boosters routine."],["Most launches in the world","Its Falcon 9 rocket launches more often than any other."]],
l:"SpaceX (Space Exploration Technologies Corp.) is an American aerospace company founded in 2002 by entrepreneur Elon Musk with the goal of lowering the cost of spaceflight and eventually sending people to Mars. After three failures, its small Falcon 1 rocket became the first privately developed liquid-fueled rocket to reach orbit on 28 September 2008. Its Dragon capsule became the first commercial spacecraft to deliver cargo to the International Space Station in May 2012. On 21 December 2015, SpaceX landed the first stage of a Falcon 9 rocket back on land after an orbital launch, and it went on to make booster landings and reuse routine, with some boosters flying more than 30 times. The Falcon Heavy, first launched on 6 February 2018, sent a Tesla car toward Mars orbit as a test payload. On 30 May 2020, Crew Dragon carried NASA astronauts to the space station, the first crewed orbital launch by a private company. SpaceX’s Starlink network of more than 10,000 satellites provides internet service worldwide, and it is developing Starship, the largest rocket ever flown. SpaceX now launches far more often than any country, and on 12 June 2026 its shares began trading on the Nasdaq stock exchange after the largest public share sale in history, which raised about 75 billion dollars.",
s:[["Early struggles","SpaceX’s first three Falcon 1 launches, from Kwajalein Atoll in the Pacific, failed between 2006 and 2008. Musk later said the company had money for only one more try. The fourth launch succeeded, and months later NASA awarded SpaceX a contract to resupply the space station."],["Reusable rockets","Falcon 9 boosters fly back and land on landing pads or drone ships at sea using their engines and grid fins. Reusing boosters has greatly cut the cost of launches. SpaceX also recovers and reuses the nose cones, called fairings."],["Starlink","SpaceX began launching Starlink satellites in 2019 and has put thousands in orbit, the largest satellite network ever. Starlink serves millions of customers, including ships, aircraft and remote areas, but astronomers worry about satellite streaks spoiling observations."],["Criticism and concerns","SpaceX’s rapid testing has caused spectacular explosions, and some environmental groups and residents near its Texas base have raised concerns. Its dominance has also prompted debate about how much governments should depend on one company."]],
k:[["Founded","2002, by Elon Musk"],["First private liquid-fueled rocket to orbit","Falcon 1, 28 September 2008"],["First orbital booster landing","21 December 2015"],["First crewed launch","30 May 2020"],["Headquarters","Starbase, Texas"]],
d:[["2002","SpaceX founded"],["2008","Falcon 1 reaches orbit"],["2010","First Falcon 9 launch"],["2012","Dragon delivers cargo to the ISS"],["2015","First Falcon 9 booster landing"],["2018","Falcon Heavy debut"],["2020","Crew Dragon carries astronauts to the ISS"],["2026","Shares begin trading on the Nasdaq stock exchange"]],
s2:[["Falcon Heavy’s Starman","The first Falcon Heavy carried Elon Musk’s red Tesla Roadster, with a mannequin in a spacesuit named Starman at the wheel, into an orbit around the Sun that crosses the orbit of Mars."],["Launch rate","In the 2020s, SpaceX began launching more than 100 times a year, sometimes several times in a single day, carrying most of the world’s payloads into orbit."]],
sp:["Drone ship names","SpaceX’s landing ships are named after spaceships from science fiction novels by Iain M. Banks, such as ‘Of Course I Still Love You’ and ‘A Shortfall of Gravitas’."],
g:[["Booster","The first stage of a rocket"],["Drone ship","An uncrewed ship used as a landing platform"],["Payload","The cargo a rocket carries"],["Fairing","The nose cone that protects a payload"]],
f:["SpaceX was founded in 2002.","It made reusable rocket boosters routine.","Crew Dragon first carried astronauts in 2020."],
fun:"Some SpaceX Falcon 9 boosters have flown to space and landed back on Earth more than 30 times."});

ADDART({id:"blueorigin",t:"Blue Origin",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🪶",w:"Blue Origin",r:["reusablerockets","spacetourism","commspace","artemis","rocketengines"],
p:[["Jeff Bezos’s rocket company","Blue Origin was founded by Amazon founder Jeff Bezos in 2000."],["Space tourism","Its New Shepard rocket carries tourists on short trips to space."],["New Glenn","Its large New Glenn rocket first reached orbit in 2025."]],
l:"Blue Origin is an American aerospace company founded in 2000 by Jeff Bezos, the founder of Amazon, with the long-term goal of millions of people living and working in space. Its motto is Gradatim Ferociter, Latin for ‘step by step, ferociously’. Its first rocket, New Shepard, named after astronaut Alan Shepard, is a reusable suborbital rocket that carries a capsule with up to six people past the Kármán line, about 100 kilometers up, for a few minutes of weightlessness before the capsule parachutes back to Earth. On 23 November 2015, New Shepard became the first rocket to fly to space and land vertically. Bezos himself flew on its first crewed flight on 20 July 2021, and later passengers included actor William Shatner, who at 90 became the oldest person in space at the time. Blue Origin also builds BE-4 rocket engines, which power its own New Glenn rocket and United Launch Alliance’s Vulcan. New Glenn, about 98 meters tall, reached orbit on its first flight on 16 January 2025, and on its second flight in November 2025 its booster landed on a ship at sea. After problems in 2026, including a launch on which the upper stage underperformed and a rocket explosion during a ground test, the company has been rebuilding its launch pad and plans to return New Glenn to flight in early 2027. Blue Origin is also building the Blue Moon lander for NASA’s Artemis program.",
s:[["New Shepard","New Shepard flights last about 10 to 11 minutes. The capsule has some of the largest windows ever flown in space. The booster lands upright on a pad in the West Texas desert, and capsules and boosters are reused. In early 2026, Blue Origin paused New Shepard flights to focus on New Glenn and its Moon lander."],["New Glenn","Named after John Glenn, the first American to orbit Earth, New Glenn’s first stage is designed to be reused at least 25 times. Its second flight launched NASA’s twin ESCAPADE probes toward Mars. In 2026, the rocket was grounded after an underperforming launch in April and an explosion during an engine test in May that damaged its launch pad."],["Blue Moon","In 2023, NASA selected Blue Origin to build a lander for the Artemis V mission. A smaller uncrewed lander, Blue Moon Mark 1, is designed to deliver cargo to the Moon."],["Rivalry","Blue Origin and SpaceX have competed for contracts and attention, and their founders have exchanged public jabs. Blue Origin has moved more slowly than SpaceX but has steadily added capabilities."]],
k:[["Founded","2000, by Jeff Bezos"],["Motto","Gradatim Ferociter (step by step, ferociously)"],["Suborbital rocket","New Shepard"],["Orbital rocket","New Glenn (first launch 16 January 2025)"],["Engine","BE-4"]],
d:[["2000","Blue Origin founded"],["2015","New Shepard lands after reaching space"],["2021","Jeff Bezos flies on New Shepard"],["2021","William Shatner flies to space at 90"],["2025","New Glenn reaches orbit on its first flight"],["2025","New Glenn booster lands at sea"]],
s2:[["The feather","Blue Origin’s logo features a feather, symbolizing flight, and its rockets often carry a feather design. Its headquarters are in Kent, Washington, near Seattle."],["An all-female crew","In April 2025, New Shepard flew an all-female crew of six, including singer Katy Perry, the first all-female spaceflight since Valentina Tereshkova’s solo flight in 1963."]],
sp:["The tortoise","Blue Origin’s coat of arms shows two tortoises, a reference to the fable of the tortoise and the hare, reflecting its slow-and-steady philosophy."],
g:[["Suborbital","Reaching space without going into orbit"],["Kármán line","A boundary of space, about 100 kilometers up"],["Lander","A spacecraft that touches down on a surface"],["Grounded","Not allowed to fly until problems are fixed"]],
f:["Blue Origin was founded in 2000.","New Shepard carries tourists to space.","New Glenn first reached orbit in 2025."],
fun:"William Shatner, who played Captain Kirk in Star Trek, flew to space on Blue Origin’s New Shepard at the age of 90."});

ADDART({id:"commercialcrew",t:"Crew Dragon and Starliner",c:"Space Exploration",i:"👩‍🚀",w:"Commercial Crew Program",r:["spacex","iss","commspace","spaceshuttle","spacetourism"],
p:[["Private taxis to space","NASA pays companies to carry astronauts to the space station."],["Crew Dragon","SpaceX’s Crew Dragon has flown astronauts since 2020."],["Starliner’s troubles","Boeing’s Starliner has faced repeated problems."]],
l:"NASA’s Commercial Crew Program pays private companies to build and operate spacecraft that carry astronauts to the International Space Station, rather than NASA owning the vehicles itself. After the Space Shuttle retired in 2011, the United States relied on Russian Soyuz spacecraft to send astronauts to orbit, paying tens of millions of dollars per seat. In 2014, NASA chose SpaceX and Boeing to develop new crew spacecraft. SpaceX’s Crew Dragon launched NASA astronauts Bob Behnken and Doug Hurley on 30 May 2020, the first crewed orbital launch from the United States in nine years, and has since flown regular crews as well as private missions such as Inspiration4 in 2021, the first all-civilian orbital flight, and Polaris Dawn in 2024, which included the first commercial spacewalk. Boeing’s CST-100 Starliner suffered software problems on an uncrewed test in 2019. Its first crewed test in June 2024 carried Butch Wilmore and Suni Williams, but thruster failures and helium leaks led NASA to bring Starliner home empty; the two astronauts returned on a Crew Dragon in March 2025 after about nine months in space instead of about a week. Starliner’s next flight is planned to carry only cargo.",
s:[["How it works","Unlike the Space Shuttle, owned and run by NASA, commercial crew spacecraft are owned by the companies, which can sell seats to other customers. NASA set safety requirements and paid fixed prices for development and flights."],["Crew Dragon","Crew Dragon docks automatically with the station, has touchscreen controls and splashes down under parachutes in the ocean. It can carry up to four astronauts on NASA missions, and capsules are reused for several flights."],["Starliner’s problems","On its 2019 test, a clock error left Starliner in the wrong orbit and it could not reach the station. A second uncrewed test reached the station in 2022. During the 2024 crewed test, several thrusters overheated and failed. NASA and Boeing later agreed that the next Starliner mission would fly cargo only, with crewed flights to follow."],["Private missions","Companies such as Axiom Space buy Crew Dragon flights to send private astronauts to the space station, including astronauts from countries such as India, Poland, Hungary, Turkey and Saudi Arabia."]],
k:[["Program","NASA Commercial Crew Program"],["Companies selected","SpaceX and Boeing (2014)"],["First Crew Dragon crewed flight","30 May 2020"],["First Starliner crewed flight","5 June 2024"],["Crew size","Up to 4 on NASA missions"]],
d:[["2011","Space Shuttle retires"],["2014","NASA selects SpaceX and Boeing"],["2019","Starliner uncrewed test fails to reach the ISS"],["2020","Crew Dragon’s first crewed flight"],["2021","Inspiration4 all-civilian flight"],["2024","Starliner crewed test; Polaris Dawn spacewalk"],["2025","Wilmore and Williams return on Crew Dragon"]],
s2:[["Stuck in space?","News reports often said Wilmore and Williams were ‘stranded’, but NASA and the astronauts stressed that they were safe aboard the station, working as part of the crew, and had a ride home available at all times."],["Saving money","NASA estimated that commercial crew saved billions of dollars compared with developing its own spacecraft. The program is often cited as a model for buying services from private companies."]],
sp:["The flag","When the Space Shuttle left the ISS for the last time in 2011, astronauts left a small US flag there, promising it would be collected by the next astronauts to launch from America. Hurley and Behnken retrieved it in 2020."],
g:[["Crewed","Carrying people"],["Docking","Joining two spacecraft in orbit"],["Thruster","A small rocket engine used for steering"],["Splashdown","Landing a spacecraft in water"]],
f:["Crew Dragon first carried astronauts in 2020.","Starliner’s 2024 crew returned on Crew Dragon.","Polaris Dawn made the first commercial spacewalk."],
fun:"Two astronauts expecting an eight-day test flight on Starliner in 2024 ended up spending about nine months in space."});

ADDART({id:"sls",t:"The Space Launch System and Orion",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🌙",w:"Space Launch System",r:["artemis","moon","apollo","rocket","starship"],
p:[["NASA’s Moon rocket","SLS is NASA’s giant rocket for the Artemis Moon missions."],["Orion capsule","Orion carries astronauts to the Moon and back."],["Around the Moon in 2026","Artemis II carried four astronauts around the Moon in April 2026."]],
l:"The Space Launch System (SLS) is a super heavy-lift rocket built by NASA to send astronauts and cargo to the Moon and beyond, and Orion is the crew spacecraft it launches. Developed from 2011 using engines and boosters from the Space Shuttle, SLS stands about 98 meters tall and produces about 39 meganewtons (8.8 million pounds) of thrust at liftoff, more than the Saturn V of the Apollo era. Orion, built by Lockheed Martin with a service module provided by the European Space Agency, can carry four astronauts and has a heat shield designed for the extreme speeds of returning from the Moon. After years of delays and cost increases, SLS first launched on the uncrewed Artemis I mission on 16 November 2022, sending Orion around the Moon. On 1 April 2026, Artemis II launched four astronauts, Reid Wiseman, Victor Glover, Christina Koch and Canadian Jeremy Hansen, on a ten-day flight around the Moon, the first crewed lunar voyage since 1972. Each SLS rocket is used only once and costs billions of dollars, and some critics favor cheaper reusable rockets, but Congress has continued to fund SLS for further Artemis missions.",
s:[["Parts of SLS","SLS uses a huge orange core stage with four RS-25 engines, many of them flown on Space Shuttle missions, plus two solid rocket boosters, each larger than the Shuttle’s. An upper stage then sends Orion toward the Moon."],["Orion","Orion’s capsule has more room than Apollo’s, with computers, life support and a toilet. Its European Service Module provides power, propulsion, air and water. After Artemis I, engineers studied unexpected damage to the heat shield before flying people."],["Artemis II","Artemis II flew around the far side of the Moon and returned to splash down in the Pacific Ocean on 10 April 2026. Its crew included the first woman, the first Black astronaut and the first non-American to travel to the vicinity of the Moon."],["Cost and debate","NASA’s inspector general estimated that each early SLS launch costs more than 2 billion dollars, plus the cost of Orion. Supporters say SLS provides a proven, government-controlled capability; critics argue commercial rockets could do the job more cheaply. US lawmakers in 2025–2026 continued funding SLS and Orion for later Artemis missions."]],
k:[["Height","About 98 meters"],["Liftoff thrust","About 39 meganewtons (8.8 million pounds)"],["First launch","Artemis I, 16 November 2022"],["First crewed launch","Artemis II, 1 April 2026"],["Crew capacity (Orion)","4 astronauts"]],
d:[["2011","SLS program announced"],["2014","Uncrewed Orion test flight"],["2022","Artemis I launches"],["2022","Orion splashes down after circling the Moon"],["2026","Artemis II carries four astronauts around the Moon"]],
s2:[["Shuttle heritage","SLS reuses designs and even actual hardware from the Space Shuttle program, including RS-25 engines that flew on earlier shuttle missions, which supporters said would save time and money."],["Next missions","NASA plans Artemis III as a test flight in Earth orbit in 2027 to practice docking with commercial landers, with a crewed Moon landing planned on a later mission."]],
sp:["Snoopy in space","A plush Snoopy toy flew on Artemis I as the zero-gravity indicator. When it began to float freely in the cabin, cameras showed that the spacecraft had reached weightlessness."],
g:[["Heavy-lift rocket","A rocket that carries very large payloads"],["Core stage","The main central body of a rocket"],["Service module","The part of a spacecraft that provides power and propulsion"],["Heat shield","A protective layer for reentry"]],
f:["SLS first launched in 2022.","Orion carries up to four astronauts.","Artemis II flew around the Moon in April 2026."],
fun:"Some of the RS-25 engines on SLS rockets previously flew to space on Space Shuttle missions."});

ADDART({id:"ariane",t:"Ariane Rockets",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🇪🇺",w:"Ariane (rocket family)",r:["spaceagencies","rocket","jwst","spaceports","rosetta"],
p:[["Europe’s rockets","Ariane rockets give Europe its own access to space."],["Launched from the jungle","They lift off from French Guiana in South America."],["Webb’s ride","An Ariane 5 launched the James Webb Space Telescope."]],
l:"Ariane is a family of European rockets developed for the European Space Agency (ESA) and operated by the company Arianespace, giving Europe independent access to space. The program began in the 1970s after Europe struggled to get its satellites launched by others. Ariane 1 first flew on 24 December 1979 from the Guiana Space Centre in Kourou, French Guiana, a site close to the equator, where Earth’s rotation gives rockets an extra boost. Ariane rockets became leaders in launching commercial communications satellites in the 1980s and 1990s. Ariane 5, first launched in 1996, carried many famous missions, including the Rosetta comet probe in 2004 and the James Webb Space Telescope on 25 December 2021, and flew for the last time in July 2023. Its successor, Ariane 6, made its first flight on 9 July 2024. Ariane 6 comes in two versions, with two or four strap-on boosters; the more powerful Ariane 64 first flew on 12 February 2026, carrying 32 satellites for Amazon’s Leo internet network. Europe faces strong competition from SpaceX’s reusable rockets and is studying reusable designs.",
s:[["Kourou","The Guiana Space Centre lies about 5 degrees north of the equator. Launching eastward from near the equator takes advantage of Earth’s rotation, which saves fuel. Its location also allows rockets to launch over the ocean."],["Ariane 5’s first flight","On 4 June 1996, the first Ariane 5 veered off course and self-destructed 37 seconds after launch. A software error, caused by converting a large number into a format too small to hold it, became one of the most famous bugs in computing history."],["Launching Webb","Ariane 5 placed the James Webb Space Telescope on such an accurate path that Webb saved fuel, extending its expected lifetime to perhaps 20 years."],["Ariane 6","Ariane 6 was designed to be cheaper than Ariane 5, with an upper stage that can restart several times to deliver satellites to different orbits. Its debut followed years of delays, during which Europe briefly had no rocket of its own for large satellites."]],
k:[["Operator","Arianespace"],["Launch site","Guiana Space Centre, Kourou, French Guiana"],["First Ariane launch","24 December 1979"],["Ariane 5","1996–2023"],["Ariane 6 first flight","9 July 2024"]],
d:[["1979","Ariane 1 first flight"],["1980","Arianespace founded"],["1996","Ariane 5 first flight fails"],["2004","Ariane 5 launches Rosetta"],["2021","Ariane 5 launches the James Webb Space Telescope"],["2024","Ariane 6 first flight"],["2026","First Ariane 64 launch"]],
s2:[["The name","Ariane is the French form of Ariadne, the princess in Greek mythology who helped Theseus escape the labyrinth. It was chosen in 1973 by the French minister responsible for the program."],["Vega","Europe also operates Vega, a smaller rocket for lighter satellites. Its upgraded version, Vega-C, is now operated by the Italian company Avio."]],
sp:["Webb’s Christmas launch","The James Webb Space Telescope, the most expensive science mission ever, lifted off on an Ariane 5 on Christmas Day 2021, a launch watched by millions around the world."],
g:[["Launch vehicle","A rocket that carries payloads to space"],["Strap-on booster","An extra rocket attached to the side"],["Upper stage","The top part of a rocket that reaches orbit"],["Commercial satellite","A satellite owned by a company"]],
f:["Ariane 1 first flew in 1979.","Ariane rockets launch from French Guiana.","An Ariane 5 launched the Webb telescope."],
fun:"A single software bug destroyed the first Ariane 5 rocket only 37 seconds after launch in 1996."});

ADDART({id:"asteroidmining",t:"Asteroid Mining",c:"Space Exploration",i:"⛏️",w:"Asteroid mining",r:["asteroidbelt","psyche","osirisrex","hayabusa","spacelaw"],
p:[["Mines in space","Asteroid mining means extracting materials from asteroids."],["Water and metals","Asteroids contain water, iron, nickel and precious metals."],["Not yet","No one has mined an asteroid yet, but missions have collected samples."]],
l:"Asteroid mining is the idea of extracting useful materials from asteroids, the rocky and metallic leftovers from the formation of the solar system. Some asteroids contain water ice, which could be turned into drinking water, oxygen and rocket fuel for spacecraft, and others are rich in iron, nickel, cobalt and platinum-group metals. Supporters argue that mining in space could supply materials for building in orbit and on the Moon without lifting them from Earth, reduce the need for harmful mining on Earth and create new industries. Space missions have shown that reaching and sampling asteroids is possible: Japan’s Hayabusa and Hayabusa2 returned tiny samples from the asteroids Itokawa and Ryugu, and NASA’s OSIRIS-REx brought about 121.6 grams of material from the asteroid Bennu to Earth in 2023. NASA’s Psyche mission, launched in October 2023, is traveling to a metal-rich asteroid. However, mining asteroids on a large scale faces huge challenges, including the cost of reaching them, working in very low gravity and processing materials in space. Early asteroid-mining companies in the 2010s went out of business, and the legal rules for owning resources from space are still debated.",
s:[["Types of asteroids","Carbon-rich C-type asteroids, the most common, contain water locked in minerals and organic compounds. Stony S-type asteroids contain silicate rock and some metal. Rarer M-type asteroids, such as Psyche, may be largely iron and nickel."],["Water first","Many experts think water will be the first space resource worth mining, because it is expensive to launch from Earth. Split into hydrogen and oxygen, it can fuel rockets, making asteroids and the Moon potential ‘gas stations’ in space."],["Who owns it?","The 1967 Outer Space Treaty bans countries from claiming celestial bodies but does not clearly address resources. The United States in 2015 and Luxembourg in 2017 passed laws allowing companies to own resources they extract, and the Artemis Accords support resource use, but some countries disagree."],["Companies","Planetary Resources and Deep Space Industries attracted attention in the 2010s but were sold by 2019. Newer startups such as AstroForge have launched small test missions, so far without reaching an asteroid."]],
k:[["Valuable resources","Water, iron, nickel, platinum-group metals"],["Bennu sample","About 121.6 grams (OSIRIS-REx, 2023)"],["Metal asteroid mission","Psyche (launched 2023)"],["US law allowing ownership","2015"],["Status","No commercial mining yet"]],
d:[["2010","Hayabusa returns samples from Itokawa"],["2015","US law on space resource ownership"],["2017","Luxembourg passes space resources law"],["2020","Hayabusa2 returns samples from Ryugu"],["2023","OSIRIS-REx returns samples from Bennu; Psyche launched"]],
s2:[["Trillion-dollar asteroids","Headlines sometimes claim that asteroids like Psyche are worth quadrillions of dollars. Such figures are misleading, because bringing so much metal to Earth would crash its price, and the cost of mining is unknown."],["Science first","Even if mining never pays off, asteroid samples are scientifically valuable. Ryugu and Bennu samples contained water-bearing minerals and organic molecules, including amino acids and all five bases found in DNA and RNA."]],
sp:["Mining in science fiction","Asteroid mining has long been a theme of science fiction, from early 20th-century stories to the TV series The Expanse, where miners in the asteroid belt form their own society."],
g:[["Space resources","Materials found in space that could be used"],["Platinum-group metals","Rare metals including platinum and palladium"],["Sample return","Bringing material from space to Earth"],["Outer Space Treaty","The 1967 treaty forming the basis of space law"]],
f:["No asteroid has been commercially mined yet.","OSIRIS-REx brought back about 121.6 grams of Bennu.","Water may be the first space resource mined."],
fun:"Water mined from asteroids could someday be turned into rocket fuel, creating gas stations in space."});

ADDART({id:"spacesolar",t:"Space-Based Solar Power",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🔆",w:"Space-based solar power",r:["solarpower","sat","renew","moonbases","orbits"],
p:[["Sunshine from orbit","Space-based solar power would collect sunlight in space and beam it to Earth."],["No night, no clouds","In orbit, solar panels can catch sunlight almost all the time."],["Early tests","Small experiments have beamed power in space."]],
l:"Space-based solar power is a proposed way of generating electricity by collecting sunlight with large solar panels in orbit and transmitting the energy to Earth, usually as microwaves or laser beams, where receiving antennas would convert it back into electricity. In space, there is no night for satellites in high orbits much of the time, no clouds and no atmosphere to weaken sunlight, so a panel can collect several times more energy than the same panel on the ground. The idea appeared in Isaac Asimov’s 1941 science fiction story ‘Reason’, and engineer Peter Glaser patented a detailed design in 1973. NASA and the US Department of Energy studied it in the 1970s but found it far too expensive. Interest has revived because rocket launches have become cheaper and the need for clean energy has grown. In 2023, a California Institute of Technology experiment called MAPLE became the first to beam power wirelessly in space and detect it on Earth. Japan, China, the United Kingdom and the European Space Agency are studying or planning demonstrations. Critics point to the enormous size and cost of power stations, the difficulty of building them in orbit and concerns about beam safety.",
s:[["How it would work","A solar power satellite in geostationary orbit, 36,000 kilometers up, would stay above one place on Earth. It would convert sunlight into electricity and then into a microwave beam aimed at a ground station called a rectenna, which could cover several square kilometers."],["Safety","Proposed microwave beams would be spread out and relatively weak, less intense than midday sunlight even at their center, so birds and planes passing through would not be harmed. Systems would include safeguards to shut off the beam if it wandered off target."],["Building in space","A useful power station might need solar arrays kilometers across, far larger than the International Space Station. Engineers propose building them robotically from many identical modules launched by reusable rockets."],["Advantages and doubts","Supporters say space solar power could provide steady, clean energy day and night and be directed to where it is needed, such as disaster zones. Skeptics argue that ground-based solar panels with batteries are becoming so cheap that space power may never compete."]],
k:[["Early idea","Isaac Asimov, ‘Reason’ (1941)"],["Patent","Peter Glaser, 1973"],["Transmission","Microwaves or lasers"],["First power beamed in space","Caltech MAPLE, 2023"],["Status","Experimental"]],
d:[["1941","Asimov’s story imagines a solar power station"],["1968","Peter Glaser proposes solar power satellites"],["1973","Glaser’s patent granted"],["1979","NASA and Energy Department complete a major study"],["2023","Caltech beams power in space"]],
s2:[["Japan’s plans","Japan has researched space solar power for decades and has tested microwave power transmission on the ground, with plans for small demonstrations from orbit."],["Power for the Moon","Similar technology could beam power to rovers and bases in the dark craters near the Moon’s poles, where sunlight never reaches."]],
sp:["Peter Glaser","Peter Glaser was a Czech-born American engineer. His 1968 article in the journal Science started serious study of space-based solar power."],
g:[["Rectenna","A receiving antenna that converts microwaves into electricity"],["Geostationary orbit","An orbit that keeps a satellite over one spot"],["Microwave","A type of radio wave"],["Wireless power","Sending energy without wires"]],
f:["Space solar panels can collect sunlight almost all the time.","The idea appeared in a 1941 story.","Caltech beamed power in space in 2023."],
fun:"The idea of beaming solar power from space first appeared in a science fiction story by Isaac Asimov in 1941."});

ADDART({id:"shoemakerlevy9",t:"Comet Shoemaker–Levy 9",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"💥",w:"Comet Shoemaker–Levy 9",r:["jupiter","comet","planetdefense","galileoprobe","hubblest"],
p:[["A comet hits Jupiter","In July 1994, pieces of a comet crashed into Jupiter."],["Torn apart","Jupiter’s gravity had broken the comet into more than 20 pieces."],["Scars larger than Earth","The impacts left dark marks bigger than our planet."]],
l:"Comet Shoemaker–Levy 9 was a comet that broke apart and collided with Jupiter in July 1994, the first collision between two solar system bodies ever directly observed. It was discovered in March 1993 by astronomers Carolyn and Eugene Shoemaker and David Levy using a telescope at Palomar Observatory in California; it was the ninth short-period comet the team had found together. Unusually, the comet was orbiting Jupiter rather than the Sun, having been captured by the planet’s gravity decades earlier, and it appeared as a string of fragments, described as a ‘string of pearls’. Calculations showed that the comet had passed so close to Jupiter in July 1992 that tides had torn it apart, and that the fragments would strike Jupiter two years later. Between 16 and 22 July 1994, about 21 fragments hit Jupiter’s southern hemisphere at about 60 kilometers per second, creating fireballs and dark scars, some larger than Earth, that lasted for weeks. Telescopes worldwide, including the Hubble Space Telescope, watched, and the Galileo spacecraft saw the impacts directly. The event showed how large impacts shape planets and raised public awareness of the danger asteroids and comets pose to Earth.",
s:[["The impacts","The largest fragment, labeled G, struck on 18 July 1994 with energy estimated at about 6 million megatons of TNT, hundreds of times more than all the world’s nuclear weapons combined. Plumes rose thousands of kilometers above Jupiter’s clouds."],["Jupiter the protector?","Jupiter’s strong gravity captures or flings away many comets, and some scientists suggested it shields Earth from impacts. Others argue that Jupiter also sends some comets and asteroids toward the inner solar system, so its overall effect is debated."],["Planetary defense","The spectacular impacts helped persuade the US Congress in 1998 to direct NASA to find most large near-Earth asteroids, leading to modern planetary defense programs and missions such as DART."],["Learning about Jupiter","The impacts stirred up material from below Jupiter’s cloud tops, letting scientists detect chemicals such as sulfur and ammonia compounds and study how Jupiter’s atmosphere moves."]],
k:[["Discovered","March 1993, Palomar Observatory"],["Discoverers","Carolyn and Eugene Shoemaker, David Levy"],["Broken apart","July 1992 by Jupiter’s gravity"],["Impacts","16–22 July 1994"],["Fragments","About 21"]],
d:[["1992","Comet torn apart by Jupiter’s gravity"],["1993","Comet discovered"],["1994","Fragments strike Jupiter"],["1998","US Congress directs NASA to search for near-Earth asteroids"],["2009","Another object hits Jupiter, seen by an amateur astronomer"]],
s2:[["Eugene Shoemaker","Geologist Eugene Shoemaker proved that Meteor Crater in Arizona was made by an impact and trained Apollo astronauts in geology. After his death in 1997, some of his ashes were carried to the Moon by NASA’s Lunar Prospector, making him the only person whose remains rest there."],["Carolyn Shoemaker","Carolyn Shoemaker began searching for comets in her 50s and discovered 32 comets and hundreds of asteroids, at one time more comets than any other living person."]],
sp:["Not so rare","Since 1994, amateur astronomers have recorded several smaller objects hitting Jupiter, appearing as brief flashes, showing that the giant planet is struck far more often than once thought."],
g:[["Comet","An icy body that grows a tail near the Sun"],["Tidal forces","Stretching forces caused by gravity"],["Fragment","A broken piece"],["Megaton","Energy equal to a million tons of TNT"]],
f:["Shoemaker–Levy 9 hit Jupiter in July 1994.","Jupiter’s gravity broke it into about 21 pieces.","Some impact scars were larger than Earth."],
fun:"Eugene Shoemaker, co-discoverer of the comet, is the only person whose ashes rest on the Moon."});

;
